{
  "raw": {
    "https://usyd.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/USYD_EXTERNAL_CAREER_SITE/job/Darlington-Campus/Casual-Executive-Assistant-Opportunities_0153452": {
      "title": "Casual Executive Assistant and Support Opportunities",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-01\nFull time\n\nThe University of Sydney is seeking expressions of interest into our casual Executive Assistant/Support talent pool. If you are seeking casual Executive Assistant/Support work, we would love to hear from you!   About the University   Be part of something bigger. Work with some of the brightest and most brilliant minds!  The University of Sydney is in the top 100 World University Rankings 2026. Our vibrant team of people are global leaders for good, through their research and contribution to our community. Join us as we embark on the next stage in the dynamic life of the University of Sydney.  We offer flexible employment arrangements so you can maintain a healthy work/life balance. We encourage innovation and excellence across the board. We’ve created an inspiring working environment and are always looking for people who can both contribute to and benefit from it.   About casual Executive Assistant/Support work at the University    We recruit experienced casual staff to fill temporary Executive Assistant/Support professional positions throughout the University. Many of the positions are based at the Camperdown/Darlington Campus. Typical assignments provide cover for staff absences, increases in workload, projects and new initiatives. In casual positions at the university, you will be rostered on a needs basis, and so please outline your availability when you submit your application.   Key Responsibilities Responsibilities that an Executive Assistant/Support may have in a role include; Provide high-level executive support, including calendar management, correspondence, travel arrangements, and meeting coordination.  Manage day-to-day office operations and administrative activities, ensuring efficient handling of enquiries and documentation.  Prepare agendas, papers, and minutes for board, committee, and sub-committee meetings, and coordinate governance elections and appointments.  Support communication across committee levels and develop governance documentation such as terms of reference and role descriptions.  Build and maintain effective relationships with internal stakeholders across the University and external partners, ensuring professional and confidential communication.  Facilitate the flow of information on behalf of the Executive, monitor follow-up actions, and assist with informed decision-making.  Oversee administrative systems related to finance and HR, ensuring compliance with University policies and procedures.  Maintain effective records and document management systems, and apply University policies to streamline administrative processes.  Coordinate and support the delivery of events, research conferences, workshops, and alumni activities, and undertake ad-hoc projects as required.  How to apply   Applications (including a cover letter, CV, and any additional supporting documentation) can be submitted via the Apply button at the top of the page. For a confidential discussion about opportunities, or if you require reasonable adjustment or any documents in alternate formats, please contact the Casual Recruitment team via email, on casual-recruitment.admin@sydney.edu.au    Pre-employment checks   There are some roles that have been designated as involving child-related work and may require a Working With Children Check clearance in accordance with the Child Protection (Working with Children) Act 2012. If you hold a current Working With Children Check please upload it with your application.   Your employment is conditional upon the completion of all role required pre-employment or background checks in terms satisfactory to the University. Similarly, your ongoing employment is conditional upon the satisfactory maintenance of all relevant clearances and background check requirements. If you do not meet these conditions, the University may take any necessary step, including the termination of your employment.  EEO statement    At the University of Sydney, our shared values include diversity and inclusion and we strive to be a place where everyone can thrive. We are committed to creating a University community which reflects the wider community that we serve. We deliver on this commitment through our people and culture programs, as well as key strategies to increase participation and support the careers of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander People , women , people living with a disability , people from culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds , and those who identify as LGBTIQ . We welcome applications from candidates from all backgrounds.   © The University of Sydney  The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment.  Click to view the Position Description for this role. Applications Close Wednesday 30 December 2026 11:59 PM"
    },
    "https://usyd.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/USYD_EXTERNAL_CAREER_SITE/job/Camperdown-Campus/Discipline-of-Accounting---Expression-of-Interest---Casual-Academic---University-of-Sydney-Business-School_0092798": {
      "title": "Expression of Interest - Casual Academic - School of Accounting, Governance and Regulation",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-06-17\nPart time\n\nCasual academic staff are employed to perform specified teaching-related duties, such as, presenting lectures and tutorials, conducting practical classes or workshops, and marking, under the Agreement. The casual rates for academic teaching duties include both the presentation or delivery time and the associated working time (including preparation, reasonably contemporaneous marking and student consultation). This role contributes to tutorials, workshops, assessment, marking, and student consultation in key discipline areas including Financial Information Systems, Sustainability, Financial Accounting, and Management Accounting , as well as broader topics in governance, regulation, and ethical practice. The position requires strong subject‑matter expertise, the ability to communicate complex accounting and regulatory concepts clearly, and a commitment to fostering an inclusive, engaging, and supportive learning environment. Casual Academics are expected to uphold academic standards, comply with university policies and procedures, and work collaboratively with course convenors and teaching teams to support student learning and academic success. Position Requirements To meet TEQSA and the University requirements, applicants must hold a qualification at least one AQF level higher than the level of study being taught (AQF+1), or demonstrate equivalent academic, professional, or practice‑based experience. For undergraduate teaching, applicants are required to hold a minimum of a master’s degree in accounting or a closely related discipline or possess substantial and relevant industry experience that demonstrates equivalent expertise. For postgraduate teaching, applicants are required to hold a completed PhD in Accounting or a closely related discipline, in accordance with University practice for postgraduate‑level teaching. Demonstrated expertise in one or more of the following areas: Financial Information Systems, Sustainability, Financial Accounting, and/or Management Accounting. Evidence of the capability to deliver high‑quality teaching and assessment, or relevant professional experience that supports effective student learning outcomes. Ability to work collaboratively with unit coordinators and teaching teams and to comply with university policies, academic standards, and the University of Sydney Enterprise Agreement. Requirements to Apply To be considered for this position, applicants must: Complete the online application form in full. Submit a one‑minute video outlining their areas of expertise , including relevant teaching experience, academic qualifications, and/or industry background aligned with the discipline. If your application is successful, the School will request a survey to be completed outlining specific questions, such as availability and work rights in Australia.  Since our inception, we have believed in education for all and leadership that improves lives in all the communities that we serve. Our strategy is simple: to create a place where the best researchers and most promising students can achieve their full potential.   The University values courage and creativity; openness and engagement; inclusion and diversity; and respect and integrity. As such, we see the importance of recruiting talent aligned to these values.    We are currently looking for Academic Casual staff to perform specified teaching-related duties, such as, presenting lectures and tutorials, conducting practical classes or workshops, and marking. Casual rates for academic teaching duties include both the presentation or delivery time and the associated working time (including preparation, reasonably contemporaneous marking, and student consultation) and further information can be found in The University of Sydney Enterprise Agreement 2023-2026.    Enterprise Agreement - Intranet - The University of Sydney       We look forward to receiving your application for this position.   Applications Close No End Date"
    },
    "https://usyd.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/USYD_EXTERNAL_CAREER_SITE/job/Camperdown-Campus/Expression-of-Interest---Casual-Academic-Roles---Sydney-School-of-Education-and-Social-Work_0146949": {
      "title": "Expression of Interest – Casual Academic Roles – Sydney School of Education and Social Work",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-02-06\nPart time\n\nDear Prospective Applicants, We invite applications for casual academic positions  within the  D iscipline of Social Work and Policy Studies  in the Sydney School of Education and Social Work  for Semester 1, 2026.   Key Information Before Applying: Visa & Workload Limits Student visa holders must comply with their visa’s work-hour restrictions. Australian citizens and permanent residents may not teach more than three Units of Study (UOS). Pay Rates (Indicative) TU2 Tutorial: $182.54 TU4 Repeat Tutorial: $121.69 M05 Marking: $60.51/hr A02 Other Academic Activities (including Capstone Supervision): $60.51/hr Contracts & Training Ongoing Casual Academics with active contracts must still complete the EOI form (no new contract needed). All casual academics must complete all training required by the Faculty and the School. Teaching Allocations Unit allocations may change due to enrolment fluctuations up to the census date. Location Staff must remain in Sydney for the duration of the semester, including exams. Remote overseas work is not permitted. Applications Only one application per person will be accepted. Multiple submissions will be disregarded. High application volume means not all eligible candidates will be selected. Please view and complete the application on a non-mobile device, as the form is designed to be accessed on a standard computer.   Application Process Complete the Expression of Interest (EOI) form. Upload the following required documents in PDF format: CV Select up to four preferred units. Unit Coordinators will review EOIs and may contact you for an interview. If appointed, the Coordination Team will finalise your employment, and HR will provide onboarding instructions. Please note: Onboarding may not guarantee an allocation of work. Hours will only be confirmed once the final CASS schedule has been approved and released. If you have not been contacted by  13 February 2026 , it means your application has not been successful at this stage. However, we will keep your details in our hiring pool and may get in touch if enrolments increase or if backup staff are required. Please note that being kept in the pool does not guarantee future work. We appreciate your interest and look forward to receiving your application. Best regards, Casual Academics Hiring Team Eligibility Requirements To meet  TEQSA requirements , applicants must hold a qualification at least one AQF level higher than the course of study being taught (AQF+1), or demonstrate equivalent academic, professional, or practice-based experience and expertise. Candidates who bring both professional experience and disciplinary knowledge will be highly valued. Expected Duties Depending on the role, unit of study, and your qualifications, duties may include: Delivering tutorials Supporting lectures and workshops Marking assessments and contributing to academic administration Casual academic rates will be commensurate with the duties undertaken, as outlined in the  University of Sydney Enterprise Agreement . About You We welcome EOIs from individuals who: Hold relevant qualifications or equivalent experience Can provide evidence of high-quality teaching experience aligned with their area(s) of interest Demonstrate ongoing professional and/or academic engagement Sponsorship / work rights for Australia You must have unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this employment to apply. Visa sponsorship is not available for this appointment. Pre-employment checks Your employment is conditional upon the completion of all roles requiring pre-employment or background checks in terms satisfactory to the University. Similarly, your ongoing employment is conditional upon the satisfactory maintenance of all relevant clearances and background check requirements. If you do not meet these conditions, the University may take any necessary step to ensure they are met and in the absence of this, can terminate your employment. Equal Employment Opportunity statement At the University of Sydney, our shared values are trust, accountability and excellence and we strive to be a place where everyone can thrive. We are committed to creating a University community that thrives through diversity and reflects the wider community that we serve. We deliver on this through our commitment to diversity and inclusion , evidenced by our people and culture programs, as well as key strategies to increase participation and support the careers of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander People , women , people living with a disability , people from culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds , and those who identify as LGBTIQ+ . We welcome applications from candidates from all backgrounds.  We are proud to be recognised as an Australian Workplace Equality Index (AWEI) Gold employer. Find out more about our work on diversity and inclusion How to apply Ready to join a world-class team? Applications will be assessed on a rolling basis. Early submission of your EOI is strongly recommended to ensure full consideration. EOIs can be submitted via the Apply button at the top of the page. You will be required to include: - A cover letter - Resume - Verified testamur or academic transcripts - Any additional supporting documentation, such as references, student feedback surveys Click to view the Position Description for this role. Applications Close No End Date"
    },
    "https://usyd.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/USYD_EXTERNAL_CAREER_SITE/job/Darlington-Campus/Supervisor--Jacaranda-Flame-Consulting-_0142099": {
      "title": "Supervising Engineer - Jacaranda Flame Consulting",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-02-05\nPart time\n\nCasual position located at Darlington Campus (J12), within the Faculty of Engineering, and Queen Street, Surry Hills Contribute to career and employability initiatives, supporting students to engage with industry in order to develop professional practice skills Program dates: 22 June to 31 July (6 weeks) and 30 Nov to 12 Feb 2027 (9 weeks and excludes 2 week shut down period) About the opportunity The Faculty of Engineering is inviting applications for the position of Jacaranda Flame Consulting ( JFC) Supervisor . The role supports the successful delivery of the JFC program by acting as the key liaison between students, clients and Faculty. In this program, industry partners provide real-world projects, and teams of 5–7 students work with clients across a 6-9 week period. The position ensures smooth program operations, provides student support and development, facilitates client engagement, and manages administrative and reporting requirements from pre-program preparation through to post-program wrap-up. Candidates need to have an Engineers Australia accredited degree (or equivalent as per the Washington Accord). The Supervisor works under broad supervision of the academic JFC Director, performing tasks independently to deliver against goals. They choose appropriate methods of work and plan and prioritise tasks. They will provide advice as the first point of contact for their designated area. They also resolve routine matters related to their responsibilities and collaborate with others.   Please note that this advertisement remains open year-round. Candidates will be contacted as opportunities arise, subject to the number and timing of available industry projects. Your key responsibilities: Program Preparation: Participate in supervisor planning, review project briefs, confirm group allocations, and coordinate client introductions and first meetings. Program Delivery & Student Support: Attend induction, client meetings, supervisor meetings, and site visits as required. Provide ongoing guidance to student groups, including project management, teamwork, and client liaison. Monitor student progress through weekly reflections, and SparkPlus ratings, referring issues to mentors where needed. Facilitate mock presentations, provide feedback on reports, and support final presentations. Act as the primary contact and first point of escalation for student and client matters. Administration & Compliance: Manage timesheet approvals, maintain records in Sonia, oversee workplace etiquette, and address enquiries. Program Evaluation & Wrap-Up: Conduct exit interviews, obtain client feedback, and contribute to supervisor debrief meetings to identify improvements. Approve final timesheets and work experience certificates for PEP. About you The University values courage and creativity, openness and engagement, inclusion and diversity, and respect and integrity. We would particularly welcome expressions of interest from contacts who may be semi-retired or looking for a rewarding opportunity to mentor and guide students as they deliver on industry projects. Experience working in a tertiary environment with working knowledge of using Learning Management Systems, with a sound understanding of teaching activities Effective and tactful communication skills (both verbal and written) Experience dealing with industry partners Passion for student learning and delivering an outstanding student experience Work Rights You must have unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this employment to apply. Visa sponsorship is not available for this appointment. Pre-employment checks Your employment is conditional upon the completion of all role required pre-employment or background checks in terms satisfactory to the University. Similarly, your ongoing employment is conditional upon the satisfactory maintenance of all relevant clearances and background check requirements. If you do not meet these conditions, the University may take any necessary step, including the termination of your employment.   EEO statement At the University of Sydney, our shared values are trust, accountability and excellence and we strive to be a place where everyone can thrive. We are committed to creating a University community that thrives through diversity and reflects the wider community that we serve. We deliver on this through our commitment to diversity and inclusion , evidenced by our people and culture programs, as well as key strategies to increase participation and support the careers of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander People , women , people living with a disability , people from culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds , and those who identify as LGBTIQ+ . We welcome applications from candidates from all backgrounds.   We are proud to be recognised as an Australian Workplace Equality Index (AWEI) Gold employer. Find out more about our work on diversity and inclusion .  How to apply Applications (including a cover letter, CV, and any additional supporting documentation) can be submitted via the Apply button at the top of the page.  If you are a current employee of the University or a contingent worker with access to Workday, please login into your Workday account and navigate to the Career icon on your Dashboard.  Click on USYD Find Jobs and apply. For a confidential discussion about the role, or if you require reasonable adjustment or support filling out this application, please contact jenny.seo@sydney.edu.au  from Recruitment or you can also reach out to Head of Education Services sylvia.lohrengel@sydney.edu.au The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Click to view the Position Description for this role. Applications Close Sunday 06 December 2026 11:59 PM"
    },
    "https://usyd.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/USYD_EXTERNAL_CAREER_SITE/job/Darlington-Campus/Expressions-of-Interest---Aboriginal-and-Torres-Strait-Islander-Opportunities_0127526": {
      "title": "Expressions of Interest - Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Opportunities (Identified)",
      "description": "Posted: 2025-09-30\nFull time\n\nJoin the University of Sydney and Make an Impact! The University of Sydney is committed to creating meaningful opportunities for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples. We are dedicated to building a culturally inclusive and supportive environment where Indigenous staff and students can thrive. We are seeking expressions of interest from Indigenous candidates who want to be part of a University that values diversity, respects culture, and champions the success of our Indigenous community. Why Join Us? Under our Indigenous Strategy, we are focused on increasing Indigenous employment and representation across the University. Our goal is to provide rewarding career pathways while ensuring our staff feel supported, valued, and empowered in their roles. About You We are looking for passionate and motivated individuals who: identify as Aboriginal and/or Torres Strait Islander and are recognised by their community are excited about the opportunity to work at the University of Sydney want to contribute to and support fellow Indigenous staff and students are keen to be part of our YURA Network, fostering strong connections and community. Opportunities Available We offer a wide range of roles across various areas, including but not limited to: Administration Project Management Analytics & Reporting Lecturing & Research Management & Leadership Finance & Business Operations. Benefits & Culture At the University of Sydney, we offer more than just a job. We provide an environment where you can grow, develop, and make a real impact. Some of the benefits include: cultural leave an Indigenous Employee (YURA) Network for connection and support flexible working arrangements additional leave over the Christmas period lifelong learning and career development opportunities inclusive and progressive HR policies staff discounts and other entitlements. Ready to Apply? If you’re passionate about making a difference and want to join a University that values and supports Indigenous employees, we’d love to hear from you! How to Apply: Please submit your CV and Cover Letter via the ‘Apply Now’ button. Need More Info? For further details, get in touch with our Recruitment team - recruitment@sydney.edu.au The University of Sydney is committed to equity, diversity, and inclusion. We strongly encourage applications from women, people from culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds, people living with disabilities, members of the LGBTIQ+ community, and, of course, Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples. We also provide workplace adjustments for people with disabilities and offer flexible work options for eligible staff. Be part of something bigger – join the University of Sydney and help us create a future that celebrates and empowers Indigenous voices. Applications Close No End Date"
    },
    "https://usyd.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/USYD_EXTERNAL_CAREER_SITE/job/Concord/Director--ANZAC-Research-Institute_0155786-3": {
      "title": "Director, ANZAC Research Institute",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-09\nFull time\n\nExciting opportunity for an outstanding senior academic who is a leading authority within their discipline Fixed term position for 5 years with opportunity for further appointment with an attractive remuneration package About the opportunity The ANZAC Research Institute is a biomedical research institute where discovery meets care. Guided by our history, we partner with clinicians to innovate and deliver improved health outcomes for Defence members, veterans and our broader community. The Institute was established in 1995 and is an entity of Sydney Local Health District (SLHD) operating as a research partnership with the University of Sydney. It is located on the campus of Concord Repatriation General Hospital (CRGH), a primary referral hospital within SLHD and a teaching hospital of the University of Sydney. We are seeking to appoint an outstanding academic at the level of Associate Professor or Professor (Level D or E) as the Director of the ANZAC Research Institute. The appointee will have overall responsibility for leading the implementation of the Institutes strategic plan, provide scientific guidance, and liaise with SLHD and Precinct Academic Directors on operational and financial management of the Institute. In accordance with the research collaborative agreement, the Director will have accountability to the Chief Executive of SLHD and the Dean of Faculty of Medicine and Health University of Sydney (or their appointed delegates from time to time) as designated employers of the Director. In the case of the Faculty of Medicine and Health, the Director will report to the Head of School Medical Sciences, one of seven schools in FMH. The Director will also provide regular reports to the ANZAC Advisory Council covering the overall research performance of the Institute. The position is to be based in the School of Medical Sciences at University of Sydney and the Director will be a member of the school’s leadership team. About you (skills, capabilities and experience) The successful candidate will: hold research doctoral degree (PhD, MD or equivalent) with an outstanding record of achievement in a field of biomedical research relevant to the Institute (aging or related health specialties). have proven ability to deliver research an international standard in one or more fields relevant to the research of the Institute. have an outstanding research track record as evidenced by publications, instances of research translation and/or commercialisation supervision of successful HDR students. demonstrate evidence of peer recognition through involvement and leading committees, boards and editorial appointments and track record of developing national & international collaboration. have proven ability to secure significant and sustained competitive research funding. have proven ability to deliver on strategic directions of the Institute and provide scientific leadership within a complex biomedical research environment. Capacity to promote the research and translation functions of the Institute with the SLHD and University. understand research governance policies and legislation and rules of ethical research conduct. promote a strong research culture, strengthening existing collaborations and fostering new partnerships. be managing engagement with industry partners. Sponsorship / work rights for Australia Visa sponsorship and Relocation assistance will be provided if required. Term and remuneration The successful candidate will be offered a 5-year fixed term full-time position, subject to completion of a satisfactory probation period for new appointees.  There is opportunity for further appointments subject to performance. The successful candidate will be offered an attractive remuneration package commensurate with the responsibilities of the position and the candidate’s relevant experience and qualifications. Other As this position is located on a NSW Health site, it is a regulatory requirement to complete compulsory employment checks prior to starting in this position. For more information and instructions, please visit: NSW Health employment requirements Pre-employment checks Your employment is conditional upon the successful completion of all pre-employment or background checks required for the role in terms satisfactory to the University.  Also, to meet the University’s obligations under the National Higher Education Code to Prevent and Eliminate Gender-Based Violence you will be asked to declare if you have been investigated for, or found to engaged in, sexual harm or gender-based violence in the course of previous employment or in a legal process. Similarly, your ongoing employment is conditional upon the satisfactory maintenance of all relevant clearances and background check requirements. If you do not meet these conditions, the University may take any necessary step, including the termination of your employment. EEO statement   At the University of Sydney, our shared values are trust, accountability and excellence and we strive to be a place where everyone can thrive. We are committed to creating a University community that thrives through diversity and reflects the wider community that we serve. We deliver on this through our commitment to diversity and inclusion , evidenced by our people and culture programs, as well as key strategies to increase participation and support the careers of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander People , women , people living with a disability , people from culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds , and those who identify as LGBTQIA+ . We welcome applications from candidates from all backgrounds.  We are proud to be recognised as an Australian Workplace Equality Index (AWEI) Platinum Employer. Find out more about our work on diversity and inclusion .  How to apply Applications ( including a CV and cover letter outlining how your skills, capabilities and experience meet the requirements for the role ) can be submitted via the Apply button at the top of the page. Please refer to the candidate information brochure for further details. Candidate Information Brochure For employees of the University or contingent workers, please login into your Workday account and navigate to the Career icon on your Dashboard.  Click on USYD Find Jobs and apply. Intending applicants are welcome to seek further information about the position from: Professor Mary Collins, Head of School, School of Medical Sciences, mary.collins@sydney.edu.au  Faculty of Medicine and Health. For a confidential discussion about the role, or if you require reasonable adjustment or support filling out this application, please contact: Dr Mushtaq Sobhan (Senior Talent Partner, Recruitment Operations, Human Resources) by email mushtaq.sobhan@sydney.edu.au   Applications Close Friday 09 October 2026 11:59 PM"
    },
    "https://usyd.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/USYD_EXTERNAL_CAREER_SITE/job/Darlington-Campus/Postdoctoral-Research-Associate-in-Electrochemical-Engineering_0156960-1": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Research Associate in Electrochemical Engineering",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-17\nFull time\n\nFull time, fixed term for 1.5 years  Apply your expertise in electrochemical device development and chemical process design to advance sustainable technologies Base Salary $113,634 - $125,896 + 17% superannuation  About the opportunity The School of Chemical and Biomolecular Engineering at the University of Sydney, established in 1946, offered the first university-level chemical engineering program in Australia. The School is committed to teaching excellence and our graduates are highly regarded and sought after by employers in Australia and overseas. The School is research-intensive and considered a world leader in many cutting edge areas of chemical and biomolecular engineering. This is an exciting opportunity from the School of Chemical and Biomolecular Engineering for a  Postdoctoral Research Associate in Electrochemical Engineering to work in the Electrochemical Engineering Lab led by Dr Fengwang Li . This position offers an opportunity to contribute to an applied research program focused on electrochemical technologies for sustainable chemical production and energy conversion. You will combine hands-on experimentation with device and process engineering to develop, evaluate and improve electrochemical reactors and their supporting systems. We are particularly interested in candidates with practical experience in electrolysers or fuel cells who can translate engineering concepts into working experimental systems. The role will involve device design and assembly, electrochemical performance and durability testing, process development, and contributions to scale-up and system integration. You will work collaboratively with researchers, students and technical specialists, contributing both to fundamental understanding and to the development of reliable, scalable electrochemical technologies.  Your key responsibilities will be to: Design, assemble and improve electrochemical cells and reactor prototypes, including the selection and integration of electrodes, membranes, flow-field components and sealing systems. Develop, commission and operate experimental test systems, integrating electrochemical devices with fluid-handling equipment, instrumentation and data acquisition. Plan and conduct electrochemical experiments to evaluate performance, efficiency and durability, identify limitations and failure mechanisms, and implement evidence-based improvements. Apply chemical process engineering principles to develop process configurations, perform material and energy balances, prepare process flow diagrams and piping and instrumentation diagrams, and select appropriate equipment. Contribute to device scale-up and system integration, addressing practical challenges in flow distribution, heat and mass transfer, sealing, reproducibility and stable operation. Translate device concepts into CAD models and engineering drawings, and coordinate component fabrication, procurement and assembly as required. Analyse and interpret experimental data, maintain reproducible research records and engineering documentation, and prepare technical reports and research publications in accordance with confidentiality and intellectual property requirements. Collaborate effectively with colleagues and research partners, support student training and mentoring, and contribute to risk assessments, operating procedures and safe laboratory practices. About you The University values courage and creativity; openness and engagement; inclusion and diversity; and respect and integrity. As such, we see the importance of recruiting talent aligned to these values and are looking for a Postdoctoral Research Associate in Electrochemical Engineering who has: A PhD degree (or near completion) in Chemical Engineering, Electrochemical Engineering, Mechanical Engineering, Materials Science, Chemistry or a closely related discipline. Demonstrated hands-on experience with electrolysers or fuel cells, including device assembly, operation, performance testing and troubleshooting. Demonstrated ability to design electrochemical devices or associated experimental hardware, including the use of CAD software to produce models and engineering drawings, with consideration of materials compatibility, fabrication, sealing and assembly. Demonstrated capability in chemical process design for experimental or prototype systems, including material and energy balances, process flow diagrams, piping and instrumentation diagrams, and equipment selection. A strong understanding of electrochemical performance and relevant transport processes, together with practical experience in electrochemical characterisation, performance evaluation and durability testing. The ability to plan and deliver experimental research independently, resolve practical engineering problems, critically analyse data and maintain accurate technical records. Strong written and verbal communication skills, with research achievements appropriate to career stage and opportunity, demonstrated through publications, validated prototypes, patents or substantive technical reports. The ability to work collaboratively in a multidisciplinary team, together with a commitment to safe working practices, research integrity and the responsible handling of confidential information and intellectual property. The following experience would be highly regarded: Scaling electrochemical devices from laboratory cells to larger-area devices, multi-cell stacks or integrated prototype systems. Electrode fabrication, membrane electrode assembly preparation, or integration of gas diffusion electrodes into working devices. Computational fluid dynamics or multiphysics modelling to support device design and interpretation of experimental results, using COMSOL, ANSYS Fluent or equivalent tools. Process simulation or techno-economic assessment using Aspen Plus, Aspen HYSYS or equivalent approaches. Automated experimental testing, instrument control or data acquisition using tools such as LabVIEW, Python or MATLAB. Pre-employment checks and declarations  Your employment is conditional upon the successful completion of all pre-employment or background checks required for the role in terms satisfactory to the University.  Also, to meet the University’s obligations under the National Higher Education Code to Prevent and Eliminate Gender-Based Violence you will be asked to declare if you have been investigated for, or found to engaged in, sexual harm or gender-based violence in the course of previous employment or in a legal process. Similarly, your ongoing employment is conditional upon the satisfactory maintenance of all relevant clearances and background check requirements. If you do not meet these conditions, the University may take any necessary step, including the termination of your employment. EEO statement   At the University of Sydney, our shared values are trust, accountability and excellence and we strive to be a place where everyone can thrive. We are committed to creating a University community that thrives through diversity and reflects the wider community that we serve. We deliver on this through our commitment to diversity and inclusion , evidenced by our people and culture programs, as well as key strategies to increase participation and support the careers of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander People , women , people living with a disability , people from culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds , and those who identify as LGBTQIA+ . We welcome applications from candidates from all backgrounds.  We are proud to be recognised as an Australian Workplace Equality Index (AWEI) Platinum Employer. Find out more about our work on diversity and inclusion .  How to apply Applications (including a cover letter, CV, and any additional supporting documentation) can be submitted via the Apply button at the top of the page.  For employees of the University or contingent workers, please login into your Workday account and navigate to the Career icon on your Dashboard.  Click on USYD Find Jobs and apply. For a confidential discussion about the role, or if you require reasonable adjustment or support filling out this application, please contact Rebecca Astar or Cherie Goodwin, Recruitment Consultants at recruitment.sea@sydney.edu.au © The University of Sydney The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Click to view the Position Description for this role. Applications Close Sunday 11 October 2026 11:59 PM"
    },
    "https://usyd.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/USYD_EXTERNAL_CAREER_SITE/job/Camperdown-Campus/Lecturer-in-Geology_0156260-2": {
      "title": "Lecturer in Geology",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-18\nFull time\n\nFull-time, Fixed term until 31 December 2029 on the Camperdown Campus at the School of Geosciences Opportunity to make valuable contributions to teaching and research in Geology Base Salary $132,533 - $157,381 p.a  + 17% superannuation About the opportunity The University of Sydney is seeking applications for a Lecturer in Geology. The primary function of this position is to undertake research and teaching in the field of geology while also making a significant collaborative contribution to research using data science tools/methodologies applied to geology and geochemistry. Fieldwork experience and a research profile that demonstrates a potential connection towards critical mineral exploration is highly desirable. The position has responsibility for teaching courses in new and existing curricula at the undergraduate and postgraduate level under the broad theme of critical minerals and the energy transition. The applicant will be expected to teach fundamental and advanced concepts in geology effectively in the classroom, lab, and field, supported by digital-based approaches including virtual training and teaching. Your key responsibilities will be to: Contribute to knowledge within geology and geochemistry engage in high-quality teaching and learning experiences (at undergraduate, honours and postgraduate levels) which meet the University’s expectations and standards for education design and deliver quality educational programs and experiences that support student needs and meet curriculum requirements influence outstanding teaching and learning outcomes contribute to research, education, and administrative activities develop an academic profile through conferences, seminars and industry engagement enhance a positive workplace culture and strategic direction of the University.  The School of Geosciences at the University of Sydney is a dynamic and collegial multi-disciplinary community of scholars and students encompassing the fields of geology and geophysics, marine science, environmental studies, and physical and human geography. It has a strong global reputation for the quality of its research and teaching across those domains. Our common passion is to engage in research and teaching that promotes a better understanding of our world and its challenges, and we address key issues facing society including climate change, resource management and sustainability. The School of Geosciences is firmly committed to creating a diverse workplace by improving equity, access and opportunity. We want to create an environment where individuality is welcomed and celebrated, and a workplace that is open, supportive and safe for everyone. We are actively seeking to improve the diversity of our staffing profile, and welcome applications from women, people of culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds, people of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander descent, and members of the LGBTIQ+ community. To learn more about the School of Geosciences, click here About you The University values courage and creativity; openness and engagement; inclusion and diversity; and respect and integrity. As such, we see the importance of recruiting talent aligned to these values and are looking for a Lecturer in Geology who has: a PhD in geology or a cognate discipline a combination of skills across data science tools/methods and fieldwork the demonstrated ability to carry out high quality teaching in geology, particularly in the area of critical minerals and energy transitions a high level of interpersonal skills including the ability to work collaboratively with colleagues good organisational and administrative skills with attention to detail a high level of written and verbal communication skills an ability to engage productively with partners both within and outside of the University to develop and promote their teaching and research activities. Work Rights You must have unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this employment to apply. Visa sponsorship is not available for this appointment. Pre-employment checks Your employment is conditional upon the completion of all role required pre-employment or background checks in terms satisfactory to the University. Similarly, your ongoing employment is conditional upon the satisfactory maintenance of all relevant clearances and background check requirements. If you do not meet these conditions, the University may take any necessary step, including the termination of your employment. EEO statement At the University of Sydney, our shared values include diversity and inclusion and we strive to be a place where everyone can thrive. We are committed to creating a University community that reflects the wider community that we serve. We deliver on this commitment through our people and culture programs, as well as key strategies to increase participation and support the careers of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander People , women , people living with a disability, people from culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds , and those who identify as LGBTIQ . We welcome applications from candidates from all backgrounds. We are proud to be recognised as an Australian Workplace Equality Index (AWEI) Platinum Employer. Find out more about our work on diversity and inclusion .  How to apply Applications (including a cover letter, CV, and any additional supporting documentation) can be submitted via the Apply button at the top of the page. For employees of the University or contingent workers, please login into your Workday account and navigate to the Career icon on your Dashboard. Click on USYD Find Jobs and apply. For a confidential discussion about the role, please contact the Head of School of Geosciences, Professor Kerrylee Rogers via email: kerrylee.rogers@sydney.edu.au If you require reasonable adjustment or any documents in alternate formats, please contact Deepna Chand, Recruitment Operations by email to recruitment.sea@sydney.edu.au . © The University of Sydney The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Click to view the Position Description for this role. Applications Close Sunday 18 October 2026 11:59 PM"
    },
    "https://usyd.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/USYD_EXTERNAL_CAREER_SITE/job/Camperdown-Campus/Lecturer-Igneous-Geology--Women-Only-_0157069-1": {
      "title": "Lecturer Igneous Geology (Women Only)",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-18\nFull time\n\nFull time, continuing position, located on the Camperdown Campus at the School of Geosciences Opportunity to make valuable contributions to teaching and research in geology Base Salary Level B, $132,533 - $157,381 p.a + 17% superannuation About the opportunity This position is exempt under section 126 of the Anti-Discrimination Act 1977, to conduct targeted recruitment programs for women. The University of Sydney is seeking applications for a Lecturer in Igneous Geology. The primary function of this position is to undertake research and teaching in the field of geology while also making a significant collaborative contribution to research using methodologies applied to igneous petrology and/or igneous geochemistry. Fieldwork experience is essential and a research profile that demonstrates a potential connection towards external collaboration with industry and government is highly desirable. The position has responsibility for teaching courses in new and existing curricula at the undergraduate and postgraduate level under the broad theme of igneous petrology. The applicant will be expected to teach fundamental and advanced concepts in geology effectively in the classroom, field and laboratory, supported by digital-based approaches including virtual training and teaching. Your key responsibilities will be to: deliver high-quality teaching in geology and cross-disciplinary settings across undergraduate, honours and postgraduate levels, in classroom, field and laboratory environments design and deliver engaging educational programs and learning experiences that respond to student needs and meet curriculum requirements lead and undertake fieldwork, including the collection of field data to enrich teaching build partnerships with industry, academic and government organisations to advance teaching, learning and research outcomes develop a research profile appropriate to career stage and opportunity, evidenced through high-quality publications and success in attracting external research funding contribute effectively to administrative and service activities across the School foster a positive, inclusive and collaborative workplace culture support the strategic direction of the School, Faculty and University. The School of Geosciences at the University of Sydney is a dynamic and collegial multi-disciplinary community of scholars and students encompassing the fields of geology and geophysics, marine science, environmental studies, and physical and human geography. It has a strong global reputation for the quality of its research and teaching across those subdisciplines. Our common passion is to engage in research and teaching that promotes a better understanding of our world and its challenges, and we address key issues facing society including climate change, resource management and sustainability. The School of Geosciences is firmly committed to creating a diverse workplace by improving equity, access and opportunity. We want to create an environment where individuality is welcomed and celebrated, and a workplace that is open, supportive and safe for everyone. We are actively seeking to improve the diversity of our staffing profile, and welcome applications from women, people of culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds, people of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander descent, and members of the LGBTIQ+ community. To learn more about the School of Geosciences, click here About you The University values courage and creativity; openness and engagement; inclusion and diversity; and respect and integrity. As such, we see the importance of recruiting talent aligned to these values and are looking for a Lecturer in Igneous Geology who has: PhD in geology, with specialisation in igneous petrology, Earth materials and/or magmatic processes knowledge of igneous petrology and igneous geochemistry, and familiarity with metamorphic processes skills spanning light and electron microscopy, mineral chemistry, thermobarometry and phase equilibrium application, or skills in other relevant analytical or interpretive methods holds a current driver’s license. Pre-employment checks and declarations Your employment is conditional upon the successful completion of all pre-employment or background checks required for the role in terms satisfactory to the University.  Also, to meet the University’s obligations under the National Higher Education Code to Prevent and Eliminate Gender-Based Violence you will be asked to declare if you have been investigated for, or found to engaged in, sexual harm or gender-based violence in the course of previous employment or in a legal process. Similarly, your ongoing employment is conditional upon the satisfactory maintenance of all relevant clearances and background check requirements. If you do not meet these conditions, the University may take any necessary step, including the termination of your employment. EEO statement At the University of Sydney, our shared values are trust, accountability and excellence and we strive to be a place where everyone can thrive. We are committed to creating a University community that thrives through diversity and reflects the wider community that we serve. We deliver on this through our commitment to diversity and inclusion , evidenced by our people and culture programs, as well as key strategies to increase participation and support the careers of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander People , women , people living with a disability , people from culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds , and those who identify as LGBTQIA+ . We welcome applications from candidates from all backgrounds.  We are proud to be recognised as an Australian Workplace Equality Index (AWEI) Platinum Employer. Find out more about our work on diversity and inclusion .  How to apply Applications (including a cover letter, CV, and any additional supporting documentation) can be submitted via the Apply button at the top of the page. For employees of the University or contingent workers, please login into your Workday account and navigate to the Career icon on your Dashboard. Click on USYD Find Jobs and apply. For a confidential discussion about the role, please contact the Head of School of Geosciences, Professor Kerrylee Rogers via email: kerrylee.rogers@sydney.edu.au   If you require reasonable adjustment or any documents in alternate formats, please contact Deepna Chand, Recruitment Operations by email to recruitment.sea@sydney.edu.au . © The University of Sydney The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Click to view the Position Description for this role. Applications Close Sunday 18 October 2026 11:59 PM"
    },
    "https://usyd.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/USYD_EXTERNAL_CAREER_SITE/job/Camperdown-Campus/Lecturer-Geology--Geophysics-or-Earth-Science--Women-Only-_0155683": {
      "title": "Lecturer Geology, Geophysics or Earth Science (Women Only)",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-18\nFull time\n\nFull time, continuing position, located on the Camperdown Campus at the School of Geosciences Opportunity to make valuable contributions to teaching and research in geology Base Salary $132,533 - $157,381 p.a + 17% superannuation About the opportunity This position is exempt under section 126 of the Anti-Discrimination Act 1977, to conduct targeted recruitment programs for women. The University of Sydney is seeking applications for a Lecturer in Geology, Geophysics or Earth Science. The primary function of this position is to undertake research and teaching in the field of geology, geophysics or Earth science while also making a significant collaborative contribution to research using methodologies applied to economic geology. Fieldwork experience is essential and a research profile that demonstrates a potential connection towards external collaboration with industry and government is highly desirable. The position has responsibility for teaching courses in new and existing curricula at the undergraduate and postgraduate level under the broad theme of economic geology. The applicant will be expected to teach fundamental and advanced concepts in geology effectively in the classroom, field and laboratory, supported by digital-based approaches including virtual training and teaching. Your key responsibilities will be to: deliver high-quality teaching in geology, geophysics or Earth science and cross-disciplinary settings across undergraduate, honours and postgraduate levels, in classroom, field and laboratory environments design and deliver engaging educational programs and learning experiences that respond to student needs and meet curriculum requirements lead and undertake fieldwork, including the collection of field data to enrich teaching build partnerships with industry, academic and government organisations to advance teaching, learning and research outcomes develop a research profile appropriate to career stage and opportunity, evidenced through high-quality publications and success in attracting external research funding contribute effectively to administrative and service activities across the School foster a positive, inclusive and collaborative workplace culture support the strategic direction of the School, Faculty and University. The School of Geosciences at the University of Sydney is a dynamic and collegial multi-disciplinary community of scholars and students encompassing the fields of geology and geophysics, marine science, environmental studies, and physical and human geography. It has a strong global reputation for the quality of its research and teaching across those subdisciplines. Our common passion is to engage in research and teaching that promotes a better understanding of our world and its challenges, and we address key issues facing society including climate change, resource management and sustainability. The School of Geosciences is firmly committed to creating a diverse workplace by improving equity, access and opportunity. We want to create an environment where individuality is welcomed and celebrated, and a workplace that is open, supportive and safe for everyone. We are actively seeking to improve the diversity of our staffing profile, and welcome applications from women, people of culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds, people of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander descent, and members of the LGBTIQ+ community. To learn more about the School of Geosciences, click here About you The University values courage and creativity; openness and engagement; inclusion and diversity; and respect and integrity. As such, we see the importance of recruiting talent aligned to these values and are looking for a Lecturer in Geology, Geophysics or Earth Science who has: PhD in geology, geophysics or Earth science demonstrated expertise in economic geology, including the formation, distribution and exploration of geological resources skills spanning geophysics, remote-sensing, GeoAI, and/or large data manipulation and analyses holds a current driver’s license.   Pre-employment checks Your employment is conditional upon the successful completion of all pre-employment or background checks required for the role in terms satisfactory to the University.  Also, to meet the University’s obligations under the National Higher Education Code to Prevent and Eliminate Gender-Based Violence you will be asked to declare if you have been investigated for, or found to engaged in, sexual harm or gender-based violence in the course of previous employment or in a legal process. Similarly, your ongoing employment is conditional upon the satisfactory maintenance of all relevant clearances and background check requirements. If you do not meet these conditions, the University may take any necessary step, including the termination of your employment. EEO statement At the University of Sydney, our shared values are trust, accountability and excellence and we strive to be a place where everyone can thrive. We are committed to creating a University community that thrives through diversity and reflects the wider community that we serve. We deliver on this through our commitment to diversity and inclusion , evidenced by our people and culture programs, as well as key strategies to increase participation and support the careers of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander People , women , people living with a disability , people from culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds , and those who identify as LGBTQIA+ . We welcome applications from candidates from all backgrounds.  We are proud to be recognised as an Australian Workplace Equality Index (AWEI) Platinum Employer. Find out more about our work on diversity and inclusion .  How to apply Applications (including a cover letter, CV, and any additional supporting documentation) can be submitted via the Apply button at the top of the page. For employees of the University or contingent workers, please login into your Workday account and navigate to the Career icon on your Dashboard. Click on USYD Find Jobs and apply. For a confidential discussion about the role, please contact the Head of School of Geosciences, Professor Kerrylee Rogers via email: kerrylee.rogers@sydney.edu.au   If you require reasonable adjustment or any documents in alternate formats, please contact Deepna Chand, Recruitment Operations by email to recruitment.sea@sydney.edu.au . © The University of Sydney The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Click to view the Position Description for this role. Applications Close Sunday 18 October 2026 11:59 PM"
