    "https://usyd.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/USYD_EXTERNAL_CAREER_SITE/job/Darlington-Campus/Postdoctoral-Researcher-in-Microwave-and-Antenna-Engineering-for-CubeSat-Applications_0156859": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Researcher in Microwave and Antenna Engineering for CubeSat Applications",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-18\nFull time\n\nFull time, fixed term for 2 years with the possibility of extension   Join the School of Electrical and Computer Engineering and contribute to pioneering research  redefining CubeSat technology Base Salary Academic Level A $113,634 - $125,869 p.a or Academic Level B $132,533 - $157,381 p.a. + 17% superannuation  About the opportunity The School of Electrical and Computer Engineering is seeking a highly motivated Postdoctoral Research Associate/Fellow (Level A or Level B) to contribute to a research project focused on the development of advanced microwave and antenna technologies for CubeSat applications, including additively manufactured and 4D-printed shape-morphing antenna systems. The Postdoctoral Researcher will provide specialist technical expertise in microwave circuits, antenna engineering, electromagnetic modelling, additive manufacturing, materials characterisation and experimental validation. The appointee will work closely with the Chief Investigator (CI), Partner Investigators (PIs), research students and external/internal collaborators to address key engineering challenges and support the technical delivery of the project. Your key responsibilities will be to: Design, model and optimise microwave circuits, antennas and antenna systems for CubeSat and related space applications. Undertake full-wave electromagnetic simulation and numerical modelling of microwave and antenna components using industry-standard electromagnetic simulation software. Develop additively manufactured and 4D-printed antenna prototypes, including the preparation and optimisation of fabrication processes. Conduct electrical, electromagnetic and materials characterisation of 3D- and 4D-printed materials and structures relevant to microwave applications. Perform hands-on fabrication, integration and experimental testing of microwave and antenna hardware. Develop and experimentally demonstrate antenna functions including reconfigurability, shape morphing and two-dimensional beam steering for CubeSat applications. Support system- and component-level development, integration and validation of antenna subsystems. Analyse experimental and simulation results and contribute to design iterations and technical problem solving. Work collaboratively with the CI, PIs, research staff, PhD candidates, Honours students and external project partners. Assist the CI and PIs with the technical supervision and mentoring of PhD and Honours students participating in the project. Provide technical guidance to students undertaking antenna modelling, additive manufacturing, prototyping and experimental measurements. Contribute to research publications, technical reports, presentations, project milestones and other research outputs. Participate in project meetings and communicate research progress effectively to academic and industry collaborators. Contribute to the effective operation of the research laboratory and ensure that experimental activities are undertaken in accordance with relevant laboratory and WHS requirements. About you The University values courage and creativity; openness and engagement; inclusion and diversity; and respect and integrity. As such, we see the importance of recruiting talent aligned to these values and are looking for a Postdoctoral Researcher in Microwave and Antenna Engineering for CubeSat Applications who has: A PhD in Electrical Engineering, Electronic Engineering, Microwave Engineering, Antenna Engineering, Electromagnetics, RF Engineering, or a closely related discipline. Strong knowledge of electromagnetic theory, microwave engineering and antenna theory. Demonstrated experience in the design and modelling of microwave circuits and antennas, preferably for high-frequency, satellite, CubeSat or related applications. Demonstrated proficiency with electromagnetic simulation and design software, such as CST Studio Suite, Ansys HFSS, Keysight ADS, or equivalent platforms. Demonstrated hands-on experience in microwave and antenna hardware development, fabrication, prototyping and experimental validation. Experience with 3D printing/additive manufacturing, particularly for microwave, RF, antenna or electronic applications. Experience in the characterisation of materials and/or fabricated components relevant to microwave and antenna applications. Experience in shape memory materials modelling, training and manufacturing Demonstrated capability to undertake both component-level and system-level engineering development. To be considered for appointment at Level B, we’re looking for evidence of: Knowledge of satellite and CubeSat systems, including antenna integration constraints for small satellites. Experience with reconfigurable, deployable, origami-inspired or shape-morphing antenna technologies. Experience with 4D printing, smart materials or shape-memory materials. Experience in microwave materials characterisation, including dielectric and/or conductive material measurements. Experience with antenna measurement systems, including near-field or far-field antenna characterisation. Experience in phased-array antennas, beam steering or electronically/mechanically reconfigurable antenna systems. Experience working on collaborative research projects involving universities, industry partners or international collaborators. Demonstrated publication record in high-quality journals and conferences in microwave engineering, antennas, additive manufacturing or related fields. Previous experience assisting with the supervision or mentoring of postgraduate and undergraduate research students. Pre-employment checks and declarations  Your employment is conditional upon the successful completion of all pre-employment or background checks required for the role in terms satisfactory to the University.  Also, to meet the University’s obligations under the National Higher Education Code to Prevent and Eliminate Gender-Based Violence you will be asked to declare if you have been investigated for, or found to engaged in, sexual harm or gender-based violence in the course of previous employment or in a legal process. Similarly, your ongoing employment is conditional upon the satisfactory maintenance of all relevant clearances and background check requirements. If you do not meet these conditions, the University may take any necessary step, including the termination of your employment. EEO statement   At the University of Sydney, our shared values are trust, accountability and excellence and we strive to be a place where everyone can thrive. We are committed to creating a University community that thrives through diversity and reflects the wider community that we serve. We deliver on this through our commitment to diversity and inclusion , evidenced by our people and culture programs, as well as key strategies to increase participation and support the careers of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander People , women , people living with a disability , people from culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds , and those who identify as LGBTQIA+ . We welcome applications from candidates from all backgrounds.  We are proud to be recognised as an Australian Workplace Equality Index (AWEI) Platinum Employer. Find out more about our work on diversity and inclusion .  How to apply Applications (including a cover letter, CV, and any additional supporting documentation) can be submitted via the Apply button at the top of the page.  For employees of the University or contingent workers, please login into your Workday account and navigate to the Career icon on your Dashboard.  Click on USYD Find Jobs and apply. For a confidential discussion about the role, or if you require reasonable adjustment or any documents in alternate formats, please contact Rebecca Astar or Cherie Goodwin, Recruitment Operations by email to recruitment.sea@sydney.edu.au © The University of Sydney The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Click to view the Position Description for this role. Applications Close Monday 19 October 2026 11:59 PM"
    },
    "https://usyd.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/USYD_EXTERNAL_CAREER_SITE/job/Camperdown-Campus/Specialist-in-Small-Animal-Anaesthesia_0157099-1": {
      "title": "Specialist in Small Animal Anaesthesia",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-18\nFull time\n\nBe part of building the future of veterinary clinical education at the University of Sydney Full time continuing employment* Located on Camperdown Campus at the Sydney School of Veterinary Science in The University of Sydney Opportunity to inspire and train the next generation of veterinarians, engage with the school ranked # 1 in Australia, within a world-class university Base Salary HEO 9 (38 hours), $165,080 - $174,177 p.a. + 17% superannuation + specialist loading when registered in NSW as a specialist to a total package of up to $234,737 p.a *Continuing contingent on the continuation of external funding The University of Sydney Veterinary Teaching Hospital is looking for a new colleague to work in a growing team supporting the University Veterinary Teaching Hospital and guiding the development of Doctor of Veterinary Medicine students. This role does not require previous academic experience but rather a strong clinical background and interest in supporting student development. About the opportunity Your chance to have a marked impact on the future of the veterinary profession as you care for the lives of animals. We are seeking to appoint a Specialist In Small Animal Anaesthesia to join a team of outstanding world-class clinical colleagues at the University Veterinary Teaching Hospital Sydney. Small animal anaesthesia is a core discipline in the training of the future leaders of the veterinary profession through a globally accredited training program, the Doctor of Veterinary Medicine as well as grow this essential discipline through supporting residency training. The University Veterinary Teaching Hospital is one of the core teaching and research facilities in the Sydney School of Veterinary Science, a globally ranked veterinary school. The academic environment is different to commercial specialty practice with the academic mission of teaching and research being central to operations. In this role, you will take an active role in the training of students working in the University Veterinary Teaching Hospital. Depending on your level you will be expected to assist in and be part of the development & implementation of the Unit’s Strategic Plan actively contributing to its management, including contributing to the financial viability of the Unit. You will also be expected to pursue clinical research to further knowledge of your specialty. The key benefits be part of developing the next generation of veterinarians be stimulated by an academic environment focused on teaching and research opportunity to learn and enhance your skills and experience in the unique teaching and research environment of a Veterinary Teaching hospital opportunity for a different professional pathway where you can leverage your clinical expertise, enhance work life balance, and find purpose in academic opportunities salary packaging available 15% veterinary clinical specialist loading is available once registered as specialist by NSW Veterinary Practitioners Board Industry-leading leave provisions including generous paid parental leave up to 38 weeks entitlements (pending eligibility) compressed work week available generous relocation allowance and access to research and publication support access to fitness passport, as well as discounted rates on university sports and fitness centres discounted Staff Parking available on-site gain the recognition of working in a leading Veterinary School in an outstanding University. We support and encourage excellence in all aspects of the role, from patient care, teaching and learning, and research. The school values highly the tenants of mutual support, respect, empathy, collegiality and integrity. Join an organization that has over a hundred years of experience in clinical training and innovation. Be part of the transformational process supporting students as they transition to become veterinarians. A privileged role for someone who wants more than a job- come and do work with a purpose. Your key responsibilities will be to maintain reputation for academic excellence provide high-quality veterinary clinical service in veterinary anaesthesia services for clients of UVTHS during business hours and contribute to afterhours anaesthesia coverage develop an expanding client base for the hospital’s anaesthesia service participate in provision of education to DVM students and continuing veterinary education provided by the unit coordinate the daily activities in the anaesthesia unit and provide additional support when required for case-management of patients in other units in the UVTHS continually improving the standard of practice provide anaesthesia services to Camden clinic on a rotating basis for equine and small animal clients as required actively participate in meetings, planning and development in the UVTHS at the discretion of the Hospital Director and Hospital Manager. Since its founding in 1910, the mission of the Sydney School of Veterinary Science (SSVS) has been to provide the nation’s best training in the health, welfare, and conservation of animals. The Sydney School of Veterinary Science is internationally accredited with the American Veterinary Medical Association, Canadian Veterinary Medical Association, the Australasian Veterinary Boards Council and Royal College of Veterinary Surgeons (UK) by reciprocal agreement. Veterinary Science at Sydney is ranked 1st in Australia, within a world-class university. To learn more about the Sydney School of Veterinary Science click here About you The University values courage and creativity; openness and engagement; inclusion and diversity; and respect and integrity. As such, we see the importance of recruiting talent aligned to these values and are looking for a Veterinary Specialist in Small Animal Anaesthesia : Veterinary Specialist in Small Animal Anaesthesia (HEO level 9) who is a veterinarian with at least partial responsibility for the management of a unit or division, including the management of a range of staff working within the relevant discipline or sub-discipline, who has: a degree in veterinary science registrable in New South Wales, Australia a Diplomate or Fellowship permitting registration with the NSW Veterinary Scientists Board as a Specialist in small animal anaesthesia. The commencement level will be determined based on the candidate’s skills, experience and knowledge. The 15% veterinary clinical specialist loading is available once registered as specialist by NSW Veterinary Practitioners Board. Sponsorship / work rights for Australia Work Rights: you must have unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this employment to apply. Visa sponsorship is not available for this appointment. Pre-employment checks Your employment is conditional upon the completion of all the role required pre-employment or background checks in terms satisfactory to the University. Similarly, your ongoing employment is conditional upon the satisfactory maintenance of all relevant clearances and background check requirements. If you do not meet these conditions, the University may take any necessary step, including the termination of your employment. EEO statement At the University of Sydney, our shared values are trust, accountability and excellence and we strive to be a place where everyone can thrive. We are committed to creating a University community that thrives through diversity and reflects the wider community that we serve. We deliver on this through our commitment to diversity and inclusion , evidenced by our people and culture programs, as well as key strategies to increase participation and support the careers of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander People , women , people living with a disability , people from culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds , and those who identify as LGBTIQ+ . We welcome applications from candidates from all backgrounds.  We are proud to be recognised as an Australian Workplace Equality Index (AWEI) Gold employer. Find out more about our work on diversity and inclusion .  How to apply Applications (including a cover letter, CV, and any additional supporting documentation) can be submitted via the Apply button at the top of the page.   If you are a current employee of the University or a contingent worker with access to Workday, please login into your Workday account and navigate to the Career icon on your Dashboard.  Click on USYD Find Jobs and apply. For a confidential discussion about the role, or if you require reasonable adjustment or any documents in alternate formats, please contact Chris Masaoka, Recruitment Consultant. Recruitment Operations, Human Resources by email recruitment.sea@sydney.edu.au © The University of Sydney The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Click to view the Position Description for this role. Applications Close Sunday 27 September 2026 11:59 PM"
    },
    "https://usyd.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/USYD_EXTERNAL_CAREER_SITE/job/Camperdown-Campus/Postdoctoral-Research-Associate-in-AI-Enabled-Atomic-Scale-Materials-Characterisation_0156863": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Research Associate in AI Enabled Atomic-Scale Materials Characterisation",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-23\nFull time\n\nFull time 12 month fixed term opportunity Base Salary, Academic Level A (6-8) offering a base salary of $117,936 - $125,896 p.a. + 17% superannuation    Work in a highly collaborative, international, and interdisciplinary research team   About the opportunity   The School of Aerospace Mechanical and Mechatronic Engineering (AMME) is one of Australia’s leading research and teaching Engineering Schools, with degree programs in Mechanical, Mechatronic, Aeronautical and Space Engineering. The school has a student body of over 1,000 students and has seen strong growth in demand for teaching at the undergraduate and postgraduate levels. The school supports teaching and research activities across disciplines including aerospace, combustion, design, fluid mechanics, materials, mechanical, mechatronic and robotics engineering. To learn more about the School click here   We are currently seeking a Postdoctoral Research Associate in AI Enabled Atomic-Scale Materials Characterisation to work within a highly collaborative and interdisciplinary research environment involving researchers, HDR students and international collaborators across academia and industry.   The successful candidate will undertake research at the intersection of advanced microscopy, atom probe tomography (APT), artificial intelligence and materials science. Working with world-class characterisation facilities, they will develop quantitative and AI-enabled approaches to understand atomic-scale structures, defects and structure-property relationships in advanced materials, contributing to next-generation materials discovery.   The successful candidate will be expected to:   ·       conduct innovative research using advanced microscopy and characterisation techniques to investigate atomic-scale structure, chemistry and materials behaviour ·       develop and apply quantitative, computational and AI-enabled approaches to materials characterisation, data analysis and scientific discovery ·       collaborate with multidisciplinary research teams, industry partners and international collaborators to deliver high-impact research outcomes ·       contribute to student supervision, research training, grant development and the dissemination of research through leading journals and conferences.   About you   ·       a PhD in Materials Science and Engineering, Physics, Materials Characterisation, Computational Materials Science, or a closely related discipline ·       demonstrated expertise in advanced microscopy and microanalysis techniques, particularly atom probe tomography (APT)  ·       experience with complementary characterisation techniques such as TEM, SEM, EBSD and XRD, and the analysis of complex materials datasets ·       proficiency in quantitative analysis, computational methods and programming, with experience in AI, machine learning or data-driven materials research highly desirable ·       a strong publication record, excellent communication skills, and the ability to work effectively within multidisciplinary research teams and supervise research students.     Sponsorship / work rights for Australia   You must have unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this employment to be eligible to apply. Visa sponsorship is not available for this appointment.   Pre-employment checks and declarations     Your employment is conditional upon the successful completion of all pre-employment or background checks required for the role in terms satisfactory to the University.  Also, to meet the University’s obligations under the National Higher Education Code to Prevent and Eliminate Gender-Based Violence you will be asked to declare if you have been investigated for, or found to engaged in, sexual harm or gender-based violence in the course of previous employment or in a legal process. Similarly, your ongoing employment is conditional upon the satisfactory maintenance of all relevant clearances and background check requirements. If you do not meet these conditions, the University may take any necessary step, including the termination of your employment.   EEO statement     At the University of Sydney, our shared values are trust, accountability and excellence and we strive to be a place where everyone can thrive. We are committed to creating a University community that thrives through diversity and reflects the wider community that we serve. We deliver on this through our commitment to diversity and inclusion , evidenced by our people and culture programs, as well as key strategies to increase participation and support the careers of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander People , women , people living with a disability , people from culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds , and those who identify as LGBTQIA+ . We welcome applications from candidates from all backgrounds.    We are proud to be recognised as an Australian Workplace Equality Index (AWEI) Platinum Employer. Find out more about our work on diversity and inclusion .    How to apply   Applications (including a cover letter, CV, and any additional supporting documentation) can be submitted via the Apply button at the top of the page.    For employees of the University or contingent workers, please login into your Workday account and navigate to the Career icon on your Dashboard.  Click on USYD Find Jobs and apply.   For a confidential discussion about the role, or if you require reasonable adjustment or any documents in alternate formats, please contact Chris Masaoka, Recruitment Operations by email to recruitment.sea@sydney.edu.au.   © The University of Sydney   The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment.   Click to view the Position Description for this role. Applications Close No End Date"
    },
    "https://usyd.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/USYD_EXTERNAL_CAREER_SITE/job/Camperdown-Campus/Lecturer-Exercise-and-Sport-Sciences--Education-Focused-_0157163-1": {
      "title": "Lecturer-Exercise and Sport Sciences (Education Focused)",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-23\nPart time\n\n2-year part time fixed term contract available Be a part of The School of Health Sciences, which is ranked number three in the world for Sports-Related Subjects Base Salary ranging from $117,936- $157,381+ 17% superannuation About the opportunity The Sydney School of Health Sciences is undergoing significant growth and investment as part of the Faculty of Medicine and Health’s strategic vision. We are seeking a Lecturer (Level A/B) with expertise in Exercise and Sport Science. The successful candidate will make a substantial contribution to teaching and learning through the delivery, design, and evaluation of high-quality educational programs. They will also be expected to build their research profile, strengthen their disciplinary expertise, and contribute to research impact within the School and broader academic community. The Sydney School of Health Sciences is ranked among the world’s leading institutions for sport-related disciplines, including sport science, physiotherapy, and rehabilitation. As part of the University of Sydney, which is consistently recognised as one of Australia’s top universities, the School benefits from a strong reputation for excellence in research, teaching, innovation, and sustainability.   The School is seeking to appoint a Lecturer (Education Focused, Level A/B) on a fixed-term basis. Based in the state-of-the-art Susan Wakil Health Building at the Camperdown campus, the successful candidate will contribute to the delivery of teaching across strength and conditioning, sport science, and exercise science units of study. This role offers an exciting opportunity to make a significant contribution to teaching and learning through the development, delivery, and evaluation of high-quality educational experiences. The successful candidate will be a collaborative and engaged academic who is committed to excellence in teaching and ready to contribute across a broad range of academic activities. The position also provides opportunities to contribute to research and scholarship in Exercise and Sport Science as part of a multidisciplinary team within the Faculty of Medicine and Health.   Your key responsibilities include: Undertake educational innovation, curriculum design and review, contributing to outstanding educational delivery and outcomes Engage in high-quality teaching and learning experiences which meet the University’s expectations and standards for education Contribute to the School’s research activities, expertise, and impact. Engage in supervision of higher-degree research students Establish collaborations and partnerships to enhance the research profile and reputation of the university Carry out administrative tasks primarily connected with the area of discipline Contribute to a positive workplace culture in which diversity of thought, contribution, collaboration, excellence and achieving outcomes are valued and enabled Undertake other duties appropriate within the classification level as required.   About you PhD in an academic field relevant to the discipline is preferred Current registration with Exercise and Sport Science Australia (ESSA) as an Accredited Exercise Scientist or Accredited Sports Scientist (ASpS) and /or current registration with Australian Strength and Conditioning Association (ASCA) would be preferred Experience in teaching and learning in a tertiary environment, as evidenced by coordination of units and supervision of students in practicals, on placements and/or in discipline-related simulation teaching Relevant experience and expertise in strength and conditioning and sports science Demonstrated success in designing implementing and evaluating teaching and learning initiatives Demonstrated ability to foster inclusive, equitable and respectful learning environments A developing network of relationships with key academic, clinical and professional institutions, partners and stakeholders.   Sponsorship / work rights for Australia   Work Rights: you must have unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this employment to apply. Visa sponsorship is not available for this appointment.    Pre-employment checks   Your employment is conditional upon the completion of all role required pre-employment or background checks in terms satisfactory to the University. Similarly, your ongoing employment is conditional upon the satisfactory maintenance of all relevant clearances and background check requirements. If you do not meet these conditions, the University may take any necessary step, including the termination of your employment.     EEO statement     At the University of Sydney, our shared values are trust, accountability and excellence and we strive to be a place where everyone can thrive. We are committed to creating a University community that thrives through diversity and reflects the wider community that we serve. We deliver on this through our commitment to diversity and inclusion , evidenced by our people and culture programs, as well as key strategies to increase participation and support the careers of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander People , women , people living with a disability , people from culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds , and those who identify as LGBTIQ+ . We welcome applications from candidates from all backgrounds.    We are proud to be recognised as an Australian Workplace Equality Index (AWEI) Gold employer. Find out more about our work on diversity and inclusion .    How to apply   Applications (including a cover letter, CV, and any additional supporting documentation) can be submitted via the Apply button at the top of the page.      To submit an application for this role please provide the following information: 1. A cover letter which includes a written response to selection criteria addressing your ability to meet the requirements outlined above within the context of the key responsibilities of the role and relevant discipline (maximum 1000 words). 3. Your current CV or resume, including two referees. Please ensure your cover letter is clear, concise, addressing the selection criteria and states the type of employment in which you are applyin g.       For employees of the University or contingent workers, please login into your Workday account and navigate to the Career icon on your Dashboard.  Click on USYD Find Jobs and apply.   For a confidential discussion about the role, or if you require reasonable adjustment or any documents in alternate formats, please contact Rachel Ann Yazigi, Recruitment Operations by email to rachel.yazigi@sydney.edu.au .   © The University of Sydney The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Click to view the Position Description for this role. Applications Close Sunday 25 October 2026 11:59 PM"
    },
    "https://usyd.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/USYD_EXTERNAL_CAREER_SITE/job/Camperdown-Campus/Magma-Research-Associate---Magma-Research-Fellow_0156699-1": {
      "title": "Magma Research Associate / Magma Research Fellow",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-23\nFull time\n\n2 x full time, 3-year fixed term positions, located on the Camperdown Campus at the School of Mathematics and Statistics Opportunity to drive research in computational pure mathematics through the development of mathematical software Base Salary, Academic level A/B $113,634 - $157,381 + 17% superannuation    About the opportunity   The School of Mathematics and Statistics is currently seeking up to two Magma Research Associates / Magma Research Fellows to develop and maintain the Magma computer algebra system ( http://magma.maths.usyd.edu.au/magma/ ).   These positions are based at the University of Sydney in a team led by John Voight as part of an international collaboration funded by the Simons Foundation between the University of Sydney and the Massachusetts Institute of Technology (MIT) in Cambridge, Massachusetts.                                                                                                   Your key responsibilities will be to:   design and implement mathematical algorithms, and facilitate their integration into Magma engage with users, researchers, and developers, both internally at the University of Sydney and externally across multiple institutions publish research papers on mathematical theory and the development and analysis of algorithms explore how artificial intelligence can support the development and use of computer algebra systems, including mathematical programming, software verification and testing, documentation, and new ways for researchers to interact with computational mathematics software compile data sets relevant to research in these areas and integrate them into Magma participate in and support workshops and meetings.   About you   The University values courage and creativity; openness and engagement; inclusion and diversity; and respect and integrity. As such, we see the importance of recruiting talent aligned to these values and are looking for Magma Research Associate / Magma Research Fellows who have:   a PhD in mathematics, computer science, or a related discipline a strong background in computational mathematics, computer algebra, or mathematical software, with experience relevant to specific areas such as algebra, arithmetic geometry, number theory, group theory, representation theory, commutative algebra, or algebraic geometry experience in the design, analysis, or implementation of algorithms strong programming skills; experience with Magma or another computer algebra system is highly desirable experience with C/C++ and Linux, or the demonstrated ability to work effectively in comparable systems-programming and Unix environments strong communication skills, both written and verbal the ability to travel and present research results.   Desirable experience includes:   experience developing substantial mathematical or scientific software experience with modern software development practices, including version control, testing, profiling, optimisation, and collaborative development experience with artificial intelligence or machine-learning tools and methods, particularly their application to mathematical research or software development. This may include AI-assisted programming, automated testing and documentation, mathematical reasoning, code analysis, or the development of AI-enabled interfaces and research tools.   Pre-employment checks and declarations     Your employment is conditional upon the successful completion of all pre-employment or background checks required for the role in terms satisfactory to the University.  Also, to meet the University’s obligations under the National Higher Education Code to Prevent and Eliminate Gender-Based Violence you will be asked to declare if you have been investigated for, or found to engaged in, sexual harm or gender-based violence in the course of previous employment or in a legal process. Similarly, your ongoing employment is conditional upon the satisfactory maintenance of all relevant clearances and background check requirements. If you do not meet these conditions, the University may take any necessary step, including the termination of your employment.     EEO statement    At the University of Sydney, our shared values are trust, accountability and excellence and we strive to be a place where everyone can thrive. We are committed to creating a University community that thrives through diversity and reflects the wider community that we serve. We deliver on this through our commitment to diversity and inclusion , evidenced by our people and culture programs, as well as key strategies to increase participation and support the careers of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander People , women , people living with a disability , people from culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds , and those who identify as LGBTQIA+ . We welcome applications from candidates from all backgrounds.    We are proud to be recognised as an Australian Workplace Equality Index (AWEI) Platinum Employer. Find out more about our work on diversity and inclusion .    How to apply   Applications (including a cover letter, CV, and any additional supporting documentation) can be submitted via the Apply button at the top of the page.    For employees of the University or contingent workers, please login into your Workday account and navigate to the Career icon on your Dashboard.  Click on USYD Find Jobs and apply.   For a confidential discussion about the role, or if you require reasonable adjustment or any documents in alternate formats, please contact Rebecca Astar or Cherie Goodwin, Recruitment Operations by email to recruitment.sea@sydney.edu.au © The University of Sydney The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Click to view the Position Description for this role. Applications Close Tuesday 20 October 2026 11:59 PM"
    },
    "https://usyd.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/USYD_EXTERNAL_CAREER_SITE/job/Darlington-Campus/Learning-Success-Advisor--Language-and-Learning-_0152997-2": {
      "title": "Learning Success Advisor (Language and Learning)",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-23\nFull time\n\nFull time, continuing (contingent on funding) Make a meaningful impact on student success by delivering tailored learning support in a collaborative university environment Base Salary $117,133 + 17% superannuation     About the opportunity   The Learning Success Advisor is a member of a team of specialist professional staff focusing on student facing support responsible for the facilitation of post-entry academic language and learning support initiatives for all students (domestic and international) and contributes to the University’s strategic initiatives for the provision of learning support.    The Learning Success Advisor works in partnership with faculties, schools, and the library to facilitate the development of discipline specific integrated and embedded programs, develop and evaluate resources for learning skills programs, provide groups and individual learning skills sessions, contribute to the development and delivery of peer-learning initiatives, advise students on personalised learning plans and direct students to relevant learning support resources, monitor student engagement via the centralised learning support portal, administer online diagnostic tools and milestone tasks and contribute to the development of plans, strategies and procedures in line with the University’s strategy.    Your key responsibilities will be to:   work within a dynamic team, reporting to the Learning Hub Lead  design and create learning and teaching materials for the area  contribute to the development, implementation and evaluation of post-entry academic language and learning support initiatives for all students through providing specialist guidance and advice  deliver learning support, advising and training activities to students, peer-facilitators and staff for up to 20 hours a week  work collaboratively with the Learning Hub Lead, Learning Success Advisors and colleagues across CET + Learning Hub to ensure effective contribution to learning support programs and initiatives  contribute to and engage actively with the post-entry academic language and learning community beyond the University to further the reputation of the University.    About you   post graduate qualifications and/or relevant work experience in a related field (e.g., applied linguistics, Higher Education, TESOL)  experience facilitating learning support initiatives within the higher education/knowledge sector  experience in developing and facilitating high-quality, innovative programs to support post-entry academic language and learning  theoretical and practical understanding of effective educational practice in the post-entry, higher education space  demonstrated experience in designing and delivering discipline-specific academic language support programs  experience using learning analytics to inform learning design, support student progress and monitor student outcomes  experience using innovative educational technology platforms to facilitate language and communication support to diverse cohorts of students.    Sponsorship / work rights for Australia   You must have unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this employment to be eligible to apply. Visa sponsorship is not available for this appointment.     Pre-employment checks and declarations       Your employment is conditional upon the successful completion of all pre-employment or background checks required for the role in terms satisfactory to the University.  Also, to meet the University’s obligations under the National Higher Education Code to Prevent and Eliminate Gender-Based Violence you will be asked to declare if you have been investigated for, or found to engaged in, sexual harm or gender-based violence in the course of previous employment or in a legal process. Similarly, your ongoing employment is conditional upon the satisfactory maintenance of all relevant clearances and background check requirements. If you do not meet these conditions, the University may take any necessary step, including the termination of your employment.   This position is designated as involving child-related work. To undertake or remain in this position, you are required to apply for and obtain a Working with Children Check clearance in accordance with the Child Protection (Working with Children) Act 2012.   EEO statement     At the University of Sydney, our shared values are trust, accountability and excellence and we strive to be a place where everyone can thrive. We are committed to creating a University community that thrives through diversity and reflects the wider community that we serve. We deliver on this through our commitment to diversity and inclusion , evidenced by our people and culture programs, as well as key strategies to increase participation and support the careers of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander People , women , people living with a disability , people from culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds , and those who identify as LGBTQIA+ . We welcome applications from candidates from all backgrounds.    We are proud to be recognised as an Australian Workplace Equality Index (AWEI) Platinum Employer. Find out more about our work on diversity and inclusion .    How to apply   Applications (including a cover letter, CV, and any additional supporting documentation) can be submitted via the Apply button at the top of the page.     For employees of the University or contingent workers, please login into your Workday account and navigate to the Career icon on your Dashboard.  Click on USYD Find Jobs and apply.   For a confidential discussion about the role, or if you require reasonable adjustment or any documents in alternate formats, please contact Tooba Fida, Recruitment Operations by email to tooba.fida@sydney.edu.au   © The University of Sydney   The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Click to view the Position Description for this role. Applications Close Thursday 22 October 2026 11:59 PM"
    },
    "https://usyd.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/USYD_EXTERNAL_CAREER_SITE/job/Camperdown-Campus/Senior-Lecturer--Teaching-and-Research--Behavioural-and-Social-Sciences-in-Health_0157035-1": {
      "title": "Senior Lecturer (Teaching and Research)-Behavioural and Social Sciences in Health",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-22\nFull time\n\nFull time continuing position available Exciting opportunity for a Lecturer to contribute to teaching and research within the Discipline of Behavioural and Social Sciences in Health within in The Sydney School of Health Sciences Base Salary ranging from $132,533- $187,198 + 17% superannuation  About the opportunity The Sydney School of Health Sciences is in a period of exciting and significant investment by the Faculty of Medicine and Health. In order to deliver on the University’s ambitious strategic plan, the University is seeking academics who are passionate about improving health outcomes and/or health equity in the local, national and global communities, who are committed to teaching and research, and area team player ready to contribute across the broad field of academic activities. The Discipline of Behavioural and Social Sciences in Health (BSSH) brings a social science perspective to understanding health and healthcare, exploring the social, cultural, economic and political factors that influence health outcomes and contribute to inequities. The University is seeking to employee a Lecturer/ Senior Lecturer ( Level B or Level C - Teaching and Research) to play a key role in advancing the discipline's strategic focus on health equity, social determinants of health, health services research and research methods education. Working within a collaborative and interdisciplinary environment, the successful candidate will contribute to innovative teaching, impactful research and strong partnerships across the University and external health sectors.   This is a continuing full time 40% (teaching), 40% (research), and 20% (service) position, and offers an opportunity to develop and contribute to research and teaching in The Discipline of Behavioural and Social Sciences in Health (BSSH) as part of a multi-disciplinary team at the Faculty of Medicine and Health.   Your key responsibilities will be to:   Design, coordinate and deliver high-quality teaching across quantitative, qualitative and mixed-methods research approaches Contribute to research methods education across undergraduate, honours and postgraduate programs Coordinate units of study, including assessment design, moderation, quality assurance and management of teaching teams Supervise honours students and contribute to the supervision of Higher Degree by Research (HDR) candidates Support curriculum development, review and integration of research methods teaching across the School Maintain an active and impactful research program aligned with the Discipline's strategic priorities Contribute to research funding applications, scholarly publications and collaborative research initiatives Provide research methods advice and capacity-building opportunities for students and academic colleagues Collaborate with allied health, clinical and interdisciplinary teams to enhance teaching and research outcomes Participate in academic service activities, including committees, student advising, outreach and community engagement.   About you Demonstrated experience teaching university-level research methods across quantitative methodologies Experience in curriculum design, unit coordination and assessment development within higher education Proven ability to supervise or support student research projects at honours and/or postgraduate level Strong knowledge of research design, statistical analysis, quantitative methodologies and mixed-methods approaches Established or emerging research profile, including peer-reviewed publications and research collaborations Experience securing or contributing to competitive research funding is highly desirable Excellent written, verbal and interpersonal communication skills, with the ability to explain complex methodological concepts to diverse audiences Commitment to equity, social justice and culturally safe teaching and research practices Demonstrated ability to work collaboratively across disciplinary boundaries and contribute positively to an academic team Ability to engage and support students from diverse backgrounds, including those with limited confidence in research methods and statistics Commitment to continuous improvement, innovation in teaching, and the broader academic life of the Discipline, School and Faculty.   Pre-employment checks and declarations       Your employment is conditional upon the successful completion of all pre-employment or background checks required for the role in terms satisfactory to the University.  Also, to meet the University’s obligations under the National Higher Education Code to Prevent and Eliminate Gender-Based Violence you will be asked to declare if you have been investigated for, or found to engaged in, sexual harm or gender-based violence in the course of previous employment or in a legal process. Similarly, your ongoing employment is conditional upon the satisfactory maintenance of all relevant clearances and background check requirements. If you do not meet these conditions, the University may take any necessary step, including the termination of your employment.   EEO statement     At the University of Sydney, our shared values are trust, accountability and excellence and we strive to be a place where everyone can thrive. We are committed to creating a University community that thrives through diversity and reflects the wider community that we serve. We deliver on this through our commitment to diversity and inclusion , evidenced by our people and culture programs, as well as key strategies to increase participation and support the careers of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander People , women , people living with a disability , people from culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds , and those who identify as LGBTQIA+ . We welcome applications from candidates from all backgrounds.    We are proud to be recognised as an Australian Workplace Equality Index (AWEI) Platinum Employer. Find out more about our work on diversity and inclusion .    How to apply   Applications (including a cover letter, CV, and any additional supporting documentation) can be submitted via the Apply button at the top of the page. To submit an application for this role please provide the following information: 1. A cover letter which includes a written response to selection criteria addressing your ability to meet the requirements outlined above within the context of the key responsibilities of the role and relevant discipline (maximum 1000 words). 3. Your current CV or resume, including two referees. Please ensure your cover letter is clear, concise, addressing the selection criteria and states the type of employment in which you are applyin g.     For employees of the University or contingent workers, please login into your Workday account and navigate to the Career icon on your Dashboard.  Click on USYD Find Jobs and apply.   For a confidential discussion about the role, or if you require reasonable adjustment or any documents in alternate formats, please contact Rachel Yazigi, Recruitment Operations by email to rachel.yazigi@sydney.edu.au.   © The University of Sydney The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Applications Close Sunday 25 October 2026 11:59 PM"
    },
    "https://usyd.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/USYD_EXTERNAL_CAREER_SITE/job/Camperdown-Campus/Postdoctoral-Research-Associate-in-Polymer-Chemistry-and-Hydrogel-Materials_0157297-1": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Research Associate in Polymer Chemistry and Hydrogel Materials",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-25\nFull time\n\nFull time fixed term for 1 year at Camperdown (potential for extension contingent upon funding and performance) Opportunity to contribute to innovative polymer chemistry research Base Salary Level A, $93,424 - $125,896 + 17% superannuation About the opportunity The School of Chemistry is seeking a highly motivated Postdoctoral Research Associate in Polymer Chemistry and Hydrogel Materials to join a dynamic research team focused on the design, synthesis and characterisation of advanced polymeric materials. The successful candidate will undertake cutting-edge research, contribute to high-impact publications, and collaborate with academic and industry partners to advance fundamental and applied polymer science.   This position offers an exciting opportunity to work in a leading research environment with access to state-of-the-art facilities under the supervision of Professor Markus Muellner and to contribute to the University’s research excellence agenda. Click here to learn more School of Chemistry & Polymer Nanostructures Group Your key responsibilities will be to: conduct independent and collaborative research in polymer chemistry and materials science design, synthesise and characterise novel polymeric materials and hydrogels using contemporary synthetic methodologies analyse and interpret experimental data and prepare manuscripts for publication in peer-reviewed journals contribute to the development of research proposals and funding applications present research outcomes at seminars, conferences and stakeholder meetings supervise and provide guidance to higher degree research students where appropriate maintain accurate research records and comply with laboratory safety and regulatory requirements collaborate effectively with multidisciplinary research teams and external partners About you a PhD in Polymer Chemistry, Chemistry, Materials Science or a closely related discipline demonstrated experience in polymer synthesis, characterisation and materials analysis a strong publication record relative to career stage in high-quality peer-reviewed journals experience using relevant analytical techniques such as NMR, SEC, DSC, DMA, rheology or related methods evidence of the ability to undertake independent research and contribute to collaborative projects strong written and verbal communication skills, including scientific publication and presentation experience demonstrated capacity to supervise or mentor students and junior researchers commitment to research integrity, laboratory safety and inclusive research practices Pre-employment checks and declarations Your employment is conditional upon the successful completion of all pre-employment or background checks required for the role in terms satisfactory to the University.  Also, to meet the University’s obligations under the National Higher Education Code to Prevent and Eliminate Gender-Based Violence you will be asked to declare if you have been investigated for, or found to engaged in, sexual harm or gender-based violence in the course of previous employment or in a legal process. Similarly, your ongoing employment is conditional upon the satisfactory maintenance of all relevant clearances and background check requirements. If you do not meet these conditions, the University may take any necessary step, including the termination of your employment. EEO statement At the University of Sydney, our shared values are trust, accountability and excellence and we strive to be a place where everyone can thrive. We are committed to creating a University community that thrives through diversity and reflects the wider community that we serve. We deliver on this through our commitment to diversity and inclusion , evidenced by our people and culture programs, as well as key strategies to increase participation and support the careers of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander People , women , people living with a disability , people from culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds , and those who identify as LGBTQIA+ . We welcome applications from candidates from all backgrounds.    We are proud to be recognised as an Australian Workplace Equality Index (AWEI) Platinum Employer. Find out more about our work on diversity and inclusion .  How to apply Applications (including a cover letter, CV, and any additional supporting documentation) can be submitted via the Apply button at the top of the page. For employees of the University or contingent workers, please login into your Workday account and navigate to the Career icon on your Dashboard. Click on USYD Find Jobs and apply. For a confidential discussion about the role, or if you require reasonable adjustment or any documents in alternate formats, please contact Deepna Chand, Recruitment Operations by email to recruitment.sea@sydney.edu.au . © The University of Sydney The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Click to view the Position Description for this role. Applications Close Monday 26 October 2026 11:59 PM"
    },
    "https://usyd.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/USYD_EXTERNAL_CAREER_SITE/job/Darlington-Campus/Postdoctoral-Research-Associate-in-Electrocatalysis_0155949": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Research Associate in Electrocatalysis",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-25\nFull time\n\n·       Full time, fixed term for 2 years  ·       Use your expertise in the synthesis of functional materials and their application in electrocatalytic chemical conversion ·       Base Salary $113,634 - $117,936 + 17% superannuation    About the opportunity   The School of Chemical and Biomolecular Engineering at the University of Sydney, established in 1946, offered the first university-level chemical engineering program in Australia. The School is committed to teaching excellence and our graduates are highly regarded and sought after by employers in Australia and overseas. The School is research-intensive and considered a world leader in many cutting edge areas of chemical and biomolecular engineering.   We are currently seeking a Postdoctoral Research Associate to undertake computational research in electrocatalysis to contribute to the externally funded GETCO2 projects and collaborate with experimental researchers.    Your key responsibilities will be to: perform first-principles/DFT studies of electrocatalytic reactions, catalyst surfaces and interfaces, reaction mechanisms, activity and selectivity develop reproducible atomistic simulation and high-throughput workflows using Python, ASE and relevant DFT software analyse electrochemical free-energy landscapes and connect elementary-step energetics to catalytic performance apply machine-learning interatomic potentials and microkinetic models where appropriate collaborate with experimental researchers, publish and present research outcomes, and contribute to student mentoring and group activities. About you   The University values courage and creativity; openness and engagement; inclusion and diversity; and respect and integrity. As such, we see the importance of recruiting talent aligned to these values and are looking for a Postdoctoral Research Associate in Electrocatalysis who has:   a PhD in chemical engineering, chemistry, materials science, physics or a closely related discipline, completed by the start date deep understanding of electrochemistry/electrocatalysis and interfacial reaction mechanisms demonstrated proficiency in DFT and at least one major electronic-structure package (for example VASP, Quantum ESPRESSO, CP2K, GPAW or equivalent) strong Python, ASE, Linux/HPC, data-analysis and reproducible-workflow skills strong research publication, communication and collaboration record appropriate to career stage.   The following experience will be highly regarded: experience with machine-learning interatomic potentials (such as MACE, NequIP, GAP, DeePMD or similar) experience with microkinetic modelling (such as CatMAP or custom Python implementations) experience with constant-potential simulations, explicit-solvent methods, ab initio molecular dynamics or electrochemical-interface modelling.     Pre-employment checks and declarations     Your employment is conditional upon the successful completion of all pre-employment or background checks required for the role in terms satisfactory to the University.  Also, to meet the University’s obligations under the National Higher Education Code to Prevent and Eliminate Gender-Based Violence you will be asked to declare if you have been investigated for, or found to engaged in, sexual harm or gender-based violence in the course of previous employment or in a legal process. Similarly, your ongoing employment is conditional upon the satisfactory maintenance of all relevant clearances and background check requirements. If you do not meet these conditions, the University may take any necessary step, including the termination of your employment.     EEO statement     At the University of Sydney, our shared values are trust, accountability and excellence and we strive to be a place where everyone can thrive. We are committed to creating a University community that thrives through diversity and reflects the wider community that we serve. We deliver on this through our commitment to diversity and inclusion , evidenced by our people and culture programs, as well as key strategies to increase participation and support the careers of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander People , women , people living with a disability , people from culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds , and those who identify as LGBTQIA+ . We welcome applications from candidates from all backgrounds.    We are proud to be recognised as an Australian Workplace Equality Index (AWEI) Platinum Employer. Find out more about our work on diversity and inclusion .    How to apply   Applications (including a cover letter, CV, and any additional supporting documentation) can be submitted via the Apply button at the top of the page.    For employees of the University or contingent workers, please login into your Workday account and navigate to the Career icon on your Dashboard.  Click on USYD Find Jobs and apply.   For a confidential discussion about the role, or if you require reasonable adjustment or support filling out this application, please contact Rebecca Astar or Cherie Goodwin, Recruitment Consultants at recruitment.sea@sydney.edu.au     © The University of Sydney   The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Click to view the Position Description for this role. Applications Close Monday 26 October 2026 11:59 PM"
    },
    "https://usyd.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/USYD_EXTERNAL_CAREER_SITE/job/Camperdown-Campus/Postdoctoral-Research-Associate_0156806-1": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Research Associate",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-25\nFull time\n\nFull time 4 year fixed term contract avavablie Be apart of the SICK-day Medication Education to Decrease Harm in people with diabetes, cardiovascular or kidney disease (SICKMEDS) project Base Salary starting from $ 117,936 + 17% superannuatio n About the opportunity The Sydney School of Pharmacy is seeking a highly motivated and research-focused Postdoctoral Research Associate (Level A) to join an exciting externally funded research project investigating medication safety in people living with diabetes, cardiovascular disease and chronic kidney disease. Medication-related problems (MRPs) are responsible for approximately 250,000 hospital admissions annually in Australia, costing the health system an estimated $1.4 billion each year. Importantly, many of these admissions are preventable through optimised prescribing and improved consumer support regarding medicine use. This position will contribute to the SICK-day Medication Education to Decrease Harm in people with diabetes, cardiovascular or kidney disease (SICKMEDS) project. The project aims to evaluate the impact of SICK-MEDS education on reducing acute kidney injury, hospitalisation rates in culturally and linguistically diverse people. Working closely with Associate Professor Ronald Castelino and a multidisciplinary research team, the successful candidate will play a leading role in the management and delivery of the research program, including patient engagement, co-design activities, data collection, analysis and dissemination of findings. Your key responsibilities will be to: Coordinate and manage day-to-day delivery of the SICKMEDS clinical research project Lead participant recruitment, engagement and retention activities Facilitate and support co-design workshops with patients, consumers and healthcare professionals Deliver patient education and training relating to SICK-MEDS implementation Conduct qualitative and quantitative data collection and analysis. Prepare research reports, publications, conference presentations and project outputs Contribute to ethics submissions, governance processes and regulatory requirements Collaborate with academic, clinical and consumer stakeholders across the project Support supervision and mentoring of students where appropriate. Contribute to grant reporting and dissemination activities. Participate in School, Faculty and University committees and meetings as required Occasional travel within Western Sydney for participant engagement, project activities and stakeholder meetings. About you Essential Criteria Bachelor of Pharmacy (BPharm) and/or Master of Pharmacy (MPharm) PhD in Pharmacy, Clinical Pharmacy, Health Services Research, Public Health or a related discipline Current registration as a Pharmacist with the Australian Health Practitioner Regulation Agency (AHPRA) Demonstrated understanding of medication safety, chronic disease management and related healthcare research. Hospital pharmacy experience Experience conducting original research and/or scholarly activity. Demonstrated expertise in qualitative and quantitative research methodologies Experience conducting co-design and consumer-engaged research. Proven ability to manage research projects and work independently while contributing effectively within multidisciplinary teams. Evidence of peer-reviewed publications and research dissemination. Strong communication, stakeholder engagement and relationship management skills Ability to analyse complex data and prepare high-quality research outputs and reports A developing research profile within the discipline or area of expertise A developing network of academic, industry and professional stakeholders and partners. Desirable Criteria Experience in medication safety, chronic disease management, nephrology, diabetes or cardiovascular research Experience managing clinical or implementation research projects. Familiarity with patient-centred intervention design and evaluation methodologies Experience working with culturally and linguistically diverse (CALD) communities Demonstrated success in securing competitive research funding or supporting grant-funded research programs. To keep our community safe, please be aware of our COVID safety precautions which form our conditions of entry for all staff, students and visitors coming to campus. Work Rights You must have unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this employment to apply. Visa sponsorship is not available for this appointment. Pre-employment checks Your employment is conditional upon the completion of all role required pre-employment or background checks in terms satisfactory to the University. Similarly, your ongoing employment is conditional upon the satisfactory maintenance of all relevant clearances and background check requirements. If you do not meet these conditions, the University may take any necessary step, including the termination of your employment. EEO statement At the University of Sydney, our shared values include diversity and inclusion and we strive to be a place where everyone can thrive. We are committed to creating a University community that reflects the wider community that we serve. We deliver on this commitment through our people and culture programs, as well as key strategies to increase participation and support the careers of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander People , women , people living with a disability, people from culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds , and those who identify as LGBTIQ . We welcome applications from candidates from all backgrounds. How to apply Applications (including a cover letter, CV, and any additional supporting documentation) can be submitted via the Apply button at the top of the page. For employees of the University or contingent workers, please login into your Workday account and navigate to the Career icon on your Dashboard. Click on USYD Find Jobs and apply. For a confidential discussion about the role, or if you require reasonable adjustment or any documents in alternate formats, please contact Rachel Yazigi, Recruitment Operations by email to rachel.yazigi@sydney.edu.au. © The University of Sydney The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Click to view the Position Description for this role. Applications Close Saturday 17 October 2026 11:59 PM"
    },
    "https://usyd.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/USYD_EXTERNAL_CAREER_SITE/job/Darlington-Campus/Senior-Manager--Enrolment-Services_0157228-1": {
      "title": "Senior Manager, Enrolment Services",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-28\nFull time\n\nFull time, fixed term until 31st January 2028 Lead Enrolment Services and drive improvements that enhance the student experience Base Salary $162,528 + 17% superannuation    About the opportunity   An exciting opportunity exists for a Senior Manager, Enrolment Services to lead the Enrolment Services team within Student Services. Working closely with the Director, Student Services and senior stakeholders, you will lead the effective delivery of enrolment services across the student lifecycle while driving strategic initiatives, service improvements and a positive, high-performing team culture.   Your key responsibilities will be to:   work closely with the Director, Student Services, senior leaders, students and key stakeholders across the University lead the Enrolment Services team, aligning workforce and resources to deliver high-quality services oversee operational planning and delivery of enrolment services across the student lifecycle drive continuous improvement of enrolment systems, processes and services to enhance the student experience provide strategic advice and insights on enrolment services, operational performance, risks and opportunities build strong stakeholder partnerships to support effective service delivery and strategic initiatives.   About you   proven senior management experience leading large service delivery teams within a complex organisational environment demonstrated experience in student administration or student services, with an understanding of issues impacting the student experience demonstrated experience managing client services using ServiceNow or similar service management platforms demonstrated capability in workforce planning, people leadership, performance management and resolving complex HR matters experience in financial management, resource planning and managing operational priorities and performance relevant bachelor’s degree or an equivalent combination of relevant training and experience.   Sponsorship / work rights for Australia   You must have unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this employment to be eligible to apply. Visa sponsorship is not available for this appointment.     Pre-employment checks and declarations       Your employment is conditional upon the successful completion of all pre-employment or background checks required for the role in terms satisfactory to the University.  Also, to meet the University’s obligations under the National Higher Education Code to Prevent and Eliminate Gender-Based Violence you will be asked to declare if you have been investigated for, or found to engaged in, sexual harm or gender-based violence in the course of previous employment or in a legal process. Similarly, your ongoing employment is conditional upon the satisfactory maintenance of all relevant clearances and background check requirements. If you do not meet these conditions, the University may take any necessary step, including the termination of your employment.   EEO statement     At the University of Sydney, our shared values are trust, accountability and excellence and we strive to be a place where everyone can thrive. We are committed to creating a University community that thrives through diversity and reflects the wider community that we serve. We deliver on this through our commitment to diversity and inclusion , evidenced by our people and culture programs, as well as key strategies to increase participation and support the careers of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander People , women , people living with a disability , people from culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds , and those who identify as LGBTQIA+ . We welcome applications from candidates from all backgrounds.    We are proud to be recognised as an Australian Workplace Equality Index (AWEI) Platinum Employer. Find out more about our work on diversity and inclusion .    How to apply   Applications (including a cover letter, CV, and any additional supporting documentation) can be submitted via the Apply button at the top of the page.     For employees of the University or contingent workers, please login into your Workday account and navigate to the Career icon on your Dashboard.  Click on USYD Find Jobs and apply.   For a confidential discussion about the role, or if you require reasonable adjustment or any documents in alternate formats, please contact Tooba Fida, Recruitment Operations by email to tooba.fida@sydney.edu.au   © The University of Sydney   The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Click to view the Position Description for this role. Applications Close Sunday 18 October 2026 11:59 PM"
    },
    "https://usyd.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/USYD_EXTERNAL_CAREER_SITE/job/Camperdown-Campus/Lecturer-in-Statistics_0157737-1": {
      "title": "Lecturer in Statistics",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-29\nFull time\n\nOne 5-year and one 3-year full-time fixed term position, located on the Camperdown Campus at the School of Mathematics and Statistics Join a world-class community of statisticians, data scientists and mathematicians Base Salary, Academic Level B $132,533 – $157,381 + 17% superannuation   About the opportunity   The School of Mathematics and Statistics is currently seeking a Lecturer in Statistics who will have the opportunity to make a significant contribution to teaching and learning design, delivery, and evaluation in statistics while continuing to build their own research program and network. These roles are replacements for staff members who are on fellowships.   Please specify the preferred duration of the contract in your cover letter.   Your key responsibilities will be to: teach graduate and undergraduate courses in statistics and data science for students from different disciplinary backgrounds conduct high-quality research in statistics or related areas supervise and coordinate graduate and undergraduate students contribute to the administration and collegial life of the School, working closely with colleagues in statistics and related areas attract external funding.   About you   a PhD (or near completion) in statistics or a closely related area evidence of independent research, compatible with the standards at The University of Sydney evidence of ability, versatility, and commitment to excellence in teaching statistics.   The following will be highly regarded:   5-year position: demonstrated ability to build productive collaborations with researchers from diverse disciplinary backgrounds and to translate statistical methodology into practice through consultation and collaborative research experience teaching statistics or data science to students from a range of disciplinary backgrounds evidence of research impact through collaborative publications, methodological adoption, or contributions to successful funding applications   3-year position: experience working with industry, government or other external partners a strong record in methodological or applied statistics, with demonstrated impact experience in teaching leadership and administration (e.g. unit coordination)   Pre-employment checks and declarations     Your employment is conditional upon the successful completion of all pre-employment or background checks required for the role in terms satisfactory to the University.  Also, to meet the University’s obligations under the National Higher Education Code to Prevent and Eliminate Gender-Based Violence you will be asked to declare if you have been investigated for, or found to engaged in, sexual harm or gender-based violence in the course of previous employment or in a legal process. Similarly, your ongoing employment is conditional upon the satisfactory maintenance of all relevant clearances and background check requirements. If you do not meet these conditions, the University may take any necessary step, including the termination of your employment.   EEO statement     At the University of Sydney, our shared values are trust, accountability and excellence and we strive to be a place where everyone can thrive. We are committed to creating a University community that thrives through diversity and reflects the wider community that we serve. We deliver on this through our commitment to diversity and inclusion , evidenced by our people and culture programs, as well as key strategies to increase participation and support the careers of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander People , women , people living with a disability , people from culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds , and those who identify as LGBTQIA+ . We welcome applications from candidates from all backgrounds.    We are proud to be recognised as an Australian Workplace Equality Index (AWEI) Platinum Employer. Find out more about our work on diversity and inclusion .    How to apply   Applications (including a cover letter, CV, and any additional supporting documentation) can be submitted via the Apply button at the top of the page.     For employees of the University or contingent workers, please login into your Workday account and navigate to the Career icon on your Dashboard.  Click on USYD Find Jobs and apply.   For a confidential discussion about the role, or if you require reasonable adjustment or any documents in alternate formats, please contact Simon Drew, Recruitment Operations by email to recruitment.sea@sydney.edu.au   © The University of Sydney   The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Click to view the Position Description for this role. Applications Close Sunday 01 November 2026 11:59 PM"
    },
    "https://usyd.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/USYD_EXTERNAL_CAREER_SITE/job/Darlington-Campus/International-Development-and-Sponsorships-Manager_0157279-1": {
      "title": "International Development and Sponsorships Manager",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-29\nFull time\n\nFull-time, continuing opportunity Lead Global Partnerships and Grow International Sponsorship Opportunities HE0 9: $152,045 - $160,430 plus 17% Superannuation     About the opportunity     The International Development and Sponsorships Manager is a senior leadership role within Sydney Future Students' International team, responsible for driving the growth and success of the University's international sponsorship portfolio. Leading a small team, you will develop and strengthen relationships with government agencies, scholarship bodies, sponsors and international partners to increase sponsored student enrolments and support the University's internationalisation strategy.   Working at a strategic level, you will oversee sponsorship programs, including Australia Awards and other sponsored student cohorts, while acting as the University's key relationship manager for sponsors and government stakeholders. You will work closely with embassies, consulates, scholarship agencies and DFAT to identify new opportunities, strengthen existing partnerships and ensure the University remains a preferred education partner for sponsored students globally.   While your team manages the day-to-day administration and support of sponsored students, your focus will be on partnership development, business growth, stakeholder engagement and delivering long-term strategic outcomes. This is an exciting opportunity for an experienced international education professional to influence institutional growth, represent the University on a global stage and build meaningful partnerships that create life-changing educational opportunities.       Key responsibilities   Develop and implement strategies to grow sponsored student enrolments and expand the University's international sponsorship portfolio. Build and maintain strategic relationships with sponsors, government agencies, embassies, consulates, scholarship bodies and other key international stakeholders. Lead, coach and develop a high-performing team, ensuring exceptional service delivery and operational excellence. Act as the University's primary liaison with sponsors and government stakeholders, including DFAT, on strategic sponsorship and partnership matters. Identify emerging market opportunities, deliver business development initiatives and provide strategic advice to support recruitment growth. Represent the University locally and internationally through stakeholder engagement activities, events and partnership initiatives.   About you   Demonstrated leadership experience managing and developing high-performing teams within international education, partnerships, business development or student recruitment environments. Proven success building and managing strategic relationships with government agencies, sponsors, scholarship providers or other complex external stakeholders. Strong business development experience with the ability to identify opportunities, influence outcomes and deliver sustainable growth. Experience engaging with senior stakeholders across diverse cultural environments, with highly developed communication, negotiation and relationship management skills. Strong understanding of the international higher education sector and the factors influencing international student recruitment and sponsorship programs. Relevant tertiary qualifications or equivalent experience, with the ability to travel domestically and internationally as required.   Join a globally recognised institution and play a pivotal role in strengthening international partnerships, growing sponsored student opportunities and enhancing the University's global impact.   Sponsorship / work rights for Australia   You must have unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this employment to be eligible to apply. Visa sponsorship is not available for this appointment.     Pre-employment checks and declarations     Your employment is conditional upon the successful completion of all pre-employment or background checks required for the role in terms satisfactory to the University.  Also, to meet the University’s obligations under the National Higher Education Code to Prevent and Eliminate Gender-Based Violence you will be asked to declare if you have been investigated for, or found to engaged in, sexual harm or gender-based violence in the course of previous employment or in a legal process. Similarly, your ongoing employment is conditional upon the satisfactory maintenance of all relevant clearances and background check requirements. If you do not meet these conditions, the University may take any necessary step, including the termination of your employment.   This position is designated as involving child-related work. To undertake or remain in this position, you are required to apply for and obtain a Working with Children Check clearance in accordance with the Child Protection (Working with Children) Act 2012.     EEO statement     At the University of Sydney, our shared values are trust, accountability and excellence and we strive to be a place where everyone can thrive. We are committed to creating a University community that thrives through diversity and reflects the wider community that we serve. We deliver on this through our commitment to diversity and inclusion , evidenced by our people and culture programs, as well as key strategies to increase participation and support the careers of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander People , women , people living with a disability , people from culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds , and those who identify as LGBTQIA+ . We welcome applications from candidates from all backgrounds.    We are proud to be recognised as an Australian Workplace Equality Index (AWEI) Platinum Employer. Find out more about our work on diversity and inclusion .      How to apply   Applications (including a cover letter, CV, and any additional supporting documentation) can be submitted via the Apply button at the top of the page.    For a confidential discussion about the role, or if you require reasonable adjustment or support filling out this application, please contact Kristy Fairweather, Recruitment Operations, by email to kristy.fairweather@sydney.edu.au   © The University of Sydney   The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Click to view the Position Description for this role. Applications Close Sunday 18 October 2026 11:59 PM"
    },
    "https://usyd.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/USYD_EXTERNAL_CAREER_SITE/job/Camperdown-Campus/Research-Fellow---Coastal-Geomorphology_0156265-1": {
      "title": "Research Fellow - Coastal Geomorphology",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-29\nFull time\n\nFull time Fixed term for 3 years at Camperdown Join an exciting new research initiative in coastal wetland sedimentology Base Salary $132,533 - $157,381 + 17% superannuation About the opportunity The School of Geosciences is seeking a Research Fellow - Coastal Geomorphology to join a newly established research position. This role will contribute to a major research project focused on coastal wetland sedimentology, advancing understanding of sediment processes, landscape evolution and environmental change in coastal systems.   Working under broad direction, you will undertake high-quality independent and collaborative research, contribute to publications and research outputs, and participate in field-based investigations across coastal environments. The position offers an outstanding opportunity to build a strong research profile while collaborating with leading academics and external partners. Your key responsibilities will be to: undertake research in coastal wetland sedimentology and related geomorphological processes conduct fieldwork, sample collection and data analysis across coastal environments publish high-quality research outputs in leading scholarly journals contribute to conference presentations, seminars and research dissemination activities support the development of competitive research funding applications develop productive collaborations with academic, industry and government partners contribute to the supervision and mentoring of higher degree research students where appropriate maintain excellence in research quality, safety and project delivery contribute to a positive and inclusive research culture within the School. About you PhD in coastal geomorphology, sedimentology, Earth science, environmental science or a closely related discipline demonstrated experience conducting original research and developing a research profile in a relevant field expertise in coastal wetland processes, sediment dynamics and/or geomorphological research proven experience undertaking field-based research in coastal environments strong publication record relative to career stage excellent quantitative, analytical and scientific communication skills ability to work independently and collaboratively within multidisciplinary research teams developing network of academic, industry and professional stakeholders The School of Geosciences at the University of Sydney is a dynamic and collegial multi-disciplinary community of scholars and students encompassing the fields of geology and geophysics, marine science, environmental studies, and physical and human geography. It has a strong global reputation for the quality of its research and teaching across those domains. Our common passion is to engage in research and teaching that promotes a better understanding of our world and its challenges, and we address key issues facing society including climate change, resource management and sustainability.   The School of Geosciences is firmly committed to creating a diverse workplace by improving equity, access and opportunity. We want to create an environment where individuality is welcomed and celebrated, and a workplace that is open, supportive and safe for everyone. We are actively seeking to improve the diversity of our staffing profile, and welcome applications from women, people of culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds, people of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander descent, and members of the LGBTIQ+ community.   To learn more about the School of Geosciences, click here Pre-employment checks Your employment is conditional upon the successful completion of all pre-employment or background checks required for the role in terms satisfactory to the University.  Also, to meet the University’s obligations under the National Higher Education Code to Prevent and Eliminate Gender-Based Violence you will be asked to declare if you have been investigated for, or found to engaged in, sexual harm or gender-based violence in the course of previous employment or in a legal process. Similarly, your ongoing employment is conditional upon the satisfactory maintenance of all relevant clearances and background check requirements. If you do not meet these conditions, the University may take any necessary step, including the termination of your employment. EEO statement At the University of Sydney, our shared values are trust, accountability and excellence and we strive to be a place where everyone can thrive. We are committed to creating a University community that thrives through diversity and reflects the wider community that we serve. We deliver on this through our commitment to diversity and inclusion , evidenced by our people and culture programs, as well as key strategies to increase participation and support the careers of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander People , women , people living with a disability , people from culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds , and those who identify as LGBTQIA+ . We welcome applications from candidates from all backgrounds.    We are proud to be recognised as an Australian Workplace Equality Index (AWEI) Platinum Employer. Find out more about our work on diversity and inclusion .  How to apply Applications (including a cover letter, CV, and any additional supporting documentation) can be submitted via the Apply button at the top of the page. For employees of the University or contingent workers, please login into your Workday account and navigate to the Career icon on your Dashboard. Click on USYD Find Jobs and apply. For a confidential discussion about the role, please contact the Head of School of Geosciences, Professor Kerrylee Rogers via email: kerrylee.rogers@sydney.edu.au   If you require reasonable adjustment or any documents in alternate formats, please contact Deepna Chand, Recruitment Operations by email to recruitment.sea@sydney.edu.au . © The University of Sydney The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Click to view the Position Description for this role. Applications Close Thursday 29 October 2026 11:59 PM"
    },
    "https://usyd.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/USYD_EXTERNAL_CAREER_SITE/job/Newtown/Daffodil-Centre-Research-Fellow---Senior-Research-Fellow_0157465": {
      "title": "Daffodil Centre Research Fellow / Senior Research Fellow",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-29\nFull time\n\nFull-time, 2-year fixed-term opportunities (with a possible 1-year extension) at the Daffodil Centre, based in Moore College (Camperdown campus) Join the Daffodil Centre as either a Research Fellow or Senior Research Fellow, and use your expertise, research leadership and national profile to deliver high-quality cancer research with a population focus Academic Level B $132,533 - $157,381 or Academic Level C $162,347 - $187,198 + 17% superannuation     About the opportunity   The Daffodil Centre is a world-leading research centre, directly influencing policy decisions and real-world public health and clinical practice. The Centre is a partnership that combines the complementary strengths of the University of Sydney and Cancer Council NSW to accelerate progress in cancer prevention, early detection and cancer outcomes across the population. The partnership has created an unparalleled centre of national and international research excellence where research results in real-world improvements to reduce the burden from cancer in the community. Join the Daffodil Centre at the University of Sydney and contribute to research that is shaping the future of cancer control in Australia and internationally. Either as a Research Fellow or Senior Research Fellow, you will bring recognised expertise and an established research profile to an environment where research excellence is translated into meaningful outcomes for policy, practice and communities.   Working with a high degree of autonomy and under broad direction, you will lead impactful research, pursue competitive funding opportunities, build strategic partnerships and provide expert advice within your area of expertise, ideally related to cancer prevention or early detection with a population focus. You will play an important role in strengthening the Centre’s research standing and influence, while mentoring emerging researchers and contributing to its broader strategic, academic and governance priorities. This is an opportunity to build on your national reputation for research excellence, expand your influence within your discipline and contribute to the Daffodil Centre’s mission to deliver research that makes a real difference to cancer control.   We would like to hear from early- or mid-career researchers who have completed a PhD related to public health, epidemiology, health services research, health economics, biostatistics, implementation science, behavioural science, health policy or clinical fields. Experience working with priority populations as defined in the Australian Cancer Plan will be highly regarded. Mentoring and career development opportunities will be provided.   A description of the Daffodil Centre and its teams is available on the Centre website . Have a preference for a certain research project and/or research team? Feel free to specify within your application when applying for the role.   Your key responsibilities as an Academic Level B Research Fellow:   Conduct high-quality original cancer research independently and collaboratively, contributing to publications, conference presentations and competitive research funding applications. Build and maintain a strong research profile through peer-reviewed publications, conference and seminar presentations, and other scholarly outputs. Develop research collaborations and contribute to translational research that informs policy and practice and delivers meaningful outcomes for the community, profession and government. Contribute to the development of competitive research grant applications and research activities that generate impact and support the strategic priorities of the Daffodil Centre and University. Provide training, informal mentoring and guidance to colleagues and postgraduate research students, where appropriate, while contributing to a positive, collaborative and inclusive research culture. Develop productive relationships with research partners, government, industry and other external stakeholders, contributing expertise to forums, seminars, conferences and public debate.   In addition to the above, as an Academic Level C Senior Research Fellow:   Establish and nurture partnerships and collaborations that support cancer research and deliver meaningful outcomes across policy, practice and research. Mentor and support early-career researchers, contributing to a collaborative culture of academic excellence, inclusion and innovation. Contribute to the Centre’s research, governance and strategic priorities, including academic committees, community engagement and activities that strengthen the impact and profile of the Daffodil Centre.   About you as an Academic Level B Research Fellow:   Completed PhD, with demonstrated expertise in cancer research methodologies, design and delivery. A developing research profile, with evidence of original research, scholarly publications, presentations and/or other research outputs relevant to your discipline or area of expertise. Demonstrated ability to work independently and collaboratively, applying sound judgement to research activities and providing informed advice and recommendations. A developing network of academic, government, and professional partners and stakeholders, with strong communication and relationship-building skills. Proven experience in planning and organising research activities, as well as supervising, mentoring or supporting colleagues and/or research students where appropriate.   In addition to the above, as an Academic Level C Senior Research Fellow:   A proven track record of research leadership, with the ability to develop influential research programs and contribute to expanding knowledge and practice. Significant experience supervising and mentoring researchers, with a commitment to supporting the development of emerging academic talent. An established network across academic, government, and professional institutions, partners and stakeholders, with the ability to leverage these relationships to strengthen research collaboration and impact.     Pre-employment checks and declarations     Your employment is conditional upon the successful completion of all pre-employment or background checks required for the role in terms satisfactory to the University.  Also, to meet the University’s obligations under the National Higher Education Code to Prevent and Eliminate Gender-Based Violence you will be asked to declare if you have been investigated for, or found to engaged in, sexual harm or gender-based violence in the course of previous employment or in a legal process. Similarly, your ongoing employment is conditional upon the satisfactory maintenance of all relevant clearances and background check requirements. If you do not meet these conditions, the University may take any necessary step, including the termination of your employment.   EEO statement     At the University of Sydney, our shared values are trust, accountability and excellence and we strive to be a place where everyone can thrive. We are committed to creating a University community that thrives through diversity and reflects the wider community that we serve. We deliver on this through our commitment to diversity and inclusion , evidenced by our people and culture programs, as well as key strategies to increase participation and support the careers of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander People , women , people living with a disability , people from culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds , and those who identify as LGBTQIA+ . We welcome applications from candidates from all backgrounds.    We are proud to be recognised as an Australian Workplace Equality Index (AWEI) Platinum Employer. Find out more about our work on diversity and inclusion .    How to apply   Applications (including a cover letter, CV, and any additional supporting documentation) can be submitted via the Apply button at the top of the page. In your cover letter (no more than 2 pages), please outline your research area of interest and proposed research ideas for the Fellowship, and how it would fit with the Daffodil Centre.   For employees of the University or contingent workers, please login into your Workday account and navigate to the Career icon on your Dashboard. Click on USYD Find Jobs and apply.   For a confidential discussion about the role, or if you require reasonable adjustment or any documents in alternate formats, please contact Roshik Prasad, Recruitment Operations by email to roshik.prasad@sydney.edu.au   © The University of Sydney   The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Click to view the Level C Senior Research Fellow Position Description . Click to view the Level B Research Fellow Position Description . Applications Close Sunday 01 November 2026 11:59 PM"
    },
    "https://usyd.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/USYD_EXTERNAL_CAREER_SITE/job/Darlington-Campus/Director--Australian-Centre-for-Robotics_0157383-1": {
      "title": "Director, Australian Centre for Robotics",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-29\nFull time\n\nLead one of Australia’s premier robotics centres to shape the future of autonomous systems through world-class research, innovation, and strategic partnerships. Five-year fixed-term position with an attractive remuneration package, including the possibility of a substantive continuing academic appointment at Level D/E or Professor of Practice depending on candidate profile.   About the opportunity The Faculty of Engineering seeks to appoint an exceptional academic or industry leader as Director of the Australian Centre for Robotics (ACFR). The ACFR is a global leader in robotics, autonomous systems, and intelligent machines, recognised for its pioneering research and real-world impact across industry and government. Appointed at an appropriate senior academic level (Professor, Associate Professor, or Professor of Practice), the Director will provide strategic, academic, and operational leadership to ensure ACFR remains at the forefront of robotics research and innovation. They will lead the Centre’s strategic direction, research performance, financial sustainability, and engagement with industry, government, and international collaborators. Working closely with the Associate Dean (Research), the Dean, Heads of School, and senior University leaders, the Director will align ACFR’s strategy with institutional priorities in robotics, artificial intelligence, and intelligent systems. Externally, they will represent ACFR to national and global partners, strengthening its reputation as Australia’s leading robotics research hub. Exceptional leaders from academia, industry, government, or applied research organisations are encouraged to apply. The substantive academic appointment will be tailored to the successful candidate’s qualifications, experience, and career trajectory. The Director may also contribute to teaching, supervision, mentoring, or professional training in line with their academic level. About you (Skills, capabilities and experience) You will be an accomplished academic or industry leader with deep expertise in robotics or autonomous systems and a demonstrated record of excellence in research, translation, or major engineering achievements. Candidates without a higher education qualification but with exceptional professional achievements and recognised leadership in robotics or autonomous systems may be considered for appointment as Professor of Practice. The successful candidate will: hold a PhD or equivalent terminal qualification in robotics, autonomous systems, or a closely related field demonstrate excellence in robotics and autonomous systems through scholarly outputs, major engineering achievements, translational impact, or commercial innovation be recognised internationally as a leading authority in robotics or related domains, evidenced through significant achievements, honours, or leadership roles demonstrate a strong track record of industry engagement, including partnerships with government, industry, and professional associations possess strong planning and organisational capabilities, with the ability to deliver multiple concurrent strategic initiatives show excellent communication and stakeholder management skills, with the capacity to influence and inspire at senior levels have proven people leadership experience, including mentoring, performance management, and building high-performing teams foster a collaborative and inclusive culture, with a strong commitment to equity, diversity, and inclusion contribute to teaching, supervision, mentoring, or professional training in line with the substantive academic appointment.   Sponsorship / work rights for Australia Visa sponsorship and relocation assistance will be provided if required. Term and remuneration This is a Full-time, Fixed term opportunity for 5 years . The successful candidate will be offered a competitive remuneration package commensurate with the responsibilities of the position and the candidate’s relevant experience and qualifications. Pre-employment checks Your employment is conditional upon the successful completion of all pre-employment or background checks required for the role in terms satisfactory to the University. Also, to meet the University’s obligations under the National Higher Education Code to Prevent and Eliminate Gender-Based Violence you will be asked to declare if you have been investigated for, or found to engaged in, sexual harm or gender-based violence in the course of previous employment or in a legal process. Similarly, your ongoing employment is conditional upon the satisfactory maintenance of all relevant clearances and background check requirements. If you do not meet these conditions, the University may take any necessary step, including the termination of your employment.     EEO statement  At the University of Sydney, our shared values are trust, accountability and excellence and we strive to be a place where everyone can thrive. We are committed to creating a University community that thrives through diversity and reflects the wider community that we serve. We deliver on this through our commitment to diversity and inclusion , evidenced by our people and culture programs, as well as key strategies to increase participation and support the careers of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander People , women , people living with a disability , people from culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds , and those who identify as LGBTIQ+ . We welcome applications from candidates from all backgrounds.  We are proud to be recognised as an Australian Workplace Equality Index (AWEI) Gold employer. Find out more about our work on diversity and inclusion .    How to apply Applications ( including a CV and cover letter outlining how your skills, capabilities and experience meet the requirements for the role ) can be submitted via the Apply button at the top of the page. Please refer to the candidate information brochure for further details. Candidate information brochure can be downloaded via this link: https://filesender.aarnet.edu.au/?s=download&token=5a17b1c1-5847-4071-8820-7c9e876c6efb The University has engaged executive search firm The Insight Group to assist with this appointment. The Insight Group team will support the Selection Committee to identify the widest possible field of qualified candidates and to assess candidates against the requirements for the role. For a confidential discussion about the role, please contact Rohan Carr or Emily Witts at The Insight Group, P: +61 3 9654 3288 or email: rohancarr@insightgroup.com.au , emilywitts@insightgroup.com.au © The University of Sydney The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Applications Close Wednesday 28 October 2026 11:59 PM"
    },
    "https://usyd.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/USYD_EXTERNAL_CAREER_SITE/job/Camperdown-Campus/Senior-Administration-Officer_0156484-2": {
      "title": "Senior Administration Officer",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-28\nFull time\n\nFull-time fixed-term opportunity from January 2027 to June 2027 HEO Level 6, Base Salary starting from $107,312 p.a. + 17% superannuation Suitable as a fixed-term appointment or secondment opportunity for internal candidates Join the Sydney Pharmacy School and play a key role in supporting operational and academic administration activities About the opportunity   Sydney Pharmacy School is internationally recognised for excellence in education and research and is committed to delivering outstanding support services that enable academic and professional staff to thrive. The school is seeking an experienced and highly organised Senior Administration Officer to join our professional services team on a fixed-term basis from January 2027 to June 2027 to cover a period of staff leave. This opportunity is also available as a secondment for eligible internal candidates Working closely with the School Manager, the Senior Administration Officer is a key member of the Executive Support team and provides high-level administrative, operational and project support across a broad range of school activities. This role serves as a central point of coordination for administration, human resources, finance, facilities, events and stakeholder engagement activities, helping to ensure the efficient and effective operation of the School. The successful candidate will provide expert administrative support, manage complex enquiries and operational matters, contribute to continuous improvement initiatives and maintain strong relationships across the Faculty, University and external stakeholders. This is an excellent opportunity for an experienced administrator who enjoys variety, problem-solving and working in a collaborative and customer-focused environment. Key responsibilities Provide high-level administrative and operational support to the school manager and executive support team manage a broad range of administrative activities, ensuring efficient and professional service delivery to staff, students and stakeholders respond to complex enquiries and administrative matters, exercising sound judgement, discretion and initiative provide advice and guidance on university policies, procedures and administrative processes support school staffing and human resource administration activities, including assisting with hr processes, leave management and policy enquiries coordinate meetings, events, workshops and conferences, including scheduling, logistics, travel arrangements, agendas and minutes manage correspondence and ensure matters are appropriately prioritised, actioned and escalated where required support financial administration activities including invoice processing, purchasing, budget tracking, travel acquittals and reporting prepare reports, data summaries, presentations and documentation to support decision-making and operational planning maintain accurate records and ensure compliance with university policies and procedures build and maintain productive relationships with academic and professional staff across the faculty and university identify opportunities for process improvement and contribute to initiatives that enhance operational effectiveness collaborate with administrative colleagues across the faculty and contribute to a culture of continuous improvement and service excellence undertake other duties commensurate with the classification level of the position.   About you Relevant tertiary qualifications in business administration or a related discipline, or an equivalent combination of training and experience demonstrated administrative experience within a large and complex organisation, preferably within the higher education sector experience providing high-level administrative and executive support in a fast-paced environment well-developed organisational and time management skills, with the ability to manage competing priorities and meet deadlines strong problem-solving skills and the ability to exercise sound judgement and initiative excellent verbal and written communication skills and a commitment to delivering high-quality customer service demonstrated ability to build effective working relationships with a diverse range of stakeholders experience supporting financial administration processes, including purchasing, invoice management, expenditure monitoring and reporting strong attention to detail and commitment to accuracy. advanced computer skills, including proficiency in Microsoft office and administrative systems experience working collaboratively within a team environment and contributing positively to workplace culture a commitment to fostering inclusive, respectful and professional working environments. To keep our community safe, please be aware of our COVID safety precautions which form our conditions of entry for all staff, students and visitors coming to campus. Work Rights You must have unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this employment to apply. Visa sponsorship is not available for this appointment. Pre-employment checks Your employment is conditional upon the completion of all role required pre-employment or background checks in terms satisfactory to the University. Similarly, your ongoing employment is conditional upon the satisfactory maintenance of all relevant clearances and background check requirements. If you do not meet these conditions, the University may take any necessary step, including the termination of your employment. EEO statement At the University of Sydney, our shared values include diversity and inclusion and we strive to be a place where everyone can thrive. We are committed to creating a University community that reflects the wider community that we serve. We deliver on this commitment through our people and culture programs, as well as key strategies to increase participation and support the careers of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander People , women , people living with a disability, people from culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds , and those who identify as LGBTIQ . We welcome applications from candidates from all backgrounds. How to apply Applications (including a cover letter, CV, and any additional supporting documentation) can be submitted via the Apply button at the top of the page. For employees of the University or contingent workers, please login into your Workday account and navigate to the Career icon on your Dashboard. Click on USYD Find Jobs and apply. For a confidential discussion about the role, or if you require reasonable adjustment or any documents in alternate formats, please contact Rachel Yazigi Recruitment Operations by email to rachel.yazigi@sydney.edu.au.   Applications should include: A cover letter addressing your suitability for the role A current resume/CV   For employees of the University of Sydney, internal applicants interested in a secondment opportunity are encouraged to discuss their application with their current manager prior to applying. © The University of Sydney The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Click to view the Position Description for this role. Applications Close Sunday 18 October 2026 11:59 PM"
    },
    "https://usyd.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/USYD_EXTERNAL_CAREER_SITE/job/Darlington-Campus/Senior-Manager--Indigenous-Employment-and-Engagement--Identified-_0157675-1": {
      "title": "Senior Manager, Indigenous Employment and Engagement (Identified)",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-30\nFull time\n\nFull-time, continuing opportunity Drive Indigenous employment and engagement initiatives, fostering an inclusive, culturally safe workplace and supporting the attraction, retention and development of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander staff Offering a competitive remuneration package including 17% superannuation   This position has been designated for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander people only under the provisions of section 126 of the Anti-Discrimination Act 1977 (NSW). Should your application be progressed, applicants will be required to provide confirmation of Aboriginal and/or Torres Strait Islander identity in line with university requirements.   About the opportunity   Lead the delivery of Indigenous employment and engagement strategies that strengthen attraction, retention, career development and wellbeing for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander staff, while fostering a culturally safe, inclusive and supportive workplace across the University.   Your key responsibilities will be to:   lead the development and delivery of Indigenous employment and engagement programs, initiatives and staff networks that support a culturally safe, inclusive and welcoming workplace drive Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander recruitment, retention, career development and leadership pathways, partnering with stakeholders across the university to achieve workforce goals develop, monitor and report on key workforce metrics, targets and strategic objectives, using data and insights to inform decision-making and measure progress provide expert advice on Indigenous employment, cultural safety, diversity and inclusion, influencing policies, practices and organisational strategies build strong partnerships with internal and external stakeholders to promote best practice, enhance capability and support the successful delivery of Indigenous workforce initiatives lead and develop a high-performing team while managing key projects, budgets, governance reporting and strategic initiatives aligned with the university's Indigenous workforce plan   About you   tertiary or postgraduate qualifications in a relevant field, or an equivalent combination of training and experience extensive experience delivering diversity, inclusion and Indigenous employment outcomes within complex organisations, with a strong understanding of strategic hr and industrial relations frameworks demonstrated expertise in Indigenous employment, cultural competence, and the attraction, retention and development of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples and other diverse workforce groups proven ability to build and influence relationships with a broad range of stakeholders, using strong communication, consultation and relationship management skills to deliver strategic outcomes strong leadership, strategic planning and project management capability, with experience managing multiple complex initiatives, budgets, contractors and competing priorities highly developed analytical, written and verbal communication skills, with the ability to use workforce data, research and insights to inform recommendations, drive cultural change and achieve measurable results   Why work with us We know our people are what make the University's mission possible. Alongside meaningful work, we offer a range of benefits designed to support you financially, personally and professionally. Key benefits include: Paid Cultural and Ceremonial Leave: Indigenous employees are entitled to 5 days of paid leave and 10 days of unpaid leave each year to participate in cultural and ceremonial activities and fulfil community obligations Career Development : Access to Indigenous professional and career development programs, including mentoring, leadership programs, and development funding Community and Connection: Be part of a vibrant community with opportunities to connect through staff networks (our YURA Network), mentoring, cultural events and professional Development opportunities Flexible Work and Exceptional Benefits: Enjoy flexible work arrangements, 17% employer superannuation, Fitness Passport, generous leave provisions, staff discounts and access to a wide range of University facilities and services.   Sponsorship / work rights for Australia Please note: You must have unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this employment to apply. Visa sponsorship is not available for this appointment. Pre-employment checks and declarations   Your employment is conditional upon the successful completion of all pre-employment or background checks required for the role in terms satisfactory to the University.  Also, to meet the University’s obligations under the National Higher Education Code to Prevent and Eliminate Gender-Based Violence you will be asked to declare if you have been investigated for, or found to engaged in, sexual harm or gender-based violence in the course of previous employment or in a legal process. Similarly, your ongoing employment is conditional upon the satisfactory maintenance of all relevant clearances and background check requirements. If you do not meet these conditions, the University may take any necessary step, including the termination of your employment. EEO statement   At the University of Sydney, our shared values are trust, accountability and excellence and we strive to be a place where everyone can thrive. We are committed to creating a University community that thrives through diversity and reflects the wider community that we serve. We deliver on this through our commitment to diversity and inclusion , evidenced by our people and culture programs, as well as key strategies to increase participation and support the careers of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander People , women , people living with a disability , people from culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds , and those who identify as LGBTIQ+ . We are proud to be recognised as an Australian Workplace Equality Index (AWEI) Gold employer. Find out more about our work on diversity and inclusion .  How to apply Applications (including a cover letter, CV, and any additional supporting documentation) can be submitted via the Apply button at the top of the page.  This is an identified opportunity for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander people. Applicants will be asked to provide confirmation of Aboriginal and/or Torres Strait Islander identity as part of the application process. We understand that everyone applies differently. If you’d prefer to have an informal conversation before applying, we welcome you to get in touch so we can talk through the role and process. In addition, if you require reasonable adjustment or any documents in alternate formats, please contact Victoria Ford-Rogers by email to  victoria.ford-rogers@sydney.edu.au © The University of Sydney The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Click to view the Position Description for this role. Applications Close Sunday 18 October 2026 11:59 PM"
    },
    "https://usyd.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/USYD_EXTERNAL_CAREER_SITE/job/Darlington-Campus/Associate-Director--Faculty-Partnerships_0157595-2": {
      "title": "Associate Director, Faculty Partnerships",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-30\nFull time\n\nFull-time, continuing position as an Associate Director, Faculty Partnerships Key leadership position supporting Sydney Future Students   SPS A – Competitive remuneration package plus 17% superannuation   About the opportunity     The Associate Director, Faculty Partnerships , is a key operational leadership role responsible for supporting Sydney Future Students in developing and implementing faculty- and school-level recruitment strategies and services for the University of Sydney. This includes establishing, implementing, and reviewing evidence-based approaches to resource planning, priority setting, and decision-making across the Faculty Partnerships function, and using data, market intelligence, insights and other relevant information to develop recruitment plans to support university and faculty level priorities in collaboration with the Sydney Future Students leadership team.      The role of Associate Director, Faculty Partnerships has been designed to lead the team in providing a highly professional, specialised, and effective student recruitment service through activities and strategic partnerships. This is an excellent opportunity to bring your extensive higher education, operational expertise, service model, people leadership and exceptional stakeholder management skills to the forefront of your role. This role is one where your initiative, creativity, and leadership experience will help to deliver excellence to our stakeholders and support the Sydney Future Students portfolio in delivering best-in-market student recruitment expertise and product insights.     The Associate Director, Faculty Partnerships will work under the broad direction of the Director, Domestic Recruitment and Access, and will work collaboratively with stakeholders to continuously improve the Faculty Partnerships offering on behalf of Sydney Future Students. The Associate Director will apply significant knowledge and experience to focus on continuous improvement approaches to support the delivery of highly professional student recruitment outcomes in line with the broader University and faculty/schools’ strategic goals. This role is pivotal in shaping the team's service offering and representing the team at senior levels across the institution.        Your key responsibilities will be to:       lead the team’s key strategic planning activities, including the development of strategic, operational, and business plans aligned with university-wide targets and faculty priorities  lead and provide high-level analysis and information in support of the achievement of SFS’s strategic goals  work with the leadership team to develop and deliver high-quality reports on market and student demand trends, intake scenarios, profiles, course and field of education, and subject-specific rankings and changes, and competitors’ activities. ensure the Faculty Partnerships team has a clear understanding of faculty and school-level objectives and a means to provide regular updates on student recruitment activities to the Associate Vice-President, SFS leadership, Deans, faculty and school staff and faculty/school Marketing and Communications teams    support university product development, review and market analysis projects, contributing to university-wide and faculty curriculum projects, engaging and coordinating input from other members of the Sydney Future Students leadership team manage the allocation of resources, ensuring alignment with the strategic direction of student recruitment, including developing and monitoring the allocated faculty/school-level budgets within Faculty Partnerships facilitate evidence-based decision-making by supporting the development of data, product, market, and student recruitment reporting literacy for the Faculty Partnerships team as required build and foster a culture of innovation, creativity, excellence, collaboration and accountability.         About you      substantial understanding of university faculty/school-based needs in managing their student recruitment objectives   high-level planning and organisational skills, and the ability to coordinate multiple streams of stakeholders   highly developed leadership, communication, and interpersonal skills, including skills in large-scale, complex team and stakeholder management and internal relationship building, negotiation, influencing and motivation, and the ability to prioritise and effectively manage team workloads   superior relationship management skills and capacity to manage competing needs of diverse stakeholders, with various time and resource limitations previous experience in higher education leadership, and working knowledge of university, faculty, and school-level planning, reporting and governance.       Sponsorship / work rights for Australia   You must have unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this employment to be eligible to apply. Visa sponsorship is not available for this appointment.     Pre-employment checks and declarations     Your employment is conditional upon the successful completion of all pre-employment or background checks required for the role in terms satisfactory to the University.  Also, to meet the University’s obligations under the National Higher Education Code to Prevent and Eliminate Gender-Based Violence you will be asked to declare if you have been investigated for, or found to engaged in, sexual harm or gender-based violence in the course of previous employment or in a legal process. Similarly, your ongoing employment is conditional upon the satisfactory maintenance of all relevant clearances and background check requirements. If you do not meet these conditions, the University may take any necessary step, including the termination of your employment.   This position is designated as involving child-related work. To undertake or remain in this position, you are required to apply for and obtain a Working with Children Check clearance in accordance with the Child Protection (Working with Children) Act 2012.     EEO statement     At the University of Sydney, our shared values are trust, accountability and excellence and we strive to be a place where everyone can thrive. We are committed to creating a University community that thrives through diversity and reflects the wider community that we serve. We deliver on this through our commitment to diversity and inclusion , evidenced by our people and culture programs, as well as key strategies to increase participation and support the careers of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander People , women , people living with a disability , people from culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds , and those who identify as LGBTQIA+ . We welcome applications from candidates from all backgrounds.    We are proud to be recognised as an Australian Workplace Equality Index (AWEI) Platinum Employer. Find out more about our work on diversity and inclusion .      How to apply   Applications (including a cover letter, CV, and any additional supporting documentation) can be submitted via the Apply button at the top of the page.    For a confidential discussion about the role, or if you require reasonable adjustment or support filling out this application, please contact Kristy Fairweather, Recruitment Operations, by email to kristy.fairweather@sydney.edu.au   © The University of Sydney   The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Click to view the Position Description for this role. Applications Close Sunday 18 October 2026 11:59 PM"
    },
    "https://usyd.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/USYD_EXTERNAL_CAREER_SITE/job/Brownlow-Hill/Veterinary-Specialist-in-Equine-Surgery_0157518-1": {
      "title": "Veterinary Specialist in Equine Surgery",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-30\nFull time\n\nBe part of building the future of veterinary clinical education at the University of Sydney   Full time three year fixed term opportunity Located on Camden Campus   Opportunity to inspire and train the next generation of veterinarians, engage with the school ranked # 1 in Australia, within a world-class university Base Salary HEO 9 (38 hours), $165,080 - $174,177 p.a. + 17% superannuation + specialist loading when registered in NSW as a specialist to a total package of up to $234,737 p.a     About the opportunity   Your chance to have a marked impact on the future of the veterinary profession as you care for the lives of animals.   We are seeking to appoint a Veterinary Specialist in Equine Surgery to join a team of outstanding world-class clinical colleagues at the University Veterinary Teaching Hospital Camden.   We support and encourage excellence in all aspects of the role, from patient care, teaching and learning, and research. The school values highly the tenants of mutual support, respect, empathy, collegiality and integrity.   Join an organization that has over a hundred years of experience in clinical training and innovation. Be part of the transformational process supporting students as they transition to become veterinarians. A privileged role for someone who wants more than a job- come and do work with a purpose.   Your key responsibilities will be to   maintain reputation for academic excellence provide high-quality specialist equine surgical services for clients of the UVTHC during business hours develop and support an expanding client base and equine caseload for the hospital participate in the provision of education to DVM students and continuing veterinary education activities provided by the unit coordinate the daily activities of the equine unit and provide additional support as required contribute to the continuous improvement of clinical standards and practice provide support to the Camden Equine Hospital after-hours service on a rotating basis actively participate in meetings, planning and development activities within the UVTHC, as required by the Hospital Director and Hospital Manager.   To learn more about the Sydney School of Veterinary Science click here   About you   The University values courage and creativity; openness and engagement; inclusion and diversity; and respect and integrity. As such, we see the importance of recruiting talent aligned to these values and are looking for a Veterinary Specialist in Equine Surgery :   Veterinary Specialist in Equine Surgery (HEO level 9) who is a veterinarian with at least partial responsibility for the management of a unit or division, including the management of a range of staff working within the relevant discipline or sub-discipline, who has:   ·       a degree in veterinary science registrable in New South Wales, Australia ·       a Diplomate or Fellowship permitting registration with the NSW Veterinary Scientists Board as a Specialist in Equine / Large Animal Surgery.   The commencement level will be determined based on the candidate’s skills, experience and knowledge. The 15% veterinary clinical specialist loading is available once registered as specialist by NSW Veterinary Practitioners Board.   The key benefits   opportunity to learn and enhance your skills and experience in the unique teaching and research environment of a Veterinary Teaching hospital salary packaging available 17% of your base salary, which significantly exceeds the standard Australian superannuation guarantee rate 15% veterinary clinical specialist loading is available once registered as specialist by NSW Veterinary Practitioners Board industry-leading leave provisions including generous paid parental leave up to 38 weeks entitlements (pending eligibility) compressed work week available pending eligiblity generous relocation allowance when moving interstate or internationally access to fitness passport, as well as discounted rates on university sports and fitness centres staff discounts on selected bank products and health insurance gain the recognition of working in a leading Veterinary School in an outstanding University.   Sponsorship / work rights for Australia   Visa sponsorship is available   Pre-employment checks   Your employment is conditional upon the completion of all the role required pre-employment or background checks in terms satisfactory to the University. Similarly, your ongoing employment is conditional upon the satisfactory maintenance of all relevant clearances and background check requirements. If you do not meet these conditions, the University may take any necessary step, including the termination of your employment.   EEO statement   At the University of Sydney, our shared values are trust, accountability and excellence and we strive to be a place where everyone can thrive. We are committed to creating a University community that thrives through diversity and reflects the wider community that we serve. We deliver on this through our commitment to diversity and inclusion , evidenced by our people and culture programs, as well as key strategies to increase participation and support the careers of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander People , women , people living with a disability , people from culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds , and those who identify as LGBTIQ+ . We welcome applications from candidates from all backgrounds.    We are proud to be recognised as an Australian Workplace Equality Index (AWEI) Gold employer. Find out more about our work on diversity and inclusion .    How to apply   Applications (including a cover letter, CV, and any additional supporting documentation) can be submitted via the Apply button at the top of the page.   If you are a current employee of the University or a contingent worker with access to Workday, please login into your Workday account and navigate to the Career icon on your Dashboard.  Click on USYD Find Jobs and apply.   For a confidential discussion about the role, or if you require reasonable adjustment or any documents in alternate formats, please contact Chris Masaoka, Recruitment Consultant. Recruitment Operations, Human Resources by email recruitment.sea@sydney.edu.au   © The University of Sydney   The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Click to view the Position Description for this role. Applications Close Sunday 01 November 2026 11:59 PM"
    },
    "https://usyd.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/USYD_EXTERNAL_CAREER_SITE/job/Darlington-Campus/Multiple-Continuing--Tenure-Track--Academic-Positions---Faculty-of-Engineering--The-University-of-Sydney_0157992-1": {
      "title": "Multiple Continuing (Tenure-Track) Academic Positions,  Faculty of Engineering, The University of Sydney",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-30\nFull time\n\nJoin a thriving Faculty of Engineering at a University ranked amongst the world’s best teaching and research institutions and help shape the future of artificial intelligence and its impact across every field.  Located in the heart of Sydney’s bustling inner west, close to beaches, parks, public transport and shopping districts.  We are seeking outstanding academics at Level B to D who want to be part of how AI is reshaping the world, whether by leveraging AI to advance their field, seeking to understand how AI works, or building the technologies that make it possible.  About the opportunity The Faculty of Engineering at The University of Sydney (Sydney, Australia) invites applications for several academic positions at Lecturer, Senior Lecturer, and Associate Professor (Level B-D) across our Engineering schools. We are seeking academics whose research engages with AI including, but not limited to: LEVERAGE AI: Use AI to advance research, discovery, engineering practice, design, computation, education and other fields — applying AI to new problems and unlocking new possibilities. UNDERSTAND AI: Investigate how AI works, from its mathematical, computational and engineering foundations to its capabilities, limitations, behaviour, impacts and implications. BUILD AI: Develop the next generation of AI methods, models, systems, tools and technologies, and the engineering and computational foundations that enable them. We welcome researchers whose connection to AI is established today, as well as those who can articulate how AI will shape their current or future research.  Our Engineering Schools School of Aerospace, Mechanical and Mechatronic Engineering   School of Biomedical Engineering    School of Chemical and Biomolecular Engineering     School of Civil Engineering     School of Computer Science   School of Electrical and Computer Engineering   School of Project Management   All positions are full-time, continuing (tenure-track), teaching and research roles based in Sydney.  Successful applicants will join a group of enthusiastic, highly research-active and collegiate academics who deliver a world-class education to our undergraduate and postgraduate students. Successful applicants will have an excellent research track record, a commitment to high-quality teaching with the ability to teach in a range of courses, and a vision for how AI can advance research, education and practice across their field. The role offers strong opportunities for collaboration with world-class researchers and University key partners, including the Sydney Biomedical Accelerator , as well as opportunities to develop new interdisciplinary collaborations across the University. About you The University values trust, accountability and excellence . As such, we see the importance of recruiting talent aligned to these values. You will develop an independent research program and engage in collaborations within and outside the University, including with industry partners. You will contribute to curriculum development and delivery at both undergraduate and postgraduate levels and contribute to leadership of the School/Faculty. You will be able to demonstrate:  a PhD in a relevant field. Applicants who have submitted their PhD for examination or will do so in the immediate future can be considered a strong track record in research appropriate to the discipline and level of appointment (relative to opportunity and taking career disruptions into account)  ability to teach, engage, and support the learning of students using innovative and modern pedagogies, including the organisation and delivery of a unit of study, with an eye to how AI is reshaping teaching and learning in your field outstanding communication skills and collegiality  high-level problem-solving skills with the ability to address complex issues as they arise. The Faculty of Engineering is committed to increasing the gender equity and diversity of its academic staff and strongly encourages women and candidates from underrepresented groups in Engineering to apply. How to apply Candidates should submit a CV, a research statement (up to 2 pages), and a teaching statement (up to 2 pages). We recognise that individual circumstances can impact career opportunity and trajectory and encourage the inclusion of a statement outlining any career disruptions or relative to opportunity information in the candidate CV. Please include the names and email addresses of three referees. Current employees of the University or a contingent worker with access to Workday, please login into your Workday account and navigate to the Career icon on your Dashboard. Click on USYD Find Jobs and apply. Please note while all applications are welcome, individual Schools may prioritise candidates aligned with specific focus areas and, with the candidate’s permission, redirect applications to other Schools where there is stronger alignment. Term and Remuneration The level of appointment will be commensurate with qualifications and experience. Successful applicants should expect an internationally competitive salary, a generous start-up package and first-class research, learning and teaching environment. Pre-employment checks Your employment is conditional upon the successful completion of all pre-employment or background checks required for the role in terms satisfactory to the University.  Also, to meet the University’s obligations under the National Higher Education Code to Prevent and Eliminate Gender-Based Violence you will be asked to declare if you have been investigated for, or found to engaged in, sexual harm or gender-based violence in the course of previous employment or in a legal process. Similarly, your ongoing employment is conditional upon the satisfactory maintenance of all relevant clearances and background check requirements. If you do not meet these conditions, the University may take any necessary step, including the termination of your employment. About us The University of Sydney is Australia’s first university with an outstanding global reputation for academic and research excellence. Since our inception 175 years ago, The University of Sydney has led to improve the world around us. We believe in education for all, and that effective leadership in education and research makes lives better. These same values are reflected in our approach to diversity and inclusion and underpin our long-term strategy for growth. Across our campuses, we employ over 7600 academic and non-academic staff who support over 70,000 students.  We are undergoing significant transformative change which brings opportunity for innovation, progressive thinking, breaking with convention, challenging the status quo, and improving the world around us. As part of its Strategic Plan 2025–2028 the Faculty of Engineering is committed to driving world-leading research with global impact, cultivating future-ready engineers, delivering innovative, student-centred education, and fostering a diverse, supportive, and collaborative community.  The University of Sydney is recognised as one of the top research and teaching universities in the world. We are a globally ranked university , ranked 28th in QS University World Rankings 2027, 3rd for sustainability in Australia.  EEO statement At The University of Sydney, we are committed to creating a University community that thrives through diversity and reflects the wider community that we serve. We deliver on this through our commitment to diversity and inclusion , evidenced by our people and culture programs, as well as key strategies to increase participation and support the careers of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander People , women , people living with a disability , people from culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds , and those who identify as LGBTIQ+ . We welcome applications from candidates from all backgrounds, including women. We are proud to be recognised with a Silver Athena Swan Award and as an Australian Workplace Equality Index (AWEI) Platinum employer. Find out more about our work on diversity and inclusion .  Anticipated timelines Please refer to the Please refer to the Engineering Recruitment Guidelines for key information about this recruitment campaign. Contact Information For a confidential discussion about the role, or if you require reasonable adjustment or any documents in alternate formats, please contact Helen Efstathiou, Recruitment Consultant. Recruitment Operations, Human Resources on +61 2 8627 7137 or by email hr.engineeringcampaign@sydney.edu.au © The University of Sydney The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Position Descriptions:   Click to view the  Position Description for Lecturer – Level B role. Click to view the  Position Description for Senior Lecturer - Level C role. Click to view the  Position Description for Associate Professor – Level D role. Applications Close Monday 30 November 2026 11:59 PM"
    },
    "https://usyd.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/USYD_EXTERNAL_CAREER_SITE/job/Camperdown-Campus/Lecturer-in-Applied-Mathematics_0157732-1": {
      "title": "Lecturer in Applied Mathematics",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-30\nFull time\n\nFull-time 4 years fixed term position, l ocated on the Camperdown Campus at the School of Mathematics and Statistics Opportunity to make significant contributions to teaching and learning practice, design and evaluation while continuing to build research expertise Base Salary, Academic Level B $132,533 – $157,381 + 17% superannuation   About the opportunity The School of Mathematics and Statistics is currently seeking a Lecturer in Applied Mathematics who will have the opportunity to make a significant contribution to teaching and learning design, delivery, and evaluation in pure mathematics while continuing to build research contribution, expertise, connections, and impact. This role is a replacement for a staff member who is on a fellowship. Your key responsibilities will be to: work closely with other School members in applied mathematics, or related areas conduct high-quality research in applied mathematics, or related areas teach graduate and undergraduate courses in mathematics for students from different disciplinary backgrounds supervise and coordinate graduate and undergraduate students apply for external funding. About you a PhD (or near completion) in mathematics, applied mathematics, or a related area evidence of independent research, compatible with the standards at The University of Sydney evidence of ability, versatility, and commitment to excellence in teaching. Pre-employment checks and declarations     Your employment is conditional upon the successful completion of all pre-employment or background checks required for the role in terms satisfactory to the University.  Also, to meet the University’s obligations under the National Higher Education Code to Prevent and Eliminate Gender-Based Violence you will be asked to declare if you have been investigated for, or found to engaged in, sexual harm or gender-based violence in the course of previous employment or in a legal process. Similarly, your ongoing employment is conditional upon the satisfactory maintenance of all relevant clearances and background check requirements. If you do not meet these conditions, the University may take any necessary step, including the termination of your employment.   EEO statement     At the University of Sydney, our shared values are trust, accountability and excellence and we strive to be a place where everyone can thrive. We are committed to creating a University community that thrives through diversity and reflects the wider community that we serve. We deliver on this through our commitment to diversity and inclusion , evidenced by our people and culture programs, as well as key strategies to increase participation and support the careers of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander People , women , people living with a disability , people from culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds , and those who identify as LGBTQIA+ . We welcome applications from candidates from all backgrounds.    We are proud to be recognised as an Australian Workplace Equality Index (AWEI) Platinum Employer. Find out more about our work on diversity and inclusion .    How to apply   Applications (including a cover letter, CV, and any additional supporting documentation) can be submitted via the Apply button at the top of the page.     For employees of the University or contingent workers, please login into your Workday account and navigate to the Career icon on your Dashboard.  Click on USYD Find Jobs and apply.   For a confidential discussion about the role, or if you require reasonable adjustment or any documents in alternate formats, please contact Simon Drew, Recruitment Operations by email to recruitment.sea@sydney.edu.au   © The University of Sydney   The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Click to view the Position Description for this role. Applications Close Sunday 01 November 2026 11:59 PM"
