    "https://usyd.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/USYD_EXTERNAL_CAREER_SITE/job/Camperdown-Campus/Senior-Lecturer-in-TESOL_0157493": {
      "title": "Senior Lecturer in TESOL",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-02\nFull time\n\nFull-time, continuing role based at the Camperdown Campus in the Sydney School of Education and Social Work Opportunity to lead and innovate high quality TESOL education at a leading global university Base Salary Level C $162,347 p.a. - $187,198 p.a. + 17% superannuation      About the opportunity   Join the Sydney School of Education and Social Work within the Faculty of Arts and Social Sciences, the Senior Lecturer in TESOL role plays a key part in delivering high quality Teaching English to Speakers of Other Languages (TESOL), focuses on the teaching and learning of languages and literacy in both English-speaking and non-English-speaking nations.   You'll dive into the learning and teaching of English, languages other than English and English as a second or additional language/dialect (EAL/D). You’ll understand the implications of pedagogy, curriculum design and assessment and evaluation in schools, community organisations and higher education institutions.   Reporting to the Head of School, your key responsibilities will be:   coordinate and teach units related to TESOL and EAL/D curriculum in initial teacher education programs across primary and secondary education support ongoing education and research programs in the area of Community Languages education build and maintain reputation of academic excellence make original and innovative contribution to research and education which expand knowledge or practice maintain a research profile which meets University expectations and standards lead the development in research which enhance the reputation of the School, such as leading participation in internationally-funded research projects or partner-engaged research projects contribute to outstanding teaching and learning outcomes actively contribute to research, education and administrative activities contribute to collaborative teaching in the School and work flexibly and collaboratively in a team environment actively participate in governance and academic community at School and Faculty levels.     About you   We are seeking an exceptional Senior Lecturer in TESOL who has:   a PhD in the area of EAL/D or languages education or a related field experience in teaching and learning EAL/D and/or languages in primary and/or secondary and tertiary contexts demonstrated success in designing, implementing and evaluating teaching and learning initiatives in the field of EAL/D and/or languages developing network of relationships with key academic, community, industry and professional institutions, partners and stakeholders experience in supervising or mentoring others and/or coordinating team activities and research a developing knowledge and understanding of the key strategic issues in the EAL/D and languages discipline and higher education sector a developing network of academic, community, industry and professional partners and stakeholders.     Sponsorship / work rights for Australia   You must have unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this employment to apply. Visa sponsorship is not available for this appointment.     Pre-employment checks and declarations    Your employment is conditional upon the successful completion of all pre-employment or background checks required for the role in terms satisfactory to the University.  Also, to meet the University’s obligations under the National Higher Education Code to Prevent and Eliminate Gender-Based Violence you will be asked to declare if you have been investigated for, or found to engaged in, sexual harm or gender-based violence in the course of previous employment or in a legal process. Similarly, your ongoing employment is conditional upon the satisfactory maintenance of all relevant clearances and background check requirements. If you do not meet these conditions, the University may take any necessary step, including the termination of your employment.   This position is designated as involving child-related work. To undertake or remain in this position, you are required to apply for and obtain a Working with Children Check clearance in accordance with the Child Protection (Working with Children) Act 2012.     EEO statement     At the University of Sydney, our shared values are trust, accountability and excellence and we strive to be a place where everyone can thrive. We are committed to creating a University community that thrives through diversity and reflects the wider community that we serve. We deliver on this through our commitment to diversity and inclusion , evidenced by our people and culture programs, as well as key strategies to increase participation and support the careers of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander People , women , people living with a disability , people from culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds , and those who identify as LGBTIQ+ . We welcome applications from candidates from all backgrounds.    We are proud to be recognised as an Australian Workplace Equality Index (AWEI) Gold employer. Find out more about our work on diversity and inclusion .      How to apply   Applications (including a cover letter, CV, and any additional supporting documentation) can be submitted via the Apply button at the top of the page. For employees of the University or contingent workers, please login into your Workday account and navigate to the Career icon on your Dashboard.  Click on USYD Find Jobs and apply.   For a confidential discussion about the role, or if you require reasonable adjustment or any documents in alternate formats, please contact Helen Efstathiou, Recruitment Consultant, Recruitment Operations on +61 2 8627 7137 or by email helen.efstathiou@sydney.edu.au   © The University of Sydney   The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Click to view the Position Description for this role. Applications Close Sunday 25 October 2026 11:59 PM"
    },
    "https://usyd.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/USYD_EXTERNAL_CAREER_SITE/job/Camperdown-Campus/Senior-Manager--Risk-and-Resilience_0156533-1": {
      "title": "Senior Manager, Risk and Resilience",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-02\nFull time\n\nFull time, fixed term opportunity until 28th Jan 2028   Provide strategic leadership in strengthening the University’s enterprise risk and resilience landscape    Offering a competitive salary package including 17% superannuation       About the opportunity     The University of Sydney is seeking an experienced and highly motivated Senior Manager, Risk and Resilience to join our Risk Management team.    This is an exciting opportunity to lead and shape the University's organisational resilience, crisis management and business continuity capability at one of Australia's leading universities.    Working closely with senior leaders across the University, you will play a key role in helping the organisation anticipate, prepare for, respond to and recover from disruption. You will lead initiatives that strengthen business continuity, support critical incident and crisis management arrangements, design and deliver resilience exercises, and embed practical resilience capability across faculties, schools and portfolios.    No two days are the same. One day you may be facilitating a crisis exercise with senior executives, the next supporting an incident response, reviewing business continuity plans, preparing advice for governance committees or helping stakeholders navigate a complex emerging issue before it escalates.    You will also play an important role in identifying emerging risks, recognising patterns and themes across incidents, business continuity activities and operational issues, and translating local insights into strategic advice for senior leaders. By connecting what is happening across the University, you will help ensure significant issues, resilience vulnerabilities and emerging threats are appropriately escalated, understood and considered within the University's enterprise risk framework and governance processes.    Working across a broad network of stakeholders, you will act as a trusted adviser on organisational resilience, preparedness, crisis management and emerging risk issues, helping leaders make informed decisions in complex and often time-sensitive situations.    As part of a highly regarded and collaborative Risk Management team, you will have the opportunity to influence enterprise-wide decision-making, contribute to meaningful organisational change and help strengthen the University's ability to navigate uncertainty, disruption and emerging challenges.      Your key responsibilities will be to:     provide strategic leadership for the University’s organisational resilience arrangements, including the Organisational Resilience Framework, Crisis Management Plan, business continuity approach, scenario exercises and post-incident review requirements  lead business continuity and preparedness uplift across the University, including Faculty, School and portfolio Business Continuity Plans (BCPs), Crown Jewel business continuity planning, testing, monitoring and continuous improvement  coordinate and support the University’s critical incident and crisis management arrangements, including Critical Incident Response Teams and Crisis Management Committee preparedness, activation support, escalation pathways, action and decision records, stand-down arrangements and post-incident review activities  design, facilitate and evaluate crisis simulations, scenario exercises, preparedness activities and post-incident reviews to strengthen organisational capability and lessons learned  support emerging risk and cross functional preparedness groups by providing risk and resilience advice on complex issues before they escalate into formal critical incidents or crises  identify systemic risks, resilience gaps and themes from incidents, exercises, business continuity activity, emerging risks, and risk registers, and translate these into practical recommendations for senior leaders and governance forums  lead the integration of resilience insights into the University’s Risk Management Framework, including risk appetite, risk profiles, control effectiveness, governance reporting and enterprise-level risk advice  deliver high-quality risk analysis, briefings and reports to support senior leadership and governance decision-making on enterprise risk, organisational resilience, preparedness and emerging issues  coordinate and guide the broader risk and resilience community, including risk owners, BCP coordinators and key stakeholders, to support consistent practice and continuous improvement.  act as a trusted adviser during incidents and periods of disruption, helping leaders make informed decisions in complex and time-sensitive situations.       About you     postgraduate qualifications In a relevant field or an equivalent combination of relevant training and experience  excellent at interpreting data with highly developed problem solving and analytical techniques and the ability to translate complex data into high quality, succinct reports  extensive experience in risk management frameworks, including best practices, methodologies, and tools  extensive experience in strategic planning, analysis, decision-making support and advisory services  demonstrated professional experience and expertise in the application of risk identification and assessment and compliance and assurance techniques in an organisation operating in highly complex industry and environment  demonstrated adaptability and resourcefulness when meeting complex and conflicting demands, solving non-routine problems and priorities  superior written and oral communication and interpersonal skills including high level report writing, presentation and facilitation skills; understanding of a range of communication modes, including their suitability for different target audiences.   an interest in helping organisations prepare for uncertainty, strengthen resilience and continuously improve how they respond to disruption.      Sponsorship / work rights for Australia     You must have unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this employment to be eligible to apply. Visa sponsorship is not available for this appointment.      Pre-employment checks and declarations       Your employment is conditional upon the successful completion of all pre-employment or background checks required for the role in terms satisfactory to the University.  Also, to meet the University’s obligations under the National Higher Education Code to Prevent and Eliminate Gender-Based Violence you will be asked to declare if you have been investigated for, or found to engaged in, sexual harm or gender-based violence in the course of previous employment or in a legal process. Similarly, your ongoing employment is conditional upon the satisfactory maintenance of all relevant clearances and background check requirements. If you do not meet these conditions, the University may take any necessary step, including the termination of your employment.      EEO statement       At the University of Sydney, our shared values are trust, accountability and excellence and we strive to be a place where everyone can thrive. We are committed to creating a University community that thrives through diversity and reflects the wider community that we serve. We deliver on this through our commitment to diversity and inclusion , evidenced by our people and culture programs, as well as key strategies to increase participation and support the careers of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander People , women , people living with a disability , people from culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds , and those who identify as LGBTQIA+ . We welcome applications from candidates from all backgrounds.      We are proud to be recognised as an Australian Workplace Equality Index (AWEI) Platinum Employer. Find out more about our work on diversity and inclusion .        How to apply     Applications (including a cover letter, CV, and any additional supporting documentation) can be submitted via the Apply button at the top of the page.      For employees of the University or contingent workers, please login into your Workday account and navigate to the Career icon on your Dashboard.  Click on USYD Find Jobs and apply.    For a confidential discussion about the role, or if you require reasonable adjustment or any documents in alternate formats, please contact Kevin Lim, Recruitment Operations by email to kevin.lim@sydney.edu.au .     © The University of Sydney    The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Click to view the Position Description for this role. Applications Close Sunday 18 October 2026 11:59 PM"
    },
    "https://usyd.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/USYD_EXTERNAL_CAREER_SITE/job/Camperdown-Campus/Deputy-Dean--Faculty-of-Arts-and-Social-Sciences_0156501-1": {
      "title": "Deputy Dean, Faculty of Arts and Social Sciences",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-02\nFull time\n\nLocated in Camperdown 5 year FIXED-TERM position with an attractive remuneration package About the opportunity The Faculty of Arts and Social Sciences (FASS) at the University of Sydney has commenced an international search for the key leadership role of Deputy Dean. The new Deputy Dean will be joining at a key time, as the University enters the second phase of its ten-year strategic cycle (Sydney in 2032) and the Faculty progresses its own strategic plan. The new Deputy Dean will work alongside the Dean, Professor Lisa Adkins, as FASS looks to consolidate its position as a world-leading centre for education and research across the humanities, arts, and social science disciplines. The Faculty of Arts and Social Sciences at Sydney is the oldest and one of the most comprehensive of its kind in Australia. The Faculty remains proud of its past while focused on the future. The Faculty’s distinctiveness lies in the depth and breadth of its disciplines, with its many research and teaching strengths earning it a deserved reputation for excellence nationally and internationally. The Faculty’s research is driven by the pursuit of multidisciplinary knowledge that benefits Australia and the wider world. In 2025 we welcomed two new flagship centres, the Centre for AI, Trust and Governance, which advances responsible AI development through multidisciplinary research, fosters public trust, and informs policy decisions in complex regulatory environments; and the Contemporary Inequalities Centre, which brings together multidisciplinary expertise to pursue collaborative, problem-driven research, aiming to respond to emerging social and economic challenges to build the evidence base needed to shape new policy responses. Our other flagship research centres include the Vere Gordon Childe Centre, and the Sydney Centre for Healthy Societies. Other research centres, institutes, and groups in the Faculty include the Sydney Institute for Community Languages Education, the CREATE Centre, and the Sydney Social Sciences and Humanities Advanced Research Centre (SSSHARC). For our students, and in collaboration with our partners, the Faculty offers internships and work placements, overseas exchange and interdisciplinary collaboration around complex, real-world problems. As we mature our connections with industry, community and government, the Faculty is seeking to establish anchoring partnerships operating across its research and education portfolios.   About you (skills, capabilities and experience) The Deputy Dean is a key member of the FASS leadership team, providing academic leadership, and contributing to strategy, operations, financial management, people management and internal and external stakeholder engagement, as well as deputising for the Dean (as required). A key responsibility of this role is strategically guiding and advising Disciplines and Schools to ensure effective collaboration and partnership across the University and to accelerate and enhance priority initiatives. The position also provides strategic input around the development of an equitable, diverse, and future-focused academic workforce. Priorities going forward are the implementation of key initiatives as part of the second phase of our Faculty of Arts and Social Sciences 2032 strategy ; the development of leadership capabilities in FASS; enhancing our external engagement activities and connections with industry, community and government, and ensuring the safety, health, and well-being of all staff and students. The appointee will hold PhD qualifications in a relevant FASS discipline with demonstrated research excellence and scholarship. They will bring a deep commitment to innovation, excellence in research and education, equity, diversity, inclusion, sustainability, and collaboration. They will have had significant experience leading academic units, including financial and operational management and strategic planning and delivery. They will have a mature understanding of workforce planning and management, research, education, and engagement in academic environments. They will also demonstrate a deep understanding of key strategic issues in the higher education and experience in the development and delivery of initiatives and projects therein.   Sponsorship / work rights for Australia Visa sponsorship and relocation assistance will be provided if required. Term and remuneration The term of the appointment is five years on a full-time basis. The successful candidate will also be offered a professorial appointment in an appropriate discipline in the Faculty and will retain that position at the conclusion of their Deputy Deanship. The successful candidate will be offered a competitive remuneration package commensurate with the responsibilities of the position and the candidate’s relevant experience and qualifications. Pre-employment checks Your employment is conditional upon the successful completion of all pre-employment or background checks required for the role in terms satisfactory to the University.  Also, to meet the University’s obligations under the National Higher Education Code to Prevent and Eliminate Gender-Based Violence you will be asked to declare if you have been investigated for, or found to engaged in, sexual harm or gender-based violence in the course of previous employment or in a legal process. Similarly, your ongoing employment is conditional upon the satisfactory maintenance of all relevant clearances and background check requirements. If you do not meet these conditions, the University may take any necessary step, including the termination of your employment. EEO statement   At the University of Sydney, our shared values are trust, accountability and excellence and we strive to be a place where everyone can thrive. We are committed to creating a University community that thrives through diversity and reflects the wider community that we serve. We deliver on this through our commitment to diversity and inclusion , evidenced by our people and culture programs, as well as key strategies to increase participation and support the careers of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander People , women , people living with a disability , people from culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds , and those who identify as LGBTQIA+ . We welcome applications from candidates from all backgrounds.  We are proud to be recognised as an Australian Workplace Equality Index (AWEI) Platinum Employer. Find out more about our work on diversity and inclusion .  How to apply Applications ( including a CV and cover letter outlining how your skills, capabilities and experience meet the requirements for the role ) can be submitted via the Apply button at the top of the page. Please refer to the candidate information brochure/position description for further details. Candidate Brochure Pack: Candidate Brochure Click to view the Position Description for this role. For employees of the University or contingent workers, please login into your Workday account and navigate to the Career icon on your Dashboard.  Click on USYD Find Jobs and apply. For a confidential discussion about the role, or if you require reasonable adjustment or any documents in alternate formats, please contact Jackie Besley from Devereaux Consulting at DeputyFASS@devereauxconsulting.com © The University of Sydney The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment.   Applications Close Sunday 01 November 2026 11:59 PM"
    },
    "https://usyd.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/USYD_EXTERNAL_CAREER_SITE/job/Darlington-Campus/School-Manager_0158263-1": {
      "title": "School Manager",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-12\nFull time\n\nFull time, fixed term / continuing opportunity, located on the Darlington Campus, within the Faculty of Engineering Provide strategic and operational support to a School within the Faculty of Engineering Base Salary $146,197 - $154,260 (HEO9) p.a. + 17% superannuation      About the opportunity   Reporting to the Faculty General Manager, the School Manager will be a key member of the school leadership team, contributing to the planning, communication, and implementation of the school’s academic and operational strategies, goals, and initiatives.   In this role, you will lead the delivery of infrastructure, operational plans, and processes that support the school’s activities across education, research, research training, and external engagement. You will be accountable for operationalising strategic and business planning decisions, managing financial performance, strengthening stakeholder relationships, ensuring regulatory compliance and effective controls, and maintaining a safe and healthy workplace.   As a people leader, you will guide, develop, and support a team of professional staff while actively contributing to decision‑making processes within the school leadership team.   Some of your key responsibilities will include:   acting as a strategic partner to the Head of School, providing insight and advice to support effective decision‑making leading the formulation, communication, and implementation of the school’s strategic priorities providing strong operational leadership and effective day‑to‑day management across all school activities driving continuous improvement initiatives to enhance the school’s systems, processes, and service delivery fostering a culture of high performance, engagement, and accountability within the professional staff team overseeing financial management, governance, and internal controls to ensure sustainable and compliant operations optimising the allocation and utilisation of resources to support the school’s academic and operational goals ensuring adherence to the University’s governance frameworks, policies, and regulatory requirements leading risk management and ensuring a safe, healthy, and compliant work environment for all staff and students.     To learn more about the Faculty of Engineering click here .     About you   The University values courage and creativity; openness and engagement; inclusion and diversity; and respect and integrity. As such, we see the importance of recruiting talent aligned to these values and are looking for a School Manager who has:   a degree qualification in an applicable field (for example business administration) and/or equivalent combination of relevant training and experience exceptional interpersonal skills with the ability to develop and deploy effective working relationships across a diverse range of people excellent communication, consultation skills, and negotiation skills to drive internal and external engagement demonstrated track record in managing medium scale budgets and financial plans experience in business, infrastructure, operational and resource management experience in supporting the delivery of teaching and research within a university context.     To apply for this position, please explicitly address the questions below in a cover letter (which should be attached to your application):   Describe your experience of managing the operational functions of a business unit Describe your experience in people management and development.   Sponsorship / work rights for Australia   You must have unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this employment to be eligible to apply. Visa sponsorship is not available for this appointment.   Pre-employment checks   Your employment is conditional upon the completion of all role required pre-employment or background checks in terms satisfactory to the University. Similarly, your ongoing employment is conditional upon the satisfactory maintenance of all relevant clearances and background check requirements. If you do not meet these conditions, the University may take any necessary step, including the termination of your employment.   EEO statement     At the University of Sydney, our shared values are trust, accountability and excellence and we strive to be a place where everyone can thrive. We are committed to creating a University community that thrives through diversity and reflects the wider community that we serve. We deliver on this through our commitment to diversity and inclusion , evidenced by our people and culture programs, as well as key strategies to increase participation and support the careers of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander People , women , people living with a disability , people from culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds , and those who identify as LGBTIQ+ . We welcome applications from candidates from all backgrounds.    We are proud to be recognised as an Australian Workplace Equality Index (AWEI) Gold employer. Find out more about our work on diversity and inclusion .    How to apply   Applications (including a cover letter, CV, and any additional supporting documentation) can be submitted via the Apply button at the top of the page.     For employees of the University or contingent workers, please login into your Workday account and navigate to the Career icon on your Dashboard. Click on USYD Find Jobs and apply.   For a confidential discussion about the role, or if you require reasonable adjustment or any documents in alternate formats, please contact Simon Drew, Recruitment Operations by email to recruitment.sea@sydney.edu.au   © The University of Sydney   The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Click to view the Position Description for this role. Applications Close Monday 26 October 2026 11:59 PM"
    },
    "https://usyd.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/USYD_EXTERNAL_CAREER_SITE/job/Camperdown-Campus/Commercialisation-Associate_0157340-2": {
      "title": "Commercialisation Associate",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-12\nFull time\n\nExciting opportunity to join the Commercialisation Office at the University of Sydney and help commercialise technologies in the Life Sciences, Medtech and Biotech sectors Full-time, continuing position Base Salary $117,133 - $127,610 + 17% Superannuation Flexible working arrangements   About the opportunity   The Commercialisation Office within the Office of the Deputy Vice-Chancellor (Research) has an exciting opportunity for a Commercialisation Associate to join the team.   In this role you will work with the Faculty of Medicine and Health, one of the largest in the country, other Faculties and Research Partners to identify Intellectual Property (IP) suitable for commercial development and translation, develop and execute different commercialisation pathways.   As a Commercialisation Associate , you will support the Commercialisation Managers to develop and facilitate engagement between researchers, industry and investors to optimise the translation of the University's research, technology and expertise and execute related agreements with external organisations.   Your key responsibilities will be to:   work within a tight-knit team, reporting to a Commercialisation Manager managing a portfolio of Medtech and Biotech opportunities prepare preliminary due diligence evaluations for new invention disclosures, using a supported and proven framework support commercialisation projects through market and competitor analysis, finding comparable deals, preparing technology marketing flyers, investor opportunity disclosures and identifying potential licensees  analyse technical and scientific literature and reports and research budgets ensure that project files and relevant databases are kept up to date in line with protocol and policies undertake appropriate administrative duties required of working within a university environment.   About you   If you are driven, organised, have a commercial mindset and are able to support a large and varied portfolio of commercialisation projects, you are who we are looking for.     tertiary qualification in medicine, health or life sciences, biotechnology commercial experience the medtech, pharmaceuticals or biotech industry, or in a university tech transfer environment is highly desirable ability to foster an entrepreneurial culture and build partnerships between the research, business and government communities understanding of the innovation or commercialisation lifecycle high level of oral and written communication skills and ability to manage competing demands experience in or ability to review, develop and negotiate legal contracts understanding of intellectual property protection and management able to assess industrial applicability of advanced technology, associated business development and marketing of academic research   Sponsorship / work rights for Australia   You must have unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this employment to be eligible to apply. Visa sponsorship is not available for this appointment.     Pre-employment checks and declarations     Your employment is conditional upon the successful completion of all pre-employment or background checks required for the role in terms satisfactory to the University.  Also, to meet the University’s obligations under the National Higher Education Code to Prevent and Eliminate Gender-Based Violence you will be asked to declare if you have been investigated for, or found to engaged in, sexual harm or gender-based violence in the course of previous employment or in a legal process. Similarly, your ongoing employment is conditional upon the satisfactory maintenance of all relevant clearances and background check requirements. If you do not meet these conditions, the University may take any necessary step, including the termination of your employment.   EEO statement     At the University of Sydney, our shared values are trust, accountability and excellence and we strive to be a place where everyone can thrive. We are committed to creating a University community that thrives through diversity and reflects the wider community that we serve. We deliver on this through our commitment to diversity and inclusion , evidenced by our people and culture programs, as well as key strategies to increase participation and support the careers of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander People , women , people living with a disability , people from culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds , and those who identify as LGBTQIA+ . We welcome applications from candidates from all backgrounds.    We are proud to be recognised as an Australian Workplace Equality Index (AWEI) Platinum Employer. Find out more about our work on diversity and inclusion .    How to apply   Applications (including a cover letter, CV, and any additional supporting documentation) can be submitted via the Apply button at the top of the page.     For employees of the University or contingent workers, please login into your Workday account and navigate to the Career icon on your Dashboard.  Click on USYD Find Jobs and apply.   For a confidential discussion about the role, or if you require reasonable adjustment or any documents in alternate formats, please contact Kale Claffey Recruitment Operations by email to kale.claffey@sydney.edu.au   © The University of Sydney   The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Click to view the Position Description for this role. Applications Close Monday 19 October 2026 11:59 PM"
    },
    "https://usyd.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/USYD_EXTERNAL_CAREER_SITE/job/Camperdown-Campus/Learning-Experience-Designer-Graduate--Targeted-_0155880-2": {
      "title": "Learning Experience Designer Graduate (Targeted)",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-12\nFull time\n\nFull time, fixed term role until end of June 2027 Be valued for your technical design knowledge within a collaborative team environment Base Salary $93,345 + 17% superannuation  Indigenous Targeted Role  This is a Targeted Aboriginal and/or Torres Strait Islander Position. While we welcome applications from all candidates, this is a targeted role for Aboriginal and/or Torres Strait Islander people. As part of our commitment to diversity and inclusion, Aboriginal and/or Torres Strait Islander candidates will be assessed as a priority. This Exemption is claimed under Section 21 of the Anti-Discrimination Act 1977.  About the opportunity The Learning Designer Graduate takes a learner-centred approach and contributes to projects that design, build, test, implement and evaluate educational experience that enhance the student experience. The successful applicant will draw on their recently acquired user experience knowledge and skills to contribute to the ongoing innovation of learning experiences and services. This position assists other designers to design and deliver solutions that facilitate the sustainable and scalable delivery of skill development and student experience programs. The Learning Designer Graduate will be a recent graduate of an undergraduate or postgraduate qualification in educational design, e-learning, design computing or user experience design. About you demonstrated capability to work with others to reach common goals, sharing information, supporting and building positive and constructive relationships strong problem solving and adaptability skills displaying independent judgement, initiative, and creativity that can be applied successfully in a collaborative team-based work environment sound organisational and analytical skills along with excellent communication, interpersonal, presentation and influencing skills demonstrated ability to successfully manage a busy workload and achieve agreed outcomes, with the ability to initiate projects and opportunities that have the potential to improve outcomes and performance of the work area demonstrated ability to maintain skills, knowledge and experience to innovate practices, processes and/or procedures at work the ability to work independently and understand and respond to changing priorities and trends in the Library and University environments demonstrated coding skills, with HTML5, CSS3 and JavaScript experience preferred. proficient with Adobe Creative Suite (Illustrator, Premier, InDesign) to create graphics, edit videos and design wireframes Why work with us  We are committed to creating a culturally safe, supportive and inclusive workplace for Aboriginal and/or Torres Strait Islander staff.  Key benefits include:   generous cultural leave and flexible working arrangements  access to Indigenous professional and career development programs, including mentoring, leadership programs, and development funding  participation in an internal Indigenous staff network, providing cultural connection, peer support and professional development opportunities  YURA network (promoting sharing of experiences, and professional support for Aboriginal and/or Torres Strait Islander staff members)  tax-efficient salary packaging options for cars, laptops, and additional contributions to your superannuation fund  Sponsorship / work rights for Australia You must have unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this employment to be eligible to apply. Visa sponsorship is not available for this appointment. Pre-employment checks and declarations  Your employment is conditional upon the successful completion of all pre-employment or background checks required for the role in terms satisfactory to the University.  Also, to meet the University’s obligations under the National Higher Education Code to Prevent and Eliminate Gender-Based Violence you will be asked to declare if you have been investigated for, or found to engaged in, sexual harm or gender-based violence in the course of previous employment or in a legal process. Similarly, your ongoing employment is conditional upon the satisfactory maintenance of all relevant clearances and background check requirements. If you do not meet these conditions, the University may take any necessary step, including the termination of your employment. EEO statement   At the University of Sydney, our shared values are trust, accountability and excellence and we strive to be a place where everyone can thrive. We are committed to creating a University community that thrives through diversity and reflects the wider community that we serve. We deliver on this through our commitment to diversity and inclusion , evidenced by our people and culture programs, as well as key strategies to increase participation and support the careers of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander People , women , people living with a disability , people from culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds , and those who identify as LGBTQIA+ . We welcome applications from candidates from all backgrounds.  We are proud to be recognised as an Australian Workplace Equality Index (AWEI) Platinum Employer. Find out more about our work on diversity and inclusion .  How to apply Applications (including a cover letter, CV, and any additional supporting documentation) can be submitted via the Apply button at the top of the page.   For employees of the University or contingent workers, please login into your Workday account and navigate to the Career icon on your Dashboard.  Click on USYD Find Jobs and apply. For a confidential discussion about the role, or if you require reasonable adjustment or any documents in alternate formats, please contact Riviera Son, Recruitment Operations by email to riviera.son@sydney.edu.au. © The University of Sydney The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Click to view the Position Description for this role. Applications Close Sunday 25 October 2026 11:59 PM"
    },
    "https://usyd.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/USYD_EXTERNAL_CAREER_SITE/job/Darlington-Campus/Research-Data-Storage-Specialist-_0158218-2": {
      "title": "Research Data Storage Specialist​",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-12\nFull time\n\nSupport world-class research through enterprise-scale data storage and infrastructure services  Enable researchers to tackle complex data-intensive workloads and scientific challenges Salary range between $152,045 to $160,430 + 17% Superannuation    Solve by Talent is partnering with the University of Sydney to recruit a Research Data Storage Specialist within Information and Communications Technology (ICT). This role will contribute to the delivery and ongoing operation of research systems that support cutting-edge research activity across one of Australia’s leading universities.    Why join the University of Sydney?     Join one of Australia’s leading universities at a pivotal time, where technology is evolving to better support student learning, teaching excellence, and research at global scale.    ICT is central to the University’s mission, delivering the platforms, services, and partnerships that enable student success, strengthen teaching, and accelerate research impact.    With strong investment and ambitious transformation underway, you’ll work in a large and complex environment where your contribution will have visible, meaningful impact across the University community.    The opportunity   This role sits within the Research Infrastructure team, responsible for delivering and evolving the research infrastructure and data storage that underpin world-class research across the University.    You will play a key role in managing and evolving the University’s research infrastructure environment, working across on-premises and cloud-based platforms to ensure researchers have secure, scalable, resilient and effective technology services.  Working closely with researchers, ICT teams, vendors and service providers, you’ll help shape how research data and infrastructure are managed, protected and modernised across the institution, enabling researchers to tackle complex problems and drive innovation.    What you’ll do     Manage, optimise and evolve research data storage platforms and associated research infrastructure services, including compute and supporting systems.  Work with researchers and stakeholders to understand current and future infrastructure, storage and computing requirements and deliver fit-for-purpose solutions.  Support infrastructure and data lifecycle management, including capacity, performance, retention, archiving, accessibility and governance.  Contribute to backup, recovery, business continuity and disaster recovery strategies that support resilient and reliable research services.  Collaborate with vendors, managed service providers, Cyber Security and ICT teams to deliver secure, reliable and well-integrated research infrastructure services. Contribute to the planning, modernisation and continuous improvement of research infrastructure, storage and computing technologies. Evaluate emerging infrastructure, storage, cloud and advanced computing technologies and identify opportunities to enhance research capability. What we’re looking for Experience working in research organisations or institutions, with an understanding of research projects, workflows and lifecycles.  Experience managing and optimising enterprise data storage systems and associated infrastructure platforms, preferably within an academic or research environment.  Strong technical experience across research data storage, infrastructure and computing services, including cloud and on-premises environments.  Understanding of infrastructure and data lifecycle management, including capacity, performance, retention, archiving, data management and governance.  Understanding of security, access management, backup, recovery and resilience requirements within enterprise technology environments.  Experience evaluating or implementing infrastructure, storage, cloud or advanced computing technologies.  Strong stakeholder engagement and communication skills, with the ability to work effectively with researchers, vendors, service providers and technical teams.    How to apply   If you're looking for an opportunity to contribute to meaningful work while building your career in a world-class university environment, we would love to hear from you.  Applications (including a cover letter, CV, and any additional supporting documentation) can be submitted via the  Apply  button at the top of the page.      For employees of the University or contingent workers, please login into your  Workday  account and navigate to the Career icon on your Dashboard.  Click on USYD Find Jobs and apply.   Accessibility and inclusion  The University of Sydney is committed to creating an inclusive recruitment experience for all candidates. If you require adjustments or additional support at any stage of the recruitment process, we encourage you to let us know.  This may include adjustments to the interview format, additional time, breaks, accessible interview locations, receiving materials in advance, or any other practical support that will help you participate comfortably.    For a confidential discussion about the role, or if you require reasonable adjustment or any documents in alternate formats, please contact Nick Barwick,  nick.barwick@sydney.edu.au .  ​  EEO statement   At the University of Sydney, our shared values are  trust, accountability and excellence  and we strive to be a place where everyone can thrive. We are committed to creating a University community that thrives through diversity and reflects the wider community that we serve. We deliver on this through our commitment to  diversity and inclusion , evidenced by our people and culture programs, as well as key strategies to increase participation and support the careers of  Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander People ,  women ,  people living with a disability ,  people from culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds , and those who identify as  LGBTIQ+ . We welcome applications from candidates from all backgrounds.      We are proud to be recognised as an Australian Workplace Equality Index (AWEI) Gold employer.  Find out more about our work on diversity and inclusion .      Pre-employment checks  Your employment is conditional upon the completion of all role required pre-employment or background checks in terms satisfactory to the University.  Also, to meet the University’s obligations under the National Higher Education Code to Prevent and Eliminate Gender-Based Violence you will be asked to declare if you have been investigated for, or found to engaged in, sexual harm or gender-based violence in the course of previous employment or in a legal process.    Sponsorship / work rights for Australia  You must have unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this employment to be eligible to apply. Visa sponsorship is not available for this appointment.  The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Click to view the Position Description for this role. Applications Close Sunday 01 November 2026 11:59 PM"
    },
    "https://usyd.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/USYD_EXTERNAL_CAREER_SITE/job/Camperdown-Campus/Technical-Officer--Workshops---Mechatronics-_0157403-1": {
      "title": "Technical Officer (Workshops – Mechatronics)",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-12\nFull time\n\nFull time, continuing position. Located at Susan Wakil Health Building (D18) on the Camperdown Campus Work with the team to design, assemble and troubleshoot specialised equipment and instrumentation for research and teaching Base Salary HEO6 $107,312 - $115,686 p.a. + 17% superannuation        About the opportunity     The Technical Support Services (TSS) Workshops team provides specialised design, fabrication and technical support services to teaching and research activities across the University. Working across multiple schools and faculties, the team partners with researchers and educators to develop custom equipment, prototypes and technical solutions that enable innovative teaching and research outcomes.   We are seeking a Technical Officer (Workshops – Mechatronics) to join this dynamic and collaborative team. In this hands-on role, you will work closely with clients to understand technical requirements, develop practical solutions and deliver bespoke equipment through a combination of design, prototyping, fabrication and systems integration. The Technical Officer (Workshops – Mechatronics) reports to the Project & Design Engineer, together they represent the wider TSS Workshops Camperdown Team in supporting clients in the Faculty of Medicine & Health. This is an exciting opportunity to contribute to a diverse range of projects, applying your engineering, prototyping and problem-solving skills to bring ideas from concept through to implementation.     Key responsibilities:   participate in consultation with researchers and teaching staff to identify requirements, evaluate design options and translate concepts into practical prototypes and technical solutions develop technical solutions through the integration of mechanical, electrical and electronic systems, including instrumentation, sensors, data acquisition and computer interfacing operate and maintain mechanical and electronic workshop equipment, including additive manufacturing, electronic benchtop testing instruments and general workshop tools support the Project & Design Engineer in selection and procurement of off-the shelf parts and components including motors, drivers and software contribute to the safe operation, maintenance and continuous improvement of workshop facilities, equipment and procedures work collaboratively with the wider TSS Workshops team to deliver high-quality technical services across the University About you:  qualification in mechatronics, electronics engineering, engineering technology, or an equivalent combination of relevant training and experience demonstrated experience designing, integrating, testing and troubleshooting mechanical and electronic systems demonstrated experience using benchtop test equipment including multimeters, oscilloscopes and power supplies experience using CAD software and rapid prototyping – using 3D printing and general workshop equipment working knowledge of sensors, instrumentation, data acquisition and control systems strong communication skills with the ability to build and maintain effective relationships with clients and colleagues demonstrated strong practical problem-solving skills, with the ability to develop innovative solutions from concept through to implementation. Work Rights You must have unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this employment to apply. Visa sponsorship is not available for this appointment. Pre-employment checks Your employment is conditional upon the completion of all role required pre-employment or background checks in terms satisfactory to the University. Similarly, your ongoing employment is conditional upon the satisfactory maintenance of all relevant clearances and background check requirements. If you do not meet these conditions, the University may take any necessary step, including the termination of your employment. EEO statement At the University of Sydney, our shared values are trust, accountability and excellence and we strive to be a place where everyone can thrive. We are committed to creating a University community that thrives through diversity and reflects the wider community that we serve. We deliver on this through our commitment to diversity and inclusion , evidenced by our people and culture programs, as well as key strategies to increase participation and support the careers of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander People , women , people living with a disability , people from culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds , and those who identify as LGBTQIA+ . We welcome applications from candidates from all backgrounds.    We are proud to be recognised as an Australian Workplace Equality Index (AWEI) Platinum Employer. Find out more about our work on diversity and inclusion .    How to apply Applications (including a cover letter, CV, and any additional supporting documentation) can be submitted via the Apply button at the top of the page.    For employees of the University or contingent workers, please login into your Workday account and navigate to the Career icon on your Dashboard.  Click on USYD Find Jobs and apply.   For a confidential discussion about the role, or if you require reasonable adjustment or any documents in alternate formats, please contact Kevin Lim, Recruitment Operations by email to kevin.lim@sydney.edu.au . © The University of Sydney The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Click to view the Position Description for this role. Applications Close Sunday 25 October 2026 11:59 PM"
    },
    "https://usyd.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/USYD_EXTERNAL_CAREER_SITE/job/Camperdown-Campus/Faculty-General-Manager_0158085-1": {
      "title": "Faculty General Manager",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-12\nFull time\n\nLead the strategic and operational management of a world-class faculty, driving innovation and excellence across education, research, and engagement Located in Camperdown Campus Full-time, continuing opportunity with an attractive remuneration package About the opportunity The Faculty of Arts and Social Sciences at the University of Sydney is one of the largest and most preeminent arts faculties in Australia with six schools spanning the arts, humanities, and social sciences. The University of Sydney is consistently ranked as one of the top arts universities in Australia and the world and was recently ranked 1st in Australia for Arts and Humanities in the Times Higher Education (THE) World University Rankings by Subject 2026. The Faculty General Manager is a key member of the Faculty leadership team, responsible for delivering strategic and operational business management to support the Faculty’s academic, research, and engagement objectives. This role acts as the primary conduit between the Faculty and central services, ensuring alignment of operational strategies with the University’s overarching goals. Working closely with the Dean, the Faculty General Manager will support the implementation of the Faculty’s new strategic plan, build trusted relationships across the University, and drive cultural change that fosters collaboration and excellence. The Faculty General Manager will lead professional staff with vision and influence, empowering teams while navigating complex HR, operational, and education and research settings. You will lead the planning and implementation of infrastructure, financial, and resource strategies, while fostering a culture of collaboration and excellence. The position offers the opportunity to influence faculty-wide initiatives and contribute to the University’s vision of research and education excellence. The role reports to the Dean of Arts and Social Sciences, Professor Lisa Adkins. Your responsibilities Contribute to the execution of the Faculty’s strategic plan Oversee operational management, including infrastructure, systems, and service delivery Manage faculty budgets and financial performance in collaboration with the Dean and Finance Director Lead and mentor professional staff, ensuring effective workforce planning and development Provide strategic input into external engagement, marketing, and alumni relations Ensure compliance with governance, risk management, and WHS standards Drive continuous improvement and operational reform initiatives across the Faculty. About you (skills, capabilities and experience) We are looking for a dynamic leader with: Tertiary qualifications in business administration or a related field, or equivalent experience Extensive experience in operational and resource management within a large, complex organisation Proven ability to manage large-scale budgets and deliver financial sustainability Demonstrated success in leading transformational projects and change initiatives Strong interpersonal, negotiation, and communication skills In-depth understanding of the higher education sector and its strategic challenges. Term and remuneration The successful candidate will be offered a continuing position on a full-time basis. The successful candidate will be offered a competitive remuneration package commensurate with the responsibilities of the position and the candidate’s relevant experience and qualifications. Pre-employment checks Your employment is conditional upon the successful completion of all pre-employment or background checks required for the role in terms satisfactory to the University.  Also, to meet the University’s obligations under the National Higher Education Code to Prevent and Eliminate Gender-Based Violence you will be asked to declare if you have been investigated for, or found to engaged in, sexual harm or gender-based violence in the course of previous employment or in a legal process. Similarly, your ongoing employment is conditional upon the satisfactory maintenance of all relevant clearances and background check requirements. If you do not meet these conditions, the University may take any necessary step, including the termination of your employment. EEO statement   At the University of Sydney, our shared values are trust, accountability and excellence and we strive to be a place where everyone can thrive. We are committed to creating a University community that thrives through diversity and reflects the wider community that we serve. We deliver on this through our commitment to diversity and inclusion , evidenced by our people and culture programs, as well as key strategies to increase participation and support the careers of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander People , women , people living with a disability , people from culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds , and those who identify as LGBTQIA+ . We welcome applications from candidates from all backgrounds.  We are proud to be recognised as an Australian Workplace Equality Index (AWEI) Platinum Employer. Find out more about our work on diversity and inclusion .  How to apply Applications ( including a CV and cover letter outlining how your skills, capabilities and experience meet the requirements for the role ) can be submitted via the Apply button at the top of the page. Please refer to the position description for further details. For employees of the University or contingent workers, please login into your Workday account and navigate to the Career icon on your Dashboard.  Click on USYD Find Jobs and apply. For a confidential discussion about the role, or if you require reasonable adjustment or any documents in alternate formats, please contact Sajad Bahram (Senior Talent Partner, Senior Talent Acquisition, Recruitment Operations) by email sajad.bahram@sydney.edu.au © The University of Sydney The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment.   Click to view the Position Description for this role. Applications Close Sunday 25 October 2026 11:59 PM"
    },
    "https://usyd.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/USYD_EXTERNAL_CAREER_SITE/job/Camperdown-Campus/Lecturer-in-Primary-English_0157871-1": {
      "title": "Lecturer in Primary English",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-09\nFull time\n\nFull-time, continuing role based at the Camperdown Campus in the Sydney School of Education and Social Work Opportunity to lead and innovate high-quality education at a leading global university·         Base Salary Level B $132,533 p.a. - $157,381 p.a. + 17% superannuation    About the opportunity  Join the Sydney School of Education and Social Work within the Faculty of Arts and Social Sciences, the Lecturer in Primary English role plays a key part in applying learning through research and scholarly activity to inform their education and research practice. They make significant contribution to teaching and learning practice, design and evaluation in initial teacher education programs while continuing to build their research contribution, expertise, and impact. Reporting to the Head of School , your key responsibilities will be:        develop and teach units in the area of literacy and English education in the primary education programs        build and maintain reputation of academic excellence make original and innovative contribution to research and education which expand knowledge or practice maintain a research profile which meets University expectations and standards development in research which enhance the reputation of the School, such as leading participation in internationally-funded research projects or partner-engaged research projects contribute to outstanding teaching and learning outcomes actively contribute to research, education and administrative activities contribute to collaborative teaching in the School and work flexibly and collaboratively in a team environment actively participate in governance and academic community at School and Faculty levels.     About you We are seeking an exceptional Lecturer in Primary English who has: a PhD in the area of literacy education, particularly primary English education or a related field experience in teaching and learning, as evidenced by participation in team teaching within an established program and experience in teaching in a variety of settings demonstrated success in designing, implementing and evaluating teaching and learning initiatives developing network of relationships with key academic, industry and professional institutions, partners and stakeholders experience in supervising or mentoring others and/or coordinating team activities a developing knowledge and understanding of the key strategic issues in the discipline and higher education sector a developing network of academic industry and professional partners and stakeholders.   Sponsorship / work rights for Australia You must have unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this employment to apply. Visa sponsorship is not available for this appointment. Pre-employment checks and declarations    Your employment is conditional upon the successful completion of all pre-employment or background checks required for the role in terms satisfactory to the University.  Also, to meet the University’s obligations under the National Higher Education Code to Prevent and Eliminate Gender-Based Violence you will be asked to declare if you have been investigated for, or found to engaged in, sexual harm or gender-based violence in the course of previous employment or in a legal process. Similarly, your ongoing employment is conditional upon the satisfactory maintenance of all relevant clearances and background check requirements. If you do not meet these conditions, the University may take any necessary step, including the termination of your employment.   This position is designated as involving child-related work. To undertake or remain in this position, you are required to apply for and obtain a Working with Children Check clearance in accordance with the Child Protection (Working with Children) Act 2012.     EEO statement     At the University of Sydney, our shared values are trust, accountability and excellence and we strive to be a place where everyone can thrive. We are committed to creating a University community that thrives through diversity and reflects the wider community that we serve. We deliver on this through our commitment to diversity and inclusion , evidenced by our people and culture programs, as well as key strategies to increase participation and support the careers of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander People , women , people living with a disability , people from culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds , and those who identify as LGBTIQ+ . We welcome applications from candidates from all backgrounds.    We are proud to be recognised as an Australian Workplace Equality Index (AWEI) Gold employer. Find out more about our work on diversity and inclusion .      How to apply   Applications (including a cover letter, CV, and any additional supporting documentation) can be submitted via the Apply button at the top of the page. For employees of the University or contingent workers, please login into your Workday account and navigate to the Career icon on your Dashboard.  Click on USYD Find Jobs and apply.   For a confidential discussion about the role, or if you require reasonable adjustment or any documents in alternate formats, please contact Helen Efstathiou, Recruitment Consultant, Recruitment Operations on +61 2 8627 7137 or by email helen.efstathiou@sydney.edu.au   © The University of Sydney   The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment.   Click to view the Position Description for this role. Applications Close Sunday 25 October 2026 11:59 PM"
    },
    "https://usyd.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/USYD_EXTERNAL_CAREER_SITE/job/Camperdown-Campus/Postdoctoral-Research-Associate---Immunology_0157845-1": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Research Associate - Immunology",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-09\nFull time\n\nFull time, fixed term until December 2027 Exciting opportunity to be a Postdoctoral Research Associate within the School of Medical Sciences Base Salary starting at $117,936 + 17% superannuation  About the opportunity The Postdoctoral Research Associate will contribute to the research agenda of the Faculty and School through high-quality research and scholarly activities within the discipline. The role will apply and develop research expertise while contributing to independent and collaborative research projects under the guidance of senior academic staff. The successful candidate will conduct research activities that contribute to discipline knowledge, maintain research excellence and support the advancement of the University’s research priorities. The role will also contribute to student mentoring, academic activities and collaboration with research partners and stakeholders to support impactful research outcomes. Your key responsibilities will be to: conduct research, scholarly or professional activities independently or as part of a research team, contributing to discipline knowledge develop and refine independent research skills under the supervision of more senior academic staff, with increasing autonomy maintain quality, standards and excellence across research activities and outputs provide informal mentoring and advice to students within the discipline where appropriate carry out administrative tasks and contribute to Faculty and School meetings interact with students, staff and members of the University community in a professional, collegial and respectful manner maintain an understanding of the University’s strategic agenda and contribute to furthering its priorities contribute to the strategic direction of the Faculty, School or Portfolio through research activities and engagement undertake other duties and contribute to projects appropriate within the classification level as required.   About you a doctorate or PhD in a relevant field demonstrated understanding of the subject matter within the discipline experience conducting original research and engaging in scholarly activity a developing research profile within the relevant discipline or area of expertise developing networks with academic, industry and professional partners and stakeholders strong research capability with experience contributing to research outputs excellent communication skills with the ability to collaborate effectively with researchers, students and stakeholders strong planning and organisational skills with the ability to manage competing priorities ability to deliver presentations effectively and communicate research findings to relevant audiences strong computer literacy skills and ability to utilise research-related technologies.   Sponsorship / work rights for Australia Work Rights: you must have unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this employment to apply. Visa sponsorship is not available for this appointment.   Pre-employment checks Your employment is conditional upon the successful completion of all pre-employment or background checks required for the role in terms satisfactory to the University. Also, to meet the University’s obligations under the National Higher Education Code to Prevent and Eliminate Gender-Based Violence you will be asked to declare if you have been investigated for, or found to engaged in, sexual harm or gender-based violence in the course of previous employment or in a legal process. Similarly, your ongoing employment is conditional upon the satisfactory maintenance of all relevant clearances and background check requirements. If you do not meet these conditions, the University may take any necessary step, including the termination of your employment. EEO statement   At the University of Sydney, our shared values include diversity and inclusion and we strive to be a place where everyone can thrive. We are committed to creating a University community which reflects the wider community that we serve. We deliver on this commitment through our people and culture programs, as well as key strategies to increase participation and support the careers of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander People , women , people living with a disability , people from culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds , and those who identify as LGBTIQ . We welcome applications from candidates from all backgrounds.  How to apply Applications (including a cover letter, CV, and any additional supporting documentation) can be submitted via the Apply button at the top of the page.   For employees of the University or contingent workers, please login into your Workday account and navigate to the Career icon on your Dashboard.  Click on USYD Find Jobs and apply. For a confidential discussion about the role, or if you require reasonable adjustment or any documents in alternate formats, please contact Ajaypal Singh, Recruitment Operations by email to ajaypal.singh@sydney.edu.au. © The University of Sydney The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment.   Click to view the Position Description for this role. Applications Close Thursday 22 October 2026 11:59 PM"
    },
    "https://usyd.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/USYD_EXTERNAL_CAREER_SITE/job/Narrabri/Postdoctoral-Research-Fellow-in-Soil-Security-for-Cotton-Farming-Systems_0156263-1": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Research Fellow in Soil Security for Cotton Farming Systems",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-09\nFull time\n\nFull time fixed term for 3 years with the possibility of extension. Located in Narrabri, NSW  Make a tangible impact on Australian agriculture Base Salary, Academic Level B $132,533 - $157,381 + 17% superannuation    About the opportunity   The University of Sydney and the Cotton Research and Development Corporation (CRDC) have established a strategic research partnership to strengthen the productivity, climate resilience and environmental performance of Australian cotton farming systems. The partnership brings together expertise in soil science, digital agriculture, farming-systems agronomy, biodiversity, natural capital and industry engagement to develop practical solutions that support measurable on-farm change.   We are currently seeking a Postdoctoral Research Fellow to contribute to the partnership's Capturing Value from Soil Security project. Based in Narrabri and working under the guidance of the Soils Partnership Project Lead and an interdisciplinary research team, the position will develop and apply a regionally relevant Soil Security assessment framework for cotton farming systems.   In this role you will:   Conduct research within Australian cotton farming systems, contributing to agreed project plans, milestones and CRDC reporting requirements. Contribute to regional baseline assessment using pedogenons, genosoils and phenosoils as the organising soil-landscape framework, including the development and management of GIS layers and mapping products. Identify and evaluate soil functions, ecosystem services and threats relevant to cotton systems, and develop scientifically robust, measurable indicators, thresholds, utility functions and scoring approaches across the dimensions of Soil Security. Design and coordinate defined components of a stratified, multi-site soil-sampling program, including field logistics, randomisation, metadata, quality assurance and quality control. Undertake regional fieldwork and coordinate the collection and laboratory characterisation of physical, chemical and biological soil properties using standardised and safe research protocols. Review evidence and collaborate with growers, consultants, industry representatives and researchers to develop practical management responses, monitoring approaches and incentive options. Work with CRDC, CottonInfo, grower groups, consultants, policymakers and other project partners through workshops, interviews, field days, conferences and other co-design or knowledge-exchange activities. Contribute to the supervision and mentoring of undergraduate and postgraduate research students, including students undertaking projects connected to the Soil Security program, where formally allocated and appropriate to the classification. Contribute to occasional undergraduate teaching, which may include practical classes, field-based teaching, tutorials, guest lectures, assessment and student support, where formally allocated and within the agreed workload.   About you   The University values courage and creativity; openness and engagement; inclusion and diversity; and respect and integrity. As such, we see the importance of recruiting talent aligned to these values and are looking for a Postdoctoral Research Fellow who has:   a PhD awarded by commencement in soil science, pedology, agricultural or environmental science, agronomy, pedometrics, geospatial science or a closely related discipline. demonstrated research experience in quantitative soil assessment, soil-landscape analysis, soil functions, soil condition or a closely related area. experience designing or implementing soil field-sampling programs and working with physical, chemical or biological soil laboratory data. strong skills in GIS, spatial statistics, digital soil mapping, geostatistics or related quantitative methods. proficiency in reproducible data analysis using R, Python or equivalent software. demonstrated ability to manage research data, apply QA/QC procedures and deliver work to agreed milestones. a research-publication record appropriate to career stage and opportunity. excellent written and verbal communication skills, including the ability to explain technical findings to growers, industry partners and other non-specialist audiences. demonstrated ability to work with increasing autonomy while collaborating effectively within multidisciplinary, geographically distributed and multi-partner research teams, including contributing to shared planning, reporting and delivery. the interpersonal skills and judgement required to engage respectfully with growers, consultants and industry stakeholders. demonstrated experience designing and analysing surveys or other human-participant research, including preparing or contributing to human research ethics applications, participant materials, consent processes and appropriate research-data governance. a demonstrated commitment to research integrity, ethics, data governance, inclusive practice and safe fieldwork. willingness and ability to be based in Narrabri and undertake regular regional fieldwork, with occasional overnight or interstate travel as the project requires.     Pre-employment checks and declarations     Your employment is conditional upon the successful completion of all pre-employment or background checks required for the role in terms satisfactory to the University.  Also, to meet the University’s obligations under the National Higher Education Code to Prevent and Eliminate Gender-Based Violence you will be asked to declare if you have been investigated for, or found to engaged in, sexual harm or gender-based violence in the course of previous employment or in a legal process. Similarly, your ongoing employment is conditional upon the satisfactory maintenance of all relevant clearances and background check requirements. If you do not meet these conditions, the University may take any necessary step, including the termination of your employment.   EEO statement     At the University of Sydney, our shared values are trust, accountability and excellence and we strive to be a place where everyone can thrive. We are committed to creating a University community that thrives through diversity and reflects the wider community that we serve. We deliver on this through our commitment to diversity and inclusion , evidenced by our people and culture programs, as well as key strategies to increase participation and support the careers of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander People , women , people living with a disability , people from culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds , and those who identify as LGBTQIA+ . We welcome applications from candidates from all backgrounds.    We are proud to be recognised as an Australian Workplace Equality Index (AWEI) Platinum Employer. Find out more about our work on diversity and inclusion .    How to apply   Applications (including a cover letter, CV, and any additional supporting documentation) can be submitted via the Apply button at the top of the page.     For employees of the University or contingent workers, please login into your Workday account and navigate to the Career icon on your Dashboard.  Click on USYD Find Jobs and apply.   For a confidential discussion about the role, or if you require reasonable adjustment or support filling out this application, please contact Rebecca Astar or Cherie Goodwin, Recruitment Consultants at recruitment.sea@sydney.edu.au   © The University of Sydney   The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Click to view the Position Description for this role. Applications Close Friday 23 October 2026 11:59 PM"
    },
    "https://usyd.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/USYD_EXTERNAL_CAREER_SITE/job/Darlington-Campus/Service-Delivery-Manager--Professional-Services-Review_0157345-1": {
      "title": "Service Delivery Manager, Service Excellence",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-09\nFull time\n\nFull-time, fixed term opportunity for an experienced Service Delivery Manager for the Service Excellence Program until September 2028 Career-defining opportunity for accomplished Managers to influence outcomes at an institutional scale and leave a lasting legacy Offering a competitive remuneration package including 17% superannuation    About the opportunity   The Service Delivery Manager will provide strategic leadership and operational oversight for a dedicated stream or streams within a major University wide transformation program.   This is a rare opportunity to step into a high-impact leadership role at the heart of a world-class University. Operating within a complex and evolving environment, you will shape the future by turning strategic objectives into tangible outcomes that benefit staff, students, and the wider community. You’ll inspire experienced colleagues, influence cross-functional practices, and embed service design and operating models that drive excellence across the institution. With responsibility for steering large-scale change initiatives, you’ll champion continuous improvement, manage risk, and ensure seamless operational transitions. Most importantly, you will foster a culture that embraces change as a catalyst for growth — making a lasting contribution to the University’s strategic direction and success.   Your key responsibilities will be to:   lead the delivery of defined functional program stream or streams, ensuring work is executed in alignment with scope, timelines, and quality standards champion service design principles to ensure transformation aligns with the institutional service blueprint and target operating model manage and coordinate teams of experienced professionals who operate with autonomy and discretion in their assignments shape and govern stream-specific delivery frameworks, tailoring governance structures to suit complexity and delivery needs partner with senior leaders within stream delivery area, to ensure alignment and delivery of program outcomes.   About you   tertiary qualifications in Business, Operations, Strategy, Psychology, Change Management, or a relevant field and/or an equivalent combination of training and experience  a minimum of 7–10 years of relevant experience, including 3–5 years in a management or leadership role demonstrated experience in managing operational delivery within a strategic transformation context experience in applying design thinking and service design methods to co-create user-centred, scalable service models extensive professional knowledge in the Higher Education sector familiarity with delivery methodologies (e.g., Agile, Waterfall) familiarity with service design and operating model methodologies (e.g., User Journey Mapping, Service Blueprinting, Personas) proven ability to lead cross-functional teams and influence executive stakeholders across diverse organisational areas strong judgement and problem-solving skills, with the ability to adapt and improve existing approaches.   Key benefits   flexible working options to support you to balance your work and family access to fitness passport, as well as discounted rates on university sports and fitness centers  staff study benefit on selected University of Sydney postgraduate coursework studies onsite healthcare general practitioner (GP) services to all staff  tax-efficient salary packaging options for cars, laptops, and additional contributions to your superannuation fund  access to staff discounts and offers for a variety of products, including University parking and flights gain the recognition of working in a leading University.   Sponsorship / work rights for Australia   You must have unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this employment to be eligible to apply. Visa sponsorship is not available for this appointment.     Pre-employment checks and declarations     Your employment is conditional upon the successful completion of all pre-employment or background checks required for the role in terms satisfactory to the University.  Also, to meet the University’s obligations under the National Higher Education Code to Prevent and Eliminate Gender-Based Violence you will be asked to declare if you have been investigated for, or found to engaged in, sexual harm or gender-based violence in the course of previous employment or in a legal process. Similarly, your ongoing employment is conditional upon the satisfactory maintenance of all relevant clearances and background check requirements. If you do not meet these conditions, the University may take any necessary step, including the termination of your employment.     EEO statement     At the University of Sydney, our shared values are trust, accountability and excellence and we strive to be a place where everyone can thrive. We are committed to creating a University community that thrives through diversity and reflects the wider community that we serve. We deliver on this through our commitment to diversity and inclusion , evidenced by our people and culture programs, as well as key strategies to increase participation and support the careers of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander People , women , people living with a disability , people from culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds , and those who identify as LGBTQIA+ . We welcome applications from candidates from all backgrounds.    We are proud to be recognised as an Australian Workplace Equality Index (AWEI) Platinum Employer. Find out more about our work on diversity and inclusion .    How to apply   Applications (including a cover letter, CV, and any additional supporting documentation) can be submitted via the Apply button at the top of the page.     For employees of the University or contingent workers, please login into your Workday account and navigate to the Career icon on your Dashboard.  Click on USYD Find Jobs and apply.   For a confidential discussion about the role, or if you require reasonable adjustment or any documents in alternate formats, please contact Victoria Ford-Rogers Recruitment Operations by email to victoria.ford-rogers@sydney.edu.au   © The University of Sydney   The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Click to view the Position Description for this role. Applications Close Thursday 15 October 2026 11:59 PM"
    },
    "https://usyd.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/USYD_EXTERNAL_CAREER_SITE/job/Darlington-Campus/Administrative-Assistant_0157773-1": {
      "title": "Administrative Assistant",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-08\nFull time\n\nAdministrative Assistant Salary $70,000 - $85,000 exclusive of super (determined based on experience) Join a supportive and inclusive team Full time, Sydney based The United States Studies Centre The United States Studies Centre (USSC) is one of Australia’s most influential and dynamic think tanks. Working with leading experts, policymakers and partners in Australia, the US and across the Indo-Pacific, the USSC delivers research and insights that contribute to informed discussion on the issues shaping our region.  Join a collaborative and purpose-driven team where your work will contribute to meaningful research, engagement and impact. About the Role This is an exciting new role where no two days are the same. You’ll be responsible for providing administrative support across the Centre, including general administration, travel bookings, expense claims, event coordination, and maintaining office supplies. This is a hybrid role that requires flexibility to work in the office and attend events as needed. Your key responsibilities include: Provide administrative support across Research, Academic, Communications, and Operations teams. Liaise with external stakeholders to facilitate reimbursements and contractual payments, including payments for peer reviews, honorariums and externally authored publications. Process expense and reimbursement claims in a timely and accurate manner. Coordinate business travel, including flights, accommodation, and related logistics. Schedule meetings, prepare meeting materials, and ensure meeting rooms are set up appropriately. Provide event coordination and administrative support, including event logistics and on-the-day assistance. Assist with office mail, ordering supplies, groceries, equipment, and other office services. Support research, academic, finance, HR, and operational processes as required. Assist with data entry, report preparation, and record management. Support management with administrative tasks and special projects as required About you Proven experience in an administrative or office support role Strong organisational skills with the ability to manage multiple tasks and priorities effectively Excellent written and verbal communication skills Proficiency in Microsoft Office applications Attention to detail and accuracy in data entry and record-keeping A professional and friendly approach with strong customer service skills Ability to resolve problems and escalate if required Ability to work independently and as part of a team Positive, can-do attitude Why Join Us?   In addition to the employee’s salary and superannuation, the Centre provides a range of benefits including: Family-friendly working hours and flexible working arrangements that let you balance your work and personal responsibilities Generous leave entitlements, including annual leave, sick leave, carers leave, long service leave, family violence leave and paid parental leave Breastfeeding rooms and a breastfeeding policy, with several childcare centres near the University Professional development allowance, plus a range of training and development opportunities provided by the University  Salary packaging for motor vehicles, laptops and superannuation Staff discounts on selected bank products and health insurance UniSuper superannuation advice, seminars and financial advisers Discounted rates to Uni sport and fitness centres Free annual flu vaccinations Free counselling through the Employee Assistance Program (EAP) service Discounted veterinary services for pets of staff Free access to the University library About our Culture   The Centre is committed to building a culture that fosters collaboration, career progression, diversity, inclusion, mental health and sustainability. We have a Social Responsibility Committee that organises regular events and training sessions and our Social Club meets monthly. The Centre won awards for Best Workplace and Culture Change.  Apply now If you would like to learn more about the role or require any accommodations during the recruitment process, please contact our HR Manager, Anh Hrincu, at anh.hrincu@sydney.edu.au. Click to view the Position Description for this role. Applications Close Thursday 05 November 2026 11:59 PM"
    },
    "https://usyd.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/USYD_EXTERNAL_CAREER_SITE/job/Camperdown-Campus/Postdoctoral-Research-Fellow_0157448-1": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Research Fellow",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-08\nPart time\n\nPart time (0.8 FTE), fixed term for 12 months with the possibility of extension for a dedicated Postdoctoral research Fellow Opportunity to contribute to nationally significant addiction medicine research programs Base Salary $132,533 p.a. + 17% superannuation (pro-rata for 0.8 FTE) About the opportunity   An exciting opportunity exists for a Postdoctoral Research Fellow to join a leading addiction medicine research program based at RPA Hospital. Working under the direction of Professor Kirsten Morley, you will contribute to the delivery of innovative research focused on mental health, substance use disorders, neuroimaging and psychopharmacotherapy. The successful candidate will play a key role in conducting high-quality clinical research, contributing to publications and grant-funded research activities, and supporting the development of junior researchers and higher degree research students.   Your key responsibilities will be to: conduct original research independently and as part of multidisciplinary research teams contribute to the design, implementation and delivery of addiction medicine and mental health research projects analyse and interpret neuroimaging data to support research outcomes contribute to psychopharmacotherapy experimental and clinical trial activities recruit and engage participants experiencing mental health and substance use disorders prepare publications, conference presentations, and other scholarly outputs contribute to research funding and grant applications build and maintain productive collaborations across health services, universities, and external stakeholders provide supervision, mentoring and guidance to junior research staff and HDR students contribute to a culture of research excellence and collaboration About you   To be successful in this opportunity, you will have: a PhD in a relevant discipline demonstrated expertise in psychophysiology, wearables and the analysis of HRV data in addiction, mental health, or related clinical research demonstrated expertise in neuroimaging and analysis of neuroimaging data in addiction, mental health, or related clinical research demonstrated experience working in psychopharmacotherapy experimental and clinical trials demonstrated experience working with and recruiting individuals with mental health and substance use disorders demonstrated experience conducting clinical research across both health-based and university-based systems a track record of publications in mental health and substance use disorders demonstrated experience supervising junior research staff and higher degree research (HDR) students To keep our community safe, please be aware of our COVID safety precautions which form our conditions of entry for all staff, students and visitors coming to campus. Sponsorship / work rights for Australia   You must have unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this employment to be eligible to apply. Visa sponsorship is not available for this appointment. Pre-employment checks and declarations     Your employment is conditional upon the successful completion of all pre-employment or background checks required for the role in terms satisfactory to the University.  Also, to meet the University’s obligations under the National Higher Education Code to Prevent and Eliminate Gender-Based Violence you will be asked to declare if you have been investigated for, or found to engaged in, sexual harm or gender-based violence in the course of previous employment or in a legal process. Similarly, your ongoing employment is conditional upon the satisfactory maintenance of all relevant clearances and background check requirements. If you do not meet these conditions, the University may take any necessary step, including the termination of your employment. EEO statement At the University of Sydney, our shared values are trust, accountability and excellence and we strive to be a place where everyone can thrive. We are committed to creating a university community that thrives through diversity and reflects the wider community that we serve. We deliver on this through our commitment to diversity and inclusion , evidenced by our people and culture programs, as well as key strategies to increase participation and support the careers of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander People , women , people living with a disability , people from culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds , and those who identify as LGBTQIA+ . We welcome applications from candidates from all backgrounds.    We are proud to be recognised as an Australian Workplace Equality Index (AWEI) Platinum Employer. Find out more about our work on diversity and inclusion .    How to apply Applications (including a cover letter, CV, and any additional supporting documentation) can be submitted via the Apply button at the top of the page. For employees of the University or contingent workers, please login into your Workday account and navigate to the Career icon on your Dashboard. Click on USYD Find Jobs and apply. For a confidential discussion about the role, or if you require reasonable adjustment or any documents in alternate formats, please contact Danielle Selinger Recruitment Consultant by email to danielle.selinger@sydney.edu.au © The University of Sydney The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Click to view the Position Description for this role. Applications Close Thursday 22 October 2026 11:59 PM"
    },
    "https://usyd.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/USYD_EXTERNAL_CAREER_SITE/job/Darlington-Campus/Faculty-of-Engineering-Casual-Academic-Expression-of-Interest_0158285": {
      "title": "Faculty of Engineering Casual Academic Expression of Interest",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-08\nPart time\n\nThe Faculty of Engineering is accepting expressions of interest for casual academic positions for teaching in Semester 1, 2027     Use this link to apply   https://sydney.au1.qualtrics.com/jfe/form/SV_6AwjEErjcL3qcXI   Applications must be submitted via this link, not through Workday     Submitting the form is not a formal job application. The Faculty will assess your suitability and may contact you for an interview. Successful applicants will move to HR onboarding. Please note that if you are not selected, you will not receive a notification; this is due to the volume of applications. However, your details may be kept on file for upcoming vacancies.     Contact:   eng-casual-academics.support@sydney.edu.au   Click to view the Position Description for this role. Applications Close Sunday 01 November 2026 11:59 PM"
    },
    "https://usyd.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/USYD_EXTERNAL_CAREER_SITE/job/Camperdown-Campus/Senior-Mechanical-Specialist_0157531-2": {
      "title": "Senior Mechanical Specialist",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-08\nFull time\n\nFull time, fixed term position until 30 June 2028, with the possibility of extension. Located on the Camperdown Campus at the School of Physics Base Salary $112,628 - $122,702 (HEO7) + 17% superannuation    About the opportunity The School of Physics is currently seeking an experienced Senior Mechanical Specialist to provide specialist technical skills in mechanical engineering for the development and delivery of complex, multidisciplinary astronomical instrumentation.  This is an exciting opportunity to work at Astralis-USyd, which is one of three nodes of Australia's Astronomical Instrumentation Consortium, Astralis. Astralis-USyd has a team of instrument scientists, engineers, project managers, astrophotonics specialists and astronomers who build astronomical instruments for the World's largest telescopes. The larger Astralis Consortium consists of over 120 people. The projects are collaborative across the three nodes of Astralis, with potential further career opportunities across the Astralis network.    In this role you will lead a small engineering team that will:  develop concepts, system and subsystem level mechanical designs and parts drawings for instrument projects perform structural, kinematic, vibration, and thermal analyses through FEA and other analytical techniques develop alignment, assembly, and test plans for the verification and acceptance of mechanical and opto-mechanical sub-systems develop technical specifications for procurements and source parts  develop, assemble, test and characterize mechanical parts and components of instruments  produce documentation for reviews, subsystem analysis and tests, and for instrument operation and maintenance manuals  take ownership of requirements management, interface control, verification and validation, and product assurance oversight—from final design through deployment and commissioning  work collaboratively with scientists and external research partners   About you The University values courage and creativity; openness and engagement; inclusion and diversity; and respect and integrity. As such, we see the importance of recruiting talent aligned to these values and are looking for a Senior Mechanical Specialist who has: a Bachelor’s degree or diploma in mechanical engineering or related discipline  demonstrated skills in CAD design and mechanical drawing production  proven capability in structural, thermal and kinematic analysis for precision mechanical engineering  proven track record in opto-mechanics or mechatronics for scientific or technical instrumentation  knowledge of and/or training in systems engineering practices for technical projects experience collaborating and working as part of a multi-discipline team, directing junior staff and engaging with external partners experience in delivery of astronomical instrumentation for large telescopes, from design to Manufacture, Integration, Assembly and Testing (MAIT), to commissioning will be highly regarded.   Work Rights: you must have unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this employment to apply. Visa sponsorship is not available for this appointment. Pre-employment checks Your employment is conditional upon the successful completion of all pre-employment or background checks required for the role in terms satisfactory to the University.  Also, to meet the University’s obligations under the National Higher Education Code to Prevent and Eliminate Gender-Based Violence you will be asked to declare if you have been investigated for, or found to engaged in, sexual harm or gender-based violence in the course of previous employment or in a legal process. Similarly, your ongoing employment is conditional upon the satisfactory maintenance of all relevant clearances and background check requirements. If you do not meet these conditions, the University may take any necessary step, including the termination of your employment. EEO statement   At the University of Sydney, our shared values are trust, accountability and excellence and we strive to be a place where everyone can thrive. We are committed to creating a University community that thrives through diversity and reflects the wider community that we serve. We deliver on this through our commitment to diversity and inclusion , evidenced by our people and culture programs, as well as key strategies to increase participation and support the careers of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander People , women , people living with a disability , people from culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds , and those who identify as LGBTIQ+ . We welcome applications from candidates from all backgrounds.  We are proud to be recognised as an Australian Workplace Equality Index (AWEI) Gold employer. Find out more about our work on diversity and inclusion .  How to apply Applications (including a cover letter addressing the listed skills above, CV, and any additional supporting documentation) can be submitted via the Apply button at the top of the page.   For employees of the University or contingent workers, please login into your Workday account and navigate to the Career icon on your Dashboard.  Click on USYD Find Jobs and apply. For a confidential discussion about the role, or if you require reasonable adjustment or support filling out this application, please contact Rebecca Astar or Cherie Goodwin, Recruitment Operations, Human Resources at recruitment.sea@sydney.edu.au © The University of Sydney The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Click to view the Position Description for this role. Applications Close Thursday 22 October 2026 11:59 PM"
    },
    "https://usyd.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/USYD_EXTERNAL_CAREER_SITE/job/Camperdown-Campus/Administrative-Officer---AUSPIN--Targeted-_0157248-1": {
      "title": "Administrative Officer - AUSPIN (Targeted)",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-08\nPart time\n\nPart time (0.4FTE), fixed term until October 2029    Exciting opportunity to be an Administration Officer with the School of Medical Sciences   Base Salary starting at $93,345 (pro rata) + 17% superannuation  About the opportunity The Administrative Officer provides high-quality administrative and operational support to ensure the effective delivery of services within the designated work area. The role is responsible for coordinating administrative processes, maintaining accurate records, supporting stakeholder communication and ensuring the efficient flow of information across the team. The successful candidate will provide professional administrative support through coordinating processes, managing information, supporting financial administration and developing productive relationships across the University. Project name: Australasian Pancreas and Islet Network (AUSPIN) This position is to support the workings of a new program to distribute human donor pancreas to researchers across Australasian working on diabetes. This administrative role involves liaison with clinicians and researchers, collating information and databases and organisation of meetings. The role will largely be conducted in normal office hours but the nature of human donations means that occasional out of hours work is necessary. The candidate will have good communication skills, be organised and be capable of working independently. This is a Targeted Aboriginal and/or Torres Strait Islander Position. While we welcome applications from all candidates, this is a targeted role for Aboriginal and/or Torres Strait Islander people. As part of our commitment to diversity and inclusion, Aboriginal and/or Torres Strait Islander candidates will be assessed as a priority. This Exemption is claimed under Section 21 of the Anti-Discrimination Act 1977.   It is required to be one day per week in office at the Charles Perkins Centre, the day is flexible but must be consistent each week. Your key responsibilities will be to: deliver general administrative services and operational support to the work area investigate administrative matters and prepare advice, recommendations and solutions manage incoming enquiries and correspondence, preparing responses and routine documentation provide administrative support including diary, travel and accommodation management gather, organise and summarise data from a range of sources to prepare reports, charts, graphs and tables coordinate administrative processes, services and the development of resources for the work area manage purchasing, payment and invoice processes to support effective operations develop and maintain productive working relationships with staff and stakeholders across the University undertake other duties relevant to the role’s key accountabilities as approved by the Manager and commensurate with the role’s classification level.   About you qualifications in a relevant field, including a Certificate IV, Diploma, Advanced Diploma, Bachelor’s degree or equivalent combination of relevant training and experience two to four years’ experience in administrative roles experience providing diary management support experience managing purchasing and invoicing processes strong computer literacy skills, including proficiency with Microsoft Office applications excellent organisational and time management skills with the ability to prioritise competing tasks and meet deadlines strong communication skills with the ability to provide professional and effective support to a range of stakeholders ability to work collaboratively as part of a team and contribute to a positive workplace culture.   Sponsorship / work rights for Australia Work Rights: you must have unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this employment to apply. Visa sponsorship is not available for this appointment.   Pre-employment checks Your employment is conditional upon the successful completion of all pre-employment or background checks required for the role in terms satisfactory to the University. Also, to meet the University’s obligations under the National Higher Education Code to Prevent and Eliminate Gender-Based Violence you will be asked to declare if you have been investigated for, or found to engaged in, sexual harm or gender-based violence in the course of previous employment or in a legal process. Similarly, your ongoing employment is conditional upon the satisfactory maintenance of all relevant clearances and background check requirements. If you do not meet these conditions, the University may take any necessary step, including the termination of your employment. EEO statement   At the University of Sydney, our shared values include diversity and inclusion and we strive to be a place where everyone can thrive. We are committed to creating a University community which reflects the wider community that we serve. We deliver on this commitment through our people and culture programs, as well as key strategies to increase participation and support the careers of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander People , women , people living with a disability , people from culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds , and those who identify as LGBTIQ . We welcome applications from candidates from all backgrounds.  How to apply Applications (including a cover letter, CV, and any additional supporting documentation) can be submitted via the Apply button at the top of the page.   For employees of the University or contingent workers, please login into your Workday account and navigate to the Career icon on your Dashboard.  Click on USYD Find Jobs and apply. For a confidential discussion about the role, or if you require reasonable adjustment or any documents in alternate formats, please contact Ajaypal Singh, Recruitment Operations by email to ajaypal.singh@sydney.edu.au. © The University of Sydney The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment.   Click to view the Position Description for this role. Applications Close Thursday 22 October 2026 11:59 PM"
    },
    "https://usyd.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/USYD_EXTERNAL_CAREER_SITE/job/Camperdown-Campus/Deputy-Vice-Chancellor--Education-and-Students-_0158091-1": {
      "title": "Deputy Vice-Chancellor (Education and Students)",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-07\nFull time\n\nFull time, 5-year fixed term position. The successful candidate will also be offered a professorial appointment in an appropriate discipline and will retain that position at the conclusion of their appointment as Deputy Vice-Chancellor (Education and Students) Opportunity to shape the future of education and student success at one of the world's leading universities Offering a competitive remuneration package including 17% superannuation The University of Sydney is seeking an exceptional academic and enterprise leader to join the University Executive as Deputy Vice-Chancellor (Education and Students), a pivotal role shaping the future of education, student success and institutional impact at one of the world's leading universities. Reporting to the Vice-Chancellor and President, this is a significant opportunity to shape the future of education and the student experience for the institution. The DVC (Education and Students) will set the strategic direction for education and students across the University, advancing educational excellence, innovation, student success and graduate outcomes. The Opportunity Higher education is entering a period of profound change. Emerging technologies and artificial intelligence, evolving student expectations, changing workforce needs and increasing regulatory complexity are reshaping how universities design and deliver education. At the same time, universities are being challenged to rethink the value, accessibility and relevance of higher education in an increasingly dynamic global environment. The DVC (Education and Students) will lead a coherent, evidence-informed agenda for educational innovation, reform and continuous improvement. Working with the University Executive, Academic Board, Senate, Deans, Associate Deans and professional leaders, the successful candidate will bring together academic and professional communities around a shared vision for a transformative, sustainable and learner-centred educational experience.  Leading one of the largest and most complex portfolios within the University, the successful candidate will influence the educational experience of tens of thousands of students across multiple campuses, disciplines and modes of delivery. This is a role for an accomplished academic leader who can combine intellectual leadership with institutional impact. The successful candidate will provide strategic direction while building the relationships, capability and alignment required to translate ambition into improved outcomes for students and graduates. Key Responsibilities Working across the University, the DVC (Education and Students) will: Lead the development and delivery of the University’s educational vision and strategy. Advance student success, graduate outcomes, employability and future readiness. Provide strategic leadership for educational innovation, digital learning and the responsible adoption of artificial intelligence to enhance learning, teaching and student success. Oversee curriculum renewal, teaching quality and educational excellence. Strengthen collaboration and alignment across faculties, schools and professional portfolios, working in partnership with Deans and Heads of University Schools. Lead an integrated and inclusive approach to student experience, engagement and support. Provide executive oversight of educational governance, quality assurance, accreditation, risk and regulatory compliance. Represent the University nationally and internationally, shaping sector priorities and strengthening strategic partnerships. Contribute to institution-wide strategy, performance, culture and decision-making as a member of the University Executive. Leadership Impact The DVC (Education and Students) will lead a significant portfolio spanning education strategy, teaching and learning, curriculum futures, student success, student experience and academic administration. Direct reports include the Academic Registrar, Portfolio General Manager and senior academic leaders responsible for students, teaching and learning, curriculum futures, and education strategy and student success.  The successful candidate will have the opportunity to shape a future-focused education agenda, strengthen institutional capability, accelerate evidence-informed decision-making and foster a culture of educational excellence, innovation and collaboration. The role will also ensure that teaching, research and the student experience remain closely aligned and responsive to the changing needs of learners, employers and society. About You You will be an outstanding academic and enterprise leader with a distinguished record in learning and teaching and the credibility to influence across a large, complex university. You will bring: A proven record of strategic leadership within a complex academic environment. Outstanding expertise and reputation in learning and teaching in higher education. A demonstrated strong track record of improving academic performance, enabling innovation and leading institutional change. Deep understanding of contemporary higher education issues, including educational technologies and artificial intelligence. Experience advancing curriculum quality, student experience, student success and graduate outcomes. Demonstrated leadership of high-performing academic and professional teams. Strong understanding of educational governance, quality assurance and regulatory compliance. The ability to work collaboratively across diverse faculties, disciplines, portfolios and leadership groups. The presence and communication skills to represent the University effectively at national and international levels. An outstanding record of scholarly achievement appropriate to appointment at professorial level.  Demonstrated ability to build alignment and influence across diverse stakeholder groups in complex and distributed environments. A doctorate or PhD in a relevant field, or an equivalent combination of training and experience, is required.  Why Join the University of Sydney? This is a rare opportunity to shape the future of education at one of the world's leading universities. As Deputy Vice-Chancellor (Education and Students), you will help define how a major global institution responds to technological disruption, changing learner expectations and evolving workforce needs, while delivering meaningful impact for students, graduates and society. As DVC (Education and Students), you will lead an agenda with direct impact on educational quality, student success and the University’s long-term contribution to society. The University seeks leaders who demonstrate excellence, trust and accountability, pursue continuous improvement, value diverse perspectives, create inclusive environments and take ownership of institutional outcomes.  Pre-employment checks   Your employment is conditional upon the successful completion of all pre-employment or background checks required for the role in terms satisfactory to the University.  Also, to meet the University’s obligations under the National Higher Education Code to Prevent and Eliminate Gender-Based Violence you will be asked to declare if you have been investigated for, or found to engaged in, sexual harm or gender-based violence in the course of previous employment or in a legal process. Similarly, your ongoing employment is conditional upon the satisfactory maintenance of all relevant clearances and background check requirements. If you do not meet these conditions, the University may take any necessary step, including the termination of your employment. EEO statement   At the University of Sydney, our shared values are trust, accountability and excellence and we strive to be a place where everyone can thrive. We are committed to creating a University community that thrives through diversity and reflects the wider community that we serve. We deliver on this through our commitment to diversity and inclusion , evidenced by our people and culture programs, as well as key strategies to increase participation and support the careers of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander People , women , people living with a disability , people from culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds , and those who identify as LGBTQIA+ . We welcome applications from candidates from all backgrounds.   We are proud to be recognised as an Australian Workplace Equality Index (AWEI) Platinum Employer. Find out more about our work on diversity and inclusion .   How to Apply To request a candidate briefing pack or arrange a confidential discussion, please contact: Dr Courtenay Atwell   Email: Courtenay.atwell@kornferry.com Applications should include a brief cover letter and curriculum vitae and be submitted by midnight on Thursday 29th October 2026 to the Korn Ferry Jobs Portal: https://jobs.candidate.kornferry.com/go/ext/HHECSH/242 © The University of Sydney The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Click to view the Position Description for this role. Applications Close Thursday 29 October 2026 11:59 PM"
    },
    "https://usyd.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/USYD_EXTERNAL_CAREER_SITE/job/Camperdown-Campus/Deputy-Dean--Research----Associate-Dean--Research---Faculty-of-Medicine-and-Health_0157867-2": {
      "title": "Deputy Dean (Research) / Associate Dean (Research), Faculty of Medicine and Health",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-07\nFull time\n\nLead research excellence and shape the future of health and medical research Full-time, Fixed term opportunity for 5 years Offering a competitive remuneration package including 17% super   About the opportunity   The University of Sydney’s Faculty of Medicine and Health (FMH) is seeking an outstanding academic leader to provide strategic leadership across its research portfolio and help shape the future of research excellence, collaboration and impact.   Working closely with the Dean, Faculty Executive, Heads of School and the University’s research leadership, the Deputy Dean (Research) / Associate Dean (Research) will lead the development and delivery of Faculty-wide research priorities, advancing research performance, capability, collaboration, translation and impact. This is a significant opportunity to influence the strategic direction of one of Australia’s leading faculties and strengthen its national and international research standing.   As a key member of the FMH leadership team, the appointee will play a pivotal role in translating the Faculty’s strategic ambitions and the University of Sydney Strategy 2032 into meaningful outcomes. They will drive initiatives across research investment, workforce development, research culture, infrastructure and governance, while fostering a high-performing, inclusive and collaborative research community.   The role will also strengthen strategic partnerships across the University and with external stakeholders, including industry, government, health and community organisations, to expand research opportunities and maximise the translation and impact of research.   Reporting to the Dean, FMH, with a dotted-line relationship to the Deputy Vice-Chancellor (Research), the appointee will work with considerable autonomy, building strong relationships and influencing across a complex and diverse academic environment. The successful candidate will remain research-active and bring a distinguished record of academic achievement, alongside the strategic vision and leadership capability to advance the Faculty’s research ambitions.   About You (skills, capabilities and experience)   The successful candidate will bring:   A PhD and a distinguished record of academic and research achievement, including competitive research funding, research supervision and recognition in their field. Significant experience in academic leadership within a complex university or research environment, with a demonstrated ability to advance research excellence, performance and capability. A proven ability to develop and implement research strategies, translate institutional priorities into practical initiatives and deliver measurable outcomes. A strong understanding of the contemporary research landscape, including funding, research impact and translation, research integrity, governance and evolving policy requirements. Exceptional ability to build relationships, influence and bring together diverse stakeholders across disciplines, Schools and organisational boundaries to achieve shared objectives. Experience in developing research leaders, supporting researcher career development and fostering a positive, inclusive and high-performing research culture. An established network and demonstrated success in building productive partnerships with universities, health organisations, government, industry and community stakeholders, both nationally and internationally. Outstanding communication, interpersonal and decision-making skills, with the ability to navigate complex issues, provide sound strategic advice and represent the Faculty effectively within the University and externally.     Sponsorship / work rights for Australia     Visa sponsorship and relocation assistance will also be provided if required   Term and remuneration   This is a Full-time, Fixed term opportunity for 5 years . The successful candidate will be offered a competitive remuneration package commensurate with the responsibilities of the position and the candidate’s relevant experience and qualifications.   Pre-employment checks   Your employment is conditional upon the successful completion of all pre-employment or background checks required for the role in terms satisfactory to the University. Also, to meet the University’s obligations under the National Higher Education Code to Prevent and Eliminate Gender-Based Violence you will be asked to declare if you have been investigated for, or found to engaged in, sexual harm or gender-based violence in the course of previous employment or in a legal process. Similarly, your ongoing employment is conditional upon the satisfactory maintenance of all relevant clearances and background check requirements. If you do not meet these conditions, the University may take any necessary step, including the termination of your employment.   EEO statement  At the University of Sydney, our shared values are trust, accountability and excellence and we strive to be a place where everyone can thrive. We are committed to creating a University community that thrives through diversity and reflects the wider community that we serve. We deliver on this through our commitment to diversity and inclusion , evidenced by our people and culture programs, as well as key strategies to increase participation and support the careers of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander People , women , people living with a disability , people from culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds , and those who identify as LGBTIQ+ . We welcome applications from candidates from all backgrounds.    We are proud to be recognised as an Australian Workplace Equality Index (AWEI) Gold employer. Find out more about our work on diversity and inclusion .    How to apply Applications ( including a CV and cover letter outlining how your skills, capabilities and experience meet the requirements for the role ) can be submitted via the Apply button at the top of the page. Please refer to the candidate information brochure and position description for further details.   Brochure Click to view the Position Description for this role. For employees of the University or contingent workers, please login into your Workday account and navigate to the Career icon on your Dashboard.  Click on USYD Find Jobs and apply.   For a confidential discussion about the role, or if you require reasonable adjustment or any documents in alternate formats, please contact Mushtaq Sobhan, Recruitment Operations by email to mushtaq.sobhan@sydney.edu.au © The University of Sydney The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Applications Close Wednesday 04 November 2026 11:59 PM"
    },
    "https://usyd.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/USYD_EXTERNAL_CAREER_SITE/job/Camperdown-Campus/Postdoctoral-Research-Associate--Pharmacy-Practice-Research-_0157443-1": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Research Associate (Pharmacy Practice Research)",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-07\nPart time\n\nPart-time, fixed-term position for 12 months Help development and delivery of innovative pharmacy practice research focused on improving healthcare outcomes Base Salary starting from $113,634 + 17% Superannuation About the opportunity The Sydney School of Pharmacy is one of Australia's leading centres for pharmacy education and research, with a long-standing reputation for excellence in pharmaceutical sciences, pharmacy practice, medicines optimisation, and health services research. Located within the University of Sydney's vibrant Camperdown campus, the School is committed to improving health outcomes through innovative research, strong industry and healthcare partnerships, and the development of the next generation of pharmacy professionals. Our researchers work collaboratively across disciplines to address complex healthcare challenges and deliver real-world impact for patients, clinicians, and communities. An exciting opportunity exists for a Postdoctoral Research Associate ( Level A) (Pharmacy Practice Research) to join the Sydney School of Pharmacy and contribute to an NHMRC Investigator Grant research program. This position will support the development and delivery of innovative pharmacy practice research focused on improving healthcare outcomes through evidence-based interventions and stakeholder engagement. Working closely with the Chief Investigator and research team, the successful candidate will play a key role in the co-design of research with clinicians and people with lived experience, preparation of ethics applications, and establishment of a randomised controlled trial. This position offers an excellent opportunity for an early career researcher to develop their research profile while working on a nationally significant research program. This is a part-time, fixed-term position for 12 months based at the University of Sydney's Camperdown campus. Your key responsibilities will be to : Coordinate and manage research projects to ensure milestones, timelines, and deliverables are achieved undertake qualitative and co-design research activities with clinicians and consumers prepare and submit human research ethics applications and study documentation assist with the establishment and implementation of a randomised controlled trial liaise and collaborate with clinicians, researchers, stakeholders, and people with lived experience contribute to the preparation of research publications and dissemination activities support the development of competitive grant applications assist with mentoring and supporting higher degree research students and junior researchers contribute to a collaborative and productive research environment. About you PhD in Pharmacy, Public Health, Health Services Research, Psychology, Allied Health, or a related discipline (or a PhD submitted and awaiting conferral) experience leading or coordinating research projects demonstrated experience in preparing research manuscripts for publication strong understanding of qualitative research methodologies excellent project management and organisational skills, with the ability to manage competing priorities strong stakeholder engagement and relationship management skills excellent written and verbal communication skills the ability to work both independently and collaboratively as part of a multidisciplinary research team an interest in mentoring students and contributing to a positive research culture. To keep our community safe, please be aware of our COVID safety precautions which form our conditions of entry for all staff, students and visitors coming to campus. Work Rights You must have unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this employment to apply. Visa sponsorship is not available for this appointment. Pre-employment checks Your employment is conditional upon the completion of all role required pre-employment or background checks in terms satisfactory to the University. Similarly, your ongoing employment is conditional upon the satisfactory maintenance of all relevant clearances and background check requirements. If you do not meet these conditions, the University may take any necessary step, including the termination of your employment. EEO statement At the University of Sydney, our shared values include diversity and inclusion and we strive to be a place where everyone can thrive. We are committed to creating a University community that reflects the wider community that we serve. We deliver on this commitment through our people and culture programs, as well as key strategies to increase participation and support the careers of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander People , women , people living with a disability, people from culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds , and those who identify as LGBTIQ . We welcome applications from candidates from all backgrounds. How to apply Applications (including a cover letter, CV, and any additional supporting documentation) can be submitted via the Apply button at the top of the page. For employees of the University or contingent workers, please login into your Workday account and navigate to the Career icon on your Dashboard. Click on USYD Find Jobs and apply. For a confidential discussion about the role, or if you require reasonable adjustment or any documents in alternate formats, please contact Rachel Ann Yazigi, Recruitment Operations by email to rachel.yazigi@sydney.edu.au. © The University of Sydney The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Click to view the Position Description for this role. Applications Close Sunday 08 November 2026 11:59 PM"
    },
    "https://usyd.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/USYD_EXTERNAL_CAREER_SITE/job/Camperdown-Campus/Senior-Research-Officer--Research-Development_0153976-3": {
      "title": "Senior Research Officer, Research Development",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-06\nFull time\n\nFull-time, 1-year fixed-term opportunity with the possibility of extension. Located at the Brain and Mind Centre Be part of a collaborative and purpose-driven research institute where you’ll coordinate impactful projects, deliver high-profile events and build meaningful stakeholder relationships within a leading university environment Base Salary $117,133 - $127,610 (HOE7) + 17% superannuation About the opportunity The Brain and Mind Centre (BMC) is the University of Sydney’s multidisciplinary hub for brain and mind research, bringing together researchers, clinicians and partners to tackle some of the most complex challenges in brain and mental health. As the BMC accelerates delivery of its new strategy, the Senior Research Officer – Research Development will play a key role in turning strategic priorities into high-impact research opportunities. Working across the Centre’s strategic pillars, you will help build a strong pipeline of major funding opportunities, multidisciplinary collaborations and research partnerships that support the BMC’s continued growth and impact. Reporting to the Clinical Research Manager, you will work at the intersection of research development, strategic partnerships and clinical research enablement. You will identify emerging grant, industry and philanthropic opportunities; coordinate major and multidisciplinary funding applications; map research capability and potential partnerships; and provide project management across the clinical research lifecycle. You will also support effective research governance, quality and continuous improvement across the Centre. This is an exciting opportunity to help shape and grow a new function within the BMC, with the scope to influence how research opportunities are identified, developed and delivered. Working closely with pillar leads, Chief Investigators and senior stakeholders across the University and external partners, you will connect ideas, expertise and opportunities to help ambitious research move forward. This role is ideally suited to someone with strong research operations and project management experience, excellent stakeholder engagement skills, and the ability to move confidently between strategic thinking and hands-on delivery. Your key responsibilities will be to: Guide research direction and priorities, ensuring projects align with broader university goals and the Brain and Mind Centre's strategic pillars Identify and coordinate grant management processes and pre-award activities, ensuring maximum funding opportunities and successful grant acquisitions Champion best practices in research, driving the adoption of innovation and ensuring stringent compliance Build and manage internal and external partnerships, fostering collaborations that elevates research stature and impact Monitor risk across research development and project activities, promote workplace health and safety, ensure alignment with university policies, and proactively address potential challenges. About you Bachelor’s degree in a relevant field with equivalent combination of relevant training and experience. Well-developed analytical and strategic problem solving skills to deliver project outcomes while being flexible and adaptable in an evolving environment Excellent written and oral communication skills including the ability to communicate to academic and non-academic audiences, to write in plain English, and to prepare complex documents, concise reports, competitive grant and proposal content, and effective agendas Demonstrated ability to coordinate and support large-scale and/or multidisciplinary research funding applications, proposals and partnerships Ability to work with broad supervision, act independently, use initiative and exercise sound judgement. Well-developed organisational, planning and time management skills including the ability to manage multiple projects and priorities and meet deadlines Demonstrated interpersonal skills and a proven ability to maintain effective collaborative working relationships in diverse environments and with a diverse range of stakeholders and partners Experience working in higher education, research or a clinical research or healthcare environment, including direct communication with clinicians Demonstrated experience developing and managing complex projects, with an understanding of the project lifecycle preferably in a scientific or medical environment and a familiarity with medical and scientific terminology Knowledge of the clinical research environment, including ICH GCP and the ethical and regulatory frameworks governing clinical trials (desirable). Sponsorship / work rights for Australia You must have unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this employment to be eligible to apply. Visa sponsorship is not available for this appointment. Pre-employment checks Your employment is conditional upon the completion of all role required pre-employment or background checks in terms satisfactory to the University. Similarly, your ongoing employment is conditional upon the satisfactory maintenance of all relevant clearances and background check requirements. If you do not meet these conditions, the University may take any necessary step, including the termination of your employment. EEO statement   At the University of Sydney, our shared values are trust, accountability and excellence and we strive to be a place where everyone can thrive. We are committed to creating a University community that thrives through diversity and reflects the wider community that we serve. We deliver on this through our commitment to diversity and inclusion , evidenced by our people and culture programs, as well as key strategies to increase participation and support the careers of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander People , women , people living with a disability , people from culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds , and those who identify as LGBTIQ+ . We welcome applications from candidates from all backgrounds.  We are proud to be recognised as an Australian Workplace Equality Index (AWEI) Gold employer. Find out more about our work on diversity and inclusion .  How to apply Applications (including a cover letter, CV, and any additional supporting documentation) can be submitted via the Apply button at the top of the page.   For employees of the University or contingent workers, please login into your Workday account and navigate to the Career icon on your Dashboard.  Click on USYD Find Jobs and apply. For a confidential discussion about the role, or if you require reasonable adjustment or any documents in alternate formats, please contact Nicole Turle, Recruitment Operations by email to nicole.turle@sydney.edu.au © The University of Sydney The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Click to view the Position Description for this role. Applications Close Tuesday 20 October 2026 11:59 PM"
    },
    "https://usyd.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/USYD_EXTERNAL_CAREER_SITE/job/Darlington-Campus/Head-of-School--School-of-Civil-Engineering_0158026": {
      "title": "Head of School, School of Civil Engineering",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-06\nFull time\n\nProvide leadership in innovative teaching and research in civil engineering in alignment with Faculty of Engineering’s strategic goals Join the leadership team of a preeminent engineering faculty, one of the largest in the world Fixed-term role for a period of 4 years with an attractive remuneration package and a professorial continuing appointment in the school   About the opportunity   The Faculty of Engineering at the University of Sydney is one of the most highly regarded engineering faculties in the world, ranked 2nd in Australia and 57th globally (Times Higher Education (THE) Rankings by Subject 2026). Backed by the reputation, resources and location of Australia’s first university, the Faculty is delivering a clear strategy built on strong foundations, with bold ambition and collective excellence in research and education at its heart.   The School of Civil Engineering has been part of the University since 1882 and is world renowned for its work in structures and geomechanics. It is ranked 3rd in Australia and 31st globally for civil and structural engineering (QS World University Rankings by Subject 2026). Its academics lead internationally recognised research centres in structural engineering, geomechanics, and wind, waves and water, and their work has shaped Australian Standards® for structural design. With this depth of talent, the School has the potential to further strengthen its international standing and set the benchmark in civil engineering research and education.   We are seeking a distinguished academic to lead the School into its next phase as Head of School, School of Civil Engineering . This is a key role within the Faculty Leadership Team, responsible for how the school contributes to the Faculty's strategy and goals. Working with colleagues, you will set the School's direction and define its areas of strength, including emerging opportunities such as AI in civil engineering. You will be responsible for fostering a collegial, inclusive and high-performing community grounded in trust, accountability and collective responsibility for excellence.   Key responsibilities include ensuring excellence and impact in teaching and research outcomes, maintaining accreditation standards, enhancing the student experience, and meeting financial targets. The Head of School holds formal financial and people management delegations, with responsibility for effective governance, fair and transparent allocation of resources and workload, risk management and workplace health and safety. The role also leads the recruitment, development and retention of talented staff. Full details are provided in the candidate information pack.   This is a role for a leader who wants to make a real impact.   About you (skills, capabilities and experience)   The successful candidate will hold a PhD and will have a respected scholarly record and recognised international standing in any sub-discipline of civil engineering. You will have a strong commitment to quality in research and education, and the ability to mentor colleagues in their scholarship.   You will have demonstrated leadership capability, with extensive experience in people leadership and management within the higher education or knowledge sector. You will have a track record of shaping the academic direction of a school or department, building its strengths in research and education and supporting colleagues to succeed, together with the sound judgement to manage its finances and infrastructure sustainably.   You will have experience nurturing a positive, high-performing culture, bringing colleagues with you through clear communication and genuine engagement, and managing difficult conversations with calm and integrity. You will have a clear commitment to equity, collegiality and leadership as service, in alignment with the University’s values of trust, accountability and excellence.   You will also bring experience working with industry and a wide network of academic, industry and professional relationships that you can draw on to enhance the standing of the School, the Faculty and the University.   Key benefits    Up to 17% employer superannuation contributions Tax-efficient salary packaging, including for cars, laptops and additional superannuation contributions Relocation assistance and visa sponsorship if you are moving from interstate or overseas Access to Fitness Passport, plus discounted rates at the University's sports and fitness centres The opportunity to lead a School at one of the world's leading universities   Term and remuneration   The successful candidate will be offered a four-year fixed term position on a full-time basis, with the possibility for renewal subject to performance and funding agreement. The appointment is subject to completion of a satisfactory probation period for new appointees.   Pre-employment checks   Your employment is conditional upon the successful completion of all pre-employment or background checks required for the role in terms satisfactory to the University.  Also, to meet the University’s obligations under the National Higher Education Code to Prevent and Eliminate Gender-Based Violence you will be asked to declare if you have been investigated for, or found to engaged in, sexual harm or gender-based violence in the course of previous employment or in a legal process. Similarly, your ongoing employment is conditional upon the satisfactory maintenance of all relevant clearances and background check requirements. If you do not meet these conditions, the University may take any necessary step, including the termination of your employment.   Other   EEO statement     At the University of Sydney, our shared values are trust, accountability and excellence and we strive to be a place where everyone can thrive. We are committed to creating a University community that thrives through diversity and reflects the wider community that we serve. We deliver on this through our commitment to diversity and inclusion , evidenced by our people and culture programs, as well as key strategies to increase participation and support the careers of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander People , women , people living with a disability , people from culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds , and those who identify as LGBTQIA+ . We welcome applications from candidates from all backgrounds.    We are proud to be recognised as an Australian Workplace Equality Index (AWEI) Platinum Employer. Find out more about our work on diversity and inclusion .    How to apply   Applications ( including a CV and cover letter outlining how your skills, capabilities and experience meet the requirements for the role ) can be submitted via email at CivilEng@devereauxconsulting.com .   For employees of the University or contingent workers, please login into your Workday account and navigate to the Career icon on your Dashboard.  Click on USYD Find Jobs and apply. For a confidential discussion, please contact Dr Jackie Besley, Managing Partner, Devereaux Consulting, on +61 (0) 413 633 195 or at jackiebesley@devereauxconsulting.com . Confidential discussions are encouraged and enquiries will be handled with discretion. © The University of Sydney   The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment.   Click here to view the Candidate Information Pack for this role. Click to view the Position Description for this role. Applications Close Sunday 08 November 2026 11:59 PM"
    },
    "https://usyd.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/USYD_EXTERNAL_CAREER_SITE/job/Forest-Lodge/Senior-Manager---Procurement-Assurance_0155798-1": {
      "title": "Senior Manager – Procurement Assurance",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-06\nFull time\n\nFull time, continuing opportunity Shape the future of procurement at the University by leading assurance, governance and continuous improvement that makes buying easier, strengthens decision making and applies risk-commensurate controls Offering a competitive remuneration package including 17% superannuation   About the opportunity   This is an opportunity to shape how procurement works across the University. As Senior Manager – Procurement Assurance, you will set the direction for assurance, governance and continuous improvement, using data and insight to identify emerging risks, strengthen controls and improve procurement outcomes. You will work with senior leaders and stakeholders to provide authoritative advice, guide risk-based decision-making and lead governance, audit and reporting activities. Just as importantly, you will help us rethink how we work: removing unnecessary complexity, improving systems and processes, and building capability so that buying is easier for the University community while maintaining the right level of oversight. Through inclusive leadership and a strong service excellence mindset, you will turn assurance insights into practical, lasting improvement.   Your key responsibilities will be to:   lead the University’s procurement assurance framework, applying risk-based and data-driven approaches to identify emerging risks, strengthen controls and focus assurance where it adds the greatest value provide authoritative advice on procurement governance, policy, controls and assurance, influencing senior stakeholders and enabling informed, risk-commensurate decision-making lead the University’s response to procurement audits, reviews, investigations and assurance activities, and oversee remediation to achieve sustainable compliance outcomes lead governance reporting and secretariat services for key procurement bodies, providing high-quality analysis, papers and recommendations that support transparent and effective decision-making build procurement capability through targeted training, guidance and awareness initiatives that improve understanding and support consistent practice across the University turn insight into action by leading improvement and change initiatives that simplify procurement, enhance systems and reporting, strengthen controls and make the stakeholder experience easier and more effective   About you   Bachelor’s Degree in a relevant discipline, or an equivalent combination of training and professional experience significant experience leading procurement compliance, assurance, audit or risk management activities in a large and complex organisation strong expertise in governance, compliance frameworks, policy and legislative interpretation, risk management, audit and internal controls a track record of building trusted partnerships, presenting with credibility and providing authoritative advice that influences senior stakeholders advanced analytical and problem-solving skills, with the ability to use data to identify emerging risks, assess control effectiveness and guide informed decisions strong leadership, communication and organisational skills, with the energy and initiative to lead change, develop people and embed a culture of service excellence and continuous improvement   Sponsorship / work rights for Australia   You must have unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this employment to be eligible to apply. Visa sponsorship is not available for this appointment.   Pre-employment checks and declarations     Your employment is conditional upon the successful completion of all pre-employment or background checks required for the role in terms satisfactory to the University.  Also, to meet the University’s obligations under the National Higher Education Code to Prevent and Eliminate Gender-Based Violence you will be asked to declare if you have been investigated for, or found to engaged in, sexual harm or gender-based violence in the course of previous employment or in a legal process. Similarly, your ongoing employment is conditional upon the satisfactory maintenance of all relevant clearances and background check requirements. If you do not meet these conditions, the University may take any necessary step, including the termination of your employment.     EEO statement     At the University of Sydney, our shared values are trust, accountability and excellence and we strive to be a place where everyone can thrive. We are committed to creating a University community that thrives through diversity and reflects the wider community that we serve. We deliver on this through our commitment to diversity and inclusion , evidenced by our people and culture programs, as well as key strategies to increase participation and support the careers of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander People , women , people living with a disability , people from culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds , and those who identify as LGBTQIA+ . We welcome applications from candidates from all backgrounds.    We are proud to be recognised as an Australian Workplace Equality Index (AWEI) Platinum Employer. Find out more about our work on diversity and inclusion .    How to apply   Applications (including a cover letter, CV, and any additional supporting documentation) can be submitted via the Apply button at the top of the page.     For employees of the University or contingent workers, please login into your Workday account and navigate to the Career icon on your Dashboard.  Click on USYD Find Jobs and apply.   For a confidential discussion about the role, or if you require reasonable adjustment or any documents in alternate formats, please contact Victoria Ford-Rogers Recruitment Operations by email to victoria.ford-rogers@sydney.edu.au   © The University of Sydney   The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Click to view the Position Description for this role. Applications Close Tuesday 27 October 2026 11:59 PM"
    },
    "https://usyd.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/USYD_EXTERNAL_CAREER_SITE/job/Surry-Hills-Campus/Lecturer-Senior-Lecturer---Orthodontics_0157978": {
      "title": "Lecturer/Senior Lecturer - Orthodontics",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-16\nFull time\n\nFull time continuing role based at the Sydney Dental Hospital Lecturer/Senior Lecturer (Orthodontics specialty) opportunity at the Sydney Dental School  Base Salary Level B $132,533 Base Salary Level C $162,347 + 17% superannuation plus clinical loading for AHPRA registration About the opportunity The Faculty of Medicine and Health has been designed to enable innovation, excellence and strategic vision in teaching, research, and clinical practice not only in medicine and health, but in all of the areas in which we operate. We are committed to challenging conventional wisdom, and to finding new paths to travel. We are focused on leading new possibilities in the way we deliver wellness and healthcare and in making a distinctive impact on healthcare, both locally and globally. Sydney Dental School (SDS) As an academic school within the Faculty of Medicine and Health, SDS is consistently ranked as one of Australia's top dental schools. Through our delivery of integrated research, clinical training, and professional education, students benefit from the opportunity to gain experience in a diverse range of clinical facilities, to interact with dental and other health professionals, and develop an understanding of real-world challenges in oral health and healthcare.  The role will report to Professor Ali Darendeliler . The Lecturer/ Senior Lecturer role supports the continued development of Orthodontics at Sydney Dental School where you will make a significant contribution to teaching and learning practice, design and evaluation within our faculty. You will also support and organise continuing education for Sydney Health Executive Education ( SHEEd) courses in dentistry and orthodontics. Your key responsibilities will be to: Lecturer undertake independent teaching in discipline, contributing to outstanding educational design, delivery, evaluation and outcomes engage in high-quality teaching and learning experiences which meet the University’s expectations and standards for education design and deliver quality educational units of study and experiences that support student needs and meet curriculum requirements contribute to the design and delivery of multi-disciplinary work-integrated learning units and content engage in supervision of research students at undergraduate level, in accordance with the requirements and expectations of the School/ Faculty/ University apply scholarship and knowledge of contemporary pedagogical practice to innovate Education carry out administrative tasks primarily connected with the area of discipline contribute to a positive workplace culture in which diversity of thought, contribution, collaboration, excellence and achieving outcomes are valued and enabled understand and contribute to furthering the university and faculty strategic agenda In addition as Senior Lecturer build and maintain a reputation for academic excellence through independent and innovative contributions to research and education in Orthodontics establish collaborations and partnerships to enhance the research profile and reputation of the University provide leadership with significant impact on teaching innovation, curriculum development, research training and supervision within the School or Faculty     About you The University values courage and creativity; openness and engagement; inclusion and diversity; and respect and integrity. As such, we see the importance of recruiting talent aligned to these values and are looking for a Lecturer who has: a Dental qualification that is registrable with the Dental Board of Australia, AHPRA a Specialist Orthodontic qualification recognized with the Dental Board of Australia, AHPRA for international candidates -overseas trained orthodontist eligible for limited registration with AHPRA experience in high quality teaching in Orthodontics for all levels of students deep and broad experience in teaching and research in a tertiary environment, as evidenced by participation in teaching within an established program and in a variety of settings demonstrated success in designing, implementing and evaluating teaching and learning initiatives proven capacity to stimulate, actively engage and educate an audience using a range of media experience in supervising or mentoring others and /or coordinating team activities ability to work with discipline heads and course coordinators to develop and mark teaching materials and assessments Specialist clinical supervision experience in Orthodontics ability to undertake high quality research studies and publish in recognised journals in line with the School’s minimum requirements the ability to source and acquire research grants in line with the faculty minimum outcome targets demonstrated excellence in research, scholarly activities relevant to the discipline and educational design and practice. strong written and verbal communication skills For overseas candidates, English requirements as per Board of Australia requirements for registration experience in providing specialist Orthodontic treatment to a wide spectrum of patients including children, patients with disabilities and people from diverse cultural backgrounds a collegiate, team-oriented nature and experience of working as part of a team advanced Orthodontic specialist clinical/surgical experience gained within the public or private sector as the candidate will be required to provide orthodontic treatment to patients eligible to receive public dental services at Sydney Dental Hospital and at Westmead Centre for Oral Health. In addition as Senior Lecturer a minimum of five years’ substantial teaching experience in Orthodontics , particularly within postgraduate dental education current full AHPRA dental and specialist orthodontics registration in Australia an established academic with recognised standing in Orthodontics and evidence of national-level contribution to the discipline   Pre-employment checks Your employment is conditional upon the successful completion of all pre-employment or background checks required for the role in terms satisfactory to the University.  Also, to meet the University’s obligations under the National Higher Education Code to Prevent and Eliminate Gender-Based Violence you will be asked to declare if you have been investigated for, or found to engaged in, sexual harm or gender-based violence in the course of previous employment or in a legal process. Similarly, your ongoing employment is conditional upon the satisfactory maintenance of all relevant clearances and background check requirements. If you do not meet these conditions, the University may take any necessary step, including the termination of your employment. As this position is located on an NSW Health site, it is a regulatory requirement to complete compulsory employment checks prior to starting in this position. For more information and instructions, please visit: NSW Health employment requirements This position is designated as involving child-related work. To undertake or remain in this position, you are required to apply for and obtain a Working with Children Check clearance in accordance with the Child Protection (Working with Children) Act 2012. EEO statement   At the University of Sydney, our shared values are trust, accountability and excellence and we strive to be a place where everyone can thrive. We are committed to creating a University community that thrives through diversity and reflects the wider community that we serve. We deliver on this through our commitment to diversity and inclusion , evidenced by our people and culture programs, as well as key strategies to increase participation and support the careers of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander People , women , people living with a disability , people from culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds , and those who identify as LGBTIQ+ . We welcome applications from candidates from all backgrounds.  We are proud to be recognised as an Australian Workplace Equality Index (AWEI) Gold employer. Find out more about our work on diversity and inclusion .  How to apply Applications (including a cover letter, CV, and any additional supporting documentation) can be submitted via the Apply button at the top of the page.  For employees of the University or contingent workers, please login into your Workday account and navigate to the Career icon on your Dashboard.  Click on USYD Find Jobs and apply. For a confidential discussion about the role, or if you require reasonable adjustment or any documents in alternate formats, please contact Cecilia Blamey, Recruitment Operations by email to cecilia.blamey@sydney.edu.au. © The University of Sydney The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Click to view the Position Description for this role. Applications Close Sunday 01 November 2026 11:59 PM"
    },
    "https://usyd.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/USYD_EXTERNAL_CAREER_SITE/job/Camperdown-Campus/Clinical-Data-Systems-Coordinator_0158024": {
      "title": "Clinical Data Systems Coordinator",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-16\nPart time\n\nCasual opportunity supporting the delivery of clinical trial data systems within a leading clinical research environment. Based at Chris O’Brien LifeHouse with a hybrid working arrangement. HEO 7, $80.18 per hour plus 17% superannuation.   About the opportunity   An exciting opportunity is available for an experienced Clinical Data Systems Coordinator to join a collaborative team supporting the delivery of clinical trials. Working within a specialist clinical trials environment, you will play a key role in the design, development, testing and maintenance of clinical data systems that support high-quality clinical research.   You will work closely with researchers, clinicians and project teams to support study development activities and the successful delivery of clinical data systems across a range of projects.   Key responsibilities will include: designing, developing and testing clinical data systems for clinical trials, including data entry forms and validation rules collaborating with project managers, clinical data managers, clinicians, biostatisticians and other stakeholders to deliver study requirements supporting the implementation, maintenance and troubleshooting of clinical data systems in production environments developing and maintaining documentation, procedures and testing plans providing user support and training to clinical research staff ensuring systems and processes comply with relevant regulations, guidelines and operating procedures   About you   To be successful in this role, you will bring: demonstrated, extensive experience working with REDCap, experience with other clinical data systems is welcome but not a requirement experience supporting the study development process within a clinical research or clinical trials environment strong stakeholder engagement skills and the ability to build effective working relationships across multidisciplinary teams exceptional attention to detail and commitment to data quality highly developed organisational and time management skills, with the ability to manage multiple priorities and meet deadlines proven problem-solving skills and the ability to work both independently and collaboratively experience providing user support, training and troubleshooting for data systems proven ability to quickly fit into a new work environment and deliver tangible outputs   Sponsorship / work rights for Australia   You must have unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this employment to be eligible to apply. Visa sponsorship is not available for this appointment.   Pre-employment checks and declarations       Your employment is conditional upon the successful completion of all pre-employment or background checks required for the role in terms satisfactory to the University.  Also, to meet the University’s obligations under the National Higher Education Code to Prevent and Eliminate Gender-Based Violence you will be asked to declare if you have been investigated for, or found to engaged in, sexual harm or gender-based violence in the course of previous employment or in a legal process. Similarly, your ongoing employment is conditional upon the satisfactory maintenance of all relevant clearances and background check requirements. If you do not meet these conditions, the University may take any necessary step, including the termination of your employment. EEO statement     At the University of Sydney, our shared values are trust, accountability and excellence and we strive to be a place where everyone can thrive. We are committed to creating a University community that thrives through diversity and reflects the wider community that we serve. We deliver on this through our commitment to diversity and inclusion , evidenced by our people and culture programs, as well as key strategies to increase participation and support the careers of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander People , women , people living with a disability , people from culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds , and those who identify as LGBTQIA+ . We welcome applications from candidates from all backgrounds.    We are proud to be recognised as an Australian Workplace Equality Index (AWEI) Platinum Employer. Find out more about our work on diversity and inclusion .    How to apply   Applications (including a cover letter, CV, and any additional supporting documentation) can be submitted via the Apply button at the top of the page.    For employees of the University or contingent workers, please login into your Workday account and navigate to the Career icon on your Dashboard.  Click on USYD Find Jobs and apply.   For a confidential discussion about the role, or if you require reasonable adjustment or any documents in alternate formats, please contact Leah Edwards, Recruitment Operations by email to leah.edwards@sydney.edu.au © The University of Sydney The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Click to view the Position Description for this role. Applications Close Sunday 25 October 2026 11:59 PM"
    },
    "https://usyd.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/USYD_EXTERNAL_CAREER_SITE/job/Camperdown-Campus/FMH-Research-Infrastructure-and-Biobanking-Officer_0158407": {
      "title": "FMH Research Infrastructure and Biobanking Officer",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-16\nPart time\n\nExciting casual opportunity within the Faculty of Medicine and Health Provide administrative and coordination support for research infrastructure and biobanking activities HEO6 step 1,  $73.45 per hour, plus superannuation   About the opportunity   The Faculty of Medicine and Health at the University of Sydney is seeking a highly organised and proactive Research Infrastructure and Biobanking Officer to provide administrative and coordination support for research infrastructure and biobanking activities. This role is primarily focused on administrative support, stakeholder communication and coordination, ensuring the smooth and efficient delivery of research-related activities. Working with researchers, laboratory staff, external vendors and other stakeholders, you will assist with the day-to-day coordination of activities that support the Faculty’s research environment. You will also help facilitate laboratory-related activities, including coordinating equipment installations, liaising with vendors, scheduling site visits and helping to ensure laboratory spaces are appropriately organised and ready for planned activities.   Your key responsibilities will be to: provide day-to-day administrative support, including responding to emails, drafting correspondence and preparing documents coordinate meetings, including scheduling, preparing agendas, recording minutes and following up on actions communicate and liaise with internal and external stakeholders, including researchers, laboratory personnel, service providers and equipment vendors coordinate research infrastructure activities, including arranging vendor visits, facilitating equipment installations and managing associated schedules and logistics assist with organising and securing appropriate laboratory space for equipment installations and other research-related activities facilitate laboratory activities by coordinating access, schedules and practical arrangements to support researchers and laboratory teams maintain accurate records, documentation and administrative processes to support research infrastructure and biobanking operations provide general coordination and administrative assistance to support the effective delivery of Faculty research activities   About you proactive and organised individual with strong administrative and communication skills and the ability to coordinate multiple activities in a busy research environment comfortable liaising with a diverse range of stakeholders, managing competing priorities and ensuring that administrative tasks and logistical arrangements are completed efficiently strong organisational and time-management skills, with the ability to coordinate meetings, schedules and competing priorities excellent written and verbal communication skills, including experience drafting documents, responding to enquiries and liaising with stakeholders. strong attention to detail and the ability to maintain accurate records and documentation a practical, solutions-focused approach to coordinating activities and resolving day-to-day logistical challenges the ability to work collaboratively with academic, professional and technical staff, as well as external service providers Desirable experience bachelor’s degree or PHD in a relevant discipline, with a background in medical, health or science research highly regarded experience working in a laboratory, medical research or scientific research environment familiarity with research infrastructure, laboratory equipment or biobanking activities experience coordinating equipment installations, vendor visits, laboratory access or other research-related logistics   Sponsorship / work rights for Australia   You must have unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this employment to be eligible to apply. Visa sponsorship is not available for this appointment.   Pre-employment checks and declarations    Your employment is conditional upon the successful completion of all pre-employment or background checks required for the role in terms satisfactory to the University.  Also, to meet the University’s obligations under the National Higher Education Code to Prevent and Eliminate Gender-Based Violence you will be asked to declare if you have been investigated for, or found to engaged in, sexual harm or gender-based violence in the course of previous employment or in a legal process. Similarly, your ongoing employment is conditional upon the satisfactory maintenance of all relevant clearances and background check requirements. If you do not meet these conditions, the University may take any necessary step, including the termination of your employment.   EEO statement    At the University of Sydney, our shared values are trust, accountability and excellence and we strive to be a place where everyone can thrive. We are committed to creating a University community that thrives through diversity and reflects the wider community that we serve. We deliver on this through our commitment to diversity and inclusion , evidenced by our people and culture programs, as well as key strategies to increase participation and support the careers of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander People , women , people living with a disability , people from culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds , and those who identify as LGBTQIA+ . We welcome applications from candidates from all backgrounds.    We are proud to be recognised as an Australian Workplace Equality Index (AWEI) Platinum Employer. Find out more about our work on diversity and inclusion .    How to apply   Applications (including a cover letter, CV, and any additional supporting documentation) can be submitted via the Apply button at the top of the page.    For employees of the University or contingent workers, please login into your Workday account and navigate to the Career icon on your Dashboard.  Click on USYD Find Jobs and apply.    For a confidential discussion about the role, or if you require reasonable adjustment or any documents in alternate formats, please contact Mari Wyn Lewis , Recruitment Operations by email to mari.lewis@sydney.edu.au   © The University of Sydney   The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment.   Click to view the Position Description for this role. Applications Close Thursday 22 October 2026 11:59 PM"
    },
    "https://usyd.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/USYD_EXTERNAL_CAREER_SITE/job/Darlington-Campus/Head-of-Social-Media-and-Content_0157918-1": {
      "title": "Head of Social Media and Content",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-16\nFull time\n\nFull-time fixed term until 18 December 2027 Lead enterprise-wide social and content strategy that engages diverse audiences, builds advocacy and strengthens reputation HEO 10 $162,528 plus 17% superannuation     About the opportunity    The Head of Social Media and Content leads a team of social media and digital content specialists responsible for the University's social media and content strategy and production across domestic and international audiences, advocacy and reputation. The role aligns this work with the Marketing and Communications strategy to support student recruitment and student experience, build advocacy in Australia and overseas, and protect the University's reputation.   The role creates strategy to connect and align activity across the University, leads a community of practice for content creation, and sets the governance and standards for how the University uses social media. It leads major strategic projects, is responsible for how the team operates, and uses audience insights and performance data to guide decisions.   As a trusted advisor to senior leaders, you will work across a diverse stakeholder landscape to provide strategic guidance on content, public affairs and complex communications matters. This role requires strong judgement, political acumen and the ability to influence and align stakeholders at all levels while leading a high-performing team through a significant period of strategic focus and delivery.   Key responsibilities   Develop and deliver an integrated content strategy that aligns activity across the University and supports key organisational priorities. Lead the University's social media strategy, governance framework and content planning approach across multiple channels. Provide strategic communications advice to senior stakeholders on issues management, advocacy, public affairs opportunities and emerging risks. Foster collaboration across brand, media, communications and specialist teams to ensure content is coordinated, consistent and impactful. Manage agency and platform relationships to maximise performance, audience engagement and strategic outcomes. Lead, coach and develop a high-performing team while cultivating a culture of innovation, collaboration and continuous improvement.   About you   Significant experience leading content, communications, public affairs or related strategic functions within a large and complex organisation. Demonstrated ability to build trusted relationships and provide strategic advice to senior leaders and executive stakeholders. Strong judgement and political acumen, with experience navigating complex issues and balancing competing stakeholder priorities. Proven success developing enterprise-wide content strategies that bring together multiple teams, audiences and organisational priorities. Exceptional presentation, communication and influencing skills, with the ability to engage diverse audiences and tailor your approach from specialist teams through to executive leadership. Experience leading high-performing teams and managing budgets, agency partnerships and resources to deliver strategic outcomes.     Sponsorship / work rights for Australia   You must have unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this employment to be eligible to apply. Visa sponsorship is not available for this appointment.     Pre-employment checks and declarations     Your employment is conditional upon the successful completion of all pre-employment or background checks required for the role in terms satisfactory to the University.  Also, to meet the University’s obligations under the National Higher Education Code to Prevent and Eliminate Gender-Based Violence you will be asked to declare if you have been investigated for, or found to engaged in, sexual harm or gender-based violence in the course of previous employment or in a legal process. Similarly, your ongoing employment is conditional upon the satisfactory maintenance of all relevant clearances and background check requirements. If you do not meet these conditions, the University may take any necessary step, including the termination of your employment.   This position is designated as involving child-related work. To undertake or remain in this position, you are required to apply for and obtain a Working with Children Check clearance in accordance with the Child Protection (Working with Children) Act 2012.     EEO statement     At the University of Sydney, our shared values are trust, accountability and excellence and we strive to be a place where everyone can thrive. We are committed to creating a University community that thrives through diversity and reflects the wider community that we serve. We deliver on this through our commitment to diversity and inclusion , evidenced by our people and culture programs, as well as key strategies to increase participation and support the careers of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander People , women , people living with a disability , people from culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds , and those who identify as LGBTQIA+ . We welcome applications from candidates from all backgrounds.    We are proud to be recognised as an Australian Workplace Equality Index (AWEI) Platinum Employer. Find out more about our work on diversity and inclusion .      How to apply   Applications (including a cover letter, CV, and any additional supporting documentation) can be submitted via the Apply button at the top of the page.    For a confidential discussion about the role, or if you require reasonable adjustment or support filling out this application, please contact Kristy Fairweather, Recruitment Operations, by email to kristy.fairweather@sydney.edu.au   © The University of Sydney   The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Click to view the Position Description for this role. Applications Close Sunday 01 November 2026 11:59 PM"
    },
    "https://usyd.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/USYD_EXTERNAL_CAREER_SITE/job/Darlington-Campus/Food-and-Beverage-Manager_0155455-1": {
      "title": "Food and Beverage Manager - Seymour Centre",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-16\nFull time\n\nFull-time, continuing opportunity with one work-from-home administration day per week Enjoy a 38-hour working week while leading hospitality at one of Sydney's premier performing arts venues Competitive Salary plus 12% Superannuation     About the opportunity     The Food and Beverage Manager plays a key role in delivering exceptional hospitality experiences at the Seymour Centre, leading all food and beverage operations across bars, catering services and event hospitality. This hands-on leadership role is responsible for delivering outstanding customer service, driving commercial performance and creating memorable experiences for patrons attending performances, events and functions.   This is a full-time position that provides a level of work-life balance rarely found in hospitality management. While some evening and weekend work is required to support performances, opening nights and special events, the role offers a 38-hour working week and the flexibility of one work-from-home administration day per week.   Working closely with operational leaders, you will oversee supplier partnerships, stock management, staffing, financial performance and compliance activities while identifying opportunities to enhance service delivery, improve profitability and drive revenue growth. You will negotiate supplier agreements, manage budgets and ensure food and beverage offerings remain competitive, profitable and aligned with industry best practice.   This is an exciting opportunity for an experienced hospitality professional who thrives in a fast-paced events environment. Combining strategic oversight with day-to-day operational leadership, you will lead and develop a high-performing team while contributing to the ongoing success and reputation of one of Sydney's leading performing arts venues.   Key responsibilities   Lead the day-to-day operation of food and beverage outlets, delivering outstanding customer experiences and maintaining exceptional service standards. Manage supplier relationships, negotiate contracts and curate attractive, high-quality food and beverage offerings that enhance the visitor experience. Build, mentor and lead a high-performing hospitality team, fostering a culture of service excellence and continuous improvement. Drive commercial success through effective budget management, stock control, pricing strategies, cash management and financial oversight. Oversee catering services for performances, functions and events, ensuring seamless delivery and exceptional client service. Ensure compliance with WHS and RSA requirements while championing continuous improvement across hospitality operations.   About you   Demonstrated experience managing food and beverage, hospitality, catering or event operations, ideally within arts, entertainment, live performance or venue environments. A proven people leader who can inspire, motivate and develop high-performing teams. Strong commercial and financial acumen, with experience managing budgets, forecasting and identifying opportunities to improve profitability. Excellent relationship-building and stakeholder engagement skills, with the ability to develop effective partnerships with suppliers, clients and colleagues. Experience using POS systems, managing stock control processes and overseeing cash handling and reconciliation activities. A genuine passion for hospitality, live events and delivering exceptional customer experiences. A proactive, adaptable and solutions-focused approach, with the ability to thrive in a dynamic and fast-paced environment.     Sponsorship / work rights for Australia   You must have unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this employment to apply. Visa sponsorship is not available for this appointment.     Pre-employment checks   Your employment is conditional upon the completion of all role required pre-employment or background checks in terms satisfactory to the University. Similarly, your ongoing employment is conditional upon the satisfactory maintenance of all relevant clearances and background check requirements. If you do not meet these conditions, the University may take any necessary step, including the termination of your employment.   EEO statement     At the University of Sydney, our shared values are trust, accountability and excellence and we strive to be a place where everyone can thrive. We are committed to creating a University community that thrives through diversity and reflects the wider community that we serve. We deliver on this through our commitment to diversity and inclusion , evidenced by our people and culture programs, as well as key strategies to increase participation and support the careers of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander People , women , people living with a disability , people from culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds , and those who identify as LGBTQIA+ . We welcome applications from candidates from all backgrounds.    We are proud to be recognised as an Australian Workplace Equality Index (AWEI) Platinum Employer. Find out more about our work on diversity and inclusion .      How to apply   Applications (including a cover letter, CV, and any additional supporting documentation) can be submitted via the Apply button at the top of the page.    For a confidential discussion about the role, or if you require reasonable adjustment or support filling out this application, please contact Kristy Fairweather, Recruitment Operations, by email to kristy.fairweather@sydney.edu.au   © The University of Sydney   The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Click to view the Position Description for this role. Applications Close Sunday 01 November 2026 11:59 PM"
    },
    "https://usyd.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/USYD_EXTERNAL_CAREER_SITE/job/Camperdown-Campus/Lecturer-in-Italian-Studies_0158103-1": {
      "title": "Lecturer in Italian Studies",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-15\nFull time\n\nFull-time, continuing role based at the Camperdown Campus in the School of Languages and Cultures An exciting opportunity to conduct research and deliver teaching in the Discipline of Italian Studies and the School of Languages and Cultures Academic Level B Base Salary $132,533 p.a. - $157,381 p.a. + 17% superannuation      About the opportunity   Join the School of Languages and Cultures , within the Faculty of Arts and Social Sciences, the Lecturer in Italian Studies position is open to a candidate capable of providing high-quality teaching, unit development, coordination, and high-quality research within the Discipline of Italian Studies. We particularly welcome applicants with expertise in Italian applied linguistics, sociolinguistics, or language pedagogy, while remaining open to candidates from across the field of Italian Studies, including modern and contemporary Italian literature and Italian social and cultural history. In all cases, applicants should demonstrate the capacity to contribute to teaching across a broad curriculum in Italian culture.   The candidate will also participate in the coordination and teaching of language units at all levels (beginner, intermediate, and advanced), contribute to the review and renewal of the language curriculum, and will take an active role in the supervision of Honours, Master's, and PhD students.   To be eligible for the position, applicants must hold a PhD in Italian Studies, or a related discipline, and have experience in teaching and research within higher education institutions. Applicants should have a strong record of research and academic publications in one or more areas of Italian Studies, demonstrable expertise in language teaching, and be willing to undertake organisational and administrative responsibilities within the Discipline of Italian Studies and the School of Languages and Cultures.   Reporting to the Head of School , your key responsibilities will be: design and deliver high-quality teaching in Italian Studies and Italian language courses across all proficiency levels, including the integration of emerging technologies and AI in language education provide academic leadership and contribute to the effective administration of the Discipline collaborate across the Discipline, School, and related language and culture programs to support teaching and learning initiatives supervise Honours and postgraduate research students in Italian Studies maintain an active research profile through publications, grant funding applications, conferences, seminars, and public engagement foster engagement with the Italian community and contribute to School and Faculty governance, outreach, and promotional activities.     About you   We are seeking an exceptional Lecturer in Italian Studies.     To be considered for this role, please address the following in your cover letter, by demonstrating:   your ability to teach across a broad range of topics in Italian Studies, and outline your approach to language pedagogy and curriculum renewal; the significance and impact of your current research, and provide a brief outline of your future research agenda; and your capacity as an innovative and creative academic who can strengthen and expand the program by developing and sustaining relationships with key stakeholders in Italian Studies.     Sponsorship / work rights for Australia   You must have unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this employment to apply. Visa sponsorship is not available for this appointment.     Pre-employment checks and declarations    Your employment is conditional upon the successful completion of all pre-employment or background checks required for the role in terms satisfactory to the University.  Also, to meet the University’s obligations under the National Higher Education Code to Prevent and Eliminate Gender-Based Violence you will be asked to declare if you have been investigated for, or found to engaged in, sexual harm or gender-based violence in the course of previous employment or in a legal process. Similarly, your ongoing employment is conditional upon the satisfactory maintenance of all relevant clearances and background check requirements. If you do not meet these conditions, the University may take any necessary step, including the termination of your employment.     EEO statement     At the University of Sydney, our shared values are trust, accountability and excellence and we strive to be a place where everyone can thrive. We are committed to creating a University community that thrives through diversity and reflects the wider community that we serve. We deliver on this through our commitment to diversity and inclusion , evidenced by our people and culture programs, as well as key strategies to increase participation and support the careers of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander People , women , people living with a disability , people from culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds , and those who identify as LGBTIQ+ . We welcome applications from candidates from all backgrounds.    We are proud to be recognised as an Australian Workplace Equality Index (AWEI) Gold employer. Find out more about our work on diversity and inclusion .      How to apply   Applications (including a cover letter, CV, and any additional supporting documentation) can be submitted via the Apply button at the top of the page. For employees of the University or contingent workers, please login into your Workday account and navigate to the Career icon on your Dashboard.  Click on USYD Find Jobs and apply.   For a confidential discussion about the role, or if you require reasonable adjustment or any documents in alternate formats, please contact Helen Efstathiou, Recruitment Consultant, Recruitment Operations on +61 2 8627 7137 or by email helen.efstathiou@sydney.edu.au   © The University of Sydney   The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Click to view the Position Description for this role. Applications Close Sunday 15 November 2026 11:59 PM"
    },
    "https://usyd.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/USYD_EXTERNAL_CAREER_SITE/job/Eveleigh/Program-Manager-in-Radiotherapy-Innovations_0147357-1": {
      "title": "Research Fellow/Senior Research Fellow - Program Coordination, Radiotherapy Innovations",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-15\nFull time\n\nJoin an internationally leading research program improving cancer outcomes through equitable next generation radiotherapy technology development Located at South Eveleigh Biomedical building in the Faculty of Medicine and Health Four-year full time fixed term contract, with a base salary of $132,533-$162,347 p.a., plus leave loading and a 17% superannuation     About the opportunity   Radiotherapy is used in the care of around half of all people with cancer, yet many patients experience side effects that significantly reduce quality of life. While emerging technologies can substantially reduce these effects, access is often limited by cost, complexity, or infrastructure. The Image X Institute has been awarded a major Translational Program Grant to address this challenge by developing and translating scalable, low-cost radiotherapy and imaging innovations with the potential to improve outcomes and reduce inequities in cancer care. We are seeking a Research Fellow/Senior Research Fellow - Program Coordination, Radiotherapy Innovations (Level B/C) to support the successful delivery of this ambitious, multi‑year research program. Working closely with the Program Director ( Professor Paul Keall ), Chief Investigators, clinical partners, and stakeholders, you will conduct research on a theme of the program and provide program‑level coordination, governance support, and operational leadership. As Program Manager, you will play a central role in the Program’s research, as well as taking a lead role in the coordinating the delivery of the Program, ensuring activities progress in line with funding requirements, milestones, and timelines. This role sits at the interface of academic leadership, research operations, and stakeholder engagement, and contributes directly to the Program’s long‑term impact goals. This role suits a researcher who combines strong organisational capability with experience in research programs and a commitment to translation and impact.     Your key responsibilities will be to:   Manage the research program, develop and maintain program plans, milestones, meetings, schedules, and risk registers, liaise with key stakeholders Perform research in medical physics, working diligently to develop and translate scalable, low-cost radiotherapy and imaging innovations with the potential to improve outcomes and reduce inequities in cancer care. The duties may include: -clinical-trial involvement -algorithm development -data analysis Contribute to written materials, reports, presentations, and communications Mentor junior academic staff and students as necessary to achieve the program objectives.   About you:   Ph.D. in a relevant discipline (e.g. Medical Physics, Radiology, Computer Science, Physics, Mathematics, Medicine, or Engineering) Demonstrated experience or knowledge of one or more of the following: computational algorithm development medical imaging expertise medical device development clinical trials A demonstrated ability to conduct research/scholarly activities with high quality publications Experience supporting or managing research projects or programs ·       Excellent interpersonal and communication skills, and the ability to work effectively with diverse stakeholders   Sponsorship / work rights for Australia   Please note: Visa sponsorship is not available for this position. For a continuing position, you must be an Australian or New Zealand citizen or an Australian Permanent Resident.   Australian Temporary Residents currently employed at the University of Sydney may be considered for a fixed term contract for the length of their visa, depending on the requirements of the hiring area and the reasons for the position.     Pre-employment checks   Your employment is conditional upon the completion of all role required pre-employment or background checks in terms satisfactory to the University. Similarly, your ongoing employment is conditional upon the satisfactory maintenance of all relevant clearances and background check requirements. If you do not meet these conditions, the University may take any necessary step, including the termination of your employment.     EEO statement     At the University of Sydney, our shared values include diversity and inclusion and we strive to be a place where everyone can thrive. We are committed to creating a University community which reflects the wider community that we serve. We deliver on this commitment through our people and culture programs, as well as key strategies to increase participation and support the careers of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander People , women , people living with a disability , people from culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds , and those who identify as LGBTIQ . We welcome applications from candidates from all backgrounds.    How to apply   Applications (including a cover letter, CV, and any additional supporting documentation) can be submitted via the Apply button at the top of the page.    For employees of the University or contingent workers, please login into your Workday account and navigate to the Career icon on your Dashboard.  Click on USYD Find Jobs and apply.   For a confidential discussion about the role, or if you require reasonable adjustment or any documents in alternate formats, please contact Rachel Ann Yazigi, Recruitment Operations by email to rachel.yazigi@sydney.edu.au.   © The University of Sydney   The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Click to view the Position Description for this role. Applications Close Sunday 01 November 2026 11:59 PM"
    },
    "https://usyd.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/USYD_EXTERNAL_CAREER_SITE/job/Darlington-Campus/Postdoctoral-Research-Associate-in-Fungal-Process-Systems-Engineering-and-AI_0157269-2": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Research Associate in Fungal Process Systems Engineering and AI",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-15\nFull time\n\nFull time, fixed term until 30 June 2028 Shape the future of sustainable plastics through AI and biotechnology Base Salary $117,936 - $125,896 + 17% superannuation    About the opportunity   The School of Chemical and Biomolecular Engineering at the University of Sydney, established in 1946, offered the first university-level chemical engineering program in Australia. The School is committed to teaching excellence and our graduates are highly regarded and sought after by employers in Australia and overseas. The School is research-intensive and considered a world leader in many cutting edge areas of chemical and biomolecular engineering.   We are currently seeking a Postdoctoral Research Associate to work under the supervision of Professor Ali Abbas, to advance fungal process systems engineering for plastics biodegradation and circular bioprocesses. The role combines mechanistic modelling and AI with laboratory validation, including developing self-driving laboratory workflows for autonomous discovery and process optimisation.   The appointee will also coordinate the day-to-day operation of the research group under Professor Abbas’s direction, supporting research planning, laboratory operations, team coordination and delivery of the group’s approved research activities.   In this role you will: develop and validate mechanistic and hybrid AI models of fungal growth, polymer degradation and bioreactor performance. apply machine learning and optimisation to guide experiments and improve fungal process performance. design and undertake laboratory experiments with biological and analytical collaborators to validate computational predictions. develop automated workflows connecting experimental design, execution, data analysis and model updating. contribute to publications, reproducible code and datasets, and project reporting. coordinate day-to-day research group activities, including work plans, group meetings, milestone tracking and follow-up of agreed actions. coordinate laboratory access, equipment readiness, consumables and experimental scheduling with relevant technical staff, supporting safe and efficient operations. support research students and junior researchers through practical mentoring, onboarding and coordination of experimental and computational activities. assist the CI with project reporting, publication coordination, funding applications and partner engagement within the approved research scope. undertake teaching and education related activities in accordance with the Faculty of Engineering local area workload policy requirements for research focused academic staff.   The University values courage and creativity; openness and engagement; inclusion and diversity; and respect and integrity. As such, we see the importance of recruiting talent aligned to these values and are looking for a Postdoctoral Research Associate who has:   a completed PhD in chemical engineering, biotechnology, computational science or a related discipline. demonstrated experience in mechanistic modelling, numerical simulation or optimisation of biological or process systems. experience applying AI or machine learning to scientific problems, with proficiency in Python or equivalent programming tools. experience in experimental design and validating computational predictions against laboratory data research outputs appropriate to career stage and opportunity. desirable experience in fungal (or other microbial) biotechnology, plastics biodegradation, laboratory automation or autonomous experimentation. demonstrated experience coordinating research activities or laboratory workflows, including supporting researchers and tracking project deliverables. desirable experience in tertiary level teaching and education related activities.     Pre-employment checks and declarations     Your employment is conditional upon the successful completion of all pre-employment or background checks required for the role in terms satisfactory to the University.  Also, to meet the University’s obligations under the National Higher Education Code to Prevent and Eliminate Gender-Based Violence you will be asked to declare if you have been investigated for, or found to engaged in, sexual harm or gender-based violence in the course of previous employment or in a legal process. Similarly, your ongoing employment is conditional upon the satisfactory maintenance of all relevant clearances and background check requirements. If you do not meet these conditions, the University may take any necessary step, including the termination of your employment.     EEO statement     At the University of Sydney, our shared values are trust, accountability and excellence and we strive to be a place where everyone can thrive. We are committed to creating a University community that thrives through diversity and reflects the wider community that we serve. We deliver on this through our commitment to diversity and inclusion , evidenced by our people and culture programs, as well as key strategies to increase participation and support the careers of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander People , women , people living with a disability , people from culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds , and those who identify as LGBTQIA+ . We welcome applications from candidates from all backgrounds.    We are proud to be recognised as an Australian Workplace Equality Index (AWEI) Platinum Employer. Find out more about our work on diversity and inclusion .    How to apply   Applications (including a cover letter, CV, and any additional supporting documentation) can be submitted via the Apply button at the top of the page.     For employees of the University or contingent workers, please login into your Workday account and navigate to the Career icon on your Dashboard.  Click on USYD Find Jobs and apply.   For a confidential discussion about the role, or if you require reasonable adjustment or support filling out this application, please contact Rebecca Astar or Cherie Goodwin, Recruitment Consultants at recruitment.sea@sydney.edu.au   © The University of Sydney   The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Click to view the Position Description for this role. Applications Close Friday 13 November 2026 11:59 PM"
    },
    "https://usyd.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/USYD_EXTERNAL_CAREER_SITE/job/Eveleigh/Research-Associate_0158312-1": {
      "title": "Research Associate",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-15\nFull time\n\nFull time, 12 month fixed term Contribute to the use of AI in medical imaging Base Salary $ 117,936 - $125,896 (Academic level A) + 17% superannuation    About the opportunity   We have an opportunity for a Postdoctoral Research Associate (Level A) in Clinical Imaging to join a successful and supportive research team to work on a research project harnessing the power of artificial-intelligence (AI) to improve radiation therapy treatment for cancer patients. The Postdoctoral Research Associate Clinical Imaging will build and clinically translate new cancer targeting technologies based on Artificial Intelligence and x-ray imaging that solve the challenges of complex patient motion during radiation therapy.   Based at South Eveleigh, the Image X Institute’s research portfolio spans development of new treatment ideas, clinical trials, preclinical imaging, device development, radiation therapy physics, and computational physics. We engage with industry, hospitals, international collaborators and universities to forge relationships that help take our projects from lab bench to patient bedside. Enjoy working in a supportive environment with access to unique medical data sets and a chance to make practice changing impacts in real time image guidance for cancer radiotherapy.   Your key responsibilities will be to:   advance the algorithms of imaging and targeted radiation therapy create, develop and experimentally and clinically implement AI based algorithms support the acquisition and analysis of clinical data, and imaging, software and equipment infrastructure contribute to scientific publications, research dissemination and the development of competitive funding proposals undertake clinical site visits to support technology installation, testing and implementation, as required.   About you completion or nearing completion of PhD in physics, computer science, applied mathematics or biomedical engineering or relevant discipline demonstrated track record of publishing research findings in peer-reviewed scientific journals knowledge of software and mathematical algorithm development, especially in artificial intelligence some experience supporting the clinical integration, implementation and translation of research technologies into clinical practice. some experience collaborating with multidisciplinary teams, including radiation oncologists, medical physicists, engineers and other clinical professionals.   Sponsorship / work rights for Australia You must have unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this employment to be eligible to apply. Visa sponsorship is not available for this appointment.      Pre-employment checks   Your employment is conditional upon the completion of all role required pre-employment or background checks in terms satisfactory to the University. Similarly, your ongoing employment is conditional upon the satisfactory maintenance of all relevant clearances and background check requirements. If you do not meet these conditions, the University may take any necessary step, including the termination of your employment.   EEO statement     At the University of Sydney, our shared values are trust, accountability and excellence and we strive to be a place where everyone can thrive. We are committed to creating a University community that thrives through diversity and reflects the wider community that we serve. We deliver on this through our commitment to diversity and inclusion , evidenced by our people and culture programs, as well as key strategies to increase participation and support the careers of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander People , women , people living with a disability , people from culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds , and those who identify as LGBTQIA+ . We welcome applications from candidates from all backgrounds.    We are proud to be recognised as an Australian Workplace Equality Index (AWEI) Platinum Employer. Find out more about our work on diversity and inclusion .    How to apply   Applications (including a cover letter, CV, and any additional supporting documentation) can be submitted via the Apply button at the top of the page.     For employees of the University or contingent workers, please login into your Workday account and navigate to the Career icon on your Dashboard.  Click on USYD Find Jobs and apply.   For a confidential discussion about the role, or if you require reasonable adjustment or any documents in alternate formats, please contact Lena Germinarios , Recruitment Operations by email to lena.germinarios@sydney.edu.au .   © The University of Sydney   The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Click to view the Position Description for this role. Applications Close Sunday 01 November 2026 11:59 PM"
    },
    "https://usyd.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/USYD_EXTERNAL_CAREER_SITE/job/Darlington-Campus/Sustainability-Lead--Climate-and-Circularity-_0158422-1": {
      "title": "Sustainability Lead (Climate and Circularity)",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-15\nFull time\n\nFull-time, fixed term until 31st December 2029 Located on Camperdown Campus on Gadigal Country Base salary from $131,108 plus 17% superannuation About the team The Sustainability Team sits within the Office of the Senior Deputy Vice-Chancellor and Provost and leads the University’s environmental sustainability agenda; translating our institutional Climate and Nature Transition Plan 2026–2032 into practical action, measurable outcomes and lasting change. The Climate and Nature Transition Plan (CNTP) is the University’s roadmap for addressing the defining challenges of climate change and nature loss, along with the interconnected challenges that underpin and shape them. It establishes a clear pathway to 2032, with measurable targets, defined actions, accountable owners and transparent reporting. The Sustainability Team plays a central role in enabling delivery of this agenda across the University. Working across operations, research, education, and engagement, the team brings together strategic leadership, specialist sustainability expertise, project and program delivery, data, reporting and compliance, sustainability governance, behaviour change and stakeholder engagement to turn ambition into action, and to embed sustainability into decision-making and everyday practice. The team works collaboratively with academics, professional staff, students, University leadership and external partners to drive meaningful and lasting change. As visible champions for sustainability across the University, the team strives to build the institutional capabilities, behaviours, partnerships and systems needed to embed and scale climate and nature action and deliver meaningful outcomes.   About the opportunity The Sustainability Lead (Climate and Circularity) provides technical leadership and project management expertise to deliver the University’s Climate and Nature Transition Plan. Reporting to the Senior Manager Sustainability (Climate Mitigation and Circularity), the role leads the planning, development and implementation of strategic initiatives that advance the University’s climate mitigation, net zero and circularity priorities. Your key responsibilities will be to: lead the planning, coordination and delivery of complex sustainability projects and programs, managing workplans, risks, performance and milestones to deliver the University's Climate and Nature Transition Plan lead the delivery of the Climate Mitigation and Circularity focus areas of the University's Climate and Nature Transition Plan, driving implementation across the University and monitoring progress against strategic objectives and targets provide technical leadership in climate mitigation and net zero initiatives, including decarbonisation, electrification, renewable energy, energy efficiency, greenhouse gas accounting, carbon management and offsetting lead the development and implementation of the University's circularity program, advancing waste reduction, reuse systems, resource recovery and circular economy principles across University operations and procurement apply behavioural science and engagement approaches to build organisational capability, influence decision-making and embed climate mitigation and circularity practices across the University community build strategic relationships and provide specialist technical advice across the University and with external stakeholders, facilitating project governance, stakeholder forums and collaborative initiatives to support delivery, inform decision-making and achieve sustainability outcomes. About you tertiary qualifications in a relevant field, such as environmental science, environmental management, sustainability, climate science, engineering, resource management, circular economy, law or policy, or a related field, or equivalent experience demonstrated technical expertise across decarbonisation and net zero pathways, energy and electrification, Scope 1, 2 and 3 greenhouse gas accounting and carbon management, including knowledge of TPG24-33 climate-related financial disclosure and experience applying climate and transition risk assessment, scenario analysis and mitigation strategies to support organisational decision-making, reporting and disclosure experience in circular economy principles and practices, including waste prevention, reuse systems, resource recovery and sustainable procurement, with experience developing and implementing circularity initiatives across complex organisations to reduce resource consumption and waste generation experience managing and engaging a diverse group of internal and external stakeholders across a large or complex organisation, including operational and infrastructure teams, technical experts, procurement and supply chain stakeholders, suppliers, government and regulatory organisations, researchers and academic experts experience in leading, managing and developing team members, including fostering a positive, inclusive and high-performance work culture. Sponsorship / work rights for Australia Please note: You must have unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this employment to apply. Visa sponsorship is not available for this appointment. Pre-employment checks and declarations  Your employment is conditional upon the successful completion of all pre-employment or background checks required for the role in terms satisfactory to the University.  Also, to meet the University’s obligations under the National Higher Education Code to Prevent and Eliminate Gender-Based Violence you will be asked to declare if you have been investigated for, or found to engaged in, sexual harm or gender-based violence in the course of previous employment or in a legal process. Similarly, your ongoing employment is conditional upon the satisfactory maintenance of all relevant clearances and background check requirements. If you do not meet these conditions, the University may take any necessary step, including the termination of your employment. EEO statement   At the University of Sydney, our shared values are trust, accountability and excellence and we strive to be a place where everyone can thrive. We are committed to creating a University community that thrives through diversity and reflects the wider community that we serve. We deliver on this through our commitment to diversity and inclusion , evidenced by our people and culture programs, as well as key strategies to increase participation and support the careers of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander People , women , people living with a disability , people from culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds , and those who identify as LGBTIQ+ . We welcome applications from candidates from all backgrounds. We are proud to be recognised as an Australian Workplace Equality Index (AWEI) Gold employer. Find out more about our work on diversity and inclusion .  How to apply Applications can be submitted via the Apply button at the top of the page. Please ensure, as part of your application, you have addressed in a brief statement how your skills, qualifications and experience meet the requirements of the details in the 'about you' section above.   We understand that everyone applies differently. If you’d prefer to have an informal conversation before applying, we welcome you to get in touch so we can talk through the role and process. In addition, if you require reasonable adjustment or any documents in alternate formats, please contact Sade Mintah via sade.mintah@sydney.edu.au   © The University of Sydney The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Click to view the Position Description for this role. Applications Close Sunday 01 November 2026 11:59 PM"
    },
    "https://usyd.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/USYD_EXTERNAL_CAREER_SITE/job/Darlington-Campus/Marketing-and-Communications-Assistant----International---China-_0156266-2": {
      "title": "Marketing and Communications Assistant – (International - China)",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-15\nFull time\n\n·        Full time fixed term until December 2027 ·        Be the driving force behind the Centre’s digital presence and engagement based in Sydney ·        Base Salary $93K + 17% superannuation   About the Opportunity This role will help support the Centre in China by managing digital communications, social media, email communications and digital content. You’ll work with University teams and external partners to develop and deliver engaging communications and impactful campaigns that promote the University’s activities and strengthen engagement with Chinese audiences.   Key Responsibilities ·        create, manage and publish engaging content across WeChat OA, WeChat mini programs, the website and other digital channels ·        develop and coordinate digital communications, including email campaigns, newsletters and targeted communications to key audiences ·        create, adapt and translate engaging digital content, including written content, photography and video ·        support media and communications activities and monitor coverage across China and Australia ·        monitor digital and social media trends and audience insights to inform content and communications strategies ·        coordinate communications projects, manage timelines and stakeholders, and report on campaign and channel performance   About You ·        fluent in both Chinese and English, with strong written and verbal communication skills ·        experience managing Chinese social media platforms, with strong user familiarity and cultural fluency desirable ·        experience developing digital communications and content across channels such as websites, email and social media ·        ability to create, adapt and translate engaging content for diverse audiences ·        strong communication, organisational and project coordination skills ·        proactive approach with the ability to manage multiple communications projects and priorities     Sponsorship / work rights for Australia   You must have unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this employment to be eligible to apply. Visa sponsorship is not available for this appointment.     Pre-employment checks and declarations       Your employment is conditional upon the successful completion of all pre-employment or background checks required for the role in terms satisfactory to the University.  Also, to meet the University’s obligations under the National Higher Education Code to Prevent and Eliminate Gender-Based Violence you will be asked to declare if you have been investigated for, or found to engaged in, sexual harm or gender-based violence in the course of previous employment or in a legal process. Similarly, your ongoing employment is conditional upon the satisfactory maintenance of all relevant clearances and background check requirements. If you do not meet these conditions, the University may take any necessary step, including the termination of your employment.     EEO statement     At the University of Sydney, our shared values are trust, accountability and excellence and we strive to be a place where everyone can thrive. We are committed to creating a University community that thrives through diversity and reflects the wider community that we serve. We deliver on this through our commitment to diversity and inclusion , evidenced by our people and culture programs, as well as key strategies to increase participation and support the careers of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander People , women , people living with a disability , people from culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds , and those who identify as LGBTQIA+ . We welcome applications from candidates from all backgrounds.    We are proud to be recognised as an Australian Workplace Equality Index (AWEI) Platinum Employer. Find out more about our work on diversity and inclusion .    How to apply   Applications (including a cover letter, CV, and any additional supporting documentation) can be submitted via the Apply button at the top of the page.     For employees of the University or contingent workers, please login into your Workday account and navigate to the Career icon on your Dashboard.  Click on USYD Find Jobs and apply.   For a confidential discussion about the role, or if you require reasonable adjustment or any documents in alternate formats, please contact Riviera Son Recruitment Operations by email to riviera.son@sydney.edu.au   © The University of Sydney   The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Click to view the Position Description for this role. Applications Close Wednesday 28 October 2026 11:59 PM"
