    "https://usyd.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/USYD_EXTERNAL_CAREER_SITE/job/Darlington-Campus/Health--Safety-and-Wellbeing-Director-Systems-and-Enablement_0158068-2": {
      "title": "Health, Safety and Wellbeing Director Systems and Enablement",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-14\nFull time\n\nFull-time, continuing opportunity Located on Camperdown Campus on Gadigal Country Competitive remuneration package including 17% superannuation   About the team The Health, Safety & Wellbeing (HSW) team plays a critical role in enabling safe, healthy and sustainable ways of working across the University. Through listening, learning and a commitment to continuous improvement, HSW promotes consistent best practice, frameworks and standards across all faculties and business areas. Together, the team helps shape a culture where health, safety and wellbeing are embedded into everyday work, supporting organisational excellence and long-term success. About the opportunity The HSW Director Systems and Enablement is a senior University leadership role. In this position, you will be accountable for setting and driving the strategic direction of the University’s HSW systems, governance, assurance, reporting, and digital enablement functions. Operating at a senior strategic level with influence extending to University Executive and Senate, the Director shapes a proactive agenda, provides trusted advice to senior leaders, and ensures sustained organisational focus on strategic priorities, risk management, and performance. The role plays a critical leadership function in embedding a cohesive and forward-looking approach to health, safety and wellbeing risks across the institution. Your key responsibilities will be to: lead the design and development of HSW Systems and Enablement function strategic objectives and goals for the University team leadership and budget management set and evolve the HSW Systems and Enablement operating model, ensuring clarity of roles, service offerings, and integration across systems, governance, assurance, and reporting functions set and evolve the governance and reporting framework for the HSW function lead the University wide assurance/audit program provide strategic oversight and direction for HSW systems and digital enablement and continuous improvement lead the University’s HSW Program Management activities. This role will suit a contemporary HSW leader who combines strong systems and governance capability with the ability to set direction and lead digital and technology enabled change, while bringing people with them through complex organisational change.   About you graduate degree/ professional qualifications or equivalent experience in a WHS or related discipline extensive experience within Work Health and Safety field with a demonstrated experience of relevant legislation and policies demonstrated leadership in developing an effective and sustainable safety culture and conceptualising, developing and implementing practical compliance and safety programs extensive leadership experience leading multi-disciplinary Work Health and Safety specialist professionals within large, complex and highly regulated organisations demonstrated experience in applying H&S Safety Management strategies in complex environments advanced knowledge of Risk Management practices and the application of government legislation, codes of practice and Australian standards advanced knowledge of relevant Australian Safety Standards as well as relevant Safe Work Australia, SafeWork NSW and Workcover publications extensive experience with the development of HSMS systems and provision of technical supporting tools. Sponsorship / work rights for Australia Please note: You must have unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this employment to apply. Visa sponsorship is not available for this appointment. How to apply The Safe Step is assisting the University of Sydney with this appointment. Applications (including a cover letter, CV, and any additional supporting documentation) can be submitted via the Apply button at the top of the page. For further information and a confidential conversation about the role please contact Stephen Coldicutt on scoldicutt@thesafestep.com.au or 02 8256 2529. Pre-employment checks and declarations Your employment is conditional upon the successful completion of all pre-employment or background checks required for the role in terms satisfactory to the University.  Also, to meet the University’s obligations under the National Higher Education Code to Prevent and Eliminate Gender-Based Violence you will be asked to declare if you have been investigated for, or found to engaged in, sexual harm or gender-based violence in the course of previous employment or in a legal process. Similarly, your ongoing employment is conditional upon the satisfactory maintenance of all relevant clearances and background check requirements. If you do not meet these conditions, the University may take any necessary step, including the termination of your employment. EEO statement At the University of Sydney, our shared values are trust, accountability and excellence and we strive to be a place where everyone can thrive. We are committed to creating a University community that thrives through diversity and reflects the wider community that we serve. We deliver on this through our commitment to diversity and inclusion , evidenced by our people and culture programs, as well as key strategies to increase participation and support the careers of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander People , women , people living with a disability , people from culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds , and those who identify as LGBTIQ+ . We welcome applications from candidates from all backgrounds. We are proud to be recognised as an Australian Workplace Equality Index (AWEI) Gold employer. Find out more about our work on diversity and inclusion . © The University of Sydney The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Click to view the Position Description for this role. Applications Close Sunday 01 November 2026 11:59 PM"
    },
    "https://usyd.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/USYD_EXTERNAL_CAREER_SITE/job/Darlington-Campus/Solution-Designer_0146190-2": {
      "title": "Solution Designer",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-14\nFull time\n\nWork across applications, integrations, cloud,   infrastructure   and security in a varied solution design role   Help shape technology solutions that support the University’s teaching,   research   and strategic priorities   Salary   $131,108   to   $147,858   + 17% Superannuation   Are you an experienced Solution Designer who enjoys turning complex business needs into practical technology solutions? Do you thrive at the intersection of business and ICT, working with stakeholders and technical teams to design solutions that can be successfully delivered?   Solve by Talent is partnering with the University of Sydney to recruit a Solution Designer to develop robust and practical technology solutions across key ICT and   university   initiatives. Working within the ICT Solution Architecture team, you will help shape solutions that support the University’s strategic   and operational   priorities   to suppor t its core   education   and research   goals   This is an exciting opportunity to contribute to a broad range of strategic   and operational   projects while working across a large and complex technology environment within one of the world’s leading universities.   Why join the University of Sydney?   Join one of Australia’s leading universities at a pivotal point of change, where technology is evolving to better support student learning, empower teaching excellence, and enable research delivery at global scale.       ICT is central to delivering the University’s academic mission. Working across faculties, disciplines, and research communities   and operational teams , you will   contribute to   the platforms, services, and partnerships that enable student success, strengthen teaching, and accelerate research impact.       This work spans both the critical services that keep the University running every day and the ongoing improvement of systems and experiences.       With strong investment and clear direction   in a digital   stratgey ,   you’ll   operate   in a large and complex environment where your contribution   is part of a   visible, meaningful impact across the University.       You’ll   collaborate across academic and professional communities to help shape a more connected and capable University, supporting teaching excellence, research impact, and student success.     The Opportunity   As a Solution Designer, you will work from business and project requirements to   determine   practical technology solutions within the University’s enterprise architecture standards and guardrails. You will develop and document high-level and detailed solution designs, work closely with business stakeholders and technical specialists, and provide technical leadership throughout solution delivery.   You will collaborate across application, infrastructure, cloud,   security   and other   technology   teams, preparing designs for stakeholder and governance review and helping delivery teams implement approved solutions. You will also contribute to solution design standards,   templates   and strategies, and share knowledge across the team.   You’ll   join a collaborative and supportive environment where diverse perspectives are valued, with flexible working arrangements and opportunities to contribute to   impactful   projects .   This role is ideal for a technically strong and collaborative professional who can translate business needs into practical solutions, communicate confidently with a wide range of stakeholders, and manage competing priorities in a complex project environment.   What   you’ll   do   Develop high-level and detailed solution designs for ICT and business initiatives   Work with business stakeholders and project teams to determine   appropriate solution   options from agreed requirements   Design and document how applications, integrations and technology components work together   Collaborate with application specialists, infrastructure, cloud   operations   and security teams throughout solution delivery   Prepare solution designs for stakeholder,   governance   and peer review, and communicate risks,   issues   and decisions   Provide technical leadership and guidance to delivery teams throughout projects   Contribute to solution design standards,   templates   and strategies, while mentoring and sharing knowledge with other team members   What   we’re   looking for   Proven experience developing and documenting high-level and detailed solution designs   Strong understanding of applications, application integration, technology   stacks   and cloud environments   Understanding of non-functional requirements and how they influence solution design, including security considerations   Good   communication   skills with   the ability to work effectively across business and technical teams   Experience working across complex ICT projects and managing competing priorities within agreed   timeframes   Experience working in both agile and waterfall delivery environments, with a relevant postgraduate qualification and/or equivalent combination of training and experience   W illingness to   adapt   to , and   apply   new   and emerging   techniques   and skills   in your   work   practices .     What’s   in it for you?   At the University of Sydney, we support our staff with a range of benefits designed to enhance your career,   wellbeing   and work–life balance, including:   17% superannuation   to support your financial future   G enerous parental leave entitlements.   Competitive salary with salary packaging options   Flexible and hybrid working   arrangements   Career development and learning opportunities   Health and wellbeing programs including   Fitness Passport   Access to a vibrant, globally recognised university community   How to apply   If you're looking for an opportunity to contribute to meaningful work while building your career in a world-class university environment, we would love to hear from you.  Applications (including a cover letter, CV, and any additional supporting documentation) can be submitted via the  Apply  button at the top of the page.    For employees of the University or contingent workers, please login into your  Workday  account and navigate to the Career icon on your Dashboard.  Click on USYD Find Jobs and apply. Accessibility and inclusion  The University of Sydney is committed to creating an inclusive recruitment experience for all candidates. If you require adjustments or additional support at any stage of the recruitment process, we encourage you to let us know.  This may include adjustments to the interview format, additional time, breaks, accessible interview locations, receiving materials in advance, or any other practical support that will help you participate comfortably.  For a confidential discussion about the role, or if you require reasonable adjustment or any documents in alternate formats, please contact  camila.garcia@sydney.edu.au  to learn more about this role.  ​   EEO statement       At the University of Sydney, our shared values are  trust, accountability and excellence  and we strive to be a place where everyone can thrive. We are committed to creating a University community that thrives through diversity and reflects the wider community that we serve. We deliver on this through our commitment to  diversity and inclusion , evidenced by our people and culture programs, as well as key strategies to increase participation and support the careers of  Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander People ,  women ,  people living with a disability ,  people from culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds , and those who identify as  LGBTIQ+ . We welcome applications from candidates from all backgrounds.       We are proud to be recognised as an Australian Workplace Equality Index (AWEI) Gold employer.  Find out more about our work on diversity and inclusion .     Pre-employment checks  Your employment is conditional upon the completion of all role required pre-employment or background checks in terms satisfactory to the University.  Also, to meet the University’s obligations under the National Higher Education Code to Prevent and Eliminate Gender-Based Violence you will be asked to declare if you have been investigated for, or found to engaged in, sexual harm or gender-based violence in the course of previous employment or in a legal process.  Sponsorship / work rights for Australia  You must have unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this employment to be eligible to apply. Visa sponsorship is not available for this appointment.  The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Click to view the Position Description for this role. Applications Close Wednesday 28 October 2026 11:59 PM"
    },
    "https://usyd.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/USYD_EXTERNAL_CAREER_SITE/job/Darlington-Campus/Specialist-Health-and-Wellbeing_0158080-1": {
      "title": "Specialist Health and Wellbeing",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-14\nFull time\n\nFull-time, continuing opportunity Located on Camperdown Campus on Gadigal Country Base Salary from $131,108 plus 17% superannuation About the team   The Health, Safety & Wellbeing (HSW) team plays a critical role in enabling safe, healthy and sustainable ways of working across the University. Through listening, learning and a commitment to continuous improvement, HSW promotes consistent best practice, frameworks and standards across all faculties and business areas. Together, the team helps shape a culture where health, safety and wellbeing are embedded into everyday work, supporting organisational excellence and long-term success. About the opportunity As Specialist Health and Wellbeing , you will lead the end-to-end management of health and wellbeing activities and program initiatives. Working in close partnership with the Psychosocial Safety, Health and Wellbeing, HSW Systems and Enablement and HSW Partnering teams, this role will ensure health and wellbeing programs and initiatives are aligned with enterprise HSMS frameworks, effectively translated into communication, systems and tools, and consistently embedded across operational areas. In this role you will support the development and delivery of preventative based tools, resources and initiatives that form part of the Health and Wellbeing Program, and support local area implementation and embedding activities, ensuring robust awareness and engagement across Faculties, Schools, Portfolios and Business Units. Your key responsibilities will be to: design, develop and coordinate health and wellbeing education programs and initiatives manage the University Wellbeing Champion Network manage the coordination, communication and implementation of all learning and development offerings that form part of the Health and Wellbeing Program coordinate and promote health promotion initiatives, managing associated partnerships and contracts with external providers provide subject matter expertise into psychosocial, health and wellbeing communications, building awareness of offerings across the University community partner with HSW teams to embed psychosocial, health and wellbeing offerings across diverse University portfolios. About you Graduate qualifications in psychology, organisational development, learning and development, or occupational health and safety oy or equivalent combination of training and experience  experience managing multiple projects and activities according to agreed deliverables experience designing end-end initiatives from conceptualisation through to implementation demonstrated experience in the preparation and provision of responsive, clear, and concise specialist advice, recommendations, and solutions both verbally and in writing workshop facilitation, including experience presenting information in non-technical terms to a wide variety of end-users with differing needs demonstrated understanding of relevant legislation, Australian standards and guidelines. Sponsorship / work rights for Australia Please note: You must have unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this employment to apply. Visa sponsorship is not available for this appointment. Pre-employment checks and declarations  Your employment is conditional upon the successful completion of all pre-employment or background checks required for the role in terms satisfactory to the University.  Also, to meet the University’s obligations under the National Higher Education Code to Prevent and Eliminate Gender-Based Violence you will be asked to declare if you have been investigated for, or found to engaged in, sexual harm or gender-based violence in the course of previous employment or in a legal process. Similarly, your ongoing employment is conditional upon the satisfactory maintenance of all relevant clearances and background check requirements. If you do not meet these conditions, the University may take any necessary step, including the termination of your employment. EEO statement   At the University of Sydney, our shared values are trust, accountability and excellence and we strive to be a place where everyone can thrive. We are committed to creating a University community that thrives through diversity and reflects the wider community that we serve. We deliver on this through our commitment to diversity and inclusion , evidenced by our people and culture programs, as well as key strategies to increase participation and support the careers of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander People , women , people living with a disability , people from culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds , and those who identify as LGBTIQ+ . We welcome applications from candidates from all backgrounds. We are proud to be recognised as an Australian Workplace Equality Index (AWEI) Gold employer. Find out more about our work on diversity and inclusion .  How to apply Applications (including a cover letter, CV, and any additional supporting documentation) can be submitted via the Apply button at the top of the page.  We understand that everyone applies differently. If you’d prefer to have an informal conversation before applying, we welcome you to get in touch so we can talk through the role and process. In addition, if you require reasonable adjustment or any documents in alternate formats, please contact Sade Mintah via sade.mintah@sydney.edu.au   © The University of Sydney  The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Click to view the Position Description for this role. Applications Close Sunday 01 November 2026 11:59 PM"
    },
    "https://usyd.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/USYD_EXTERNAL_CAREER_SITE/job/Camperdown-Campus/Deputy-Dean--Research-_0158401-3": {
      "title": "Deputy Dean (Research)",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-14\nFull time\n\nLocated in Camperdown 5 year FIXED-TERM position with an attractive remuneration package About the opportunity The Faculty of Arts and Social Sciences (FASS) at the University of Sydney has commenced its search for the key leadership role of Deputy Dean (Research). The new Deputy Dean (Research) will be joining at a key time, as the University enters the second phase of its ten-year strategic cycle (Sydney in 2032) and the Faculty progresses its own second strategic plan. The new Deputy Dean (Research) will work alongside the Dean, Professor Lisa Adkins, as FASS looks to consolidate its position as a world-leading centre for research and education across the humanities, arts, and social sciences. The Faculty of Arts and Social Sciences at Sydney is the oldest and one of the most comprehensive of its kind in Australia. The Faculty remains proud of its past while focused on the future. The Faculty’s distinctiveness lies in the depth and breadth of its disciplines, with its many research and teaching strengths earning it a deserved reputation for excellence nationally and internationally. The Faculty’s research is driven by the pursuit of multidisciplinary knowledge that benefits Australia and the wider world. In 2025 we welcomed two new flagship centres, the Centre for AI, Trust and Governance, which advances responsible AI development through multidisciplinary research, fosters public trust, and informs policy decisions in complex regulatory environments; and the Contemporary Inequalities Centre, which brings together multidisciplinary expertise to pursue collaborative, problem-driven research, aiming to respond to emerging social and economic challenges to build the evidence base needed to shape new policy responses. Our other flagship research centres include the Vere Gordon Childe Centre, and the Sydney Centre for Healthy Societies. Other research centres, institutes, and groups in the Faculty include the Sydney Institute for Community Languages Education, the CREATE Centre, and the Sydney Social Sciences and Humanities Advanced Research Centre (SSSHARC). For our students, and in collaboration with our partners, the Faculty offers internships and work placements, overseas exchange and interdisciplinary collaboration around complex, real-world problems. As we mature our connections with industry, community and government, the Faculty is seeking to establish anchoring partnerships operating across its research and education portfolios.    About you (skills, capabilities and experience) The Deputy Dean (Research) is a senior academic leader responsible for the Faculty of Arts and Social Sciences research portfolio and for achieving the strategic and operational objectives in research set out in the Faculty’s strategic plan . Working in partnership with the Faculty’s Head of Research and Development, and as a key member of the Faculty leadership team, the role provides strategic leadership for the Faculty’s research agenda, driving the implementation of key research initiatives and aligning priorities, investment and capability with broader University objectives. Working closely with the Dean and key stakeholders across the Faculty, the Deputy Dean (Research) fosters a high-performing, inclusive and collaborative research culture that supports excellence, innovation and interdisciplinary impact. The role provides oversight of research centres, research infrastructure, and research performance. It strengthens research capability and capacity, supports career development, and champions research integrity and responsible research practices. As a senior representative of the Faculty, the Deputy Dean (Research) will build strong relationships across the University and with industry, government, community and global partners, identifying opportunities for funding, collaboration, translation and impact while ensuring the Faculty’s research activities remain strategically aligned, financially sustainable and positioned for long-term success. The appointee will hold a doctoral level qualification in a FASS discipline and have an excellent track record in research and research funding. They will have a proven record in research leadership at scale with demonstrated achievements in the implementation of research strategy and research performance uplift. Sponsorship / work rights for Australia Visa sponsorship and relocation assistance will be provided if required. Term and remuneration The term of the appointment is five years on a full-time basis. The successful candidate will also be offered a professorial appointment in an appropriate discipline in the Faculty and will retain that position at the conclusion of their Deputy Deanship. The successful candidate will be offered a competitive remuneration package commensurate with the responsibilities of the position and the candidate’s relevant experience and qualifications. Pre-employment checks Your employment is conditional upon the successful completion of all pre-employment or background checks required for the role in terms satisfactory to the University.  Also, to meet the University’s obligations under the National Higher Education Code to Prevent and Eliminate Gender-Based Violence you will be asked to declare if you have been investigated for, or found to engaged in, sexual harm or gender-based violence in the course of previous employment or in a legal process. Similarly, your ongoing employment is conditional upon the satisfactory maintenance of all relevant clearances and background check requirements. If you do not meet these conditions, the University may take any necessary step, including the termination of your employment. EEO statement   At the University of Sydney, our shared values are trust, accountability and excellence and we strive to be a place where everyone can thrive. We are committed to creating a University community that thrives through diversity and reflects the wider community that we serve. We deliver on this through our commitment to diversity and inclusion , evidenced by our people and culture programs, as well as key strategies to increase participation and support the careers of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander People , women , people living with a disability , people from culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds , and those who identify as LGBTQIA+ . We welcome applications from candidates from all backgrounds.  We are proud to be recognised as an Australian Workplace Equality Index (AWEI) Platinum Employer. Find out more about our work on diversity and inclusion .  How to apply Applications ( including a CV and cover letter outlining how your skills, capabilities and experience meet the requirements for the role ) can be submitted via the Apply button at the top of the page. Please refer to the candidate information brochure/position description for further details. Candidate Brochure: Candidate Brochure DDR Click to view the Position Description for this role. For employees of the University or contingent workers, please login into your Workday account and navigate to the Career icon on your Dashboard.  Click on USYD Find Jobs and apply. For a confidential discussion about the role, or if you require reasonable adjustment or any documents in alternate formats, please contact Jackie Besley from Devereaux Consulting at FASSResearch@devereauxconsulting.com © The University of Sydney The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Applications Close Tuesday 10 November 2026 11:59 PM"
    },
    "https://usyd.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/USYD_EXTERNAL_CAREER_SITE/job/Darlington-Campus/Senior-Specialist-Psychosocial-Safety_0158081-3": {
      "title": "Senior Specialist Psychosocial Safety",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-14\nFull time\n\nFull-time, continuing opportunity Located on Camperdown Campus on Gadigal Country Base Salary from $152,045 plus 17% superannuation About the team   The Health, Safety & Wellbeing (HSW) team plays a critical role in enabling safe, healthy and sustainable ways of working across the University. Through listening, learning and a commitment to continuous improvement, HSW promotes consistent best practice, frameworks and standards across all faculties and business areas. Together, the team helps shape a culture where health, safety and wellbeing are embedded into everyday work, supporting organisational excellence and long-term success. About the opportunity As Senior Specialist, Psychosocial Safety , you will provide expert advice, guidance, and support across all aspects of the University’s operations and academic activities. With a strong focus on complex matters and critical risks, you will work collaboratively across functions including HSW Partnering, HR Partnering and Staff Health Support, to support the effective management and mitigation of identified psychosocial risks. This includes coaching local leaders to strengthen safety controls and embed practices that prevent future harm. Working closely with the HSW Partnering team, you will ensure proportionate risk mitigation is provided to Faculties, Schools, Portfolios and Business Units, uplifting partnering capability in how they support their client groups. In collaboration with the Psychosocial Safety, Health and Wellbeing team, you will deliver scalable prevention initiatives that support risk management and connect business areas with relevant support and solutions. Your key responsibilities will be to: provide expert psychosocial safety advice on complex matters and critical incidents analyse and interpret data to deliver meaningful and easily translatable insights that support local areas to proactively manage psychosocial risk continually mature the Health and Safety Management System (HSMS) in line with emerging legislative standards and best practice partner with HSW Teams and leadership to embed the psychosocial HSMS architecture across diverse University portfolios support institutional reporting and leadership capability uplift foster a comprehensive, systems-based approach to safety through robust worker consultation, strong cross functional relationships, and regulatory alignment. About you post-graduate qualifications in Psychology or Occupational Health and Safety or an equivalent combination of training and experience accreditation / registration with a relevant health or wellbeing professional association / organisation, or legislative body expert knowledge of risk management practices and the application of government legislation, codes of practice and Australian standards as they relate to psychosocial risk project management experience and ability to work on multiple projects and activities according to agreed schedules, timelines, and deliverables with minimal supervision and to prepare progress reports for management, project managers and clients demonstrated experience at a senior level in the preparation and provision of responsive, clear, and concise specialist advice, recommendations, and solutions both verbally and in writing, through effective research, negotiation, and consultation with key stakeholders demonstrated experience in developing an effective and sustainable safety culture and of conceptualising, developing, and implementing practical compliance and safety programs demonstrated experience in providing leadership, mentoring and guidance to senior staff to enable them to lead cultural and behavioural change. Sponsorship / work rights for Australia Please note: You must have unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this employment to apply. Visa sponsorship is not available for this appointment. Pre-employment checks and declarations  Your employment is conditional upon the successful completion of all pre-employment or background checks required for the role in terms satisfactory to the University.  Also, to meet the University’s obligations under the National Higher Education Code to Prevent and Eliminate Gender-Based Violence you will be asked to declare if you have been investigated for, or found to engaged in, sexual harm or gender-based violence in the course of previous employment or in a legal process. Similarly, your ongoing employment is conditional upon the satisfactory maintenance of all relevant clearances and background check requirements. If you do not meet these conditions, the University may take any necessary step, including the termination of your employment. EEO statement   At the University of Sydney, our shared values are trust, accountability and excellence and we strive to be a place where everyone can thrive. We are committed to creating a University community that thrives through diversity and reflects the wider community that we serve. We deliver on this through our commitment to diversity and inclusion , evidenced by our people and culture programs, as well as key strategies to increase participation and support the careers of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander People , women , people living with a disability , people from culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds , and those who identify as LGBTIQ+ . We welcome applications from candidates from all backgrounds. We are proud to be recognised as an Australian Workplace Equality Index (AWEI) Gold employer. Find out more about our work on diversity and inclusion .  How to apply Applications (including a cover letter, CV, and any additional supporting documentation) can be submitted via the Apply button at the top of the page.  We understand that everyone applies differently. If you’d prefer to have an informal conversation before applying, we welcome you to get in touch so we can talk through the role and process. In addition, if you require reasonable adjustment or any documents in alternate formats, please contact Sade Mintah via sade.mintah@sydney.edu.au   © The University of Sydney The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Click to view the Position Description for this role. Applications Close Sunday 01 November 2026 11:59 PM"
    },
    "https://usyd.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/USYD_EXTERNAL_CAREER_SITE/job/Darlington-Campus/Development-Director_0157519-1": {
      "title": "Development Director",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-13\nFull time\n\nFull time, continuing role Lead major property development and capital projects, providing strategic leadership, governance and delivery expertise across the University Offering a competitive remuneration package including 17% superannuation About the opportunity   The Development Director is responsible for leading the planning, structuring and delivery of complex property development projects and strategic initiatives across the University. The role provides strategic, commercial and governance leadership across a diverse portfolio of capital and precinct projects, drawing on multidisciplinary expertise to guide projects from feasibility through to delivery.   Your key responsibilities will be:   ·  maximising the value of university property and land assets ·  leadership and people management  ·  strategic development leadership ·  governance and approvals ·  commercial and financial management ·  stakeholder engagement ·  risk, compliance and WHS ·  support leadership of the department in the Director’s absence, including oversight of governance, delivery and stakeholder engagement.     About you   · proven ability to develop and execute long‑term strategies for complex landholdings, including identification, structuring and commercialisation of development opportunities, including structuring of partnership and divestment pathways where appropriate · extensive knowledge and experience in working with large capital works, infrastructure and development projects in a physically dispersed and complex, matrix environment · highly advanced skills in consulting, strategy development, business case development and design of transformational strategies and projects · proven expertise in compliance and risk management in a diverse environment with multiple stakeholders (internal and external) and/or with built environment / construction and/or facilities / capital works · extensive program management experience – including time and project management, prioritisation & multitasking skills to be able to meet strict deadlines across a multitude of complex programs and projects · expertise in contract and procurement management including contract administration and performance management.     Sponsorship / work rights for Australia   You must have unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this employment to be eligible to apply. Visa sponsorship is not available for this appointment.     Pre-employment checks and declarations       Your employment is conditional upon the successful completion of all pre-employment or background checks required for the role in terms satisfactory to the University.  Also, to meet the University’s obligations under the National Higher Education Code to Prevent and Eliminate Gender-Based Violence you will be asked to declare if you have been investigated for, or found to engaged in, sexual harm or gender-based violence in the course of previous employment or in a legal process. Similarly, your ongoing employment is conditional upon the satisfactory maintenance of all relevant clearances and background check requirements. If you do not meet these conditions, the University may take any necessary step, including the termination of your employment.         EEO statement     At the University of Sydney, our shared values are trust, accountability and excellence and we strive to be a place where everyone can thrive. We are committed to creating a University community that thrives through diversity and reflects the wider community that we serve. We deliver on this through our commitment to diversity and inclusion , evidenced by our people and culture programs, as well as key strategies to increase participation and support the careers of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander People , women , people living with a disability , people from culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds , and those who identify as LGBTQIA+ . We welcome applications from candidates from all backgrounds.    We are proud to be recognised as an Australian Workplace Equality Index (AWEI) Platinum Employer. Find out more about our work on diversity and inclusion .    How to apply   Applications (including a cover letter, CV, and any additional supporting documentation) can be submitted via the Apply button at the top of the page.     For employees of the University or contingent workers, please login into your Workday account and navigate to the Career icon on your Dashboard.  Click on USYD Find Jobs and apply.   For a confidential discussion about the role, or if you require reasonable adjustment or any documents in alternate formats, please contact Liz Mackinlay, Recruitment Operations by email to   liz.mackinlay@sydney.edu.au   © The University of Sydney   The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment.   Click to view the Position Description for this role. Applications Close Tuesday 27 October 2026 11:59 PM"
    },
    "https://usyd.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/USYD_EXTERNAL_CAREER_SITE/job/Camperdown-Campus/Research-Fellow--Heat-and-Health-Research-Centre_0157988": {
      "title": "Research Fellow- Heat and Health Research Centre",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-13\nFull time\n\nFull-time, 3.5-year fixed-term opportunity Advance research in heat and health Base Salary $132,533 - $157,381 + 17% superannuation     About the opportunity   Join the Heat and Health Research Centre at the University of Sydney and contribute to world-leading research addressing the growing health challenges associated with a warming world.   As a Research Fellow, you will apply and further develop your expertise across research methodology, experimental design and delivery, contributing to innovative research that strengthens the University’s standing as a national leader in the field. Working under broad direction, you will have the opportunity to exercise your subject matter expertise, provide research advice and recommendations, and independently resolve complex issues across your research projects   You will work both independently and collaboratively within a fast-paced multidisciplinary research environment, building partnerships and translating research into real-world outcomes that can inform policy, practice and community health. This role offers an opportunity to develop your research profile while contributing to a collaborative culture committed to research excellence and meaningful impact.   Your key responsibilities will be to:   Lead and contribute to high-quality original research in climate and environmental physiology, including experimental research, physiological assessment, data analysis and statistical modelling. Build and maintain a strong research profile through high-quality publications, conference presentations and other scholarly outputs, independently and collaboratively. Develop research collaborations and partnerships with academic, government, industry and community stakeholders to translate research findings into improved policy, practice and community outcomes. Contribute to competitive research funding applications and support the delivery of research projects that generate meaningful research impact. Apply expertise in experimental and physiological research methods, including venipuncture and blood analysis, to support rigorous, high-quality and innovative research. Contribute to the research culture and strategic priorities of the Heat and Health Research Centre through mentoring, postgraduate supervision, knowledge exchange and active participation in University and centre activities and initiatives.   About you   A PhD in Environmental physiology, physiology, sports and exercise science, with demonstrated experience conducting original research and contributing to high-quality scholarly outputs. Demonstrated expertise in climate and environmental physiology, experimental and physiological assessment, data analysis and statistical modelling, with experience in venipuncture and blood analysis highly regarded. An emerging research profile, including evidence of publications, presentations, research collaborations and a developing network of academic, industry and professional stakeholders. Proven ability to communicate research effectively to academic, industry and broader audiences, together with experience contributing to presentations, forums, seminars or conferences. Demonstrated ability to plan and deliver research activities effectively, while providing supervision, mentoring and guidance to students or less experienced researchers. Proven ability to work collaboratively as part of a multidisciplinary team of researchers, while upholding high professional standards and contributing positively to a collegial and supportive team culture.     Pre-employment checks and declarations     Your employment is conditional upon the successful completion of all pre-employment or background checks required for the role in terms satisfactory to the University.  Also, to meet the University’s obligations under the National Higher Education Code to Prevent and Eliminate Gender-Based Violence you will be asked to declare if you have been investigated for, or found to engaged in, sexual harm or gender-based violence in the course of previous employment or in a legal process. Similarly, your ongoing employment is conditional upon the satisfactory maintenance of all relevant clearances and background check requirements. If you do not meet these conditions, the University may take any necessary step, including the termination of your employment.   This position is designated as involving child-related work. To undertake or remain in this position, you are required to apply for and obtain a Working with Children Check clearance in accordance with the Child Protection (Working with Children) Act 2012.   EEO statement     At the University of Sydney, our shared values are trust, accountability and excellence and we strive to be a place where everyone can thrive. We are committed to creating a University community that thrives through diversity and reflects the wider community that we serve. We deliver on this through our commitment to diversity and inclusion , evidenced by our people and culture programs, as well as key strategies to increase participation and support the careers of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander People , women , people living with a disability , people from culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds , and those who identify as LGBTQIA+ . We welcome applications from candidates from all backgrounds.    We are proud to be recognised as an Australian Workplace Equality Index (AWEI) Platinum Employer. Find out more about our work on diversity and inclusion .    How to apply   Applications (including a cover letter, CV, and any additional supporting documentation) can be submitted via the Apply button at the top of the page.     For employees of the University or contingent workers, please login into your Workday account and navigate to the Career icon on your Dashboard. Click on USYD Find Jobs and apply.   For a confidential discussion about the role, or if you require reasonable adjustment or any documents in alternate formats, please contact Roshik Prasad, Recruitment Operations by email to roshik.prasad@sydney.edu.au   © The University of Sydney   The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Click to view the Position Description for this role. Applications Close Sunday 15 November 2026 11:59 PM"
    },
    "https://usyd.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/USYD_EXTERNAL_CAREER_SITE/job/Newtown/Centre-Manager-Centre-for-Disability-Research-Policy_0157856": {
      "title": "Centre Manager-Centre for Disability Research Policy",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-13\nFull time\n\nFull-time Continuing opportunity, located at Camperdown Campus Exciting opportunity for a Centre Manager to join the Centre for Disability Research and Policy Base Salary $131,108 - $147,858 + 17% superannuation     About the opportunity   The Centre for Disability Research and Policy produces collaborative research that actively influences policy and practice to improve the lives of people with disability in Australia and the Asia-Pacific and aims to reduce the disadvantage that occurs for people with disability. The Centre's research works to improve the social and economic participation, health and wellbeing of people with disability. We have an exciting opportunity for a Centre Manager to provide operational leadership for the Centre for Disability Research and Policy to meet its core objective whilst also supporting the Centre and School and Faculty’s strategic research agenda.   Your key responsibilities will be to:   collaboratively work with the Centre Director, Senior academic leaders and colleagues in the Centre for Disability Research and Policy to lead the Centre play a key role in overall business planning and implementation, monitoring and reporting drive improvement in process and overall performance and manage the workforce and operational resources, ensuring centre operations, communications, and events adhere to leading accessibility and inclusion standards deliver on budget and financial management support and manage/prepare the Centre’s budget and work collaboratively with FMH Finance provide strategic direction to ensure the Centre’s programs achieve their aims and operational objectives initiate and develop long-term relationships with internal and external stakeholders manage governance and compliance including committee membership, reporting and communications   About you   postgraduate qualifications in the field of disability, including disabled people's organisations, government, and community partners, with proven experience in research management with the ability to work on multiple projects and adapt to changes in line with overall strategies a well-developed understanding of the Australian disability sector policy landscape and the principles of co-design, with lived experience of disability viewed favorably extensive operational and administrative leadership, possessing the capability to translate strategic goals into operational initiatives advanced interpersonal, verbal and written communication skills with sound negotiating and conflict resolution skills and a demonstrated level of tact and discretion in dealing with day-to-day operational matters and stakeholders in the research process excellent oral and written communication skills and the ability to liaise effectively with all levels of staff, students, management and external stakeholders  financial management experience and literacy, experience in management budgets and controlling income and expenditure hold strong analytical, problem solving and time management skills.     Sponsorship / work rights for Australia   You must have unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this employment to be eligible to apply. Visa sponsorship is not available for this appointment.   Pre-employment checks and declarations     Your employment is conditional upon the successful completion of all pre-employment or background checks required for the role in terms satisfactory to the University.  Also, to meet the University’s obligations under the National Higher Education Code to Prevent and Eliminate Gender-Based Violence you will be asked to declare if you have been investigated for, or found to engaged in, sexual harm or gender-based violence in the course of previous employment or in a legal process. Similarly, your ongoing employment is conditional upon the satisfactory maintenance of all relevant clearances and background check requirements. If you do not meet these conditions, the University may take any necessary step, including the termination of your employment.   EEO statement     At the University of Sydney, our shared values are trust, accountability and excellence and we strive to be a place where everyone can thrive. We are committed to creating a University community that thrives through diversity and reflects the wider community that we serve. We deliver on this through our commitment to diversity and inclusion , evidenced by our people and culture programs, as well as key strategies to increase participation and support the careers of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander People , women , people living with a disability , people from culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds , and those who identify as LGBTQIA+ . We welcome applications from candidates from all backgrounds.    We are proud to be recognised as an Australian Workplace Equality Index (AWEI) Platinum Employer. Find out more about our work on diversity and inclusion .    How to apply   Applications (including a cover letter, CV, and any additional supporting documentation) can be submitted via the Apply button at the top of the page.     For employees of the University or contingent workers, please login into your Workday account and navigate to the Career icon on your Dashboard. Click on USYD Find Jobs and apply.   For a confidential discussion about the role, or if you require reasonable adjustment or any documents in alternate formats, please contact Roshik Prasad, Recruitment Operations by email to roshik.prasad@sydney.edu.au   © The University of Sydney   The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Click to view the Position Description for this role. Applications Close Monday 26 October 2026 11:59 PM"
    },
    "https://usyd.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/USYD_EXTERNAL_CAREER_SITE/job/Darlington-Campus/Senior-Systems-Consultant_0157936-2": {
      "title": "Senior Systems Consultant",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-13\nFull time\n\nFull time fixed term opportunity until January 2028    Utilise your expertise to provide support for Human Resource Workforce Systems users across the University through ensuring usability, data integrity, accuracy and compliance Base Salary $131,108 + 17% superannuation About the opportunity   The primary purpose of this role is to lead continuous improvement projects and system support ensuring usability, data integrity, accuracy, and compliance. This position will define system requirements and specifications and be Responsible for assigned testing activities with minimal supervision including participation in identifying scenarios to be included in the test execution. Working in the systems team, the successful candidate will interact with system vendors, solution architects, business analysts, business users, and management. Candidates should possess strong project skills and be able to work autonomously with little supervision. Experience in using the Workday or Ascender Pay system is highly desirable.   What are we looking for?   Ideal candidates for this role will be experienced in system analysis, design and implementation of information systems or continuous improvement initiatives within a large organisation. You will possess excellent communication skills and problem-solving ability within a business environment. We are looking for people who can build and maintain positive working relationships to influence key stakeholders and work consultatively to facilitate the delivery of quality outcomes.   Your key responsibilities will be to:   provide day to day support to the Human Resource group as well as University managers and staff in the use of the Human Resource Information System ensuring compliance with HR policies and procedures design and supply tools and materials to support Human Resource staff and other end users to effectively utilise the existing Human Resource systems for their needs, including training as needed to increase utilisation of self-service functions ensure approval flows and automated system notifications are configured correctly to meet Human Resource policy and procedure requirements in accordance with the formal delegations of authority coordinate and organise user acceptance testing, roll out of system patches, fixes and updates to minimise disruptions, and communicate system improvements, updates and outages across the University conduct detailed research and analysis as part of the broader Human Resources agenda and work with the reporting and analytics team to ensure system optimisation and data accuracy and integrity. About you   post graduate qualifications in a relevant field or an equivalent combination of training and experience demonstrated experience working with a large, entity wide HRIS dealing with extraction, analysis and maintenance of data experience managing or participating in HR system implementations, upgrades or system projects previous experience in a shared service environment (HR or operational) experience in delivering training to support the implementation or alteration to HRIS systems or processes previous experience working in a Higher Education environment or large organisations.     Sponsorship / work rights for Australia   You must have unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this employment to be eligible to apply. Visa sponsorship is not available for this appointment.     Pre-employment checks and declarations       Your employment is conditional upon the successful completion of all pre-employment or background checks required for the role in terms satisfactory to the University.  Also, to meet the University’s obligations under the National Higher Education Code to Prevent and Eliminate Gender-Based Violence you will be asked to declare if you have been investigated for, or found to engaged in, sexual harm or gender-based violence in the course of previous employment or in a legal process. Similarly, your ongoing employment is conditional upon the satisfactory maintenance of all relevant clearances and background check requirements. If you do not meet these conditions, the University may take any necessary step, including the termination of your employment.   EEO statement     At the University of Sydney, our shared values are trust, accountability and excellence and we strive to be a place where everyone can thrive. We are committed to creating a University community that thrives through diversity and reflects the wider community that we serve. We deliver on this through our commitment to diversity and inclusion , evidenced by our people and culture programs, as well as key strategies to increase participation and support the careers of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander People , women , people living with a disability , people from culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds , and those who identify as LGBTQIA+ . We welcome applications from candidates from all backgrounds.    We are proud to be recognised as an Australian Workplace Equality Index (AWEI) Platinum Employer. Find out more about our work on diversity and inclusion .    How to apply   Applications (including a cover letter, CV, and any additional supporting documentation) can be submitted via the Apply button at the top of the page.     For employees of the University or contingent workers, please login into your Workday account and navigate to the Career icon on your Dashboard.  Click on USYD Find Jobs and apply.   For a confidential discussion about the role, or if you require reasonable adjustment or any documents in alternate formats, please contact Victoria Ford-Rogers Recruitment Operations by email to victoria.ford-rogers@sydney.edu.au   © The University of Sydney   The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Click to view the Position Description for this role. Applications Close Tuesday 20 October 2026 11:59 PM"
    },
    "https://usyd.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/USYD_EXTERNAL_CAREER_SITE/job/Camperdown-Campus/Development-Manager_0158667": {
      "title": "Development Manager",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-26\nFull time\n\nWe now have a continuing opportunity as Development Manager, FMH   As Development Manager, FMH , you will be responsible for raising major financial gifts that will support projects and programs across the University, with a focus of raising funds across your faculty or school, playing a role in managing Faculty relationships. The incumbent will be required to identify and engage high net-worth donor prospects, align their interest and passions to the strategic priorities of the University, and successfully solicit gifts with a value of $100,000 or more. We are looking for the successful candidate to be able to demonstrate their experience in major gifts fundraising in their application.   About you The University values courage and creativity; openness and engagement; inclusion and diversity; and respect and integrity. As such, we see the importance of recruiting talent aligned to these values and are looking for a Development Manager, FMH , who has:    a background in major gift fundraising, with a demonstrated ability to identify and negotiate $100,000 + gift opportunities a strong track record of achievement and meeting stretch financial targets demonstrated relationship building and strong leadership skills with the ability to engage internal and external stakeholders has highly developed emotional intelligence – you are self-aware with exceptional interpersonal skills can work autonomously and is naturally a high-achiever and driven by success both professionally and personally has superior time-management, prioritisation and organisation skills.   About us   The Advancement Portfolio strengthens the University's capacity to deliver world-class education and research through philanthropy, alumni engagement and strategic partnerships. We work collaboratively across the University to build meaningful relationships and create opportunities that generate lasting impact for our communities and society.    Sponsorship / work rights for   Australia     You must have unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this employment to be eligible to apply. Visa sponsorship is not available for this appointment.    Pre-employment checks and declarations     Your employment is conditional upon the successful completion of all pre-employment or background checks required for the role in terms satisfactory to the University. Also, to meet the University's obligations under the National Higher Education Code to Prevent and Eliminate Gender-Based Violence you will be asked to declare if you have been investigated for, or found to engaged in, sexual harm or gender-based violence in the course of previous employment or in a legal process. Similarly, your ongoing employment is conditional upon the satisfactory maintenance of all relevant clearances and background check requirements. If you do not meet these conditions, the University may take any necessary step, including the termination of your employment.    EEO statement     At the University of Sydney, our shared values are trust, accountability and excellence and we strive to be a place where everyone can thrive. We are committed to creating a University community that thrives through diversity and reflects the wider community that we serve. We deliver on this through our commitment to diversity and inclusion, evidenced by our people and culture programs, as well as key strategies to increase participation and support the careers of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander People, women, people living with a disability, people from culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds, and those who identify as LGBTQIA+. We welcome applications from candidates from all backgrounds.    We are proud to be recognised as an Australian Workplace Equality Index (AWEI) Platinum Employer. Find out more about our work on diversity and inclusion.    How to apply     Applications (including a cover letter, CV, and any additional supporting documentation) can be submitted via the  Apply  button at the top of the page.    For employees of the University or contingent workers, please login into your  Workday  account and navigate to the Career icon on your Dashboard. Click on USYD Find Jobs and apply.    For a confidential discussion about the role, or if you require reasonable adjustment or support filling out this application, please contact Sarah Scott, Recruitment Partner by email to sarah.scott@sydney.edu.au     ©  The University of Sydney    The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment.  © The University of Sydney Click to view the Position Description for this role. Applications Close Tuesday 03 November 2026 11:59 PM"
    },
    "https://usyd.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/USYD_EXTERNAL_CAREER_SITE/job/Darlington-Campus/Indigenous-Recruitment-Assistant--Identified-_0157484-3": {
      "title": "Indigenous Recruitment Assistant (Identified)",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-26\nFull time\n\n·        Full time or part time, continuing opportunity ·        Outstanding opportunity to support Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander student participation in higher education ·        Base Salary $93K + 17% superannuation   About the opportunity   This position has been designated for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander people only under the provisions of section 126 of the Anti-Discrimination Act 1977 (NSW).    The Indigenous Recruitment Assistant (Identified) provides student recruitment support and promotes the University of Sydney as the preferred destination for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander students considering tertiary study. The role provides recruitment and administrative support in promoting the range of admissions pathways offered by the University. This includes coordination of outreach activities both on and off campus.   About you   ·        has experience working in a similar administrative or office environment in student recruitment within a large and complex organisation ·        identify as Aboriginal and/or Torres Strait Islander ·        knowledge and understanding of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander cultures and communities ·        is highly interpersonal and communicates articulately by way of verbal and written forums ·        creates and maintains authentic relationships with a diverse group of key stakeholders ·        demonstrates flexibility and thrives in an accommodating working environment with an administration focus ·        Works collaboratively and is a team player, with the ability to work effectively within the immediate team and the broader University community. ·        enjoys coordination of events, takes initiative and has to demonstrate capacity to priorities varied workloads.   Be part of the Yura network made up of Aboriginal and/or Torres Strait Islander staff for fortnightly yarning circles.     Sponsorship / work rights for Australia   You must have unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this employment to be eligible to apply. Visa sponsorship is not available for this appointment.     Pre-employment checks and declarations     Your employment is conditional upon the successful completion of all pre-employment or background checks required for the role in terms satisfactory to the University.  Also, to meet the University’s obligations under the National Higher Education Code to Prevent and Eliminate Gender-Based Violence you will be asked to declare if you have been investigated for, or found to engaged in, sexual harm or gender-based violence in the course of previous employment or in a legal process. Similarly, your ongoing employment is conditional upon the satisfactory maintenance of all relevant clearances and background check requirements. If you do not meet these conditions, the University may take any necessary step, including the termination of your employment. This position is designated as involving child-related work. To undertake or remain in this position, you are required to apply for and obtain a Working with Children Check clearance in accordance with the Child Protection (Working with Children) Act 2012.   EEO statement     At the University of Sydney, our shared values are trust, accountability and excellence and we strive to be a place where everyone can thrive. We are committed to creating a University community that thrives through diversity and reflects the wider community that we serve. We deliver on this through our commitment to diversity and inclusion , evidenced by our people and culture programs, as well as key strategies to increase participation and support the careers of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander People , women , people living with a disability , people from culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds , and those who identify as LGBTQIA+ . We welcome applications from candidates from all backgrounds.    We are proud to be recognised as an Australian Workplace Equality Index (AWEI) Platinum Employer. Find out more about our work on diversity and inclusion .    How to apply   Applications (including a cover letter, CV, and any additional supporting documentation) can be submitted via the Apply button at the top of the page.     For employees of the University or contingent workers, please login into your Workday account and navigate to the Career icon on your Dashboard.  Click on USYD Find Jobs and apply.   For a confidential discussion about the role, or if you require reasonable adjustment or any documents in alternate formats, please contact Riviera Son, Recruitment Operations by email to riviera.son@sydney.edu.au.   © The University of Sydney   The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment.   Click to view the Position Description for this role. Applications Close Monday 02 November 2026 11:59 PM"
    },
    "https://usyd.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/USYD_EXTERNAL_CAREER_SITE/job/Orange-Campus/Senior-Lecturer--Clinical-Education--Perinatal---Women-s-Health-Coordinator_0157098-1": {
      "title": "Senior Lecturer (Clinical Education) Perinatal & Women's Health Coordinator",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-26\nPart time\n\nPart-time (0.2 FTE) funding contingent continuing position for a Senior Clinical Lecturer in Perinatal and Women's Health Opportunity to provide leadership in clinical education, curriculum innovation, and teaching excellence in Orange, NSW Base Salary $162,347 p.a. (pro rata) + 17% superannuation + Clinical loading for the appropriate candidates About Orange, NSW. It is an opportune time to make a lifestyle change and join Orange's thriving rural community. Home to nearly 70,000 people, the Orange Region offers a unique blend of seasonal landscapes, accessible transport, abundant natural resources and some of the best regional food and wine in Australia. The city of Orange, with a population of 41,000 residents is a regional hub of medical and health expertise outside Sydney and also employs a significant number of people in the education and mining industries.  Orange is part of Wiradjuri land, and the region is home to several culturally significant sites. For more information on Orange and surrounds, see https://thinkorangeregion.com.au/ and https://orange360.com.au . About the opportunity The School of Rural Health (SRH) has been delivering long term rural placements for The University of Sydney year 3 and 4 medical students in Orange and Dubbo for over 20 years. This role undertakes a range of duties related to the implementation of the curriculum as well as teaching and learning activities for the University of Sydney’s Doctor of Medicine (MD) Program delivered to extended rural placement students in Orange, NSW.   Based in Orange, this Senior Lecturer (Clinical Education) Perinatal & Women's Health Coordinator position offers an exciting opportunity for an experienced clinician and educator to provide leadership in clinical education within perinatal and women's health. The role contributes to curriculum innovation, teaching excellence, accreditation compliance and the development of strong partnerships across the profession and healthcare sector. Working with considerable autonomy, you will play a key role in enhancing student learning outcomes and advancing the strategic objectives of the University Your key responsibilities will be to: lead the development and implementation of innovative clinical education programs, curriculum design, and review ensure teaching activities align with relevant accreditation and professional requirements deliver high-quality teaching, coaching and supervision in clinical and simulated learning environments apply clinical knowledge and expertise to innovate teaching and learning, contribute to development of pragmatic teaching resources with particular emphasis on rural practice. support academic and pastoral support for students at the Clinical School build and maintain productive relationships with clinical, industry and professional partners contribute to continuous curriculum improvement and enhanced health and education outcomes fulfil governance and compliance requirements of the Sydney Medical Program Doctor of Medicine and the Rural Health Multidisciplinary Training Program/Murray Darling Medical Schools Program Parameters support the orientation and induction of students and staff to the area support the strategic priorities of the Faculty and University. About you bachelor of Medicine (MB)/Doctor of Medicine (MD)/Bachelor of Medicine and Bachelor of Surgery (MBBS) or equivalent from a recognised tertiary institution phD in an academic field relevant to the discipline or other relevant higher academic or professional qualification recognition as a Fellow of the relevant Australasian medical specialist college or equivalent as recognised by Australian Health Professional Regulation Agency (AHPRA), e.g. FRANZCOG current registration with AHPRA or equivalent professional bodies (where relevant) significant experience in clinical teaching, learning, and educational leadership, in particular in an established Medical Program current/recent clinical experience and knowledge of the Australian healthcare system. experience in a tertiary educational institution working with academics, clinicians, and students within a rural health training environment demonstrated expertise in curriculum design, accreditation requirements, and educational innovation experience supervising, mentoring, and developing educators, clinicians, and students recognised professional standing and strong connections within the healthcare sector, including having had experience in a well-regarded leadership role in a clinical area of speciality and/or notable network of strategic alliances demonstrated ability to build productive relationships with clinical, professional and industry stakeholders demonstrable understanding of and commitment to rural health experience in providing pastoral care in a student learning environment strong communication, organisation, and project leadership skills ability to obtain and maintain all relevant clinical compliance requirements, including a Working with Children Check. Sponsorship / work rights for Australia   You must have unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this employment to be eligible to apply. Visa sponsorship is not available for this appointment.   Pre-employment checks and declarations     Your employment is conditional upon the successful completion of all pre-employment or background checks required for the role in terms satisfactory to the University.  Also, to meet the University’s obligations under the National Higher Education Code to Prevent and Eliminate Gender-Based Violence you will be asked to declare if you have been investigated for, or found to engaged in, sexual harm or gender-based violence in the course of previous employment or in a legal process. Similarly, your ongoing employment is conditional upon the satisfactory maintenance of all relevant clearances and background check requirements. If you do not meet these conditions, the University may take any necessary step, including the termination of your employment.   This position is designated as involving child-related work. To undertake or remain in this position, you are required to apply for and obtain a Working with Children Check clearance in accordance with the Child Protection (Working with Children) Act 2012 . As this position is located on an NSW Health site, it is a regulatory requirement to complete compulsory employment checks prior to starting in this position. For more information and instructions, please visit: NSW Health employment requirements EEO statement At the University of Sydney, our shared values include diversity and inclusion and we strive to be a place where everyone can thrive. We are committed to creating a University community that reflects the wider community that we serve. We deliver on this commitment through our people and culture programs, as well as key strategies to increase participation and support the careers of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander People , women , people living with a disability, people from culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds , and those who identify as LGBTIQ . We welcome applications from candidates from all backgrounds. How to apply Applications (including a cover letter, CV, and any additional supporting documentation) can be submitted via the Apply button at the top of the page. For employees of the University or contingent workers, please login into your Workday account and navigate to the Career icon on your Dashboard. Click on USYD Find Jobs and apply. For a confidential discussion about the role please contact Tuana Sanders, Administration Manager at the School of Rural Health (Dubbo/Orange) via tuana.sanders@sydney.edu.au , or if you require reasonable adjustment or support filling out this application, please contact Danielle Selinger, Recruitment Operations, by email to danielle.selinger@sydney.edu.au   © The University of Sydney The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Click to view the Position Description for this role. Applications Close Tuesday 10 November 2026 11:59 PM"
    },
    "https://usyd.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/USYD_EXTERNAL_CAREER_SITE/job/Camperdown-Campus/Postdoctoral-Research-Associate---The-Urinary-Tract-Disease-Research-Group_0158720-3": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Research Associate - The Urinary Tract Disease Research Group",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-26\nFull time\n\nFull time, fixed term for 12 months with the possibility for extension (up to 2 years) Exciting opportunity to be a Postdoctoral Research Associate in Immunology and Urinary Tract Disease within the School of Medical Sciences Based at The Charles Perkins Centre, Camperdown Campus Base Salary starting from $117,936 + 17% superannuation   About the opportunity   The School of Medical Sciences is recognised as one of the leading centres for medical science in the world. With over 100 years of excellence in education, we are proud to be training the next generation of medical scientists who will be leading members of the medical research community with an innovative integrated curriculum. Our team of researchers lead key internationally competitive research from molecules to medicines, from patient to policy across a range of therapeutic areas.   The Urinary Tract Disease Research Group within the School of Medical Sciences is seeking a highly motivated Postdoctoral Research Fellow to join our growing research program investigating chronic and severe urinary tract diseases. Led by Dr Malcolm Starkey, the team combines immunology, microbiology, preclinical disease modelling and translational research approaches to better understand the mechanisms underlying urinary tract diseases to inform future diagnostic and therapeutic strategies.   We are particularly interested in candidates with expertise in immunology and host-pathogen interactions, and an interest in applying multi-omics approaches to better understand the biological pathways driving urinary tract disease.   The successful candidate will contribute to a collaborative research program integrating preclinical models, human biospecimens and translational research approaches. The position provides opportunities to work with unique disease models and multidisciplinary collaborators spanning basic, translational and clinical research.   Current research themes include:   host mechanisms regulating urinary tract health and disease biomarker discovery for urinary tract infection and cystinuria preclinical testing of novel therapeutic approaches.   This role offers an outstanding opportunity to develop scientific independence, build an international research profile, establish collaborative networks and contribute to a growing research program in urinary tract disease   Your key responsibilities will be to: design, conduct and lead laboratory-based research projects investigating urinary tract disease employ and further develop advanced experimental techniques including immunology, molecular biology, biomarker discovery and preclinical disease models analyse and interpret complex experimental datasets and contribute to high-quality research outputs and publications assist with the supervision and mentoring of honours, PhD and research students contribute to grant applications, ethics submissions and research reporting establish and maintain productive collaborations with academic, clinical and industry stakeholders present research findings at local seminars, national conferences and international scientific meetings undertake administrative activities and contribute to school and faculty initiatives as appropriate foster a positive and inclusive research culture that values collaboration, innovation and excellence support and contribute to the strategic priorities of the school, faculty and university undertake other duties and projects appropriate to the classification of the role.   About you essential criteria PhD in immunology or a closely related biomedical discipline (e.g. microbiology, molecular biology, biomedical science, medical science) extensive hands-on experience with mouse models of disease, including experimental design, complex animal procedures, disease phenotyping, tissue collection and downstream analyses demonstrated expertise in immunology, host-pathogen interactions, inflammation, infectious disease biology demonstrated experience conducting independent biomedical research to completion strong publication record relative to career stage demonstrated experience with extensive laboratory techniques relevant to molecular, cellular and translational biomedical research strong analytical, organisational, time and problem-solving skills exceptional written and verbal communication skills, including scientific writing and presentation experience demonstrated ability to work effectively both independently and within a multidisciplinary research team.   desirable criteria highly developed bioinformatics expertise in one or more of the following areas: single-cell RNA sequencing (scRNA-seq), spatial transcriptomics, proteomics and metabolomics experience establishing, maintaining and interrogating organoid systems or advanced 3-dimensional epithelial cell culture models experience in the histological and histopathological assessment of mouse tissues and biospecimens, including tissue processing, sectioning, staining, imaging and quantitative analysis experience with confocal microscopy and quantitative image analysis experience with flow cytometric characterisation of immune cells in tissues experience with supervising undergraduate or postgraduate research students experience developing collaborations with academic, clinical or industry partners experience contributing to competitive grant, fellowship or other funding applications.   About the research group The urinary tract disease research group investigates the mechanisms, diagnosis and treatment of urinary tract infections and cystinuria. Our research combines innovative preclinical models, human cohorts and multidisciplinary approaches to address major unmet needs in urinary tract disease. We maintain collaborations with clinicians, patient advocacy organisations, philanthropic partners and industry collaborators to help guide research priorities and maximise opportunities for translation   Key strengths of the program include: access to unique and proprietary preclinical models of cystinuria strong capabilities in immunology and preclinical disease modelling access to human clinical cohorts and biospecimens national and international research collaborations partnerships with clinicians, patient advocacy groups, philanthropy and industry exposure to research spanning fundamental discovery through to translational application.   The successful candidate will join a dynamic and expanding research environment with excellent opportunities for scientific development, collaboration, networking and career progression.   Sponsorship / work rights for Australia   Work Rights: you must have unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this employment to apply. Visa sponsorship is not available for this appointment.     Pre-employment checks   Your employment is conditional upon the successful completion of all pre-employment or background checks required for the role in terms satisfactory to the University. Also, to meet the University’s obligations under the National Higher Education Code to Prevent and Eliminate Gender-Based Violence you will be asked to declare if you have been investigated for, or found to engaged in, sexual harm or gender-based violence in the course of previous employment or in a legal process. Similarly, your ongoing employment is conditional upon the satisfactory maintenance of all relevant clearances and background check requirements. If you do not meet these conditions, the University may take any necessary step, including the termination of your employment.     EEO statement     At the University of Sydney, our shared values are trust, accountability and excellence and we strive to be a place where everyone can thrive. We are committed to creating a University community that thrives through diversity and reflects the wider community that we serve. We deliver on this through our commitment to diversity and inclusion , evidenced by our people and culture programs, as well as key strategies to increase participation and support the careers of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander People , women , people living with a disability , people from culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds , and those who identify as LGBTIQ+ . We welcome applications from candidates from all backgrounds.    We are proud to be recognised as an Australian Workplace Equality Index (AWEI) Gold employer. Find out more about our work on diversity and inclusion .    How to apply   Applications (including a cover letter, CV, and any additional supporting documentation) can be submitted via the Apply button at the top of the page.    For employees of the University or contingent workers, please login into your Workday account and navigate to the Career icon on your Dashboard.  Click on USYD Find Jobs and apply.   For a confidential discussion about the role, or if you require reasonable adjustment or any documents in alternate formats, please contact, Ajaypal Singh, Recruitment Operations by email to ajaypal.singh@sydney.edu.au.   © The University of Sydney   The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Click to view the Position Description for this role. Applications Close Sunday 08 November 2026 11:59 PM"
    },
    "https://usyd.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/USYD_EXTERNAL_CAREER_SITE/job/Camperdown-Campus/Veterinary-Nurse---Small-Animal-Surgery_0152826-2": {
      "title": "Veterinary Nurse - Small Animal Surgery",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-27\nFull time\n\nX1 Full-time continuing contingent on the continuation of external funding and X1 12 Month Fixed Term opportunity Located on the Camperdown Campus at the University Veterinary Teaching Hospital Sydney (UVTHS) Exciting opportunity to provide best practice veterinary care and student teaching as part of the Small Animal Surgery team, within a multi-discipline specialty hospital Base Salary HEO3 Step 1 (38 hpw) $82,388 p.a. + 17% superannuation + appropriate shift loadings About the opportunity The University of Sydney is welcoming applications for a qualified Veterinary Nurse for our Surgery service. Supporting the University Veterinary Teaching Hospital in providing support for clinical teaching and professional nursing assistance with clinical cases. Your key responsibilities will be to:  assist clinicians and veterinary interns with treatments, prepare and assist with clinical procedures provide support for clinical teaching and professional nursing assistance with clinical cases clean and maintain clinical  spaces care for and maintain instruments and equipment support student learning, through positive engagement with students in training  provide overall high level of nursing care for animals in their respective service  undertake ad-hoc projects and other activities as required maintain stock including ordering of consumables and procurement of supplies, and control of stock quantities.  The small animal service at the University Veterinary Teaching Hospital Camperdown (UVTHS) operates in: a small animal specialist veterinary practice a wide range of all medical,surgical and emergent problems second opinion cases access to advanced equipment and imaging. As well as providing gold standard patient care, UVTHS provides an invaluable teaching experience for our final year veterinary students. You will work closely with Specialists, residents and interns in all fields. To learn more about the Sydney School of Veterinary Science click here About you a Veterinary Nursing Certificate IV (or equivalent) and advanced veterinary nursing experience in a veterinary specialist hospital environment ability to communicate with academics, veterinary professionals, and veterinary students  a passion and drive to provide teaching opportunities to students and junior staff  ability to cope with caseloads in a busy working environment  relevant knowledge and commitment to provide excellent surgical care and handling to our patients  demonstrated experience with using technical and medical surgical equipment and training in anaesthesia, radiography, and drug dispensing  demonstrated ability to work independently and in a team  a willingness and ability to work rostered shifts, Monday to Friday.  The key benefits: opportunity to learn and enhance your skills and experience in the unique teaching and research environment of a Veterinary Teaching hospital a University contribution of 17% of your base salary to your superannuation fund yearly personal and professional development planning, including support for continuing education access to the employee assistance program access to fitness passport, as well as discounted rates on university sports and fitness centres industry-leading leave provisions, including generous paid parental leave entitlements (pending eligibility up to 38 weeks*) located on the Camperdown campus, 4 km from the city centre, in a convenient central location with ample public transport options In your cover letter, please specify whether your are applying for the continuing or 12 month fixed term opportunity. Work Rights You must have unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this employment to be eligible to apply. Visa sponsorship is not available for this appointment. Pre-employment checks Your employment is conditional upon the completion of all role required pre-employment or background checks in terms satisfactory to the University. Similarly, your ongoing employment is conditional upon the satisfactory maintenance of all relevant clearances and background check requirements. If you do not meet these conditions, the University may take any necessary steps, including the termination of your employment. EEO Statement   At the University of Sydney, our shared values are trust, accountability and excellence , and we strive to be a place where everyone can thrive. We are committed to creating a University community that thrives through diversity and reflects the wider community that we serve. We deliver on this through our commitment to diversity and inclusion , evidenced by our people and culture programs, as well as key strategies to increase participation and support the careers of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander People , women , people living with a disability , people from culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds , and those who identify as LGBTIQ+ . We welcome applications from candidates from all backgrounds.  We are proud to be recognised as an Australian Workplace Equality Index (AWEI) Gold employer. Find out more about our work on diversity and inclusion .  How to apply Applications (including a cover letter, CV, and any additional supporting documentation) can be submitted via the Apply button at the top of the page or click here For employees of the University or contingent workers, please login into your Workday account and navigate to the Career icon on your Dashboard.  Click on USYD Find Jobs and apply. For a confidential discussion about the role, or if you require reasonable adjustment or any documents in alternate formats, please contact Chris Masaoka, Recruitment Consultant. Recruitment Operations, Human Resources by email recruitment.sea@sydney.edu.au © The University of Sydney The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Click to view the Position Description for this role. Applications Close Sunday 08 November 2026 11:59 PM"
    },
    "https://usyd.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/USYD_EXTERNAL_CAREER_SITE/job/Darlington-Campus/Professor-of-Practice-in-International-Business--Logistics-and-Supply-Chain_0157720-1": {
      "title": "Professor of Practice in International Business, Logistics and Supply Chain",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-26\nPart time\n\nExciting opportunity to lead and further develop the School of International Business, Logistics and Supply Chain Located in Camperdown 2-year fixed term (0.2 FTE) position with an attractive remuneration package   About the opportunity   The University of Sydney Business School is one of the Asia-Pacific region's premier centres for engaged and innovative business research and education. With a highly international outlook, more than 92,000 alumni, over 560 FTE staff, and 11,000 students from more than 65 countries, the Business School delivers a comprehensive suite of programs, including the globally recognised MBA.   We are seeking an internationally respected business leader to join us as a Professor of Practice in International Business or Supply Chain Management within the School of International Business, Logistics and Supply Chain.   A Professor of Practice is recognised as a distinguished leader in their field, known for significant professional achievements, influence, and contribution to industry. Through their extensive experience and networks, they strengthen the connection between academia and practice, enriching student learning and contributing to the advancement of their profession.   The successful appointee will contribute to the School's strategic vision through leadership, industry engagement, governance, and collaboration with students, faculty, alumni, and external stakeholders. They will strengthen the School's connections with industry and government while helping prepare graduates to address increasingly complex international business or supply chain challenges.     About you (skills, capabilities and experience)   The Professor of Practice will:   Be a recognised and influential leader in international business OR supply chain management with a distinguished record of professional achievement and impact. Lead meaningful collaborations and partnerships with business, government, industry bodies, and community organisations across domestic and international markets. Provide thought leadership on contemporary issues shaping international business, trade, globalisation, and cross-border commercial activity OR issues such as supply chain resilience, digital transformation, sustainability, risk management, and global logistics. Advance the profession by integrating professional expertise with academic scholarship and evidence-based practice. Make an outstanding contribution to student learning, professional engagement, and industry-connected education. Contribute to the School's national and international profile through engagement with industry, government, media, and professional networks. Make a significant contribution to governance and collegial life within the University. Foster a positive and inclusive workplace culture that values diversity, collaboration, excellence, and impact.   Sponsorship / work rights for Australia   You must have unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this employment to apply. Visa sponsorship is not available for this appointment.   Pre-employment checks   Your employment is conditional upon the successful completion of all pre-employment or background checks required for the role in terms satisfactory to the University. Also, to meet the University’s obligations under the National Higher Education Code to Prevent and Eliminate Gender-Based Violence you will be asked to declare if you have been investigated for, or found to engaged in, sexual harm or gender-based violence in the course of previous employment or in a legal process. Similarly, your ongoing employment is conditional upon the satisfactory maintenance of all relevant clearances and background check requirements. If you do not meet these conditions, the University may take any necessary step, including the termination of your employment.   EEO statement     At the University of Sydney, our shared values are trust, accountability and excellence and we strive to be a place where everyone can thrive. We are committed to creating a University community that thrives through diversity and reflects the wider community that we serve. We deliver on this through our commitment to diversity and inclusion , evidenced by our people and culture programs, as well as key strategies to increase participation and support the careers of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander People , women , people living with a disability , people from culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds , and those who identify as LGBTIQ+ . We welcome applications from candidates from all backgrounds.    We are proud to be recognised as an Australian Workplace Equality Index (AWEI) Platinum Employer. Find out more about our work on diversity and inclusion .    How to apply   Applications ( including a CV and cover letter outlining how your skills, capabilities and experience meet the requirements for the role )  can be submitted via the Apply button at the top of the page.     Candidate Information Brochure   For employees of the University or contingent workers, please login into your Workday account and navigate to the Career icon on your Dashboard.  Click on USYD Find Jobs and apply.   For a confidential discussion about the role, or if you require reasonable adjustment or any documents in alternate formats, please contact Lena Jiang, Recruitment Operations by email to lena.jiang@sydney.edu.au .   © The University of Sydney   The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Click to view the Position Description for this role. Applications Close Sunday 29 November 2026 11:59 PM"
    },
    "https://usyd.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/USYD_EXTERNAL_CAREER_SITE/job/St-Leonards/Education-Support-Officer--Clinical---Macquarie-Placements-_0157104-3": {
      "title": "Education Support Officer (Clinical - Macquarie Placements)",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-26\nPart time\n\nPart time (0.6FTE), fixed term until July 2028   Exciting opportunity to be an Education Support Officer Base Salary starting at $107,312 (pro rata) + 17% superannuation  About the opportunity The Education Support Officer (Clinical – Macquarie Placements) provides high-level administrative and operational support to enable the effective delivery of clinical education and student placement activities for the Macquarie University Doctor of Medicine program, supporting students and staff across clinical placements based at Royal North Shore Hospital. Working closely with academic staff, Clinical School leadership, clinical educators and hospital partners, the role coordinates key education processes, maintains accurate systems and records, and supports compliance and reporting requirements to ensure a high-quality student and staff experience across clinical placements. The role also provides advice and support to the Head of Clinical School on relevant university policies, procedures and business processes, and supports students throughout placement rotations, escalating issues where appropriate. A key focus is the coordination of orientation and induction activities, clinical assessments, and operational logistics (including teaching spaces, resources and communication) to ensure placement delivery aligns with curriculum requirements and professional standards. Due to operational requirements, the successful candidate will be required to work predominately on-site. Your key responsibilities will be to: provide high-level administrative and operational support for clinical education and placement delivery advise staff, students and clinical educators on university policies, procedures and processes; escalate complex issues as needed coordinate placement orientation and induction with Clinical School leadership and curriculum/discipline leads support Clinical Leads to ensure safe, compliant clinical experience hours are maintained. coordinate clinical assessments and related processes in line with course requirements and university policy maintain accurate education/placement records and systems (databases, contact lists, documentation), ensuring data integrity and timely reporting prepare reports and updates for relevant leaders to support placement planning and decision-making build and maintain effective relationships with internal and external stakeholders, including hospital partners support program evaluation and continuous improvement by collecting feedback and improving systems and processes provide committee and secretariat support as required comply with WHS, EEO, professional standards and codes of ethics undertake other duties and projects appropriate to the role. About you relevant tertiary qualification, or equivalent training and experience (health, education or clinical/placement administration desirable) 2–4 years’ experience in education administration/operations, preferably in tertiary and/or clinical placement settings experience supporting learning and teaching operations, including student placements and assessment-related administration ability to interpret and advise on education policies and procedures strong digital capability with university systems; high computer literacy including database/records management and data integrity strong organisational skills to manage competing priorities and deadlines with minimal supervision clear communication and strong customer service skills; able to build effective relationships with academics, clinicians, staff, students and partners strong attention to detail, including producing high-quality documentation and reports demonstrated continuous improvement mindset and experience improving administrative processes collaborative, positive team member who works effectively across teams.   Sponsorship / work rights for Australia Work Rights: you must have unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this employment to apply. Visa sponsorship is not available for this appointment.   Pre-employment checks Your employment is conditional upon the successful completion of all pre-employment or background checks required for the role in terms satisfactory to the University. Also, to meet the University’s obligations under the National Higher Education Code to Prevent and Eliminate Gender-Based Violence you will be asked to declare if you have been investigated for, or found to engaged in, sexual harm or gender-based violence in the course of previous employment or in a legal process. Similarly, your ongoing employment is conditional upon the satisfactory maintenance of all relevant clearances and background check requirements. If you do not meet these conditions, the University may take any necessary step, including the termination of your employment. EEO statement   At the University of Sydney, our shared values include diversity and inclusion and we strive to be a place where everyone can thrive. We are committed to creating a University community which reflects the wider community that we serve. We deliver on this commitment through our people and culture programs, as well as key strategies to increase participation and support the careers of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander People , women , people living with a disability , people from culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds , and those who identify as LGBTIQ . We welcome applications from candidates from all backgrounds.  How to apply Applications (including a cover letter, CV, and any additional supporting documentation) can be submitted via the Apply button at the top of the page.   For employees of the University or contingent workers, please login into your Workday account and navigate to the Career icon on your Dashboard.  Click on USYD Find Jobs and apply. For a confidential discussion about the role, or if you require reasonable adjustment or any documents in alternate formats, please contact Ajaypal Singh, Recruitment Operations by email to ajaypal.singh@sydney.edu.au. © The University of Sydney The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Click to view the Position Description for this role. Applications Close Sunday 01 November 2026 11:59 PM"
    },
    "https://usyd.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/USYD_EXTERNAL_CAREER_SITE/job/Broken-Hill/Executive-Assistant-to-Head-of-Broken-Hill-University-Department-of-Rural-Health--BHUDRH-_0158107-2": {
      "title": "Executive Assistant to Head of Broken Hill University Department of Rural Health (BHUDRH)",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-26\nFull time\n\nFull time, Funding contingent Continuing role**, located at Broken Hill, NSW Step into a role where your organisational skills directly support the education of tomorrow’s rural health workforce Base Salary $ $89,755 + 17% superannuation    About the opportunity The Broken Hill University Department of Rural Health (BHUDRH), a Rural Clinical School in the Faculty of Medicine and Health, is seeking a Executive Assistant to the Head of the Rural Clinical School. Funded through the Commonwealth Department of Health's Rural Health Multidisciplinary Training (RHMT) Program, the BHUDRH is committed to improving rural and remote health outcomes through innovative education, research and community engagement. The department delivers high-quality teaching and learning experiences for medical, nursing and allied health students undertaking placements across the region, while strengthening partnerships that enhance healthcare outcomes for rural, remote and First Nations communities. This is a dynamic and community-connected environment where the Executive Assistant provides essential support to the Head of Rural Clinical School and executive team, helping to coordinate operations, facilitate stakeholder engagement, and ensure the smooth running of key activities across the School.   Key Responsibilities: provide high-level administrative support to the Head of Rural Clinical School and executive team, including diary and email management, meeting coordination, and agenda preparation support the organisation of school events, visits, and external stakeholder engagements assist with travel and accommodation arrangements, purchasing requests, and University systems processes collaborate with campus support officers and staff to manage accommodation, logistics, and student placement agreements conduct ad hoc research, document preparation, and coordination of project-related tasks as required act as a central point of contact for internal and external inquiries, maintaining professionalism and discretion at all times   About You You are a highly organised, proactive, and adaptable Executive Assistant who thrives in a fast-paced, collaborative environment. You bring a solutions-focused approach, anticipate needs, and manage competing priorities with professionalism and discretion.   You will also demonstrate: proven experience providing high-level administrative or executive support, ideally in a tertiary, health, or complex organisational setting exceptional organisational, time management, and prioritisation skills strong attention to detail, resilience, and the ability to handle sensitive information with discretion excellent written and verbal communication skills, and the ability to engage professionally with a wide range of stakeholders advanced proficiency in Microsoft Office Suite and confidence across multiple systems and platforms flexibility, responsiveness, and a collaborative team-oriented mindset, with a willingness to support broader team activities as needed Please note that this role is full time based in the office. ** Funding Contingent Continuing Employment means Full-time or Part-time employment under a contract that is ongoing, subject to the continuing need and sufficient funding   Sponsorship / work rights for Australia   You must have unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this employment to be eligible to apply. Visa sponsorship is not available for this appointment.   Pre-employment checks and declarations     Your employment is conditional upon the successful completion of all pre-employment or background checks required for the role in terms satisfactory to the University.  Also, to meet the University’s obligations under the National Higher Education Code to Prevent and Eliminate Gender-Based Violence you will be asked to declare if you have been investigated for, or found to engaged in, sexual harm or gender-based violence in the course of previous employment or in a legal process. Similarly, your ongoing employment is conditional upon the satisfactory maintenance of all relevant clearances and background check requirements. If you do not meet these conditions, the University may take any necessary step, including the termination of your employment.   EEO statement     At the University of Sydney, our shared values are trust, accountability and excellence and we strive to be a place where everyone can thrive. We are committed to creating a University community that thrives through diversity and reflects the wider community that we serve. We deliver on this through our commitment to diversity and inclusion , evidenced by our people and culture programs, as well as key strategies to increase participation and support the careers of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander People , women , people living with a disability , people from culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds , and those who identify as LGBTQIA+ . We welcome applications from candidates from all backgrounds.    We are proud to be recognised as an Australian Workplace Equality Index (AWEI) Platinum Employer. Find out more about our work on diversity and inclusion .    How to apply   Applications (including a cover letter, CV, and any additional supporting documentation) can be submitted via the Apply button at the top of the page.     For employees of the University or contingent workers, please login into your Workday account and navigate to the Career icon on your Dashboard.  Click on USYD Find Jobs and apply.   For a confidential discussion about the role, or if you require reasonable adjustment or any documents in alternate formats, please contact Lena Germinarios, Recruitment Operations by email to recruitment.medandhealth@sydney.edu.au   © The University of Sydney   The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Click to view the Position Description for this role. Applications Close Wednesday 18 November 2026 11:59 PM"
    },
    "https://usyd.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/USYD_EXTERNAL_CAREER_SITE/job/Camperdown-Campus/Lecturer--Education-Focused--Secondary-Science-Education_0158142-2": {
      "title": "Lecturer (Education Focused) Secondary Science Education",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-23\nFull time\n\nFull-time, 12 months Fixed term role based at the Camperdown Campus in the Sydney School of Education and Social Work Opportunity to lead and innovate high quality teaching in secondary science education at a leading global university Academic Base Salary, Level B $132,533 p.a. - $157,381 p.a. + 17% superannuation      About the opportunity   Join the Sydney School of Education and Social Work within the Faculty of Arts and Social Sciences, as a Lecturer (Education Focused) in Secondary Science Education . This role plays a key part in delivering high quality teaching across undergraduate and postgraduate programs, including core units within initial teacher education.  The Lecturer (Education) dedicates the majority of their contribution to teaching and teaching-related activities (up to 70%), with up to 20% research and 10% governance, service, and engagement.   You will contribute to the education of future educators through innovative curriculum design, inclusive pedagogical practice and scholarship of teaching and learning, with a focus on supporting children and young people with additional learning needs. Working within a highly regarded academic community, you will collaborate with colleagues and external partners to support student success, contribute to educational leadership, and advance the University’s commitment to equity, inclusion and social justice.   Reporting to Professor Kelly Freebody , your key responsibilities will be:   teach, coordinate and design a broad range of topics in the field of secondary science education and general pedagogy participate in the teaching in the Science Curriculum units in initial teacher education programs contribute to collaborative teaching in the School and work flexibly and collaboratively in a team environment fulfil the necessary administrative and leadership tasks to ensure the smooth running of the Discipline maintain a research profile compatible with the Discipline, School and Faculty research programmes through publication and research grant applications (this may take the form of disciplinary research, or Scholarship of Teaching and Learning, or a mixture of both fields) engage in community outreach activities of the Discipline and the School actively participate in governance and academic community at School and Faculty levels.     About you   We are seeking an exceptional Lecturer (Education Focused) in Secondary Science Education who has:   a PhD (or near completion) in the area of Science Education or a related field recognised expertise in science education and related teaching practice demonstrated record of excellent teaching and educational design in a tertiary environment capability to undertake scholarship of teaching and learning, with evidence of peer reviewed outputs experience designing and delivering digital and blended learning experiences using a Learning Management System (LMS) willingness to undertake Advance HE Associate Fellowship or equivalent a developing network of academic industry and professional partners and stakeholders.     Sponsorship / work rights for Australia   You must have unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this employment to apply. Visa sponsorship is not available for this appointment.     Pre-employment checks and declarations    Your employment is conditional upon the successful completion of all pre-employment or background checks required for the role in terms satisfactory to the University.  Also, to meet the University’s obligations under the National Higher Education Code to Prevent and Eliminate Gender-Based Violence you will be asked to declare if you have been investigated for, or found to engaged in, sexual harm or gender-based violence in the course of previous employment or in a legal process. Similarly, your ongoing employment is conditional upon the satisfactory maintenance of all relevant clearances and background check requirements. If you do not meet these conditions, the University may take any necessary step, including the termination of your employment.   This position is designated as involving child-related work. To undertake or remain in this position, you are required to apply for and obtain a Working with Children Check clearance in accordance with the Child Protection (Working with Children) Act 2012.     EEO statement     At the University of Sydney, our shared values are trust, accountability and excellence and we strive to be a place where everyone can thrive. We are committed to creating a University community that thrives through diversity and reflects the wider community that we serve. We deliver on this through our commitment to diversity and inclusion , evidenced by our people and culture programs, as well as key strategies to increase participation and support the careers of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander People , women , people living with a disability , people from culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds , and those who identify as LGBTIQ+ . We welcome applications from candidates from all backgrounds.    We are proud to be recognised as an Australian Workplace Equality Index (AWEI) Gold employer. Find out more about our work on diversity and inclusion .      How to apply   Applications (including a cover letter, CV, and any additional supporting documentation) can be submitted via the Apply button at the top of the page. For employees of the University or contingent workers, please login into your Workday account and navigate to the Career icon on your Dashboard.  Click on USYD Find Jobs and apply.   For a confidential discussion about the role, or if you require reasonable adjustment or any documents in alternate formats, please contact Helen Efstathiou, Recruitment Consultant, Recruitment Operations on +61 2 8627 7137 or by email helen.efstathiou@sydney.edu.au   © The University of Sydney   The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Click to view the Position Description for this role. Applications Close Sunday 08 November 2026 11:59 PM"
    },
    "https://usyd.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/USYD_EXTERNAL_CAREER_SITE/job/Darlington-Campus/Health--Safety-and-Wellbeing-Advisor-Digital-Systems_0158073-2": {
      "title": "Health, Safety and Wellbeing Advisor Digital Systems",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-23\nFull time\n\nFull-time, continuing opportunity   Located on Camperdown Campus on Gadigal Country   Base Salary from $117,133 plus 17% superannuation   This is a Targeted Aboriginal and/or Torres Strait Islander Position. While we welcome applications from all candidates, this is a targeted role for Aboriginal and/or Torres Strait Islander people. As part of our commitment to diversity and inclusion, Aboriginal and/or Torres Strait Islander candidates will be assessed as a priority. This Exemption is claimed under Section 21 of the Anti-Discrimination Act 1977.     About the team   The Health, Safety & Wellbeing (HSW) team plays a critical role in enabling safe, healthy and sustainable ways of working across the University. Through listening, learning and a commitment to continuous improvement, HSW promotes consistent best practice, frameworks and standards across all faculties and business areas. Together, the team helps shape a culture where health, safety and wellbeing are embedded into everyday work, supporting organisational excellence and long-term success.  About the opportunity   As Health, Safety and Wellbeing Advisor Digital Systems , you will be responsible for the administration, maintenance, optimisation and continuous improvement of HSW digital systems and contribute to broader HSW continuous improvement initiatives beyond digital systems including optimisation of operational processes and administrative practices where systems intersect with business workflows.  In this role, you will own the effective operation and optimisation of HSW digital systems, providing expert advice on user enablement, data quality, and system performance. This role drives system upgrades and enhancements, and identifies, prioritises and implements process improvements that improve efficiency, usability and data integrity.  Your key responsibilities will be to:  maintain and administer HSW digital systems  provide user support and training  monitor and improve data quality and system performance  coordinate and support system upgrades and enhancements   drive continuous improvement across HSW digital systems, processes and operations.  About you   tertiary qualifications in information/digital systems, data management, process improvement, business, health and safety or a related discipline, or an equivalent combination of relevant training and experience  relevant systems or platform training (e.g. Enterprise system administration, CRM or EHS systems) or demonstrated equivalent experience  demonstrated experience administering and supporting enterprise digital systems or platforms, including configuration, troubleshooting and user support  experience working with system workflows, user access management, forms and data structures to support business processes  experience delivering user training, guidance and support materials to enable effective system use  experience working with multiple stakeholders to gather requirements, resolve issues and support system enhancements.  Why work with us We know our people are what make the University's mission possible. Alongside meaningful work, we offer a range of benefits designed to support you financially, personally and professionally. Key benefits include: Paid Cultural and Ceremonial Leave: Indigenous employees are entitled to 5 days of paid leave and 10 days of unpaid leave each year to participate in cultural and ceremonial activities and fulfil community obligations. Career Development : Access to Indigenous professional and career development programs, including mentoring, leadership programs, and development funding Community and Connection: Be part of a vibrant community with opportunities to connect through staff networks (our YURA Network), mentoring, cultural events and professional Development opportunities Flexible Work and Exceptional Benefits: Enjoy flexible work arrangements, 17% employer superannuation, Fitness Passport, generous leave provisions, staff discounts and access to a wide range of University facilities and services.   Sponsorship / work rights for Australia   Please note: You must have unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this employment to apply. Visa sponsorship is not available for this appointment.  Pre-employment checks and declarations    Your employment is conditional upon the successful completion of all pre-employment or background checks required for the role in terms satisfactory to the University.  Also, to meet the University’s obligations under the National Higher Education Code to Prevent and Eliminate Gender-Based Violence you will be asked to declare if you have been investigated for, or found to engaged in, sexual harm or gender-based violence in the course of previous employment or in a legal process. Similarly, your ongoing employment is conditional upon the satisfactory maintenance of all relevant clearances and background check requirements. If you do not meet these conditions, the University may take any necessary step, including the termination of your employment.  EEO statement    At the University of Sydney, our shared values are trust, accountability and excellence and we strive to be a place where everyone can thrive. We are committed to creating a University community that thrives through diversity and reflects the wider community that we serve. We deliver on this through our commitment to diversity and inclusion , evidenced by our people and culture programs, as well as key strategies to increase participation and support the careers of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander People , women , people living with a disability , people from culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds , and those who identify as LGBTIQ+ . We welcome applications from candidates from all backgrounds.  We are proud to be recognised as an Australian Workplace Equality Index (AWEI) Gold employer. Find out more about our work on diversity and inclusion .   How to apply   Applications (including a cover letter, CV, and any additional supporting documentation) can be submitted via the Apply button at the top of the page.    We understand that everyone applies differently. If you’d prefer to have an informal conversation before applying, we welcome you to get in touch so we can talk through the role and process. In addition, if you require reasonable adjustment or any documents in alternate formats, please contact Sade Mintah via sade.mintah@sydney.edu.au . © The University of Sydney  The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Click to view the Position Description for this role. Applications Close Sunday 01 November 2026 11:59 PM"
    },
    "https://usyd.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/USYD_EXTERNAL_CAREER_SITE/job/Darlington-Campus/Health--Safety-and-Wellbeing-Advisor-Enablement_0158074-2": {
      "title": "Health, Safety and Wellbeing Advisor Enablement",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-23\nFull time\n\nFull-time, continuing opportunity   Located on Camperdown Campus on Gadigal Country   Base Salary from $117,133 plus 17% superannuation   This is a Targeted Aboriginal and/or Torres Strait Islander Position. While we welcome applications from all candidates, this is a targeted role for Aboriginal and/or Torres Strait Islander people. As part of our commitment to diversity and inclusion, Aboriginal and/or Torres Strait Islander candidates will be assessed as a priority. This Exemption is claimed under Section 21 of the Anti-Discrimination Act 1977.     About the team   The Health, Safety & Wellbeing (HSW) team plays a critical role in enabling safe, healthy and sustainable ways of working across the University. Through listening, learning and a commitment to continuous improvement, HSW promotes consistent best practice, frameworks and standards across all faculties and business areas. Together, the team helps shape a culture where health, safety and wellbeing are embedded into everyday work, supporting organisational excellence and long-term success.  About the opportunity   As Health, Safety and Wellbeing Advisor Enablement, you will be responsible for enabling and driving structured coordination, communication and engagement across HSW initiatives to support the effective implementation and adoption of HSW systems, frameworks and programs across the University. The role ensures work is structured, visible and aligned to HSW priorities, translating system changes and organisational requirements into clear, practical communications, training and engagement approaches that enable sustained behavioural and operational change. This role has a strong continuous improvement focus.   Your key responsibilities will be to:  coordinate delivery of HSW initiatives and activities   drive continuous improvement and enablement across HSW systems, processes and operations  develop and support communication materials  coordinate and support stakeholder engagement, learning and change activities  maintain project documentation and records  support workshops, training and engagement initiatives  monitor and triage HSW central mailbox.  About you   tertiary qualifications in communications, business, project management, health and safety or related discipline, or an equivalent combination of relevant experience and training  demonstrated experience coordinating multiple projects, initiatives or programs, including planning, tracking activities and managing completing priorities  experience developing and delivering communication materials and content across a range of formats (e.g. emails, intranet, presentations, campaigns)  experience supporting stakeholder engagement activities, including workshops, meetings and training sessions  proven ability to translate complex or technical information into clear, practical and audience-appropriate communication  experience maintaining structured documentation, coordination tools and records to support delivery and reporting  experience working with multiple stakeholders to support implementation, engagement and adoption of initiatives.  Why work with us We know our people are what make the University's mission possible. Alongside meaningful work, we offer a range of benefits designed to support you financially, personally and professionally. Key benefits include: Paid Cultural and Ceremonial Leave: Indigenous employees are entitled to 5 days of paid leave and 10 days of unpaid leave each year to participate in cultural and ceremonial activities and fulfil community obligations. Career Development : Access to Indigenous professional and career development programs, including mentoring, leadership programs, and development funding Community and Connection: Be part of a vibrant community with opportunities to connect through staff networks (our YURA Network), mentoring, cultural events and professional Development opportunities Flexible Work and Exceptional Benefits: Enjoy flexible work arrangements, 17% employer superannuation, Fitness Passport, generous leave provisions, staff discounts and access to a wide range of University facilities and services. Sponsorship / work rights for Australia   Please note: You must have unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this employment to apply. Visa sponsorship is not available for this appointment.  Pre-employment checks and declarations    Your employment is conditional upon the successful completion of all pre-employment or background checks required for the role in terms satisfactory to the University.  Also, to meet the University’s obligations under the National Higher Education Code to Prevent and Eliminate Gender-Based Violence you will be asked to declare if you have been investigated for, or found to engaged in, sexual harm or gender-based violence in the course of previous employment or in a legal process. Similarly, your ongoing employment is conditional upon the satisfactory maintenance of all relevant clearances and background check requirements. If you do not meet these conditions, the University may take any necessary step, including the termination of your employment.  EEO statement    At the University of Sydney, our shared values are trust, accountability and excellence and we strive to be a place where everyone can thrive. We are committed to creating a University community that thrives through diversity and reflects the wider community that we serve. We deliver on this through our commitment to diversity and inclusion , evidenced by our people and culture programs, as well as key strategies to increase participation and support the careers of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander People , women , people living with a disability , people from culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds , and those who identify as LGBTIQ+ . We welcome applications from candidates from all backgrounds.  We are proud to be recognised as an Australian Workplace Equality Index (AWEI) Gold employer. Find out more about our work on diversity and inclusion .   How to apply   Applications (including a cover letter, CV, and any additional supporting documentation) can be submitted via the Apply button at the top of the page.  We understand that everyone applies differently. If you’d prefer to have an informal conversation before applying, we welcome you to get in touch so we can talk through the role and process. In addition, if you require reasonable adjustment or any documents in alternate formats, please contact Sade Mintah via sade.mintah@sydney.edu.au . © The University of Sydney The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Click to view the Position Description for this role. Applications Close Sunday 01 November 2026 11:59 PM"
    },
    "https://usyd.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/USYD_EXTERNAL_CAREER_SITE/job/Darlington-Campus/Postdoctoral-Research-Associate-in-Computational-Supersonic-Combustion_0157434-1": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Research Associate in Computational Supersonic Combustion",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-23\nFull time\n\nFull time, two year fixed-term position with potential for extension Commencing in January / February 2027. Located at our Camperdown campus in the School of Aerospace, Mechanical & Mechatronic Engineering Exciting opportunity to advance cutting-edge research in supersonic combustion and high-speed propulsion within a world-class research environment Base Salary Academic Level A $117,936 + 17% superannuation   About the opportunity   The School of Aerospace Mechanical and Mechatronic Engineering (AMME) is one of Australia’s leading research and teaching Engineering Schools, with degree programs in Mechanical, Mechatronic, Aeronautical and Space Engineering. The school has a student body of over 1,000 students and has seen strong growth in demand for teaching at the undergraduate and postgraduate levels. The school supports teaching and research activities across disciplines including aerospace, combustion, design, fluid mechanics, materials, mechanical, mechatronic and robotics engineering. To learn more about the School click here   We are currently seeking a Postdoctoral Research Associate in Computational Supersonic Combustion to join the ARC Training Centre for High-Speed Propulsion, Rocket and Launch Systems (HiPR-LS).   Your key responsibilities will be to:   conduct advanced research into supersonic combustion and compressible reacting flows contribute to research seeking to develop new computational models and solvers for supersonic combustion and compressible mixing, with relevance to advanced propulsion concepts undertake computational research using existing and new codes and high-performance computing work with state-of-the-art codes, including direct numerical simulation, large eddy simulation and probability density function methods mentor research students in HiPR-LS develop technical content for the HiPR-LS professional development program in rocket systems, including short courses, seminars and example problems.   About you   completed PhD in Mechanical or Aerospace Engineering or equivalent demonstrated research experience in combustion, compressible or high-speed fluid dynamics, supersonic/reacting flows, computational fluid dynamics and modelling experience using sophisticated computational fluid dynamics solvers, especially direct numerical simulations, laser diagnostics and probability density function approaches experience in developing code and setting up complex numerical simulations ability to work effectively within a multidisciplinary research environment and collaborate with academic researchers, research students and industry partners experienced teacher with an ability to develop and present technical educational and professional development content.   Sponsorship / work rights for Australia You must have unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this employment to be eligible to apply. Visa sponsorship is not available for this appointment.   Pre-employment checks and declarations     Your employment is conditional upon the successful completion of all pre-employment or background checks required for the role in terms satisfactory to the University.  Also, to meet the University’s obligations under the National Higher Education Code to Prevent and Eliminate Gender-Based Violence you will be asked to declare if you have been investigated for, or found to engaged in, sexual harm or gender-based violence in the course of previous employment or in a legal process. Similarly, your ongoing employment is conditional upon the satisfactory maintenance of all relevant clearances and background check requirements. If you do not meet these conditions, the University may take any necessary step, including the termination of your employment.   EEO statement     At the University of Sydney, our shared values are trust, accountability and excellence and we strive to be a place where everyone can thrive. We are committed to creating a University community that thrives through diversity and reflects the wider community that we serve. We deliver on this through our commitment to diversity and inclusion , evidenced by our people and culture programs, as well as key strategies to increase participation and support the careers of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander People , women , people living with a disability , people from culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds , and those who identify as LGBTQIA+ . We welcome applications from candidates from all backgrounds.    We are proud to be recognised as an Australian Workplace Equality Index (AWEI) Platinum Employer. Find out more about our work on diversity and inclusion .    How to apply   Applications (including a cover letter, CV, and any additional supporting documentation) can be submitted via the Apply button at the top of the page.     For employees of the University or contingent workers, please login into your Workday account and navigate to the Career icon on your Dashboard.  Click on USYD Find Jobs and apply.   For a confidential discussion about the role, or if you require reasonable adjustment or any documents in alternate formats, please contact Nicole Feain and Liam Newson, Recruitment Operations by email to recruitment.sea@sydney.edu.au   © The University of Sydney   The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Click to view the Position Description for this role. Applications Close Sunday 15 November 2026 11:59 PM"
    },
    "https://usyd.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/USYD_EXTERNAL_CAREER_SITE/job/Darlington-Campus/Health--Safety-and-Wellbeing-Advisor-Critical-Risk-and-Compliance_0158071-2": {
      "title": "Health, Safety and Wellbeing Advisor Critical Risk and Compliance",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-23\nFull time\n\nFull-time, continuing opportunity Located on Camperdown Campus on Gadigal Country Base Salary from $117,133 plus 17% superannuation About the team   The Health, Safety & Wellbeing (HSW) team plays a critical role in enabling safe, healthy and sustainable ways of working across the University. Through listening, learning and a commitment to continuous improvement, HSW promotes consistent best practice, frameworks and standards across all faculties and business areas. Together, the team helps shape a culture where health, safety and wellbeing are embedded into everyday work, supporting organisational excellence and long-term success. About the opportunity As Health, Safety and Wellbeing Advisor, Critical Risk and Compliance , you will provide scientific and technical support to the HSW Specialists team, with a primary focus on the University’s Chemical Safety and Occupational Hygiene programs. In this role you will undertake compliance activities, inspections, risk assessments and investigations to support the effective management of technical critical risks within the Specialist portfolios, enabling the team to respond to changing priorities, emerging risks and areas of greatest organisational need. Your key responsibilities will be to: support chemical safety and occupational hygiene initiatives support compliance, assurance and risk management activities provide technical advice and stakeholder support support technical critical risk initiatives contribute to governance and continuous improvement. About you tertiary qualifications in a relevant field (e.g. occupational hygiene, chemistry, health and safety, science) demonstrated experience in chemical safety management, including compliance and assurance activities demonstrated experience providing technical advice on occupational hygiene or chemical safety risk controls demonstrated experience developing technical health and safety documentation, including risk assessments, procedures, training materials and occupational hygiene monitoring reports. Sponsorship / work rights for Australia Please note: You must have unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this employment to apply. Visa sponsorship is not available for this appointment. Pre-employment checks and declarations  Your employment is conditional upon the successful completion of all pre-employment or background checks required for the role in terms satisfactory to the University.  Also, to meet the University’s obligations under the National Higher Education Code to Prevent and Eliminate Gender-Based Violence you will be asked to declare if you have been investigated for, or found to engaged in, sexual harm or gender-based violence in the course of previous employment or in a legal process. Similarly, your ongoing employment is conditional upon the satisfactory maintenance of all relevant clearances and background check requirements. If you do not meet these conditions, the University may take any necessary step, including the termination of your employment. EEO statement   At the University of Sydney, our shared values are trust, accountability and excellence and we strive to be a place where everyone can thrive. We are committed to creating a University community that thrives through diversity and reflects the wider community that we serve. We deliver on this through our commitment to diversity and inclusion , evidenced by our people and culture programs, as well as key strategies to increase participation and support the careers of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander People , women , people living with a disability , people from culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds , and those who identify as LGBTIQ+ . We welcome applications from candidates from all backgrounds. We are proud to be recognised as an Australian Workplace Equality Index (AWEI) Gold employer. Find out more about our work on diversity and inclusion .  How to apply Applications (including a cover letter, CV, and any additional supporting documentation) can be submitted via the Apply button at the top of the page.  We understand that everyone applies differently. If you’d prefer to have an informal conversation before applying, we welcome you to get in touch so we can talk through the role and process. In addition, if you require reasonable adjustment or any documents in alternate formats, please contact Sade Mintah via sade.mintah@sydney.edu.au © The University of Sydney The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Click to view the Position Description for this role. Applications Close Sunday 01 November 2026 11:59 PM"
    },
    "https://usyd.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/USYD_EXTERNAL_CAREER_SITE/job/Camperdown-Campus/Associate-Director-Development--Major-Gifts--_0157182-2": {
      "title": "Associate Director Development (Major Gifts))",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-23\nFull time\n\nAs Associate Director of Development, Law you will be responsible for raising gifts that will support projects and programs across the University. There will be a focus on new prospective donors to and on the strategic priorities of the Schools and also building on a number of successful gifts and growing the pipeline of donors. You will identify and engage high net-worth donor prospects, align their interest and passions to the strategic priorities of the University, and successfully solicit multiple gifts with a value of $100,000 or more.  About the School As one of the best law schools in the world for teaching and research, our students learn from globally recognised legal educators and highly respected professional practitioners. Our internationally-renowned researchers are working to deliver real-world change in Australia and overseas.  About you The University values courage and creativity; openness and engagement; inclusion and diversity; and respect and integrity. As such, we see the importance of recruiting talent aligned to these values and are looking for Associate Director of Development, Law who has experience in major gifts fundraising, with a demonstrated ability to identify, cultivate and negotiate $100,000 plus gift opportunities a strong track record of achievement and meeting financial targets experience working in a fast paced and dynamic environment the ability to work autonomously but also is comfortable speaking up and asking for help demonstrated relationship building and strong communication skills with the ability to engage senior internal and external stakeholders has highly developed emotional intelligence – you are self-aware with exceptional interpersonal skills  is a high-achiever and naturally driven by success both professionally and personally has excellent time-management, prioritisation and organisation skills. experienced in and/or passion for Law demonstrated in your cover letter   About us The Advancement Portfolio team at the University of Sydney is one the most successful fundraising programs in Australian higher education. Having completed the nation’s first $1BN fundraising campaign and embarking on the next, the team is not only the finest performing, its success facilitates the largest real-world impact that philanthropic giving makes possible.  Since its inception 175  years ago, the University of Sydney has led to improve the world around us. We believe in education for all and that effective leadership makes lives better. These same values are reflected in our approach to philanthropy and underpin our long-term strategy for excellence and growth. We’re Australia's first university and have an outstanding global reputation for academic and research excellence.     How to apply   Intending applicants are welcome to seek further information about the position and for recruitment-related enquiries, or if you require reasonable adjustment or support filling out this application, please contact  Sarah Scott,  Recruitment Partner (Advancement Portfolio), at  sarah.scott@sydney.edu.au     Please note: Visa sponsorship is not available for this position. For a continuing position, you must be an Australian or New Zealand citizen or an Australian Permanent Resident.   Pre-employment checks Your employment is conditional upon the completion of all role required pre-employment or background checks in terms satisfactory to the University. Similarly, your ongoing employment is conditional upon the satisfactory maintenance of all relevant clearances and background check requirements. If you do not meet these conditions, the University may take any necessary step, including the termination of your employment.   Closing date: 11:59pm Sunday 15th November 2026   The University of Sydney is committed to diversity and social inclusion. Applications from people of culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds; equity target groups including women, people with disabilities, people who identify as LGBTIQ; and people of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander descent, are encouraged.   The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Click to view the Position Description for this role. Applications Close Sunday 15 November 2026 11:59 PM"
    },
    "https://usyd.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/USYD_EXTERNAL_CAREER_SITE/job/Darlington-Campus/Robotics-and-Digital-Fabrication-Officer_0157963-2": {
      "title": "Robotics and Digital Fabrication Officer",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-22\nPart time\n\nPart-time (0.8 FTE), fixed term up to 7 February 2028, located within the Sydney School of Architecture Design and Planning, University of Sydney Use your technical expertise to support innovative teaching, learning, and research activities within the Design Modelling and Fabrication Lab. Base Salary HEO 6, $107,312 p.a. - $115,686 p.a. + 17% superannuation     About the opportunity   The Sydney School of Architecture, Design and Planning is recruiting an experienced Robotics and Digital Fabrication Officer to coordinate the operations of the Robotics Laboratory within the Design Modelling and Fabrication Lab.   The role will provide specialist technical support both in the laboratory and in the field. You will provide instruction and training to students, technical support and advice to researchers, undertaking experimental fabrication work, and develop tooling, workflows, systems and instructional content to provide access to robotic technology .   Your key responsibilities will be to:  provide specialist technical support for teaching, research, and operational activities within the Robotics and Digital Fabrication Laboratories ensure the laboratory environment effectively supports the School's learning, teaching, and research objectives develop, maintain, and enhance laboratory technologies, systems, workflows, and services oversee laboratory compliance with Workplace Health and Safety requirements, promoting a safe and effective working environment undertake fabrication and prototyping projects, including the preparation of associated documentation, reports, and technical records.     About you   We are seeking for an exceptional Robotics and Digital Fabrication Officer who possesses:           a high level of computer and digital technology literacy, including specialist design, 3D modelling, CAD and CAM software relevant to the work area demonstrated experience in a similar role in a fabrication laboratory or education environment practical knowledge of and experience in specialist robotics and digital technology relevant to the work area, industrial automation or control systems proficiency in parametric design and control software relevant to the work area ability to develop and implement safety procedures in an educational environment capability to deliver practical instruction and develop new teaching materials.     Work rights for Australia You must have unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this employment to apply. Visa sponsorship is not available for this appointment.     Pre-employment checks and declarations    Your employment is conditional upon the successful completion of all pre-employment or background checks required for the role in terms satisfactory to the University.  Also, to meet the University’s obligations under the National Higher Education Code to Prevent and Eliminate Gender-Based Violence you will be asked to declare if you have been investigated for, or found to engaged in, sexual harm or gender-based violence in the course of previous employment or in a legal process. Similarly, your ongoing employment is conditional upon the satisfactory maintenance of all relevant clearances and background check requirements. If you do not meet these conditions, the University may take any necessary step, including the termination of your employment.     EEO statement     At the University of Sydney, our shared values are trust, accountability and excellence and we strive to be a place where everyone can thrive. We are committed to creating a University community that thrives through diversity and reflects the wider community that we serve. We deliver on this through our commitment to diversity and inclusion , evidenced by our people and culture programs, as well as key strategies to increase participation and support the careers of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander People , women , people living with a disability , people from culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds , and those who identify as LGBTIQ+ . We welcome applications from candidates from all backgrounds.    We are proud to be recognised as an Australian Workplace Equality Index (AWEI) Gold employer. Find out more about our work on diversity and inclusion .      How to apply   Applications (including a cover letter, CV, and any additional supporting documentation) can be submitted via the Apply button at the top of the page. For employees of the University or contingent workers, please login into your Workday account and navigate to the Career icon on your Dashboard. Click on USYD Find Jobs and apply. For a confidential discussion about the role, or if you require reasonable adjustment or any documents in alternate formats, please contact Helen Efstathiou, Recruitment Consultant, Recruitment Operations on +61 2 8627 7137 or by email helen.efstathiou@sydney.edu.au   © The University of Sydney   The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Click to view the Position Description for this role. Applications Close Sunday 08 November 2026 11:59 PM"
    },
    "https://usyd.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/USYD_EXTERNAL_CAREER_SITE/job/Camperdown-Campus/Lecturer---Medical-Sciences-Immunology-Bacteriology_0156603-1": {
      "title": "Lecturer – Medical Sciences/Immunology/Bacteriology",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-22\nFull time\n\nFull time, fixed term until October 2031   Exciting opportunity to be a Lecturer within the School of Medical Sciences Base Salary starting from $132,533 + 17% superannuation   About the opportunity   The School of Medical Sciences is recognised as one of the leading centres for medical science education in the world. With over 100 years of excellence in education, we are proud to be training the next generation of medical scientists who will be leading members of the medical research community with an innovative integrated curriculum. Our team of researchers lead key internationally competitive research from molecules to medicines, from patient to policy across a range of therapeutic areas.   The School of Medical Sciences is seeking a Lecturer – Medical Sciences/Immunology/Bacteriology to make significant contributions to teaching and learning while engaging in educational leadership within the discipline. The role will contribute to the design, delivery, evaluation and continuous improvement of high-quality educational experiences that support student learning outcomes and align with University and Faculty expectations.   Working collaboratively with academic colleagues, the Lecturer will contribute to curriculum development, innovative teaching practices and educational initiatives across Medical Sciences, Immunology and Bacteriology. The role will also undertake scholarly activity, develop research skills and contribute to the advancement of discipline knowledge, particularly as it relates to teaching and learning practices.     Your key responsibilities will be to:   undertake independent teaching in Medical Sciences, Immunology and Bacteriology, contributing to outstanding educational design, delivery, evaluation and outcomes engage in high-quality teaching and learning experiences that meet University expectations and standards for education design and deliver quality units of study, learning activities and curriculum content that support student needs and meet curriculum requirements contribute to the design and delivery of multidisciplinary work-integrated learning units and educational experiences develop and refine independent research skills under the supervision of more senior academic staff conduct research or scholarly activity independently or as part of a team, contributing to discipline knowledge, particularly in relation to teaching and learning within the discipline engage in supervision of research students in accordance with School, Faculty and University expectations apply scholarship and knowledge of contemporary pedagogical practice to innovate and enhance education practices undertake academic administration activities that support the delivery of high-quality teaching and learning coordinate activities of academic staff and provide training, guidance and mentorship to less experienced academic staff contribute to a positive workplace culture that values diversity of thought, collaboration, excellence and achieving outcomes contribute to the strategic priorities of the University, Faculty and School engage beyond the University through conferences, seminars, outreach activities and professional networks undertake other duties appropriate within the classification level as required.   About you   doctorate or PhD in a relevant field demonstrated understanding of Medical Sciences, Immunology and/or Bacteriology within the discipline experience conducting original research and engaging in scholarly activity experience in teaching and learning, including participation in team teaching and delivery across a variety of settings demonstrated success in designing, implementing and evaluating teaching and learning initiatives developing networks with key academic, industry and professional institutions, partners and stakeholders experience supervising or mentoring students or colleagues strong planning and organisational skills with the ability to manage competing priorities excellent communication skills with the ability to engage effectively with students, academic colleagues and stakeholders commitment to innovative teaching practices, educational excellence and continuous improvement.   Sponsorship / work rights for Australia   Work Rights: you must have unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this employment to apply. Visa sponsorship is not available for this appointment.     Pre-employment checks   Your employment is conditional upon the successful completion of all pre-employment or background checks required for the role in terms satisfactory to the University. Also, to meet the University’s obligations under the National Higher Education Code to Prevent and Eliminate Gender-Based Violence you will be asked to declare if you have been investigated for, or found to engaged in, sexual harm or gender-based violence in the course of previous employment or in a legal process. Similarly, your ongoing employment is conditional upon the satisfactory maintenance of all relevant clearances and background check requirements. If you do not meet these conditions, the University may take any necessary step, including the termination of your employment.     EEO statement     At the University of Sydney, our shared values are trust, accountability and excellence and we strive to be a place where everyone can thrive. We are committed to creating a University community that thrives through diversity and reflects the wider community that we serve. We deliver on this through our commitment to diversity and inclusion , evidenced by our people and culture programs, as well as key strategies to increase participation and support the careers of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander People , women , people living with a disability , people from culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds , and those who identify as LGBTIQ+ . We welcome applications from candidates from all backgrounds.    We are proud to be recognised as an Australian Workplace Equality Index (AWEI) Gold employer. Find out more about our work on diversity and inclusion .    How to apply   Applications (including a cover letter, CV, and any additional supporting documentation) can be submitted via the Apply button at the top of the page.     For employees of the University or contingent workers, please login into your Workday account and navigate to the Career icon on your Dashboard.  Click on USYD Find Jobs and apply.   Click to view the Position Description for this role. Applications Close Monday 09 November 2026 11:59 PM"
