      "title": "Health, Safety and Wellbeing Manager STEMM Partnering",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-22\nFull time\n\nFull-time, continuing opportunity Located on Camperdown Campus on Gadigal Country Base Salary from $162,528 plus 17% superannuation About the team The Health, Safety & Wellbeing (HSW) team plays a critical role in enabling safe, healthy and sustainable ways of working across the University. Through listening, learning and a commitment to continuous improvement, HSW promotes consistent best practice, frameworks and standards across all faculties and business areas. Together, the team helps shape a culture where health, safety and wellbeing are embedded into everyday work, supporting organisational excellence and long-term success. About the opportunity  Reporting to the Director, HSW Partnering, you will provide operational leadership for health and safety partnering services across multiple portfolios and organisational units, driving consistent implementation of the University’s Health and Safety Management System and central HSW initiatives to strengthen culture, compliance and performance. As the HSW Manager Partnering STEMM , you will be accountable for the leadership, performance and capability of a HSW Partnering team, including workforce planning, role clarity, capability development and the ongoing effectiveness of the partnering model to deliver enterprise-wide, risk-based and high-impact HSW outcomes aligned to organisational priorities.    Working in close partnership with HSW Manager Systems & Enablement and the HSW Manager corresponding role, and other relevant Centre of Expertise roles, you will ensure HSW frameworks, systems, and initiatives are effectively translated into operational practice, including providing feedback on usability and effectiveness and contributing operational insight to inform continuous improvement of the HSMS, governance frameworks, and digital platforms. Your key responsibilities will be to: lead HSW Partnering Service Delivery drive continuous improvement and health and safety performance provide strategic HSW leadership and advice ensure effective implementation of the Health and Safety Management System oversee the daily delivery of HSW Partnering Services with a focus on agile, responsive, outcome driven service delivery build capability, culture and deliver strategic initiatives. About you tertiary qualifications in relevant field as appropriate for nature of portfolio or equivalent combination of training and experience e.g. STEMM relevant qualifications ICAM Lead Investigator or equivalent demonstrated experience leading and developing high-performing teams, driving capability uplift, accountability and consistent service delivery  proven ability to engage, influence and build strong relationships with senior stakeholders and coordinate cross-functional responses in complex organisational environments demonstrated experience designing strategic safety, health and wellbeing programs with strong knowledge of WH&S legislation and Codes of Practice extensive experience managing serious, high-risk or complex incidents, including driving systemic improvements, governance and regulatory engagement experience leading complex projects or initiatives, delivering organisational change and continuous improvement outcomes. Sponsorship / work rights for Australia Please note: You must have unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this employment to apply. Visa sponsorship is not available for this appointment. Pre-employment checks and declarations  Your employment is conditional upon the successful completion of all pre-employment or background checks required for the role in terms satisfactory to the University.  Also, to meet the University’s obligations under the National Higher Education Code to Prevent and Eliminate Gender-Based Violence you will be asked to declare if you have been investigated for, or found to engaged in, sexual harm or gender-based violence in the course of previous employment or in a legal process. Similarly, your ongoing employment is conditional upon the satisfactory maintenance of all relevant clearances and background check requirements. If you do not meet these conditions, the University may take any necessary step, including the termination of your employment. EEO statement   At the University of Sydney, our shared values are trust, accountability and excellence and we strive to be a place where everyone can thrive. We are committed to creating a University community that thrives through diversity and reflects the wider community that we serve. We deliver on this through our commitment to diversity and inclusion , evidenced by our people and culture programs, as well as key strategies to increase participation and support the careers of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander People , women , people living with a disability , people from culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds , and those who identify as LGBTIQ+ . We welcome applications from candidates from all backgrounds. We are proud to be recognised as an Australian Workplace Equality Index (AWEI) Gold employer. Find out more about our work on diversity and inclusion .  How to apply Applications (including a cover letter, CV, and any additional supporting documentation) can be submitted via the Apply button at the top of the page.  We understand that everyone applies differently. If you’d prefer to have an informal conversation before applying, we welcome you to get in touch so we can talk through the role and process. In addition, if you require reasonable adjustment or any documents in alternate formats, please contact Sade Mintah via sade.mintah@sydney.edu.au . © The University of Sydney The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Click to view the Position Description for this role. Applications Close Thursday 05 November 2026 11:59 PM"
    },
    "https://usyd.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/USYD_EXTERNAL_CAREER_SITE/job/Darlington-Campus/Safer-Communities-Team-Lead---Education_0158406-1": {
      "title": "Safer Communities Team Lead - Education",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-22\nFull time\n\nFull time, fixed term until 25th February 2028. Located on Darlington Campus. Lead a specialist team supporting students and staff impacted by gender-based violence and other critical incidents. Base Salary $152,045 - $160,430 (HEO9) + 17% superannuation   About the opportunity   As the Safer Communities Team Lead , you will be responsible for the leading a team of Safer Communities Case Officers, who are responsible for supporting current /former students and visitors who are impacted by gender-based violence, sexual harm, domestic/ family violence, bullying, harassment in and issues relating to modern slavery.   Working within Student Wellbeing, you will provide leadership, guidance and oversight to a team of Safer Communities Case Officers, ensuring the delivery of trauma-informed support and effective case management for members of the University community.   You will coordinate, monitor and report on disclosures and incidents, while contributing to prevention, education and continuous improvement initiatives across the Safer Communities function.   Your key responsibilities will be to: lead the continuous improvement of the Safer Communities Case Officer team by developing, coaching, and mentoring employees, and providing clinical management and case management guidance be responsible for planning and coordination of triage, risk assessment, and resource allocation across the team to ensure the timely, effective provision of support and trauma informed care for victim/survivors for the management of disclosures relating to gender-based violence, sexual harm, domestic and family violence, bullying and harassment and issues relating to modern slavery affecting members of the University community work in partnership with the Manager, Safer Communities to act as a key escalation point for complex and high-risk cases, particularly those involving staff, contributing to clinical decision-making and case oversight support coordinated engagement and communication with key stakeholders across the University to inform initial response and ensure consistent case management that meets legislative requirements, University policies and procedures, and is aligned with best practice and trauma informed principles ensure data integrity and interpretation and provides specialist advice to inform reporting under the Higher Education National Code to Prevent and Respond to Gender-based Violence, as well as annual reporting and reporting to University Executive and Senate committees hold primary responsibility for comprehensive risk assessments for all disclosures, integrating professional judgement with established risk assessment frameworks to evaluate and mitigate risk to individuals and the broader University community participate in an on-call roster, including weekends, in line with established escalation pathways, duty of care obligations, and the requirements of the Higher Education National Code to Prevent and Respond to Gender-based Violence.   About you   tertiary qualifications in Psychology, Social Work, Counselling or a related discipline, or an equivalent combination of education and experience extensive experience supporting people impacted by sexual assault, domestic and family violence, gender-based violence or other trauma-related matters demonstrated experience leading and mentoring staff within a complex support, wellbeing, health or community services environment experience managing high-risk and sensitive cases, including escalation management and risk assessment strong knowledge of relevant legislation, frameworks and best-practice approaches relating to sexual misconduct, domestic and family violence, and trauma-informed practice exceptional communication, stakeholder engagement and relationship management skills sound judgement, resilience and the ability to manage competing priorities in a complex and fast-paced environment The Safer Communities Team Lead will regularly engage with individuals who have experienced traumatic events and may be exposed to distressing information or materials. The role requires the ability to manage this exposure in a professional manner, maintain appropriate confidentiality, professional boundaries, and utilise reflective practice, supervision and wellbeing strategies to support personal resilience. Sponsorship / work rights for Australia You must have unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this employment to be eligible to apply. Visa sponsorship is not available for this appointment.   Pre-employment checks   Your employment is conditional upon the successful completion of all pre-employment or background checks required for the role in terms satisfactory to the University.  Also, to meet the University’s obligations under the National Higher Education Code to Prevent and Respond to Gender-Based Violence you will be asked to declare if you have been investigated for, or found to engaged in, sexual harm or gender-based violence in the course of previous employment or in a legal process. Similarly, your ongoing employment is conditional upon the satisfactory maintenance of all relevant clearances and background check requirements. If you do not meet these conditions, the University may take any necessary step, including the termination of your employment.   This position is designated as involving child-related work. To undertake or remain in this position, you are required to apply for and obtain a Working with Children Check clearance in accordance with the Child Protection (Working with Children) Act 2012.   EEO statement     At the University of Sydney, our shared values are trust, accountability and excellence and we strive to be a place where everyone can thrive. We are committed to creating a University community that thrives through diversity and reflects the wider community that we serve. We deliver on this through our commitment to diversity and inclusion , evidenced by our people and culture programs, as well as key strategies to increase participation and support the careers of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander People , women , people living with a disability , people from culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds , and those who identify as LGBTIQ+ . We welcome applications from candidates from all backgrounds.    We are proud to be recognised as an Australian Workplace Equality Index (AWEI) Gold employer. Find out more about our work on diversity and inclusion .    How to apply   Applications (including a cover letter, CV, and any additional supporting documentation) can be submitted via the Apply button at the top of the page.     For employees of the University or contingent workers, please login into your Workday account and navigate to the Career icon on your Dashboard.  Click on USYD Find Jobs and apply.   For a confidential discussion about the role, or if you require reasonable adjustment or any documents in alternate formats, please contact Nicole Turle, Recruitment Operations by email to nicole.turle@sydney.edu.au   © The University of Sydney   The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Click to view the Position Description for this role. Applications Close Wednesday 04 November 2026 11:59 PM"
    },
    "https://usyd.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/USYD_EXTERNAL_CAREER_SITE/job/Darlington-Campus/Workplace-Compliance-Advisor_0157850-3": {
      "title": "Workplace Compliance Advisor",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-21\nFull time\n\nx 2 Full time continuing roles available Play a vital role to support Workplace Compliance in the delivery of technical advice applicable to the University’s Enterprise Agreement, legislation and University policies Base Salary $162,528 + 17% superannuation   About the opportunity   The Workplace Compliance team, in the broader Workplace Relations and Compliance unit serves as the University's centre of expertise for workplace compliance matters. The Workplace Compliance Advisor is responsible for providing high-quality technical advice, related to the University’s Enterprise Agreement, relevant workplace legislation and University policies with a particular focus on employee entitlements, payment practices, work allocation and compliance risk.   The team maintains strong relationships with key stakeholders and works with both professional and academic staff across the University to drive the prevention, early intervention and resolution of workplace compliance matters. Key priorities include the implementation and support of strategic compliance initiatives, proactive compliance monitoring and continuous improvement to ensure the University meets its compliance obligations.   The Workplace Compliance Advisor is a senior technical specialist responsible for providing expert interpretation and strategic advice on the University's Enterprise Agreement, employment legislation and workplace compliance obligations. Operating with a high degree of autonomy, the role manages complex and often sensitive compliance matters, identifies emerging risks, and provides practical, evidence-based recommendations that influence decision-making across the institution.   The position requires highly developed Enterprise Agreement expertise and the ability to apply complex industrial instruments to diverse and often ambiguous scenarios. The role works closely with leaders across faculties and professional units to support compliant workforce practices, mitigate risk, and drive continuous improvement in systems, processes and governance frameworks.     Your key responsibilities will be to:   advise on Enterprise Agreement, legislation and policy compliance, including on complex and high-risk matters lead complex investigations and provide clear findings and recommendations develop, improve and support workplace compliance frameworks and processes analyse compliance data and deliver insights to leaders build strong relationships across HR, faculties and schools support capability uplift across workplace compliance teams. identify emerging compliance risks and develop practical mitigation strategies for faculties and professional units prepare high-quality briefings, reports and recommendations for senior leaders to support decision-making on complex workplace compliance issues contribute to the development of University-wide compliance strategies, governance frameworks and remediation initiatives act as a recognised subject matter expert on Enterprise Agreement interpretation and entitlement-related matters.     About you   demonstrated expertise interpreting and applying complex Enterprise Agreements, employment legislation and industrial instruments within a large and complex organisation excellent communicator with strong written, verbal and stakeholder engagement skills experienced in conducting investigations and preparing clear, evidence based reports skilled in analysing compliance data, identifying risks, and making informed recommendations able to work autonomously, exercise sound judgement, and manage complex matters with discretion collaborative team member who supports capability uplift and builds trusted relationships across the University. experience providing strategic advice to senior stakeholders on complex workplace relations and compliance matters demonstrated ability to identify, assess and manage compliance risks, and develop practical solutions that balance legal compliance with operational requirements highly developed analytical skills with the ability to interpret complex legislative, industrial and operational information and translate it into clear, practical advice substantial experience in workplace compliance, workplace relations, employment law, industrial relations or a related discipline, including demonstrated experience interpreting Enterprise Agreements and managing complex compliance matters.     Sponsorship / work rights for Australia   You must have unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this employment to be eligible to apply. Visa sponsorship is not available for this appointment.   Pre-employment checks and declarations     Your employment is conditional upon the successful completion of all pre-employment or background checks required for the role in terms satisfactory to the University.  Also, to meet the University’s obligations under the National Higher Education Code to Prevent and Eliminate Gender-Based Violence you will be asked to declare if you have been investigated for, or found to engaged in, sexual harm or gender-based violence in the course of previous employment or in a legal process. Similarly, your ongoing employment is conditional upon the satisfactory maintenance of all relevant clearances and background check requirements. If you do not meet these conditions, the University may take any necessary step, including the termination of your employment.   EEO statement     At the University of Sydney, our shared values are trust, accountability and excellence and we strive to be a place where everyone can thrive. We are committed to creating a University community that thrives through diversity and reflects the wider community that we serve. We deliver on this through our commitment to diversity and inclusion , evidenced by our people and culture programs, as well as key strategies to increase participation and support the careers of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander People , women , people living with a disability , people from culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds , and those who identify as LGBTQIA+ . We welcome applications from candidates from all backgrounds.    We are proud to be recognised as an Australian Workplace Equality Index (AWEI) Platinum Employer. Find out more about our work on diversity and inclusion .    How to apply   Applications (including a cover letter, CV, and any additional supporting documentation) can be submitted via the Apply button at the top of the page.     For employees of the University or contingent workers, please login into your Workday account and navigate to the Career icon on your Dashboard.  Click on USYD Find Jobs and apply.   For a confidential discussion about the role, or if you require reasonable adjustment or any documents in alternate formats, please contact Victoria Ford-Rogers Recruitment Operations by email to victoria.ford-rogers@sydney.edu.au   © The University of Sydney   The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Click to view the Position Description for this role. Applications Close Wednesday 04 November 2026 11:59 PM"
    },
    "https://usyd.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/USYD_EXTERNAL_CAREER_SITE/job/Darlington-Campus/Postdoctoral-Research-Associate-in-Carbon-Neutral-Demand-Forecasting_0158347-1": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Research Associate in Carbon-Neutral Demand Forecasting",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-21\nFull time\n\n2 x Full time, 3 year Fixed-Term positions available Located at the Faculty of Engineering, Camperdown Campus Base Salary $113,634 - $125,896 + 17% superannuation    About the opportunity   An exciting opportunity exists for two Postdoctoral Research Associate to work on an Australian Research Council (ARC) funded research project in carbon-neutral energy demand forecasting, in partnership with Mitsubishi Heavy Industries (MHI). This project supports infrastructure planning and policy for a net-zero future by developing a high-resolution, bottom-up model of future energy demand that incorporates electrification, fuel switching (hydrogen and ammonia), and behavioural change.    You will contribute to the design and implementation of the DEEPSyM-Grid platform, previously developed in collaboration between the University of Sydney and MHI, enabling precinct-scale modelling of clean energy demand and scenario-based analysis for Australia's energy transition.    You will be jointly based in the University of Sydney School of Electrical and Computer Engineering and School of Chemical and Biomolecular Engineering , led by Profs. Gregor Verbic and Ali Abbas.    Your key responsibilities will be to:    Develop, implement, and maintain an agent-based and engineering-driven demand forecasting framework within the DEEPSyM-Grid platform  Undertake scenario-based analysis and quantify uncertainty in demand projections (e.g. policy settings, technology adoption)  Engage with industry stakeholders, including MHI and other project partners, to co-design and validate modelling assumptions and outputs  Align demand modelling with supply-side system planning tools (e.g. PLEXOS) in collaboration with industry partners  Disseminate research through high-quality journal articles, conference papers, workshops, and policy briefings    About you    The University values courage and creativity, openness and engagement, inclusion and diversity, and respect and integrity. As such, we seek a Postdoctoral Research Associate who has:    A PhD (or near completion) in power and energy systems, process systems engineering, industrial decarbonisation, energy economics, or a related discipline  Strong expertise in at least one of: demand modelling, energy systems optimisation, agent-based modelling, or techno-economic forecasting  Proficiency in scientific programming (preferably Julia, Python or Matlab)  Competence in data analysis and probabilistic modelling techniques  Experience with energy modelling tools (Plexos knowledge is a plus)  Demonstrated ability to conduct high-quality research independently and in collaborative settings  Experience in writing and publishing research in international peer-reviewed journals  Strong verbal and written communication skills  Prior experience working with industry or government stakeholders is desirable      Pre-employment checks and declarations     Your employment is conditional upon the successful completion of all pre-employment or background checks required for the role in terms satisfactory to the University.  Also, to meet the University’s obligations under the National Higher Education Code to Prevent and Eliminate Gender-Based Violence you will be asked to declare if you have been investigated for, or found to engaged in, sexual harm or gender-based violence in the course of previous employment or in a legal process. Similarly, your ongoing employment is conditional upon the satisfactory maintenance of all relevant clearances and background check requirements. If you do not meet these conditions, the University may take any necessary step, including the termination of your employment.   EEO statement     At the University of Sydney, our shared values are trust, accountability and excellence and we strive to be a place where everyone can thrive. We are committed to creating a University community that thrives through diversity and reflects the wider community that we serve. We deliver on this through our commitment to diversity and inclusion , evidenced by our people and culture programs, as well as key strategies to increase participation and support the careers of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander People , women , people living with a disability , people from culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds , and those who identify as LGBTQIA+ . We welcome applications from candidates from all backgrounds.    We are proud to be recognised as an Australian Workplace Equality Index (AWEI) Platinum Employer. Find out more about our work on diversity and inclusion .    How to apply   Applications (including a cover letter, CV, and any additional supporting documentation) can be submitted via the Apply button at the top of the page.     For employees of the University or contingent workers, please login into your Workday account and navigate to the Career icon on your Dashboard.  Click on USYD Find Jobs and apply.   For a confidential discussion about the role, or if you require reasonable adjustment or any documents in alternate formats, please contact Rebecca Astar or Cherie Goodwin by email to recruitment.sea@sydney.edu.au     © The University of Sydney   The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Click to view the Position Description for this role. Applications Close Thursday 19 November 2026 11:59 PM"
    },
    "https://usyd.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/USYD_EXTERNAL_CAREER_SITE/job/Camperdown-Campus/Lawyer--Intellectual-Property--Research-and-Commercial-Law_0141110-1": {
      "title": "Lawyer, Intellectual Property, Research and Commercial Law",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-21\nFull time\n\nFull time, continuing opportunity Located at the Michael Spence Building (F23) on the Camperdown Campus Base Salary $162,528 + 17% superannuation About the opportunity The University of Sydney’s Office of General Counsel (OGC) is looking to recruit an experienced and motivated Lawyer, Intellectual Property, Research and Commercial Law to join the IP, Research and Commercial Law Team. The Lawyer, Intellectual Property, Research and Commercial Law will provide clear, practical and timely legal advice to primarily support the Deputy Vice-Chancellor (Research) and the Research Portfolio progress the University’s research and commercialisation activities. Working closely with stakeholders across the Research Portfolio—including Pre-Award and Pipeline, Post-Award (Grants and Contracts), Clinical Trial Contracts, Commercialisation and Investments—the successful candidate will manage and support senior lawyers with a diverse range of matters involving the review, drafting and negotiation of research and commercial agreements, support for strategic projects, commercialisation initiatives, and other legal issues as they arise. All work will be undertaken with a commercial and strategic mindset, aligned with OGC’s Purpose and Strategy. The Lawyer, Intellectual Property, Research and Commercial Law will work under the direction and support of a Senior Lawyer in the Intellectual Property, Research and Commercial Law Team, and will collaborate closely with senior colleagues across the team and the wider OGC, including the Director, Intellectual Property, Research and Commercial Law and the General Counsel. Your key responsibilities will be to: support Research Portfolio clients navigate complexity and give decision makers confidence in pursuing the University’s objectives and strategy provide clear, timely, strategic legal advice to support the activities of the Research Portfolio, with the support of Senior Lawyers and the Director, IP, Research and Commercial Law draft, review and negotiate a wide range of research, commercialisation and corporate agreements (e.g. funding agreements, research collaboration agreements and sub-contracts , clinical trial research agreements, data and material transfer agreements, professional services agreements (e.g. consultancy, research services and testing), IP agreements (e.g. assignments, options, licenses) and transactions as well as corporate agreements to support spin outs (e.g. SAFEs and shareholders agreements ) to promote the University’s strategic interests and objectives demonstrate excellent drafting skills using plain English drafting and assist with template creation and development manage external legal services, as required  innovative, with a keenness for use of legal AI tools to support work as appropriate contribute to other legal work, projects and initiatives as part of the broader remit of the IP, Research and Commercial Law team and OGC, as required  advise on legal developments which may impact University policies and practices, particularly relating to research and commercialisation. About you qualifications in law and admission to practice in Australia, and hold a current practicing certificate a minimum of 6+ years post admission legal experience is desired  experience and practice in intellectual property law, commercialisation and commercial law, corporate law and research experience ( with national security (e.g. sanctions, export controls, etc.) and clinical trials experience or knowledge looked favourably upon), with plain English drafting, negotiation and matter management skills an understanding of the legal and regulatory framework relating to research and the major Australian public funding schemes available for research highly developed interpersonal and communication skills, with the ability to operate with tact and professionalism across complex internal and external environments  team player – experienced collaborating in a team environment  an agile and flexible approach that is able to pivot and contribute to overall program success growth mindset – not afraid to challenge the status quo. Sponsorship / work rights for Australia You must have unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this employment to be eligible to apply. Visa sponsorship is not available for this appointment. Pre-employment checks and declarations  Your employment is conditional upon the successful completion of all pre-employment or background checks required for the role in terms satisfactory to the University.  Also, to meet the University’s obligations under the National Higher Education Code to Prevent and Eliminate Gender-Based Violence you will be asked to declare if you have been investigated for, or found to engaged in, sexual harm or gender-based violence in the course of previous employment or in a legal process. Similarly, your ongoing employment is conditional upon the satisfactory maintenance of all relevant clearances and background check requirements. If you do not meet these conditions, the University may take any necessary step, including the termination of your employment. EEO statement   At the University of Sydney, our shared values are trust, accountability and excellence and we strive to be a place where everyone can thrive. We are committed to creating a University community that thrives through diversity and reflects the wider community that we serve. We deliver on this through our commitment to diversity and inclusion , evidenced by our people and culture programs, as well as key strategies to increase participation and support the careers of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander People , women , people living with a disability , people from culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds , and those who identify as LGBTQIA+ . We welcome applications from candidates from all backgrounds.  We are proud to be recognised as an Australian Workplace Equality Index (AWEI) Platinum Employer. Find out more about our work on diversity and inclusion .  How to apply Applications (including a cover letter, CV, and any additional supporting documentation) can be submitted via the Apply button at the top of the page.   For employees of the University or contingent workers, please login into your Workday account and navigate to the Career icon on your Dashboard.  Click on USYD Find Jobs and apply. For a confidential discussion about the role, or if you require reasonable adjustment or any documents in alternate formats, please contact Nayomi Siriwardane, Recruitment Operations by email nayomi.siriwardane@sydney.edu.au . © The University of Sydney The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Click to view the Position Description for this role. Applications Close Wednesday 04 November 2026 11:59 PM"
    },
    "https://usyd.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/USYD_EXTERNAL_CAREER_SITE/job/Brownlow-Hill/Veterinary-Registrar-in-Equine-Sports-Medicine_0157001": {
      "title": "Veterinary Registrar / Specialist in Equine Sports Medicine and Rehabilitation",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-21\nFull time\n\nFull time fixed term Parental Leave Cover until 6th March 2028   Opportunity to work as part of a dynamic and growing equine team     Base Salary HEO8/9 (38 hrs p/w) $142,344 - $174,177 p.a + 15% Specialist Loading* + 17% superannuation       About the opportunity     Reporting directly to the Head Equine Unit, the Veterinary Registrar / Specialist in Equine Sports Medicine and Rehabilitation position will form part of the Camden Equine Centre  (CEC) within the UVTHC in providing equine sports medicine and rehabilitation services for the hospital. This position is designed to assist in developing and growing the equine unit’s referral practice, particularly performance horses, and participate in the clinical teaching of DVM students. This position is full time and covers some weekend and after-hours duty, following a rota with all clinicians within the equine unit for ambulatory work and inpatients.     Your key responsibilities will be to:      provide a specialist level sports medicine service for primary accession, secondary and tertiary referral cases   grow the client base by excellent client and referring practice communications and service   oversee the supervision or postgraduate /clinical master students   mentoring of junior clinical staff   provide clinical instruction to DVM students in the areas of general and specialist practice   co-ordinate and provide professional guidance to non-specialist equine clinicians in relation to cases.   CEC is recognised as a centre of clinical excellence for equine sports medicine in Australasia and a leader in the diagnosis, treatment and rehabilitation of horses with sports related injuries and illnesses through innovative research in the field whilst training the veterinarians of the future.      To learn more about the Sydney School of Veterinary Science click here       About you     a Veterinary Degree registrable in NSW   postgraduate qualifications in sports medicine and rehabilitation   more than 12 months experience following a residency program but have not yet achieved specialist status OR may have membership of the ANZCVS or similar post graduate level qualifications as well as additional advanced training excellent equine clinical skills   an ability to develop referral and primary accession practice   an ability to provide clinical instruction to postgraduate and DVM students   a willingness to mentor junior clinical staff and provide clinical instruction to postgraduate and undergraduate students   a willingness to be on call for after hours and ambulatory services.     Veterinary Specialist in Equine - Sports Medicine & Rehabilitation (HEO level 9) who is a veterinarian with at least partial responsibility for the management of a unit or division, including the management of a range of staff working within the relevant discipline or sub-discipline:       a degree in veterinary science registrable in New South Wales, Australia   a Diplomate or Fellowship permitting registration with the NSW Veterinary Scientists Board as a Specialist in Equine / Large Animal Sports Medicine & Rehabilitation (Keith Merchant)     The commencement level will be determined based on the candidate’s skills, experience and knowledge.       The 15% veterinary clinical specialist loading is available once registered as specialist by NSW Veterinary Practitioners Board.       The key benefits     be stimulated by an academic environment focused on teaching and research   opportunity for you to learn and enhance your skills and experience in a unique teaching and research environment of a veterinary teaching hospital   salary packaging available   access to employee assistance programs (EAP) at no cost   access to fitness passport, as well as discounted rates on university sports and fitness centres     Sponsorship / work rights for Australia     You must have unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this employment to be eligible to apply. Visa sponsorship is not available for this appointment.     Pre-employment checks and declarations       Your employment is conditional upon the successful completion of all pre-employment or background checks required for the role in terms satisfactory to the University.  Also, to meet the University’s obligations under the National Higher Education Code to Prevent and Eliminate Gender-Based Violence you will be asked to declare if you have been investigated for, or found to engaged in, sexual harm or gender-based violence in the course of previous employment or in a legal process. Similarly, your ongoing employment is conditional upon the satisfactory maintenance of all relevant clearances and background check requirements. If you do not meet these conditions, the University may take any necessary step, including the termination of your employment.     EEO statement         At the University of Sydney, our shared values are trust, accountability and excellence and we strive to be a place where everyone can thrive. We are committed to creating a University community that thrives through diversity and reflects the wider community that we serve. We deliver on this through our commitment to diversity and inclusion , evidenced by our people and culture programs, as well as key strategies to increase participation and support the careers of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander People , women , people living with a disability , people from culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds , and those who identify as LGBTQIA+ . We welcome applications from candidates from all backgrounds.        We are proud to be recognised as an Australian Workplace Equality Index (AWEI) Platinum Employer. Find out more about our work on diversity and inclusion .       How to apply     Applications (including a cover letter, CV, and any additional supporting documentation) can be submitted via the Apply button at the top of the page.       For employees of the University or contingent workers, please login into your Workday account and navigate to the Career icon on your Dashboard.  Click on USYD Find Jobs and apply.     For a confidential discussion about the role, or if you require reasonable adjustment or any documents in alternate formats, please contact Chris Masaoka, Recruitment Operations by email to recruitment.sea@sydney.edu.au.     © The University of Sydney     The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment.   Position Description Veterinary Registrar in Equine - Sports Medicine & Rehabilitation Position Description Veterinary Specialist in Equine - Sports Medicine & Rehabilitation Applications Close Sunday 22 November 2026 11:59 PM"
    },
    "https://usyd.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/USYD_EXTERNAL_CAREER_SITE/job/Camperdown-Campus/Executive-Officer-for-the-Chief-Governance-Officer_0158348-1": {
      "title": "Executive Officer for the Chief Governance Officer",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-21\nPart time\n\nPart-time (0.6 FTE, 21 hours per week), continuing opportunity Influence strategic decision-making across governance, Senate and executive operations HEO 9 $152,045 - $160,430 (pro rata) plus 17% superannuation     About the opportunity    The Executive Officer for the Chief Governance Officer is a pivotal role providing high-level executive support, strategic coordination and operational leadership within the University Governance Office. Supporting the Chief Governance Officer, you will manage a diverse portfolio spanning complex diary and email management, governance operations, Senate activities and strategic projects that contribute to the University's priorities.   This is far more than a traditional executive support position. Working closely with senior leaders, Fellows of Senate and key stakeholders across the University, you will lead operational initiatives, coordinate governance activities and oversee a range of projects that support effective decision-making and organisational outcomes. You will also partner with Finance to support workforce planning and forecasting activities, ensuring operational priorities are aligned with strategic objectives.   The role offers exposure to some of the University's most significant governance activities, including Senate projects, succession planning processes and executive engagement initiatives. With responsibility for navigating complex priorities and sensitive matters, you will play a key role in ensuring the Governance Office operates efficiently, professionally and strategically, while deputising for the Executive Officer to the Chancellor as required.   Key responsibilities   Provide comprehensive executive support to the Chief Governance Officer, including complex diary, email and workflow management. Lead and coordinate operational and strategic projects across the University Governance Office. Support Senate operations and bespoke governance projects, including succession planning, due diligence activities and interview coordination. Partner with Finance and internal stakeholders to support workforce planning, forecasting and resource management activities. Build and maintain effective relationships with senior executives, Fellows of Senate and key internal and external stakeholders. Deputise for the Executive Officer to the Chancellor and provide broader support across governance functions as required.   About you   Significant experience in an Executive Officer, Senior Executive Assistant or similar role supporting senior leaders within a complex organisation. Demonstrated ability to lead projects, manage competing priorities and deliver outcomes in a fast-paced environment. Strong understanding of governance, committee, board or executive office operations, with experience managing sensitive and confidential matters. Highly developed stakeholder management and relationship-building skills, with the ability to influence and work effectively across all levels of an organisation. Exceptional written communication skills, including experience preparing executive correspondence, reports, briefings and governance documentation. Strong commercial and operational acumen, with experience supporting planning, forecasting, resource management or business operations activities.     Sponsorship / work rights for Australia   You must have unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this employment to be eligible to apply. Visa sponsorship is not available for this appointment.     Pre-employment checks and declarations     Your employment is conditional upon the successful completion of all pre-employment or background checks required for the role in terms satisfactory to the University.  Also, to meet the University’s obligations under the National Higher Education Code to Prevent and Eliminate Gender-Based Violence you will be asked to declare if you have been investigated for, or found to engaged in, sexual harm or gender-based violence in the course of previous employment or in a legal process. Similarly, your ongoing employment is conditional upon the satisfactory maintenance of all relevant clearances and background check requirements. If you do not meet these conditions, the University may take any necessary step, including the termination of your employment.   This position is designated as involving child-related work. To undertake or remain in this position, you are required to apply for and obtain a Working with Children Check clearance in accordance with the Child Protection (Working with Children) Act 2012.     EEO statement     At the University of Sydney, our shared values are trust, accountability and excellence and we strive to be a place where everyone can thrive. We are committed to creating a University community that thrives through diversity and reflects the wider community that we serve. We deliver on this through our commitment to diversity and inclusion , evidenced by our people and culture programs, as well as key strategies to increase participation and support the careers of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander People , women , people living with a disability , people from culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds , and those who identify as LGBTQIA+ . We welcome applications from candidates from all backgrounds.    We are proud to be recognised as an Australian Workplace Equality Index (AWEI) Platinum Employer. Find out more about our work on diversity and inclusion .      How to apply   Applications (including a cover letter, CV, and any additional supporting documentation) can be submitted via the Apply button at the top of the page.    For a confidential discussion about the role, or if you require reasonable adjustment or support filling out this application, please contact Kristy Fairweather, Recruitment Operations, by email to kristy.fairweather@sydney.edu.au   © The University of Sydney   The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment.   Click to view the Position Description for this role. Applications Close Sunday 01 November 2026 11:59 PM"
    },
    "https://usyd.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/USYD_EXTERNAL_CAREER_SITE/job/Darlington-Campus/Assistant-Development-Manager---Student-Accommodation_0157852-1": {
      "title": "Assistant Development Manager - Student Accommodation",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-21\nFull time\n\n2 x full time, 3 and 5-year fixed term positions. Located at the Services Building (G12) on the Darlington Campus Support the delivery of the student accommodation pipeline, one of the largest infrastructure programs in the University’s history Base Salary HEO8 $131,108 p.a. + 17% superannuation      About the opportunity   The University of Sydney is seeking two Assistant Development Managers to join the Property & Development team in University Infrastructure. You will work on a multi-site program delivering up to 2,000 new student beds. It is the largest infrastructure program the University has undertaken, and you will join at the start, supporting projects from feasibility and planning through to approvals, procurement, design and delivery.   Working closely with the Development Managers and project teams, you will support the planning and delivery of each project through consultant coordination, commercial and financial analysis, project controls and governance, making sure it stays aligned with its approved business case and the University’s strategic and commercial objectives.   You will be part of a small, senior team, with real responsibility early and direct exposure to decision-making. For decades to come, the decisions you help make will shape the places where thousands of students live, connect and spend some of the most formative years of their lives.   This role supports flexible working arrangements subject to business requirements.   Your key responsibilities will be to:   support the coordination of consultants, contractors and stakeholders, and maintain project controls covering schedule, cost and risk support procurement from EOI and tender documentation through to evaluation and appointment assist with feasibility assessments, development budgets and business case inputs undertake research, data collection and analysis to support project planning and decision-making assist in preparing presentations, reports and recommendations support planning and approvals processes, including coordination of development application documentation uphold compliance with University governance, procurement, sustainability and WHS requirements.   About you a tertiary qualification in property, project management, architecture, construction management, engineering, planning or a related field experience in property development or major projects across at least one phase: feasibility, approvals, procurement, design or construction (student accommodation, build-to-rent or residential experience is an advantage) excellent organisation, with the ability to manage competing deadlines, keep accurate records and follow through trong problem-solving skills and the ability to use initiative and exercise sound judgement and ability to prioritise workload during busy periods, working both independently and as part of a close team excellent communication and interpersonal skills, and proven ability to communicate effectively with a wide range of stakeholders commercial and financial literacy, including feasibilities, budgets and cost reporting.     Sponsorship / work rights for Australia   You must have unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this employment to be eligible to apply. Visa sponsorship is not available for this appointment.     Pre-employment checks and declarations     Your employment is conditional upon the successful completion of all pre-employment or background checks required for the role in terms satisfactory to the University.  Also, to meet the University’s obligations under the National Higher Education Code to Prevent and Eliminate Gender-Based Violence you will be asked to declare if you have been investigated for, or found to engaged in, sexual harm or gender-based violence in the course of previous employment or in a legal process. Similarly, your ongoing employment is conditional upon the satisfactory maintenance of all relevant clearances and background check requirements. If you do not meet these conditions, the University may take any necessary step, including the termination of your employment.   EEO statement     At the University of Sydney, our shared values are trust, accountability and excellence and we strive to be a place where everyone can thrive. We are committed to creating a University community that thrives through diversity and reflects the wider community that we serve. We deliver on this through our commitment to diversity and inclusion , evidenced by our people and culture programs, as well as key strategies to increase participation and support the careers of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander People , women , people living with a disability , people from culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds , and those who identify as LGBTQIA+ . We welcome applications from candidates from all backgrounds.    We are proud to be recognised as an Australian Workplace Equality Index (AWEI) Platinum Employer. Find out more about our work on diversity and inclusion .      How to apply   Applications (including a cover letter, CV, and any additional supporting documentation) can be submitted via the Apply button at the top of the page.     For employees of the University or contingent workers, please login into your Workday account and navigate to the Career icon on your Dashboard.  Click on USYD Find Jobs and apply.   For a confidential discussion about the role, or if you require reasonable adjustment or any documents in alternate formats, please contact Kevin Lim, Recruitment Operations by email to kevin.lim@sydney.edu.au .   © The University of Sydney   The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Click to view the Position Description for this role. Applications Close Tuesday 03 November 2026 11:59 PM"
    },
    "https://usyd.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/USYD_EXTERNAL_CAREER_SITE/job/Darlington-Campus/Development-Manager---Student-Accommodation_0157849-1": {
      "title": "Development Manager - Student Accommodation",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-21\nFull time\n\nFull time, 5-year fixed term position. Located at the Services Building (G12) on Darlington Campus Lead the assessment, planning and delivery of property development projects across the student accommodation pipeline, one of the largest infrastructure programs in the University’s history Competitive salary package including 17% superannuation     About the opportunity The University of Sydney is seeking an experienced and motivated Development Manager - Student Accommodation to join the Property & Development team in University Infrastructure. You will lead development across a multi-site program delivering up to 2,000 new student beds. It is the largest infrastructure program the University has undertaken, and you will join at the start, as projects move from feasibility and planning into procurement, design and delivery.   Reporting to the Project Director, you will oversee the internal project managers delivering each project, supervise an Assistant Development Manager, and drive the development outcomes: feasibility, business case, approvals, procurement and budget. You will make project-level decisions within approved delegations and bring clear recommendations to senior leadership on significant strategic, financial and contractual matters.   This is a senior role in a small team, with real influence over how the program is shaped and delivered. The buildings you bring to life will be where thousands of students live, connect and spend some of the most formative years of their lives, for decades to come.   This role supports flexible working arrangements subject to business requirements.   Your key responsibilities will be to:   lead development activities across feasibility, planning, approvals, procurement, design and delivery, in line with the University governance, procurement, sustainability and WHS requirements oversee the internal project managers delivering each project, holding them to account for program, cost, quality and risk prepare business cases, feasibility studies and investment recommendations for governance and executive approval. manage planning and approvals strategy, working with planning consultants and authorities manage procurement of consultants and contractors, from EOI and tender through evaluation, negotiation and appointment supervise and develop the Assistant Development Manager, and contribute to the capability of the wider team represent the University with consultants, authorities, industry bodies and external partners, and support the Project Director with industry consultation activity, including relevant industry bodies.     About you   a tertiary qualification in property, project management, architecture, construction management, engineering, planning or a related field extensive property development experience on large capital works across feasibility, planning and approvals, procurement, design and construction (student accommodation, build-to-rent or residential experience is highly regarded) demonstrated experience overseeing project managers and consultant teams on major capital projects within a complex, matrix organisation highly developed commercial acumen, including development feasibilities, financial modelling, budgeting and business case development expertise in procurement and contract management, including contract administration and consultant performance management highly developed senior stakeholder management and influencing skills, with experience shaping strategy and recommendations in a large, complex organisation experience leading and developing staff, with sound judgement and the confidence to make decisions within delegations.     Sponsorship / work rights for Australia   You must have unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this employment to be eligible to apply. Visa sponsorship is not available for this appointment.     Pre-employment checks and declarations     Your employment is conditional upon the successful completion of all pre-employment or background checks required for the role in terms satisfactory to the University.  Also, to meet the University’s obligations under the National Higher Education Code to Prevent and Eliminate Gender-Based Violence you will be asked to declare if you have been investigated for, or found to engaged in, sexual harm or gender-based violence in the course of previous employment or in a legal process. Similarly, your ongoing employment is conditional upon the satisfactory maintenance of all relevant clearances and background check requirements. If you do not meet these conditions, the University may take any necessary step, including the termination of your employment.     EEO statement     At the University of Sydney, our shared values are trust, accountability and excellence and we strive to be a place where everyone can thrive. We are committed to creating a University community that thrives through diversity and reflects the wider community that we serve. We deliver on this through our commitment to diversity and inclusion , evidenced by our people and culture programs, as well as key strategies to increase participation and support the careers of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander People , women , people living with a disability , people from culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds , and those who identify as LGBTQIA+ . We welcome applications from candidates from all backgrounds.    We are proud to be recognised as an Australian Workplace Equality Index (AWEI) Platinum Employer. Find out more about our work on diversity and inclusion .      How to apply   Applications (including a cover letter, CV, and any additional supporting documentation) can be submitted via the Apply button at the top of the page.    For employees of the University or contingent workers, please login into your Workday account and navigate to the Career icon on your Dashboard.  Click on USYD Find Jobs and apply.   For a confidential discussion about the role, or if you require reasonable adjustment or any documents in alternate formats, please contact Kevin Lim, Recruitment Operations by email to kevin.lim@sydney.edu.au .   © The University of Sydney   The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Click to view the Position Description for this role. Applications Close Tuesday 03 November 2026 11:59 PM"
    },
    "https://usyd.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/USYD_EXTERNAL_CAREER_SITE/job/Camperdown-Campus/Veterinary-Intern--Surgery-_0156816-2": {
      "title": "Veterinary Intern (Surgery)",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-21\nFull time\n\nAn opportunity to develop your skills while transform the lives of future colleagues and caring for the lives of animals.   A unique veterinary small animal clinical training opportunity in a teaching and research intensive environment.   Full time 13 months fixed term, located on Camperdown Campus at the Sydney School of Veterinary Science in The University of Sydney Exciting internship opportunity to enhance your surgical skills and provide best practice veterinary care in small animal surgery Opportunity to inspire and train the next generation of veterinarians, engage with the school ranked # 1 in Australia, within a world-class university Base Salary HEO 5, Step 1 (38 hours), $101,344 p.a + 17% superannuation     About the opportunity   Your chance to enhance your surgical skills while having a marked impact on the future of the veterinary profession while training future veterinarians and care for the lives of animals.   We are seeking to appoint a Veterinary Intern (Surgery) to join a team of outstanding world-class clinical specialist colleagues at the University Veterinary Teaching Hospital Sydney. Reporting to the Associate Head for Veterinary Clinical Sciences, the main purpose of the Veterinary Intern (Surgery ) is to provide junior level support to the Small Animal Surgery team-residents and specialists, and support teaching of DVM students while further developing their own surgical skills. The successful applicant will have the opportunity to learn from our Small Animal Surgery specialists as well as a range of other disciplines across the hospital. They will assist other veterinarians in providing some supervision of clinical year veterinary students rotating through the service.   The Sydney School of Veterinary Science at the University of Sydney is ranked first in Australia. Regarding research, the School is the highest ranked veterinary school in Australia (ERA 5/5). This position operates within the Sydney School of Veterinary Science's teaching hospital at the Camperdown Campus. The School of Veterinary Science educates students and conducts research in veterinary and related sciences. The teaching hospital provides the clinical learning experience for students and staff engaged in professional veterinary undergraduate and postgraduate training programs. The hospital is a commercial centre that provides a range of first-class and best-practice veterinary services to the public.   The key benefits:   opportunity to learn and enhance your skills and experience in the unique teaching and research environment of a Veterinary Teaching hospital a University contribution of 17% of your base salary to your superannuation fund yearly personal and professional development planning, including support for continuing education access to the employee assistance program access to fitness passport, as well as discounted rates on university sports and fitness centres industry-leading leave provisions, including generous paid parental leave entitlements (pending eligibility up to 38 weeks) located on the Camperdown campus, 4 km from the city centre, in a convenient central location with ample public transport options. gain the recognition of working in a leading Veterinary School in an outstanding University.   We support and encourage excellence in all aspects of the role, from patient care, teaching and learning, and research. The school values highly the tenants of mutual support, respect, empathy, collegiality and integrity. Join an organisation that has over one hundred years of experience in clinical training and innovation. Be part of the transformational process supporting students as they transition to become veterinarians. A privileged role for someone who wants more than a job- come and do work with a purpose.     Your key responsibilities will be to:   provide high quality veterinary clinical services to small animals provide an effective clinical service for hopsitalised cases ensure quality and continuity of service in the Small Animal Surgery unit assist with the provision of  excellent small animal veterinary services under the supervision of experienced specialists and other senior colleagues assist with the training of DVM students including clinical rounds, tutorials and practical classes where required maintain the reputation for academic excellence. Since the founding of veterinary science in 1910, the mission of the Sydney School of Veterinary Science (SSVS) has been to provide the nation’s best training in the health, welfare, and conservation of animals. The school is internationally accredited with the American Veterinary Medical Association, Canadian Veterinary Medical Association, the Australasian Veterinary Boards Council and Royal College of Veterinary Surgeons (UK) by reciprocal agreement. Veterinary science at Sydney is ranked 1st in Australia, within a world-class university.   To learn more about the Sydney School of Veterinary Science click here   About you   The University values courage and creativity; openness and engagement; inclusion and diversity; and respect and integrity. As such, we see the importance of recruiting talent aligned to these values and are looking for a Veterinary Intern (Surgery) who has:   a degree in veterinary science that is registrable with the NSW Veterinary Practitioners Board strong clinical skills in small animal surgery an ability to work under supervision of the Small Animlal Surgery unit and to work closely with veterinarians in all referral services of the UVTHS in a collegiate manner enthusiasm and demonstrated ability for teaching a willingness to teach DVM veterinary students the desire to provide best-practice principals in provision of veterinary services excellent verbal and written communication skills to communicate with colleagues, clients and undergraduate veterinarians completion of a rotating small animal medicine and surgery internship (desirable)     Work rights for Australia   You must have unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this employment to be eligible to apply. Visa sponsorship is not available for this appointment.   Pre-employment checks   Your employment is conditional upon the completion of all the role required pre-employment or background checks in terms satisfactory to the University. Similarly, your ongoing employment is conditional upon the satisfactory maintenance of all relevant clearances and background check requirements. If you do not meet these conditions, the University may take any necessary step, including the termination of your employment.   EEO statement   At the University of Sydney, our shared values are trust, accountability and excellence and we strive to be a place where everyone can thrive. We are committed to creating a University community that thrives through diversity and reflects the wider community that we serve. We deliver on this through our commitment to diversity and inclusion , evidenced by our people and culture programs, as well as key strategies to increase participation and support the careers of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander People , women , people living with a disability , people from culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds , and those who identify as LGBTQIA+ . We welcome applications from candidates from all backgrounds.    We are proud to be recognised as an Australian Workplace Equality Index (AWEI) Platinum Employer. Find out more about our work on diversity and inclusion .    How to apply   Applications (including a cover letter, CV, and any additional supporting documentation) can be submitted via the Apply button at the top of the page.    If you are a current employee of the University or a contingent worker with access to Workday, please login into your Workday account and navigate to the Career icon on your Dashboard.  Click on USYD Find Jobs and apply.   For a confidential discussion about the role, or if you require reasonable adjustment or any documents in alternate formats, please contact Chris Masaoka, Recruitment Consultant. Recruitment Operations by email recruitment.sea@sydney.edu.au   © The University of Sydney   The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Click to view the Position Description for this role. Applications Close Wednesday 04 November 2026 11:59 PM"
    },
    "https://usyd.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/USYD_EXTERNAL_CAREER_SITE/job/Camperdown-Campus/Placement-Coordinator_0157487-2": {
      "title": "Placement Coordinator",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-20\nPart time\n\nPart-time (0.8 FTE), Fixed Term up to 30 June 2027,  located on the Camperdown Campus Opportunity to coordinate aspects of the Education Professional Experience programs at Sydney School of Education and Social Work ·         Base Salary HEO 6, $107,312 p.a. - $115,686 p.a. pro-rata + 17% superannuation   About the opportunity   The Sydney School of Education and Social Work (SSESW) are seeking a Placement Coordinator to coordinate aspects of Education Professional Experience programs and Social Work Field Education.   This role will involve coordinating and carrying out administrative processes associated with securing and facilitating student placement across social work and teacher education courses. This position will have autonomy to make decisions related to work tasks as part of a Professional Experience Team consisting of Academic and Professional Staff. The incumbent will provide timely and accurate advice and information in various forms to stakeholders and students, and be responsible for administering key processes and a range of functions relating to Professional Experience programs in the School.   Your key responsibilities will be to:   coordinate and manage all administrative processes supporting student placements across initial teacher education programs negotiate and manage relationships with existing schools and establish links with new schools act as the primary point of contact for placement-related enquiries, resolving issues and providing timely, accurate advice to students, staff, and external stakeholders liaise with schools to address queries, resolve procedural issues, and maintain strong partnerships while developing new ones maintain accurate database records on all aspects of professional experience placements, including reports and results provide guidance and support to students on placement-related matters and collaborate with academic coordinators and the Director process payments for supervisors and mentors and assist in organising professional development programs for school and tertiary mentors.     About you   We are seeking an exceptional Placement Coordinator , who possesses:   experience in negotiating and managing relationships with stakeholders demonstrated high level organisational and advanced administrative skills excellent interpersona, phone and written communication skills demonstrated capacity to assess priorities and manage a range of tasks and complex issues simultaneously demonstrated ability to work calmly and constructively under sustained pressure ability to meet strict deadlines and prioritise work in a high-volume environment excellent database and word processing skills experience of working within tertiary education (essential) experience using the software ‘Sonia’ (essential).     Work rights for Australia You must have unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this employment to apply. Visa sponsorship is not available for this appointment.     Pre-employment checks and declarations    Your employment is conditional upon the successful completion of all pre-employment or background checks required for the role in terms satisfactory to the University.  Also, to meet the University’s obligations under the National Higher Education Code to Prevent and Eliminate Gender-Based Violence you will be asked to declare if you have been investigated for, or found to engaged in, sexual harm or gender-based violence in the course of previous employment or in a legal process. Similarly, your ongoing employment is conditional upon the satisfactory maintenance of all relevant clearances and background check requirements. If you do not meet these conditions, the University may take any necessary step, including the termination of your employment.     EEO statement     At the University of Sydney, our shared values are trust, accountability and excellence and we strive to be a place where everyone can thrive. We are committed to creating a University community that thrives through diversity and reflects the wider community that we serve. We deliver on this through our commitment to diversity and inclusion , evidenced by our people and culture programs, as well as key strategies to increase participation and support the careers of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander People , women , people living with a disability , people from culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds , and those who identify as LGBTIQ+ . We welcome applications from candidates from all backgrounds.    We are proud to be recognised as an Australian Workplace Equality Index (AWEI) Gold employer. Find out more about our work on diversity and inclusion .      How to apply   Applications (including a cover letter, CV, and any additional supporting documentation) can be submitted via the Apply button at the top of the page. For employees of the University or contingent workers, please login into your Workday account and navigate to the Career icon on your Dashboard. Click on USYD Find Jobs and apply. For a confidential discussion about the role, or if you require reasonable adjustment or any documents in alternate formats, please contact Helen Efstathiou, Recruitment Consultant, Recruitment Operations on +61 2 8627 7137 or by email helen.efstathiou@sydney.edu.au   © The University of Sydney   The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment.   Click to view the Position Description for this role. Applications Close Tuesday 27 October 2026 11:59 PM"
    },
    "https://usyd.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/USYD_EXTERNAL_CAREER_SITE/job/St-Leonards/Education-Administration-Officer--Clinical---Targeted-_0158290-1": {
      "title": "Education Administration Officer (Clinical) (Targeted)",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-20\nPart time\n\n·         Part-time, fixed-term role until 29 November 2027 (0.6 FTE Mon-Wed). ·         Position will be based predominantly at Royal North Shore Hospital with occasional travel to Ryde Hospital, Hornsby Hospital and Camperdown Campus required. ·         Be part of a dynamic, supportive work environment in a large, complex organisation ·         Opportunity to provide a broad range of education administrative and operational support to academic and teaching staff ·         Base Salary $93,345 (pro-rata) + 17% superannuation    This is a Targeted Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Position. Preference will be given to applicants of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander descent. This Exemption is claimed under Section 21 of the Anti-Discrimination Act 1977 About the opportunity   The University of Sydney is ranked among the world’s leading universities, with Medicine consistently recognised as a top discipline nationally and globally. Within the Faculty of Medicine and Health, the Northern Clinical School plays a key role in delivering high-quality clinical education.   We are seeking an Education Administrative Officer (Clinical) to provide administrative and operational support to the Education Support Team. This is a 0.6 FTE fixed-term office-based role, offering variety and exposure to a broad range of education activities supporting students, tutors, and academic staff.   The role is suited to an organised and adaptable administrator who enjoys working in a fast-paced education environment and providing practical support across multiple activities.   Your key responsibilities will be: ·         supporting the implementation of program evaluation and continuous improvement initiatives for research-based and coursework programs, including managing subject and program evaluation processes ·         liaising with key stakeholders to establish and maintain effective working relationships and ensure education support, learning, curriculum, and teaching priorities are met ·         maintaining databases, records, and contact lists to support learning and teaching activities (e.g. tutor, clinician, and patient databases) ·         supporting the preparation, compilation, and publication of academic and teaching materials for print and online platforms   About you ·         strong written and verbal communication skills, with the ability to exercise sound judgement, problem-solving skills, and initiative, particularly during busy periods ·         excellent time and workload management skills, with a demonstrated ability to prioritise tasks, manage competing deadlines, and deliver outcomes ·         well-developed IT skills, including database maintenance and proficiency in Microsoft Office (particularly Teams), with experience using large, corporate-level information systems ·         experience working in a tertiary education or complex organisational environment (highly regarded)   Sponsorship / work rights for Australia You must have unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this employment to be eligible to apply. Visa sponsorship is not available for this appointment.     Pre-employment checks   Your employment is conditional upon the completion of all roles required pre-employment or background checks in terms satisfactory to the University. Similarly, your ongoing employment is conditional upon the satisfactory maintenance of all relevant clearances and background check requirements. If you do not meet these conditions, the University may take any necessary step, including the termination of your employment.     EEO statement     At the University of Sydney, our shared values are trust, accountability and excellence and we strive to be a place where everyone can thrive. We are committed to creating a University community that thrives through diversity and reflects the wider community that we serve. We deliver on this through our commitment to diversity and inclusion , evidenced by our people and culture programs, as well as key strategies to increase participation and support the careers of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander People , women , people living with a disability , people from culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds , and those who identify as LGBTQIA+ . We welcome applications from candidates from all backgrounds.    We are proud to be recognised as an Australian Workplace Equality Index (AWEI) Platinum Employer. Find out more about our work on diversity and inclusion .    How to apply   Applications (including a cover letter, CV, and any additional supporting documentation) can be submitted via the Apply button at the top of the page.     For employees of the University or contingent workers, please login into your Workday account and navigate to the Career icon on your Dashboard.  Click on USYD Find Jobs and apply.   For a confidential discussion about the role, or if you require reasonable adjustment or any documents in alternate formats, please contact Lena Germinarios, Recruitment Operations by email to lena.germinarios@sydney.edu.au   © The University of Sydney   The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Click to view the Position Description for this role. Applications Close Tuesday 27 October 2026 11:59 PM"
    },
    "https://usyd.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/USYD_EXTERNAL_CAREER_SITE/job/Darlington-Campus/Development-Manager---Retail---Place_0158175-1": {
      "title": "Development Manager - Retail & Place",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-20\nFull time\n\nFull time, 5-year fixed term position. Located at the Services Building (G12) on Camperdown Campus Lead the assessment, planning and delivery of property development projects and strategic initiatives across the University's portfolio Offering a competitive salary package including 17% superannuation      About the opportunity   The University of Sydney is welcoming applications for an experienced and motivated Development Manager – Retail & Place to join the University Infrastructure team.   Property & Development within the University Infrastructure at the University of Sydney, oversees a diverse portfolio of commercial activities, including commercial farms, student accommodation, property management, retail, property acquisition and divestment. The department also play a key role in supporting the University's broader strategic and commercial objectives. Within this division, the Retail & Place team is focused on enhancing campus, creating destinations that enrich student, staff and visitor experiences.   As Development Manager , you will lead the assessment, planning and delivery of property development projects and strategic initiatives across the University's property portfolio. Drawing on your development, commercial and project management expertise, you will oversee activities spanning feasibility assessments, business case development, planning and approvals, procurement, design and delivery, ensuring projects achieve their intended outcomes and align with the University's strategic priorities.   Working collaboratively with University stakeholders and external partners, you will drive project progress within established governance frameworks, exercising sound judgement in managing risks, budgets, resources and stakeholder relationships. You will make project-level decisions within approved delegations, provide informed recommendations on complex commercial and development matters, and work closely with Directors and senior leaders on significant strategic, financial and contractual decisions. Through your leadership, you will help deliver commercially sound, well-governed development outcomes that support the University's long-term objectives.     Your key responsibilities will be to:   lead development activities across feasibility, planning, approvals, procurement, design and delivery phases support preparation of business cases, investment proposals and strategic recommendations ensure compliance with University governance, procurement, WHS and environmental requirements coordinate stakeholder engagement activities to support project outcomes lead and mentor project teams and direct reports where applicable support the Development Director with industry consultation activity, including relevant industry bodies.   About you   strong experience in retail and place outcome in similar University, or complete mixed-use environments is highly desirable tertiary qualifications – in a relevant field or an equivalent combination of relevant experience and training extensive program management experience – including time and project management, prioritisation & multitasking skills to be able to meet strict deadlines across a multitude of complex programs and projects highly advanced financial planning skills and commercial acumen highly advanced skills in consulting, strategy development, business case development and design of transformational strategies and projects strong understanding of program management, quality assurance, and risk management principles to ensure projects are delivered with minimal disruptions and that changes are effectively managed proven ability in governance, safety and compliance management strong understanding of program management, quality assurance, and risk management principles to ensure projects are delivered with minimal disruptions and that changes are effectively managed.       Sponsorship / work rights for Australia   You must have unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this employment to be eligible to apply. Visa sponsorship is not available for this appointment.     Pre-employment checks and declarations     Your employment is conditional upon the successful completion of all pre-employment or background checks required for the role in terms satisfactory to the University.  Also, to meet the University’s obligations under the National Higher Education Code to Prevent and Eliminate Gender-Based Violence you will be asked to declare if you have been investigated for, or found to engaged in, sexual harm or gender-based violence in the course of previous employment or in a legal process. Similarly, your ongoing employment is conditional upon the satisfactory maintenance of all relevant clearances and background check requirements. If you do not meet these conditions, the University may take any necessary step, including the termination of your employment.     EEO statement     At the University of Sydney, our shared values are trust, accountability and excellence and we strive to be a place where everyone can thrive. We are committed to creating a University community that thrives through diversity and reflects the wider community that we serve. We deliver on this through our commitment to diversity and inclusion , evidenced by our people and culture programs, as well as key strategies to increase participation and support the careers of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander People , women , people living with a disability , people from culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds , and those who identify as LGBTQIA+ . We welcome applications from candidates from all backgrounds.    We are proud to be recognised as an Australian Workplace Equality Index (AWEI) Platinum Employer. Find out more about our work on diversity and inclusion .      How to apply   Applications (including a cover letter, CV, and any additional supporting documentation) can be submitted via the Apply button at the top of the page.     For employees of the University or contingent workers, please login into your Workday account and navigate to the Career icon on your Dashboard.  Click on USYD Find Jobs and apply.   For a confidential discussion about the role, or if you require reasonable adjustment or any documents in alternate formats, please contact Kevin Lim, Recruitment Operations by email to kevin.lim@sydney.edu.au . © The University of Sydney The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Click to view the Position Description for this role. Applications Close Tuesday 03 November 2026 11:59 PM"
    },
    "https://usyd.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/USYD_EXTERNAL_CAREER_SITE/job/Darlington-Campus/Assistant-Development-Manager---Retail---Place_0158172-1": {
      "title": "Assistant Development Manager – Retail & Place",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-20\nFull time\n\n2 x full time, 5-year fixed term positions. Located at the Services Building (G12) on Camperdown Campus Support the planning, coordination and delivery of property and development projects across the University's property portfolio Base Salary HEO8, $131,108 p.a. + 17% superannuation    About the opportunity   The University of Sydney is welcoming applications for 2 x  Assistant Development Manager – Retail & Place to join the University Infrastructure team.   Property & Development within the University Infrastructure at the University of Sydney, oversees a diverse portfolio of commercial activities, including commercial farms, student accommodation, property management, retail, property acquisition and divestment. The department also play a key role in supporting the University's broader strategic and commercial objectives. Within this division, the Retail & Place team is focused on enhancing campus, creating destinations that enrich student, staff and visitor experiences.   As Assistant Development Manager , you will play a key role in supporting the planning, coordination and delivery of property and development projects across the University's diverse portfolio. You will contribute to projects at every stage of the development lifecycle, from feasibility assessments, planning and approvals through to procurement, design and delivery, helping to translate strategic priorities into successful development outcomes.   Working closely with Development Managers, project teams, consultants and stakeholders, you will provide project coordination, commercial, financial and administrative support to drive effective project delivery. You will assist with project controls, reporting, stakeholder engagement and governance processes, ensuring projects remain aligned with approved business cases, organisational requirements and strategic objectives.     Your key responsibilities will be to: support the planning and delivery of development projects across feasibility, planning, approvals, procurement, design and delivery phases monitor project expenditure and assist with cost tracking and reporting assist in preparing project reports, committee papers and briefing materials coordinate communication between project teams, consultants, contractors and stakeholders. undertake research, data collection and analysis to support project planning and decision-making monitor market, planning and industry developments relevant to assigned projects.     About you   demonstrated experience in property development across one or more phases of acquisition, planning and approvals, construction and sale. Experience in retail and place is highly desirable tertiary qualifications - Property Economics / Project Management (desired) excellent communication and interpersonal skills, and proven ability to communicate effectively with a wide range of stakeholders demonstrated ability to deliver quality customer service and adapt to change strong problem-solving skills and the ability to use initiative and exercise sound judgement and ability to prioritise workload during busy periods accuracy, attention to detail and highly organised. planning, analysis and communication skills together with the ability to work according to strict deadlines and manage various demands.     Sponsorship / work rights for Australia   You must have unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this employment to be eligible to apply. Visa sponsorship is not available for this appointment.   Pre-employment checks and declarations     Your employment is conditional upon the successful completion of all pre-employment or background checks required for the role in terms satisfactory to the University.  Also, to meet the University’s obligations under the National Higher Education Code to Prevent and Eliminate Gender-Based Violence you will be asked to declare if you have been investigated for, or found to engaged in, sexual harm or gender-based violence in the course of previous employment or in a legal process. Similarly, your ongoing employment is conditional upon the satisfactory maintenance of all relevant clearances and background check requirements. If you do not meet these conditions, the University may take any necessary step, including the termination of your employment.     EEO statement     At the University of Sydney, our shared values are trust, accountability and excellence and we strive to be a place where everyone can thrive. We are committed to creating a University community that thrives through diversity and reflects the wider community that we serve. We deliver on this through our commitment to diversity and inclusion , evidenced by our people and culture programs, as well as key strategies to increase participation and support the careers of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander People , women , people living with a disability , people from culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds , and those who identify as LGBTQIA+ . We welcome applications from candidates from all backgrounds.    We are proud to be recognised as an Australian Workplace Equality Index (AWEI) Platinum Employer. Find out more about our work on diversity and inclusion .      How to apply   Applications (including a cover letter, CV, and any additional supporting documentation) can be submitted via the Apply button at the top of the page.     For employees of the University or contingent workers, please login into your Workday account and navigate to the Career icon on your Dashboard.  Click on USYD Find Jobs and apply.   For a confidential discussion about the role, or if you require reasonable adjustment or any documents in alternate formats, please contact Kevin Lim, Recruitment Operations by email to kevin.lim@sydney.edu.au .   © The University of Sydney   The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment.   Click to view the Position Description for this role. Applications Close Tuesday 03 November 2026 11:59 PM"
    },
    "https://usyd.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/USYD_EXTERNAL_CAREER_SITE/job/Camperdown-Campus/Research-Coordinator_0157572-2": {
      "title": "Research Coordinator",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-20\nPart time\n\nPart-time (0.8 FTE; 28 hours/week) 12-month opportunity for an organised Research Coordinator Play a pivotal role in the development of a childhood type 1 diabetes and coeliac disease screening trial Base Salary $107,312 p.a. (pro-rata) + 17% superannuation Flexible working arrangements, including flexible daily hours (e.g. school hours) and hybrid work options available About the opportunity As the Research Coordinator , you will provide critical support to the development of a new school-based clinical trial within the Australian Type 1 Diabetes National Screening Research Program, a landmark public health research initiative for children. Working closely with the Principal Investigator, Research Manager and research team at the Charles Perkins Centre, as well as a national multidisciplinary network of researchers, clinicians and stakeholders, you will be responsible for coordinating the roll-out of the new School Screen: Australian School Screening Program clinical trial, including operational planning, ongoing ethics and grant reporting and coordination activities to support research participation across schools and community settings. This is a unique opportunity to contribute to a landmark national screening initiative that aims to transform the early detection and care of type 1 diabetes and coeliac disease in Australia. You will join a collaborative research team delivering an impactful program for children and families, with flexibility to work reduced daily hours plus remotely and/or on campus as desired Your key responsibilities will be to: project manage the set-up of the clinical trial in collaboration with the broader research team and national multidisciplinary stakeholders prepare and manage ethics submissions and governance approvals across multiple institutions coordinate the development of study documentation and parent- and child-facing materials coordinate research activities, scheduling and bookings with participating schools and community partners ensure compliance with study protocols, ethical and regulatory requirements, and Good Clinical Practice (GCP) guidelines support research operations, including research contracts, service agreements, and trial logistics maintain accurate study records and databases to support trial delivery and reporting assist with staff recruitment, onboarding and training activities as required liaise with research partners, healthcare providers, and other key stakeholders to support trial delivery. About you tertiary qualifications, with qualifications in health or a related discipline highly regarded demonstrated experience working within a research or clinical trial environment sound knowledge and understanding of Good Clinical Practice (GCP) guidelines proven project management skills with the ability to manage competing priorities and solve complex problems exceptional organisational skills with meticulous attention to detail demonstrated ability to take initiative and work independently in a fast-paced environment experience building and maintaining relationships with a diverse range of stakeholders, including schools, families, and healthcare providers strong written and verbal communication skills with the ability to prepare high-quality documentation experience using research databases, with REDCap experience highly regarded demonstrated ability to work both independently and collaboratively within multidisciplinary teams PhD opportunity Sponsorship / work rights for Australia   You must have unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this employment to be eligible to apply. Visa sponsorship is not available for this appointment. Pre-employment checks and declarations     Your employment is conditional upon the successful completion of all pre-employment or background checks required for the role in terms satisfactory to the University.  Also, to meet the University’s obligations under the National Higher Education Code to Prevent and Eliminate Gender-Based Violence you will be asked to declare if you have been investigated for, or found to engaged in, sexual harm or gender-based violence in the course of previous employment or in a legal process. Similarly, your ongoing employment is conditional upon the satisfactory maintenance of all relevant clearances and background check requirements. If you do not meet these conditions, the University may take any necessary step, including the termination of your employment. This position is designated as involving child-related work. To undertake or remain in this position, you are required to apply for and obtain a Working with Children Check clearance in accordance with the Child Protection (Working with Children) Act 2012 . EEO statement At the University of Sydney, our shared values are trust, accountability and excellence and we strive to be a place where everyone can thrive. We are committed to creating a university community that thrives through diversity and reflects the wider community that we serve. We deliver on this through our commitment to diversity and inclusion , evidenced by our people and culture programs, as well as key strategies to increase participation and support the careers of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander People , women , people living with a disability , people from culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds , and those who identify as LGBTQIA+ . We welcome applications from candidates from all backgrounds.    We are proud to be recognised as an Australian Workplace Equality Index (AWEI) Platinum Employer. Find out more about our work on diversity and inclusion .    How to apply Applications (including a cover letter, CV, and any additional supporting documentation) can be submitted via the Apply button at the top of the page. For employees of the University or contingent workers, please login into your Workday account and navigate to the Career icon on your Dashboard. Click on USYD Find Jobs and apply. For a confidential discussion about the role, or if you require reasonable adjustment or any documents in alternate formats, please contact Danielle Selinger Recruitment Consultant by email to danielle.selinger@sydney.edu.au © The University of Sydney The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Click to view the Position Description for this role. Applications Close Tuesday 03 November 2026 11:59 PM"
    },
    "https://usyd.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/USYD_EXTERNAL_CAREER_SITE/job/Camperdown-Campus/Medical-Director---The-Healthy-Brain-Ageing-Program_0157031-1": {
      "title": "Medical Director - The Healthy Brain Ageing Program",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-19\nPart time\n\n2 x part time, fixed-term opportunities for two years   Lead innovative clinical care, research and service transformation in cognitive health and dementia Offering a competitive remuneration package including 17% superannuation   About the opportunity   The Healthy Brain Ageing Program is seeking to appoint two experienced medical leaders to support the delivery, growth and sustainability of innovative cognitive health services and research initiatives.   These senior appointments provide an opportunity to shape the future of dementia and healthy ageing care through leadership of a multidisciplinary Virtual Memory Clinic (vMC) and a Healthy Brain Ageing (HBA) Clinic. Working across clinical service delivery, research, governance and stakeholder engagement, successful candidates will play a key role in advancing integrated care, translational research and health service innovation.   Applicants may express interest in one or both positions.   In the role of Medical Director, Virtual Memory Clinic (vMC), you will:   provide clinical leadership across all vMC services, ensuring safe, high-quality and patient-centred care lead multidisciplinary clinical consensus case conferences and support clinical triage and assessment pathways oversee clinical governance, quality improvement activities, risk management and performance reporting deliver specialist medical consultations through virtual and periodic face-to-face regional clinics support research activities, ethics compliance and the translation of evidence into clinical practice build partnerships with healthcare providers, researchers, government agencies and funding bodies to support service sustainability and growth   About you medical specialist qualification with current registration or eligibility for registration in Australia substantial experience in dementia, cognitive disorders, geriatrics, neurology, old age psychiatry or a related specialty specialised training in geriatrics demonstrated experience leading multidisciplinary teams and complex clinical services experience in clinical governance, quality improvement and patient safety frameworks experience contributing to clinical research, publications and translational research initiatives commitment to improving access to specialist cognitive health services for diverse communities. In the role of Medical Director, Healthy Brain Ageing (HBA) Clinic, you will:   provide strategic leadership for research commercialisation activities across the Healthy Brain Ageing Program oversee research administration frameworks, governance processes and operational innovation initiatives drive adoption of advanced research and commercialisation platforms to support program growth establish strategic approaches to compliance, reporting, risk management and accreditation requirements lead service delivery strategy, ensuring responsive, collaborative and high-quality stakeholder support contribute to organisational strategy, leadership capability, workplace health and safety, and program sustainability   About you medical specialist qualification with current registration or eligibility for registration in Australia specialised training in geriatrics demonstrated experience leading complex health, research or academic programs experience in research commercialisation, translational research or innovation environments expertise in governance, compliance, reporting and risk management frameworks experience developing strategic partnerships across healthcare, research, industry and government sectors proven ability to drive organisational strategy and service excellence in a complex stakeholder environment.   Sponsorship / work rights for Australia   You must have unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this employment to be eligible to apply. Visa sponsorship is not available for this appointment.   Pre-employment checks and declarations     Your employment is conditional upon the successful completion of all pre-employment or background checks required for the role in terms satisfactory to the University.  Also, to meet the University’s obligations under the National Higher Education Code to Prevent and Eliminate Gender-Based Violence you will be asked to declare if you have been investigated for, or found to engaged in, sexual harm or gender-based violence in the course of previous employment or in a legal process. Similarly, your ongoing employment is conditional upon the satisfactory maintenance of all relevant clearances and background check requirements. If you do not meet these conditions, the University may take any necessary step, including the termination of your employment.   EEO statement     At the University of Sydney, our shared values are trust, accountability and excellence and we strive to be a place where everyone can thrive. We are committed to creating a University community that thrives through diversity and reflects the wider community that we serve. We deliver on this through our commitment to diversity and inclusion , evidenced by our people and culture programs, as well as key strategies to increase participation and support the careers of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander People , women , people living with a disability , people from culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds , and those who identify as LGBTQIA+ . We welcome applications from candidates from all backgrounds.    We are proud to be recognised as an Australian Workplace Equality Index (AWEI) Platinum Employer. Find out more about our work on diversity and inclusion .    How to apply   Applications (including a cover letter, CV, and any additional supporting documentation) can be submitted via the Apply button at the top of the page.     For employees of the University or contingent workers, please login into your Workday account and navigate to the Career icon on your Dashboard.  Click on USYD Find Jobs and apply.   For a confidential discussion about the role, or if you require reasonable adjustment or any documents in alternate formats, please contact Cherie Goodwin or Rebecca Astar, Recruitment Operations by email to recruitment.sea@sydney.edu.au   © The University of Sydney   The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Click to view the Position Description for this role. Applications Close Tuesday 27 October 2026 11:59 PM"
    },
    "https://usyd.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/USYD_EXTERNAL_CAREER_SITE/job/Newtown/Senior-HPC-Research-Computing-Specialist_0158148-1": {
      "title": "Senior HPC Research Computing Specialist",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-19\nFull time\n\nFull-time continuing opportunity Join the Sydney Informatics Hub (SIH) and help enable world-class research computing capability across the University Base Salary $152,045 - $160,430 + 17% superannuation   About the opportunity   As the Senior HPC Research Computing Specialist, you will be at the forefront of enabling world-class research at the University of Sydney. Working within the Sydney Informatics Hub (SIH) Platforms team, you will provide specialist expertise across high-performance computing (HPC) and cloud environments, helping researchers translate complex research challenges into scalable, efficient and reproducible computational solutions.   As part of the Sydney Research Cloud ecosystem, you will collaborate with researchers, technical specialists, ICT teams, national research computing facilities and industry partners to deliver impactful research computing services and solutions. Through the development of shared tools and services and community engagement, you will help shape the future of the Sydney Research Cloud ecosystem while building computational capability across the University's research community     Your key responsibilities will be to:   provide expert advice on HPC, cloud computing, workload optimisation and reproducible research workflows support researchers to identify appropriate computational platforms and resolve complex technical issues across HPC and cloud environments coordinate researcher access to institutional and national research computing facilities, including allocation and usage processes develop, maintain and support scientific software environments, containers, workflows and data management solutions act as a key liaison between researchers, ICT, national computing facilities and technology vendors design and deliver training, workshops, documentation and user resources that build researcher capability and independence analyse platform performance, usage trends and service demand to support continuous improvement and capacity planning contribute to the promotion and adoption of research computing services across the University research community   About you   tertiary qualifications in a relevant field, or an equivalent combination of training and experience substantial experience supporting research computing environments and high-performance computing platforms strong technical expertise in Linux-based HPC environments, storage systems and cloud computing platforms such as AWS and Azure demonstrated experience with scripting and automation using Python and shell scripting experience developing and deploying software containers, workflow languages and scientific computing pipelines experience working with HPC schedulers such as PBS and SLURM and national research computing facilities including NCI and Pawsey demonstrated ability to collaborate effectively with researchers, technical specialists and senior stakeholders to deliver successful outcomes strong written and verbal communication skills, with experience delivering researcher training, documentation and technical guidance   Sponsorship / work rights for Australia   You must have unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this employment to be eligible to apply. Visa sponsorship is not available for this appointment.     Pre-employment checks and declarations     Your employment is conditional upon the successful completion of all pre-employment or background checks required for the role in terms satisfactory to the University.  Also, to meet the University’s obligations under the National Higher Education Code to Prevent and Eliminate Gender-Based Violence you will be asked to declare if you have been investigated for, or found to engaged in, sexual harm or gender-based violence in the course of previous employment or in a legal process. Similarly, your ongoing employment is conditional upon the satisfactory maintenance of all relevant clearances and background check requirements. If you do not meet these conditions, the University may take any necessary step, including the termination of your employment.     EEO statement     At the University of Sydney, our shared values are trust, accountability and excellence and we strive to be a place where everyone can thrive. We are committed to creating a University community that thrives through diversity and reflects the wider community that we serve. We deliver on this through our commitment to diversity and inclusion , evidenced by our people and culture programs, as well as key strategies to increase participation and support the careers of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander People , women , people living with a disability , people from culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds , and those who identify as LGBTQIA+ . We welcome applications from candidates from all backgrounds.    We are proud to be recognised as an Australian Workplace Equality Index (AWEI) Platinum Employer. Find out more about our work on diversity and inclusion .    How to apply   Applications (including a cover letter, CV, and any additional supporting documentation) can be submitted via the Apply button at the top of the page.     For employees of the University or contingent workers, please login into your Workday account and navigate to the Career icon on your Dashboard.  Click on USYD Find Jobs and apply.   For a confidential discussion about the role, or if you require reasonable adjustment or any documents in alternate formats, please contact Kale Claffey Recruitment Operations by email to kale.claffey@sydney.edu.au   © The University of Sydney   The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Click to view the Position Description for this role. Applications Close Sunday 01 November 2026 11:59 PM"
    },
    "https://usyd.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/USYD_EXTERNAL_CAREER_SITE/job/Darlington-Campus/SBA-Project-Officer--Operations-_0156980-2": {
      "title": "SBA Project Officer (Operations)",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-19\nFull time\n\nFull time, fixed term until December 2028 Provide project management and specialist advice to support the Sydney Biomedical Accelerator (SBA) Base Salary HEO7, $117,133 - $127,610 p.a. + 17% superannuation About the opportunity The University of Sydney is welcoming applications for an experienced and motivated SBA Project Officer (Operations) to join the Central Operations Services (COS) team. This is a dynamic role that involves both coordination and implementation of project plans, deliverables and activities across multiple workstreams, and working effectively across organisational boundaries with partners spanning the University and Sydney Local Health District. As SBA transitions from construction to operation, this role will support the development of core operational frameworks that will underpin research activity and operations in the new facility. Working closely with resident researchers, clinicians and technical staff, you will help translate diverse requirements into workable, sustainable systems. This is a hands-on role suited to someone who enjoys engaging directly with stakeholders, solving practical problems, and building processes from the ground up in a complex, multi-institutional environment. The role supports a flexible hybrid work arrangement, enabling a combination of remote and office-based work to promote flexibility, collaboration and effective delivery of project outcomes. Sydney Biomedical Accelerator The Sydney Biomedical Accelerator (SBA) is a landmark partnership between the University of Sydney, Sydney Local Health District, and the NSW Government. Located within Tech Central, SBA brings together world-class researchers, clinicians, and entrepreneurs. SBA’s mission is to fast-track scientific discovery into real-world health outcomes by fostering interdisciplinary collaboration, translational research, and commercialisation. As SBA moves from establishment to operation, it will become a globally recognised hub for biomedical innovation. Your key responsibilities will be to: coordinate and support development of core operational frameworks for the SBA, engaging with researchers, clinicians, and cross-functional operational teams to understand their operational and technical needs support the development of workflows and other initiatives that enable staff and students to experience the SBA as an integrated, high-performing research and translation environment support communications, reporting and change management activities associated with establishing the above provide project coordination and reporting, including preparing papers, reports and project plans, and tracking delivery of milestones manage and synthesis data and information gathered through researcher engagement into clear, actionable reports and briefings for senior staff provide broader project support to the SBA as directed by the Manager. About you bachelor's degree - In a relevant field or an equivalent combination of relevant experience and training demonstrated experience in a project or operational role, with strong skills in stakeholder engagement and facilitation proven ability to work independently, manage competing priorities and drive project progress with limited supervision experience with financial modelling and analysis, with the ability to translate operational data into cost or resourcing insights experience preparing written briefs and presentations for senior audiences sound skills in managing documentation and information in a complex, multi-stakeholder environment excellent communication and interpersonal skills, with the ability to engage effectively with researchers, clinicians and operational staff. Sponsorship / work rights for Australia You must have unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this employment to be eligible to apply. Visa sponsorship is not available for this appointment. Pre-employment checks and declarations Your employment is conditional upon the successful completion of all pre-employment or background checks required for the role in terms satisfactory to the University.  Also, to meet the University’s obligations under the National Higher Education Code to Prevent and Eliminate Gender-Based Violence you will be asked to declare if you have been investigated for, or found to engaged in, sexual harm or gender-based violence in the course of previous employment or in a legal process. Similarly, your ongoing employment is conditional upon the satisfactory maintenance of all relevant clearances and background check requirements. If you do not meet these conditions, the University may take any necessary step, including the termination of your employment. EEO statement   At the University of Sydney, our shared values are trust, accountability and excellence and we strive to be a place where everyone can thrive. We are committed to creating a University community that thrives through diversity and reflects the wider community that we serve. We deliver on this through our commitment to diversity and inclusion , evidenced by our people and culture programs, as well as key strategies to increase participation and support the careers of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander People , women , people living with a disability , people from culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds , and those who identify as LGBTQIA+ . We welcome applications from candidates from all backgrounds.  We are proud to be recognised as an Australian Workplace Equality Index (AWEI) Platinum Employer. Find out more about our work on diversity and inclusion .  How to apply Applications (including a cover letter, CV, and any additional supporting documentation) can be submitted via the Apply button at the top of the page.   For employees of the University or contingent workers, please login into your Workday account and navigate to the Career icon on your Dashboard.  Click on USYD Find Jobs and apply. For a confidential discussion about the role, or if you require reasonable adjustment or any documents in alternate formats, please contact Kevin Lim, Recruitment Operations by email to kevin.lim@sydney.edu.au . © The University of Sydney The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Click to view the Position Description for this role. Applications Close Monday 02 November 2026 11:59 PM"
    },
    "https://usyd.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/USYD_EXTERNAL_CAREER_SITE/job/Camperdown-Campus/Senior-Student-Success-Officer--Gadigal-Centre--Identified-_0157593-3": {
      "title": "Senior Student Success Officer, Gadigal Centre (Identified)",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-19\nFull time\n\nFull time, continuing (contingent on funding) Support Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander students to thrive and succeed throughout their university journey Base Salary $117,133 + 17% superannuation    About the opportunity   This position has been designated for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander people only under the provisions of section 126 of the Anti-Discrimination Act 1977 (NSW).   If you identify as an Aboriginal and/or Torres Strait Islander person, please submit a copy of your Confirmation of Aboriginality with your application.   The Gadigal Centre is the University of Sydney's dedicated student centre for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander students, providing academic, pastoral and cultural support services to Indigenous students to help them achieve their full potential.   The Senior Student Success Officer, Gadigal Centre (Identified) provides culturally informed, holistic support to Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander students across their university journey. Working within the Gadigal Centre, you will support student engagement and academic progression, coordinate key programs and use student data and insights to continually strengthen services and outcomes.   Your key responsibilities will be to:   work closely with students, academic leaders, faculties and professional service teams provide holistic guidance, advocacy and case management to Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander students manage and analyse student data to inform programs, interventions and decision-making coordinate student support programs including ITAS, scholarships and student engagement initiatives develop culturally inclusive processes and strengthen student support and referral pathways support Gadigal Centre operations, events, reporting and continuous improvement initiatives.   About you   demonstrated knowledge and understanding of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander societies, cultures and contemporary issues experience supporting students, preferably within a tertiary education environment experience managing and analysing data using platforms such as Power BI and CRM systems demonstrated experience in student data management, analysis and reporting experience coordinating programs and managing multiple operational priorities tertiary qualifications in a relevant field or equivalent relevant training and experience, with a current Working with Children Check or willingness to undergo employment screening.   The University of Sydney YURA Network, formally the Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Staff Network promotes sharing of experiences, networking and professional support for career development and mentoring. It is supported by the  Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Employment Plan 2022-2024  that aims to increase employment and development opportunities for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander staff.   The University recognises that the cultural load undertaken by Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander staff on behalf of the University culturally enriches the institution. It also advances the University’s commitment to empowering Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander staff and communities. Engagement with cultural duties, engagement with Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander traditional knowledge holders and navigating cross-cultural interactions are recognised as crucial elements of this role and will be allocated appropriate time in workloads, including work plans, and will be considered in criteria for promotion.    Sponsorship / work rights for Australia   You must have unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this employment to be eligible to apply. Visa sponsorship is not available for this appointment.     Pre-employment checks and declarations       Your employment is conditional upon the successful completion of all pre-employment or background checks required for the role in terms satisfactory to the University.  Also, to meet the University’s obligations under the National Higher Education Code to Prevent and Eliminate Gender-Based Violence you will be asked to declare if you have been investigated for, or found to engaged in, sexual harm or gender-based violence in the course of previous employment or in a legal process. Similarly, your ongoing employment is conditional upon the satisfactory maintenance of all relevant clearances and background check requirements. If you do not meet these conditions, the University may take any necessary step, including the termination of your employment.   This position is designated as involving child-related work. To undertake or remain in this position, you are required to apply for and obtain a Working with Children Check clearance in accordance with the Child Protection (Working with Children) Act 2012.   EEO statement     At the University of Sydney, our shared values are trust, accountability and excellence and we strive to be a place where everyone can thrive. We are committed to creating a University community that thrives through diversity and reflects the wider community that we serve. We deliver on this through our commitment to diversity and inclusion , evidenced by our people and culture programs, as well as key strategies to increase participation and support the careers of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander People , women , people living with a disability , people from culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds , and those who identify as LGBTQIA+ . We welcome applications from candidates from all backgrounds.    We are proud to be recognised as an Australian Workplace Equality Index (AWEI) Platinum Employer. Find out more about our work on diversity and inclusion .    How to apply   Applications (including a cover letter, CV, and any additional supporting documentation) can be submitted via the Apply button at the top of the page.     For employees of the University or contingent workers, please login into your Workday account and navigate to the Career icon on your Dashboard.  Click on USYD Find Jobs and apply.   For a confidential discussion about the role, or if you require reasonable adjustment or any documents in alternate formats, please contact Tooba Fida, Recruitment Operations by email to tooba.fida@sydney.edu.au   © The University of Sydney   The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Click to view the Position Description for this role. Applications Close Sunday 01 November 2026 11:59 PM"
    },
    "https://usyd.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/USYD_EXTERNAL_CAREER_SITE/job/Camperdown-Campus/Postdoctoral-Research-Associate-in-Activity-on-the-Sun-and-Solar-like-Stars_0157092-1": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Research Associate in Activity on the Sun and Solar-like Stars",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-27\nFull time\n\nFull-time 3-year fixed term position, located in the School of Physics, Camperdown Campus Research opportunity in solar and stellar astrophysics Base Salary Level A $117,936 - $125,896 + 17% superannuation   About the opportunity   The Postdoctoral Research Associate in Activity on the Sun and Solar-like stars will use theory, data analysis, and computation to improve our understanding of solar flares and coronal mass ejections (CMEs) and their counterparts on solar-like stars. Flares and CMEs are the most energetic events in the solar system and are drivers for space weather events which directly affect the Earth. This project will involve developing new and improving existing methods of modelling and analysis of solar active regions, the source regions for flares and CMEs, and applying the results also to stellar flares. The work will involve collaboration with domestic and international researchers.   Your key responsibilities will be to: work closely with Professor Michael Wheatland on all aspects of the project perform data analysis with a range of solar data, and develop theory and computational methods for understanding and modelling flare and CME activity collaborate with other researchers at the University of Sydney and elsewhere, and assist students in research projects undertake domestic and international travel to meet the goals of the project publish results in high-impact journals communicate results at local and international meetings.   About you   a PhD (or one to be completed before starting) in physics or applied mathematics excellent computational skills, including the ability to write compiled codes for large-scale computation, as well as codes in Python or other languages for data analysis demonstrated skills in theory, data analysis, computational modelling using data, and the visualization and interpretation of data and models demonstrated ability to conduct research/scholarly activities under limited supervision, as well as to work effectively as a member of a team excellent communication (written and verbal), as well as organizational and problem solving skills with attention to detail experience in handling astrophysical datasets a history of publication in refereed journals as a sole author or in collaboration experience in research record-keeping and preparation of research papers and presentations.   Pre-employment checks and declarations     Your employment is conditional upon the successful completion of all pre-employment or background checks required for the role in terms satisfactory to the University.  Also, to meet the University’s obligations under the National Higher Education Code to Prevent and Eliminate Gender-Based Violence you will be asked to declare if you have been investigated for, or found to engaged in, sexual harm or gender-based violence in the course of previous employment or in a legal process. Similarly, your ongoing employment is conditional upon the satisfactory maintenance of all relevant clearances and background check requirements. If you do not meet these conditions, the University may take any necessary step, including the termination of your employment.   EEO statement     At the University of Sydney, our shared values are trust, accountability and excellence and we strive to be a place where everyone can thrive. We are committed to creating a University community that thrives through diversity and reflects the wider community that we serve. We deliver on this through our commitment to diversity and inclusion , evidenced by our people and culture programs, as well as key strategies to increase participation and support the careers of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander People , women , people living with a disability , people from culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds , and those who identify as LGBTQIA+ . We welcome applications from candidates from all backgrounds.    We are proud to be recognised as an Australian Workplace Equality Index (AWEI) Platinum Employer. Find out more about our work on diversity and inclusion .     How to apply Applications (including a cover letter, CV, and any additional supporting documentation) can be submitted via the Apply button at the top of the page.   For employees of the University or contingent workers, please login into your Workday account and navigate to the Career icon on your Dashboard.  Click on USYD Find Jobs and apply.   For a confidential discussion about the role, or if you require reasonable adjustment or any documents in alternate formats, please contact Liam Newson and Nicole Feain, Recruitment Operations by email to recruitment.sea@sydney.edu.au   © The University of Sydney   The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment.   Click to view the Position Description for this role. Applications Close Sunday 29 November 2026 11:59 PM"
    },
    "https://usyd.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/USYD_EXTERNAL_CAREER_SITE/job/Camperdown-Campus/Postgraduate-Teaching-Fellow_0158462-1": {
      "title": "Postgraduate Teaching Fellow",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-27\nPart time\n\n4 x Part-time (0.2 FTE, 7.5 hours per week), 2-year, fixed term positions, located on the Camperdown Campus at the School of Mathematics and Statistics Exciting opportunity to develop into the next generation of Australian University academics Base Salary (Level A) $93,424 p.a. (pro-rata) + 17% superannuation     About the opportunity   MUST BE ENROLLED IN A PHD OR MPHIL DEGREE AT THE UNIVERSITY OF SYDNEY The School of Mathematics and Statistics is currently seeking Honours graduates (or equivalent) in Mathematics and/or Statistics for appointment as a Postgraduate Teaching Fellow at Associate Lecturer level. The Postgraduate Teaching Fellows contribute to the teaching and learning activities of the School through the delivery of undergraduate tutorials; the preparation and development of tutorial materials and quizzes; exam preparation and marking; and by assisting with academic administration and supporting the work of the Director of First Year. Applicants must be enrolled in a PhD or MPhil degree within the School of Mathematics and Statistics and expect to remain enrolled until at least 1 December 2028. The salary for these positions is paid in addition to any scholarship funding. The Fellowship Program provides training and experience in tertiary teaching to outstanding postgraduate students, with the aim of developing the next generation of university academics. Comprehensive training is provided by the School, along with ongoing support and mentoring from academic staff throughout the appointment.   As part of the role, you would be expected to tutor primarily in first-year Mathematics and/or Statistics units and may be provided with opportunities to deliver some lectures.   Your key responsibilities will be to:   conduct and prepare tutorials (mostly first year) contribute to design and/or implementation of quizzes and examinations conduct examination marking respond to student enquiries undertake administrative functions prepare online and/or in-person training modules or contributing to tutorial rewrites/updates assist with the development and delivery of undergraduate education work on education-related projects aligned to the strategy of the School.   This opportunity is ideal for highly motivated postgraduate researchers who are committed to both academic excellence and delivering an outstanding learning experience for students.     About you   The University values courage and creativity; openness and engagement; inclusion and diversity; and respect and integrity. As such, we see the importance of recruiting talent aligned to these values and are looking for Postgraduate Teaching Fellows who have:   an Honours degree (or equivalent) in Mathematics or Statistics/Data Science an enrolment in a PhD or MPhil degree in the School of Mathematics and Statistics at the University of Sydney at the time of application potential to conduct original research and/or engage in scholarly activity ability to tutor undergraduate units in mathematics and/or statistics understanding of the subject matter within mathematics and/or statistics a passion for teaching good time management, organisational, and administrative skills with attention to detail proven excellence in both written and verbal communication motivation and ability to work independently developing knowledge and understanding of contemporary pedagogical practice and design.   Desirable:   experience in the use of LaTeX or TeX capabilities in programming and IT, particularly symbolic algebra packages (e.g. Matlab, Maple and/or Mathematica), statistical programming languages (e.g. R and Python), and/or online learning support systems (e.g. Canvas, EdStem and WebWork).     Sponsorship / work rights for Australia   You must have unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this employment to be eligible to apply. Visa sponsorship is not available for this appointment.   Pre-employment checks and declarations     Your employment is conditional upon the successful completion of all pre-employment or background checks required for the role in terms satisfactory to the University.  Also, to meet the University’s obligations under the National Higher Education Code to Prevent and Eliminate Gender-Based Violence you will be asked to declare if you have been investigated for, or found to engaged in, sexual harm or gender-based violence in the course of previous employment or in a legal process. Similarly, your ongoing employment is conditional upon the satisfactory maintenance of all relevant clearances and background check requirements. If you do not meet these conditions, the University may take any necessary step, including the termination of your employment.   EEO statement    At the University of Sydney, our shared values are trust, accountability and excellence and we strive to be a place where everyone can thrive. We are committed to creating a University community that thrives through diversity and reflects the wider community that we serve. We deliver on this through our commitment to diversity and inclusion , evidenced by our people and culture programs, as well as key strategies to increase participation and support the careers of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander People , women , people living with a disability , people from culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds , and those who identify as LGBTQIA+ . We welcome applications from candidates from all backgrounds.    We are proud to be recognised as an Australian Workplace Equality Index (AWEI) Platinum Employer. Find out more about our work on diversity and inclusion .    How to apply   Applications (including a cover letter, CV, and any additional supporting documentation) can be submitted via the Apply button at the top of the page.    For employees of the University or contingent workers, please login into your Workday account and navigate to the Career icon on your Dashboard.  Click on USYD Find Jobs and apply.   For a confidential discussion about the role, or if you require reasonable adjustment or support filling out this application, please contact Simon Drew, Recruitment Operations, Human Resources at recruitment.sea@sydney.edu.au   © The University of Sydney   The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Click to view the Position Description for this role. Applications Close Wednesday 11 November 2026 11:59 PM"
    },
    "https://usyd.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/USYD_EXTERNAL_CAREER_SITE/job/Darlington-Campus/Professor-of-Practice-in-Finance_0157707": {
      "title": "Professor of Practice in Finance",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-27\nPart time\n\nJoin the University of Sydney Business School as a Professor of Practice in Finance Located in Camperdown 2-year fixed term (0.2 FTE) position with an attractive remuneration package   About the opportunity   The University of Sydney Business School is one of the Asia-Pacific region’s leading centres for business research and education. With a highly international outlook, more than 92,000 alumni, over 560 academic and professional staff, and more than 11,000 students from over 65 countries, the Business School delivers a comprehensive suite of undergraduate, postgraduate and executive education programs, including its globally recognised MBA.   The School of Finance is one of Australia’s leading finance schools, with a strong international reputation for research and education. The University of Sydney is ranked 2nd in Australia and 27th globally for Accounting and Finance in the QS World University Rankings by Subject 2026, and 2nd in Australia and 42nd globally for Finance in ShanghaiRanking’s Global Ranking of Academic Subjects 2026.   The School of Finance is seeking to appoint an exceptional Professor of Practice who is recognised as a distinguished leader within the finance profession. This prestigious appointment is designed for an accomplished senior industry leader whose experience, influence and professional achievements have had a significant impact on the finance sector nationally and/or internationally.   As Professor of Practice, you will bring deep industry expertise, contemporary professional insights and strong external networks to the School. Working closely with academic colleagues, students, alumni, industry partners and other external stakeholders, you will help strengthen the connection between leading academic thinking and financial practice.   You will contribute to the School’s strategic priorities by:   providing thought leadership on major developments and emerging issues shaping the finance profession supporting curriculum innovation and helping ensure our programs remain relevant to the evolving needs of industry and society contributing to student learning, mentoring and industry-connected educational experiences strengthening strategic relationships with leading financial institutions, businesses, government, professional bodies and other external stakeholders fostering collaboration between the School and industry, including opportunities for executive engagement, industry-led initiatives and knowledge exchange contributing to strategically important initiatives and new program development within the School enhancing the School’s national and international profile through engagement with industry, government, media and professional networks.   The appointment offers considerable scope for the successful candidate to help shape the role in accordance with their expertise, experience and professional networks.   About you (skills, capabilities and experience)   To be successful in this role, you will be a highly respected and accomplished financial industry leader with substantial senior-level experience in finance or a closely related field and a strong reputation within the profession.   You will bring:   a distinguished record of leadership and professional achievement in the finance sector significant senior executive, advisory, entrepreneurial, regulatory or equivalent professional experience deep knowledge of contemporary developments affecting financial markets and institutions, which may include capital markets, investment management, banking, corporate finance, financial regulation, fintech, sustainability, artificial intelligence and other emerging technologies strong professional networks and the capacity to build meaningful partnerships across industry, government, professional bodies and the wider community, nationally and internationally demonstrated ability to provide thought leadership and contribute to public and professional debate on issues relevant to the finance sector the ability to communicate complex ideas effectively to students, academics, industry leaders and other stakeholders a strong interest in developing the next generation of finance professionals through teaching, mentoring and industry engagement a collaborative and collegial approach, with a commitment to excellence, innovation, inclusion and impact.   Academic qualifications and prior university teaching experience would be highly regarded but are not essential. Professional standing, leadership experience and demonstrated impact within the finance sector will be central to the appointment.   Sponsorship / work rights for Australia You must have unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this employment to apply. Visa sponsorship is not available for this appointment.   Pre-employment checks   Your employment is conditional upon the successful completion of all pre-employment or background checks required for the role in terms satisfactory to the University. Also, to meet the University’s obligations under the National Higher Education Code to Prevent and Eliminate Gender-Based Violence you will be asked to declare if you have been investigated for, or found to engaged in, sexual harm or gender-based violence in the course of previous employment or in a legal process. Similarly, your ongoing employment is conditional upon the satisfactory maintenance of all relevant clearances and background check requirements. If you do not meet these conditions, the University may take any necessary step, including the termination of your employment.   EEO statement     At the University of Sydney, our shared values are trust, accountability and excellence and we strive to be a place where everyone can thrive. We are committed to creating a University community that thrives through diversity and reflects the wider community that we serve. We deliver on this through our commitment to diversity and inclusion , evidenced by our people and culture programs, as well as key strategies to increase participation and support the careers of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander People , women , people living with a disability , people from culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds , and those who identify as LGBTIQ+ . We welcome applications from candidates from all backgrounds.    We are proud to be recognised as an Australian Workplace Equality Index (AWEI) Platinum Employer. Find out more about our work on diversity and inclusion .    How to apply   Applications ( including a CV and cover letter outlining how your skills, capabilities and experience meet the requirements for the role )  can be submitted via the Apply button at the top of the page.     Candidate Information Brochure For employees of the University or contingent workers, please login into your Workday account and navigate to the Career icon on your Dashboard.  Click on USYD Find Jobs and apply.   For a confidential discussion about the role, please contact Professor Buhui Qiu at buhui.qiu@sydney.edu.au .   If you require reasonable adjustment or any documents in alternate formats, please contact Lena Jiang, Recruitment Operations by email to lena.jiang@sydney.edu.au .   © The University of Sydney   The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Click to view the Position Description for this role. Applications Close Sunday 29 November 2026 11:59 PM"
    },
    "https://usyd.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/USYD_EXTERNAL_CAREER_SITE/job/St-Leonards/Clinical-Trials-Project-Manager_0158217": {
      "title": "Clinical Trials Project Manager",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-27\nFull time\n\nFull-time or Part-time (0.5 FTE minimum), 12-month fixed-term opportunity, with potential for extension Drive multi-faceted clinical trials that make a real-world impact, while leading high-performing teams within a globally recognised research environment at the University of Sydney Base Salary $131,108 (pro-rata for part-time) + 17% superannuation     About the opportunity   Join the Northern Clinical School at the University of Sydney as a Project Manager and play a pivotal role in delivering complex, high-impact clinical trials and programs of work. In this role, you will provide end-to-end project management, overseeing multiple workstreams to ensure the successful planning, implementation, and delivery of clinical trials aligned with strategic objectives.   Working under broad direction, you will establish and manage project structures, including governance frameworks, project plans, and resource allocation, to support efficient and compliant trial delivery. You will be responsible for driving progress across concurrent activities, ensuring milestones are achieved, risks are mitigated, and reporting is clear and actionable.   You will collaborate with a diverse range of stakeholders across the University and external partners, fostering strong working relationships to support effective communication, coordination, and delivery. This role requires a high degree of initiative and judgement, with accountability for resolving complex challenges and ensuring the overall success of projects within a dynamic and fast-paced clinical research environment.   Your key responsibilities will be to:   Lead the end-to-end development and delivery of high-quality complex clinical trials, including project planning, trial documentation, database oversight, and preparation of ethics and governance submissions. Manage clinical trial budgets and financial performance, including supporting grant applications, monitoring expenditure, and proactively addressing risks with investigators and program leadership. Ensure clinical trials are conducted in accordance with protocols, ICH-GCP, and ethics and governance requirements, including monitoring participant safety, tracking adverse events, and escalating issues as required. Oversee participant recruitment strategies and study visit processes to ensure targets are met and trial activities are delivered efficiently and consistently. Manage trial data integrity and reporting, including oversight of data collection, quality assurance processes, and liaison with external stakeholders such as Clinical Research Associates. Oversee the acquisition, governance and use of linked health datasets and real-world data sources, ensuring compliance with data governance requirements and supporting high-quality data-driven research outcomes. Provide leadership and people management to the clinical trial team, including workload allocation, mentoring, performance development, and supporting recruitment of staff in line with University policies.   About you   Postgraduate qualifications in Public Health, Epidemiology, Health Data Science, Science or a relevant field. Demonstrated high-level project management expertise, with the ability to effectively manage scope, time, cost, quality, risk, procurement and integration across complex projects. Proven leadership capability, with experience managing teams, exercising sound judgement, and proactively resolving challenges in a dynamic environment. Strong problem-solving skills, with the ability to identify risks, develop practical solutions, and implement strategies to minimise future issues. Excellent interpersonal, verbal and written communication skills, with the ability to influence, negotiate, and build relationships with diverse stakeholders. Experience managing complex projects and multiple workstreams within large or multifaceted organisations, including financial management and budget oversight, and delivery of complex clinical trial designs such as platform, adaptive or multi-site studies.     Sponsorship / work rights for Australia   You must have unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this employment to be eligible to apply. Visa sponsorship is not available for this appointment.   Pre-employment checks and declarations     Your employment is conditional upon the successful completion of all pre-employment or background checks required for the role in terms satisfactory to the University.  Also, to meet the University’s obligations under the National Higher Education Code to Prevent and Eliminate Gender-Based Violence you will be asked to declare if you have been investigated for, or found to engaged in, sexual harm or gender-based violence in the course of previous employment or in a legal process. Similarly, your ongoing employment is conditional upon the satisfactory maintenance of all relevant clearances and background check requirements. If you do not meet these conditions, the University may take any necessary step, including the termination of your employment.   EEO statement     At the University of Sydney, our shared values are trust, accountability and excellence and we strive to be a place where everyone can thrive. We are committed to creating a University community that thrives through diversity and reflects the wider community that we serve. We deliver on this through our commitment to diversity and inclusion , evidenced by our people and culture programs, as well as key strategies to increase participation and support the careers of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander People , women , people living with a disability , people from culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds , and those who identify as LGBTQIA+ . We welcome applications from candidates from all backgrounds.    We are proud to be recognised as an Australian Workplace Equality Index (AWEI) Platinum Employer. Find out more about our work on diversity and inclusion .    How to apply   Applications (including a cover letter, CV, and any additional supporting documentation) can be submitted via the Apply button at the top of the page.     For employees of the University or contingent workers, please login into your Workday account and navigate to the Career icon on your Dashboard. Click on USYD Find Jobs and apply.   For a confidential discussion about the role, or if you require reasonable adjustment or any documents in alternate formats, please contact Roshik Prasad, Recruitment Operations by email to roshik.prasad@sydney.edu.au   © The University of Sydney   The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Click to view the Position Description for this role. Applications Close Tuesday 10 November 2026 11:59 PM"
    },
    "https://usyd.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/USYD_EXTERNAL_CAREER_SITE/job/Camperdown-Campus/The-Parker-Hughes-Chair-of-Diagnostic-Radiology_0158788": {
      "title": "The Parker-Hughes Chair of Diagnostic Radiology",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-27\nFull time\n\nExciting opportunity for a leading clinician-scientist in diagnostic radiology and medical imaging Fixed term full-time position for 5 years with an attractive remuneration package     About the opportunity   The University of Sydney is seeking an outstanding clinician-scientist and academic leader to take up the Parker-Hughes Chair of Diagnostic Radiology, an exceptional opportunity to shape the future of diagnostic radiology and medical imaging through world-leading research, clinical collaboration and innovation.   Established through the generous Parker-Hughes Bequest, the Chair provides a platform to establish an internationally recognized research program based within the University’s Camperdown precinct. You will drive a compelling research vision, lead major research initiatives and build strategic partnerships across the University, health services, medical research institutes, industry and government, advancing imaging science and improving patient outcomes.   Working closely with clinical partners, including Sydney Local Health District, you will translate research discoveries into clinical practice, new technologies and healthcare innovation. You will also contribute to clinical radiology services, strengthening the integration of research, clinical practice and education.   You will attract competitive research funding, recruit and mentor outstanding researchers and higher degree by research candidates, and build a sustainable, high-performing research team. Through collaborative and collegial leadership, you will foster an inclusive, fair and supportive environment that values diverse expertise, encourages scientific excellence and integrity, and develops the next generation of research leaders.   This is an opportunity to build on the legacy of the Parker-Hughes Chair, unite clinical and research communities, and establish a leading program in diagnostic radiology with lasting national and international impact.   About you (Skills, capabilities and experience)   You are an internationally recognised clinician-scientist and academic leader with a distinguished record in diagnostic radiology, medical imaging or a closely related discipline. You bring research vision, clinical credibility and the collaborative leadership needed to build an internationally significant research program.   You will demonstrate:   ·       An outstanding record of influential research outputs, international recognition and sustained impact in diagnostic radiology, medical imaging or a related discipline. ·       A strong track record of securing competitive research funding and leading major research programs, grants and strategic initiatives. ·       The ability to develop and deliver an ambitious research vision, establish multidisciplinary collaborations and build productive partnerships across academia, healthcare, industry, government and research organisations. ·       A commitment to translating research discoveries into clinical practice, new technologies, policy or healthcare innovation, with evidence of meaningful clinical or community impact. ·       A strong record of recruiting, mentoring and developing researchers, supervising higher degree by research candidates, and building high-performing, sustainable teams within an inclusive and supportive research environment. ·       Appropriate clinical qualifications, registration and credentials to undertake the clinical responsibilities associated with the position. ·       The ability to engage and influence diverse stakeholders, build national and international research networks, and represent the University as a leader in imaging and radiology.     Term and remuneration   The successful candidate will be offered a 5-year fixed term full-time position, subject to completion of a satisfactory probation period for new appointees. The successful candidate will be offered an attractive remuneration package commensurate with the responsibilities of the position and the candidate’s relevant experience and qualifications. Sponsorship / work rights for Australia   Visa sponsorship and Relocation assistance will be provided if required.     Pre-employment checks    Your employment is conditional upon the successful completion of all pre-employment or background checks required for the role in terms satisfactory to the University.  Also, to meet the University’s obligations under the National Higher Education Code to Prevent and Eliminate Gender-Based Violence you will be asked to declare if you have been investigated for, or found to engaged in, sexual harm or gender-based violence in the course of previous employment or in a legal process. Similarly, your ongoing employment is conditional upon the satisfactory maintenance of all relevant clearances and background check requirements. If you do not meet these conditions, the University may take any necessary step, including the termination of your employment.     Other   As this position is located on a NSW Health site, it is a regulatory requirement to complete compulsory employment checks prior to starting in this position. For more information and instructions, please visit: NSW Health employment requirements     EEO statement       At the University of Sydney, our shared values are trust, accountability and excellence and we strive to be a place where everyone can thrive. We are committed to creating a University community that thrives through diversity and reflects the wider community that we serve. We deliver on this through our commitment to diversity and inclusion , evidenced by our people and culture programs, as well as key strategies to increase participation and support the careers of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander People , women , people living with a disability , people from culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds , and those who identify as LGBTQIA+ . We welcome applications from candidates from all backgrounds.    We are proud to be recognised as an Australian Workplace Equality Index (AWEI) Platinum Employer. Find out more about our work on diversity and inclusion .       How to apply   Applications ( including a CV and cover letter outlining how your skills, capabilities and experience meet the requirements for the role ) can be submitted via the Apply button at the top of the page. Please refer to the candidate information brochure and position description for further details.   Click to view the Position Description for this role.   Brochure   For employees of the University or contingent workers, please login into your Workday account and navigate to the Career icon on your Dashboard.  Click on USYD Find Jobs and apply.   Intending applicants are welcome to seek further information about the position from: Professor Mark Halaki, Head of School and Dean, Sydney School of Health Sciences ( mark.halaki@sydney.edu.au ), Faculty of Medicine and Health.   For a confidential discussion about the role, or if you require reasonable adjustment or support filling out this application, please contact: Dr Mushtaq Sobhan (Senior Talent Partner, Recruitment Operations, Human Resources) by email mushtaq.sobhan@sydney.edu.au       © The University of Sydney   The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Applications Close Tuesday 24 November 2026 11:59 PM"
    },
    "https://usyd.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/USYD_EXTERNAL_CAREER_SITE/job/Darlington-Campus/Campus-Connect-Consultant_0156777-2": {
      "title": "Campus Connect Consultant",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-27\nFull time\n\nFull time, 12 months fixed term position. Located at University Garage (H07) on Gadigal Country Apply your strong customer service skills in response to enquiries from University of Sydney staff and students Base Salary HEO5, $93,345 - $104,515 p.a. + 17% superannuation This is a Targeted Aboriginal and/or Torres Strait Islander Position. While we welcome applications from all candidates, this is a targeted role for Aboriginal and/or Torres Strait Islander people. As part of our commitment to diversity and inclusion, Aboriginal and/or Torres Strait Islander candidates will be assessed as a priority. This Exemption is claimed under Section 21 of the Anti-Discrimination Act 1977.      The University of Sydney is welcoming applications for an experienced and motivated Campus Connect Consultant to join the Shared Services’ Centre ICT team.   This Campus Connect Consultant position offers the opportunity to deliver exceptional customer service to University of Sydney staff and students across multiple channels, including telephone, email, web, remote, and the self-service portal.   The Campus Connect Consultant will respond to enquiries in person, by phone, or in writing, ensuring all interactions are professional, accurate, and focused on providing a positive customer experience within agreed service time-frames.   The ICT Team manages a diverse range of tasks including phone support, email, Service Now ticket management and face to face assistance at our Campus Connect Hubs. We're looking for someone with amazing customer service skills—both in writing, over the phone, and in person—who is a great multitasker and quick learner with sound computer skills. You'll report to the ICT Team Leader while potentially being cross skilled across other Shared Services Teams including HR, Consulting and Travel and Expenses team.   The role also encompasses transactional and technical administrative support, alongside investigating, analysing, and resolving basic customer enquiries or processing tasks.       What success looks like:    manage phone enquiries and Service Now tickets through to resolution provide face to face student and professional staff support at our Campus Connect Hubs situated across the Faculties identify, nominate, and participate in any continual improvement opportunities across processes, products, services, technology / systems and reporting within the Shared Service Centre work collaboratively with Shared Service Centre staff members and management, as well as other groups, cohorts, and stakeholders across the University attend and participate in scheduled individual team and Shared Service Centre wide meetings and forums, when required.     About you :   tertiary qualification in Human Resources, Information Technology or a related field, OR demonstrated substantial customer service and administrative support experience an understanding of, and respect for, the diverse cultures, histories and perspectives of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples demonstrated high level computer literacy skills and knowledge, with specific proficiency in the Microsoft Office package, email and Interactive Voice Response (IVR) technology demonstrated high level interpersonal skills and expertise, with the ability to convey information to customers with empathy – both students and staff at all levels of the organisation excellent customer service and communication skills – both written and oral, as well as a well-developed telephone manner strong customer service experience, preferably within a shared service or multi-channel support environment experience in both understanding and interpreting organisational policies and procedures, as well as enterprise agreements experience using the ServiceNow (or similar) incident logging system experience with similar building access and contractor management systems, or willingness to learn and quickly become proficient with new systems and tools (such as Gallagher and Contractor Management systems).   Why work with us    We know our people are what make the University's mission possible. Alongside meaningful work, we offer a range of benefits designed to support you financially, personally and professionally.    Key benefits include:    Paid Cultural and Ceremonial Leave: Indigenous employees are entitled to 5 days of paid leave and 10 days of unpaid leave each year to participate in cultural and ceremonial activities and fulfil community obligations.  Career Development: Access to Indigenous professional and career development programs, including mentoring, leadership programs, and development funding  Community and Connection: Be part of a vibrant community with opportunities to connect through staff networks (our YURA Network), mentoring, cultural events and professional Development opportunities  Flexible Work and Exceptional Benefits: Enjoy flexible work arrangements, 17% employer superannuation, Fitness Passport, generous leave provisions, staff discounts and access to a wide range of University facilities and services.        Sponsorship / work rights for Australia   You must have unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this employment to be eligible to apply. Visa sponsorship is not available for this appointment.     Pre-employment checks   Your employment is conditional upon the successful completion of all pre-employment or background checks required for the role in terms satisfactory to the University.  Also, to meet the University’s obligations under the National Higher Education Code to Prevent and Eliminate Gender-Based Violence you will be asked to declare if you have been investigated for, or found to engaged in, sexual harm or gender-based violence in the course of previous employment or in a legal process. Similarly, your ongoing employment is conditional upon the satisfactory maintenance of all relevant clearances and background check requirements. If you do not meet these conditions, the University may take any necessary step, including the termination of your employment.   EEO statement     At the University of Sydney, our shared values are trust, accountability and excellence and we strive to be a place where everyone can thrive. We are committed to creating a University community that thrives through diversity and reflects the wider community that we serve. We deliver on this through our commitment to diversity and inclusion , evidenced by our people and culture programs, as well as key strategies to increase participation and support the careers of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander People , women , people living with a disability , people from culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds , and those who identify as LGBTQIA+ . We welcome applications from candidates from all backgrounds.    We are proud to be recognised as an Australian Workplace Equality Index (AWEI) Platinum Employer. Find out more about our work on diversity and inclusion .      How to apply   Applications (including a cover letter, CV, and any additional supporting documentation) can be submitted via the Apply button at the top of the page.  This is a targeted opportunity for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander people.  For employees of the University or contingent workers, please login into your Workday account and navigate to the Career icon on your Dashboard.  Click on USYD Find Jobs and apply.   For a confidential discussion about the role, or if you require reasonable adjustment or any documents in alternate formats, please contact Kevin Lim, Recruitment Operations by email to kevin.lim@sydney.edu.au .   © The University of Sydney   The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Click to view the Position Description for this role. Applications Close Sunday 08 November 2026 11:59 PM"
    },
    "https://usyd.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/USYD_EXTERNAL_CAREER_SITE/job/Camperdown-Campus/Lecturer-in-Oceanography_0158615-1": {
      "title": "Lecturer in Oceanography",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-28\nFull time\n\nOne (1) Continuing Full Time position at Camperdown Opportunity to make valuable contributions to teaching and research in coastal oceanography and marine geoscience Base Salary $132,533 - $157,381 pa + 17% superannuation About the opportunity The University of Sydney is seeking applications for a Lecturer in Coastal Oceanography. The primary function of this position is to undertake research and teaching in the field of marine geoscience while also making a significant collaborative contribution to research that complements the current coastal and marine research in the School of Geosciences. Fieldwork experience is essential. A research profile that demonstrates a potential connection towards external collaboration with industry and government is desirable.   The position has responsibility for teaching courses in new and existing curricula at the undergraduate and postgraduate level under the broad theme of coastal and marine geoscience. The applicant will be expected to teach fundamental and advanced concepts in coastal oceanography effectively in the classroom, field and laboratory, supported by digital-based approaches including virtual training and teaching. Your key responsibilities will be to: deliver high-quality teaching in coastal oceanography, spanning the full range of coastal systems including coral reefs, and environmental change, with particular emphasis on i) the interplay between coastal processes and large-scale oceanographic drivers, or ii) ecomorphodynamics and biogeomorphological processes teach in cross-disciplinary settings and across undergraduate, honours and postgraduate levels, in classroom, field and laboratory environments design and deliver engaging educational programs and learning experiences that respond to student needs and meet curriculum requirements lead fieldtrips and undertake fieldwork, particularly at One Tree Island Research Station, where your responsibilities will include boat driving, collection of field data and instrument deployment to enrich teaching and training develop a research profile appropriate to career stage and opportunity, evidenced through high-quality publications contribute effectively to administrative and service activities across the School foster a positive, inclusive and collaborative workplace culture support the strategic direction of the School, Faculty and University. The School of Geosciences at the University of Sydney is a dynamic and collegial multi-disciplinary community of scholars and students encompassing the fields of coastal and marine science, geology and geophysics, environmental studies, and physical and human geography. It has a strong global reputation for the quality of its research and teaching across those subdisciplines. Our common passion is to engage in research and teaching that promotes a better understanding of our world and its challenges, and we address key issues facing society including climate change, resource management and sustainability.   The School of Geosciences is firmly committed to creating a diverse workplace by improving equity, access and opportunity. We want to create an environment where individuality is welcomed and celebrated, and a workplace that is open, supportive and safe for everyone. We are actively seeking to improve the diversity of our staffing profile, and welcome applications from women, people of culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds, people of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander descent, and members of the LGBTIQ+ community.   To learn more about the School of Geosciences, click here About you PhD in oceanography or coastal/marine geoscience demonstrated expertise in coastal oceanography, coastal geomorphology, reef systems, and marine/coastal environmental change including the interplay between coastal processes and large-scale oceanographic drivers skills spanning instrument deployment, fieldwork organisation and leadership in remote coastal environments, numerical modelling, and/or large data manipulation and analyses proficiency in swimming, snorkeling, field logistics and compliance, working in remote coastal environments Pre-employment checks Your employment is conditional upon the successful completion of all pre-employment or background checks required for the role in terms satisfactory to the University.  Also, to meet the University’s obligations under the National Higher Education Code to Prevent and Eliminate Gender-Based Violence you will be asked to declare if you have been investigated for, or found to engaged in, sexual harm or gender-based violence in the course of previous employment or in a legal process. Similarly, your ongoing employment is conditional upon the satisfactory maintenance of all relevant clearances and background check requirements. If you do not meet these conditions, the University may take any necessary step, including the termination of your employment. EEO statement At the University of Sydney, our shared values are trust, accountability and excellence and we strive to be a place where everyone can thrive. We are committed to creating a University community that thrives through diversity and reflects the wider community that we serve. We deliver on this through our commitment to diversity and inclusion , evidenced by our people and culture programs, as well as key strategies to increase participation and support the careers of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander People , women , people living with a disability , people from culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds , and those who identify as LGBTQIA+ . We welcome applications from candidates from all backgrounds.    We are proud to be recognised as an Australian Workplace Equality Index (AWEI) Platinum Employer. Find out more about our work on diversity and inclusion .  How to apply Applications (including a cover letter, CV, and any additional supporting documentation) can be submitted via the Apply button at the top of the page. For employees of the University or contingent workers, please login into your Workday account and navigate to the Career icon on your Dashboard. Click on USYD Find Jobs and apply. For a confidential discussion about the role, please contact the Head of School of Geosciences, Professor Kerrylee Rogers via email: kerrylee.rogers@sydney.edu.au   If you require reasonable adjustment or any documents in alternate formats, please contact Deepna Chand, Recruitment Operations by email to recruitment.sea@sydney.edu.au . . © The University of Sydney The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Click to view the Position Description for this role. Applications Close Sunday 29 November 2026 11:59 PM"
    },
    "https://usyd.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/USYD_EXTERNAL_CAREER_SITE/job/Darlington-Campus/Postdoctoral-Research-Associate-in-Liquid-Metals--Sensors-and-Biomedical-Technologies_0157835-1": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Research Associate in Liquid Metals, Sensors and Biomedical Technologies",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-28\nFull time\n\nFull time, 12 months fixed term, located within the School of Chemical and Biomolecular Engineering Opportunity to contribute to world-leading research within the Faculty of Engineering Base Salary, Academic Level A $117,936 - $125,896 p.a + 17% superannuation     About the opportunity   The Faculty of Engineering is seeking a Postdoctoral Research Associate in Liquid Metals, Sensors and Biomedical Technologies to contribute to its research agenda under the leadership of Professor Kourosh Kalantar Zadeh, in the School of Chemical and Biomolecular Engineering.   The successful candidate will undertake high-quality research and scholarly activities, working both independently and collaboratively within a multidisciplinary research environment. This role offers the opportunity to develop an emerging research profile while contributing to innovative research outcomes with real-world impact. Your key responsibilities will be to: work collaboratively with the team as a group member aim to create impactful scientific outcomes by developing novel liquid-metal-based systems aim to contribute to commercialisation activities participate in the supervision of research students be involved in the development of grant applications that support his or her career lead laboratory health and safety procedures.   About you   have a PhD in one of the relevant fields of Chemical Engineering, Materials Science or Chemistry show past involvement in the development of liquid-metal-based systems demonstrate commitment to and willingness to conduct experiments accurately and safely show collegiality and the ability to work in a team demonstrate strong problem-solving skills and the ability to work independently.     Sponsorship / work rights for Australia   You must have unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this employment to be eligible to apply. Visa sponsorship is not available for this appointment.   Pre-employment checks and declarations     Your employment is conditional upon the successful completion of all pre-employment or background checks required for the role in terms satisfactory to the University.  Also, to meet the University’s obligations under the National Higher Education Code to Prevent and Eliminate Gender-Based Violence you will be asked to declare if you have been investigated for, or found to engaged in, sexual harm or gender-based violence in the course of previous employment or in a legal process. Similarly, your ongoing employment is conditional upon the satisfactory maintenance of all relevant clearances and background check requirements. If you do not meet these conditions, the University may take any necessary step, including the termination of your employment.   EEO statement    At the University of Sydney, our shared values are trust, accountability and excellence and we strive to be a place where everyone can thrive. We are committed to creating a University community that thrives through diversity and reflects the wider community that we serve. We deliver on this through our commitment to diversity and inclusion , evidenced by our people and culture programs, as well as key strategies to increase participation and support the careers of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander People , women , people living with a disability , people from culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds , and those who identify as LGBTQIA+ . We welcome applications from candidates from all backgrounds.    We are proud to be recognised as an Australian Workplace Equality Index (AWEI) Platinum Employer. Find out more about our work on diversity and inclusion .    How to apply   Applications (including a cover letter, CV, and any additional supporting documentation) can be submitted via the Apply button at the top of the page.    For employees of the University or contingent workers, please login into your Workday account and navigate to the Career icon on your Dashboard.  Click on USYD Find Jobs and apply.   For a confidential discussion about the role, or if you require reasonable adjustment or support filling out this application, please contact Simon Drew, Recruitment Operations, Human Resources at recruitment.sea@sydney.edu.au   © The University of Sydney   The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Click to view the Position Description for this role. Applications Close Thursday 12 November 2026 11:59 PM"
    },
    "https://usyd.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/USYD_EXTERNAL_CAREER_SITE/job/Darlington-Campus/Program-Manager_0158532": {
      "title": "Program Manager",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-28\nFull time\n\nFull-time continuing position Lead flagship researcher entrepreneurship and commercialisation programs at the Sydney Knowledge Hub Base Salary $131,108 - $147,858 + 17% superannuation   About the opportunity   The Sydney Knowledge Hub is the University of Sydney’s venture incubator, supporting researchers to translate groundbreaking ideas into real-world impact through new venture creation, industry engagement and commercialisation. This is an exciting opportunity to shape and deliver impactful and hands-on entrepreneurship programs that accelerate research outcomes and strengthen the University's innovation ecosystem.   As the Program Manager , you will lead the delivery and continuous improvement of the Sydney Knowledge Hub’s researcher entrepreneurship programs, including the PERIscope Commercialisation Award and SPARK Sydney, as well as new programs. You will work closely with researchers, industry partners, mentors, investors and University stakeholders to support the development of new ventures, strengthen commercialisation capability and expand the University's impact through innovation. You will also play a key role in identifying emerging opportunities, securing funding, building strategic partnerships and contributing to the broader research entrepreneurship strategy. This position manages one direct report and operates with a high degree of autonomy while collaborating extensively across the University and external innovation ecosystem.   Your key responsibilities will be to:   lead the delivery and enhancement of researcher entrepreneurship and commercialisation programs, including PERIscope and SPARK Sydney build and maintain strong relationships with researchers, faculties, industry partners, mentors, investors and other key stakeholders develop program resources, learning materials, reporting frameworks and evaluation processes to maximise participant outcomes and demonstrate impact identify gaps in entrepreneurship support, develop new initiatives and contribute to funding, grant and philanthropic submissions oversee program governance, budgets, reporting and operational delivery to ensure objectives, timelines and quality standards are achieved raise the profile of research entrepreneurship across the University through engagement activities, workshops, events and strategic communications   About you   postgraduate qualifications in a relevant discipline, or an equivalent combination of education, training and experience demonstrated experience designing and delivering startup, innovation, entrepreneurship or research commercialisation programs substantial program and project management experience, including managing complex initiatives, budgets and multiple concurrent workstreams proven ability to engage, influence and collaborate with diverse internal and external stakeholders at all levels experience leading, developing and supporting staff performance and professional growth an understanding of new venture creation, ideally through lived experience, and networks into the innovation ecosystem in NSW is seen favorably   Sponsorship / work rights for Australia   You must have unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this employment to be eligible to apply. Visa sponsorship is not available for this appointment.     Pre-employment checks and declarations       Your employment is conditional upon the successful completion of all pre-employment or background checks required for the role in terms satisfactory to the University.  Also, to meet the University’s obligations under the National Higher Education Code to Prevent and Eliminate Gender-Based Violence you will be asked to declare if you have been investigated for, or found to engaged in, sexual harm or gender-based violence in the course of previous employment or in a legal process. Similarly, your ongoing employment is conditional upon the satisfactory maintenance of all relevant clearances and background check requirements. If you do not meet these conditions, the University may take any necessary step, including the termination of your employment.   EEO statement     At the University of Sydney, our shared values are trust, accountability and excellence and we strive to be a place where everyone can thrive. We are committed to creating a University community that thrives through diversity and reflects the wider community that we serve. We deliver on this through our commitment to diversity and inclusion , evidenced by our people and culture programs, as well as key strategies to increase participation and support the careers of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander People , women , people living with a disability , people from culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds , and those who identify as LGBTQIA+ . We welcome applications from candidates from all backgrounds.    We are proud to be recognised as an Australian Workplace Equality Index (AWEI) Platinum Employer. Find out more about our work on diversity and inclusion .    How to apply   Applications (including a cover letter, CV, and any additional supporting documentation) can be submitted via the Apply button at the top of the page.     For employees of the University or contingent workers, please login into your Workday account and navigate to the Career icon on your Dashboard.  Click on USYD Find Jobs and apply.   For a confidential discussion about the role, or if you require reasonable adjustment or any documents in alternate formats, please contact Kale Claffey Recruitment Operations by email to kale.claffey@sydney.edu.au   © The University of Sydney   The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Click to view the Position Description for this role. Applications Close Wednesday 11 November 2026 11:59 PM"
    },
    "https://usyd.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/USYD_EXTERNAL_CAREER_SITE/job/Camperdown-Campus/Bosch-Chair-of-Infectious-Diseases_0158718-1": {
      "title": "Bosch Chair of Infectious Diseases",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-28\nFull time\n\nExciting opportunity for an outstanding academic who is a leading authority in Infectious Diseases with demonstrated education and research experience. The role may sit within the School of Medical Sciences or the Sydney Medical School. Full-time Professor (Level E) appointment with an attractive remuneration package.   About the opportunity   The Faculty of Medicine and Health is the largest faculty at the University of Sydney, and contains seven academic schools, including the School of Medical Sciences and the Sydney Medical School. The School of Medical Sciences is recognised worldwide as one of the leading centres for medical science research and education. Our research has a clear focus on impact on a local and global scale and each year is supported by over $50M in competitive grant funding. With over 100 years of excellence in education, we are proud to be training the next generation of medical scientists who will be leaders in their chosen field of work. The Sydney Medical School is internationally recognised for research and teaching in health and medicine. With formal links to more than 50 hospitals and health care facilities across New South Wales, The Sydney Medical School is Australia’s longest established medical school, offering outstanding opportunities for academics and students to participate in sector-leading practices in education, research and clinical care.   We are seeking to appoint an outstanding senior academic as the Bosch Chair of Infectious Diseases (Professor/Level E) in teaching and research. Depending on the candidate the role may be appointed in the School of Medical Sciences or the Sydney Medical School. This position is suited to an ambitious person with a passion for building a program of work in infectious diseases. The Chair will have an internationally outstanding research program which will complement and leverage the research activities in the school and faculty. We are looking for a scientific leader who is capable of fostering connections with a wide network of academic, industry and professional institutions, partners, and stakeholders.   The Chair must have an international reputation in infectious disease research, either in basic or applied science and/or even have a slant towards a translational research program that has commercial milestones. The Chair will be required to mentor and develop the career aspirations of students and academic staff. In addition to their research program, they will actively contribute to teaching, in particular supervision of students and where appropriate provide teaching to undergraduate and or postgraduate students. The Chair will report to the relevant Head of School.     About you (skills, capabilities and experience)   The successful candidate will:   − hold a PhD in a relevant discipline and an international reputation for outstanding scholarship and research − demonstrate experience in people leadership and management of a research team within the higher education sector − have experience supervising and mentoring of higher degree by research students to completion − have an outstanding track record in obtaining grant funding and an exceptional publication record with original contributions in the field of infectious diseases − have excellent interpersonal and communication skills and proven ability and experience in working cooperatively and collegially in inclusive decision-making environments. − may have experience in educational design and delivery, including recognition for achieving improved or consistently excellent student outcomes     Term and remuneration   The successful candidate will be offered a 5-year fixed term position on a full-time basis, with the possibility for further 5 years renewal subject to performance and funding. The successful candidate will be offered an attractive remuneration package commensurate with the responsibilities of the position and the candidate’s relevant experience and qualifications.   Sponsorship / work rights for Australia   Visa Sponsorship and relocation assistance will be provided if required.      Pre-employment checks   Your employment is conditional upon the successful completion of all pre-employment or background checks required for the role in terms satisfactory to the University.  Also, to meet the University’s obligations under the National Higher Education Code to Prevent and Eliminate Gender-Based Violence you will be asked to declare if you have been investigated for, or found to engaged in, sexual harm or gender-based violence in the course of previous employment or in a legal process. Similarly, your ongoing employment is conditional upon the satisfactory maintenance of all relevant clearances and background check requirements. If you do not meet these conditions, the University may take any necessary step, including the termination of your employment.   Other    If this position is designated as involving child-related work, you will be required to apply for and obtain a Working with Children Check clearance in accordance with the Child Protection (Working with Children) Act 2012 to undertake or remain in the position.   EEO statement     At the University of Sydney, our shared values are trust, accountability and excellence and we strive to be a place where everyone can thrive. We are committed to creating a University community that thrives through diversity and reflects the wider community that we serve. We deliver on this through our commitment to diversity and inclusion , evidenced by our people and culture programs, as well as key strategies to increase participation and support the careers of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander People , women , people living with a disability , people from culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds , and those who identify as LGBTQIA+ . We welcome applications from candidates from all backgrounds.    We are proud to be recognised as an Australian Workplace Equality Index (AWEI) Platinum Employer. Find out more about our work on diversity and inclusion .    How to apply   Applications ( including a CV and cover letter outlining how your skills, capabilities and experience meet the requirements for the role ) can be submitted via the Apply button at the top of the page. Please refer to the candidate information brochure and position description for further details.   Brochure   Click to view the Position Description for this role.   For employees of the University or contingent workers, please login into your Workday account and navigate to the Career icon on your Dashboard.  Click on USYD Find Jobs and apply.   For a confidential discussion about the role, or if you require reasonable adjustment or any documents in alternate formats, please contact Dr Mushtaq Sobhan (Senior Talent Partner, Senior Talent Acquisition, Recruitment Operations) by email mushtaq.sobhan@sydney.edu.au   © The University of Sydney   The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Applications Close Saturday 28 November 2026 11:59 PM"
    },
    "https://usyd.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/USYD_EXTERNAL_CAREER_SITE/job/Camperdown-Campus/Student-Success-Officer--Identified-_0157670-1": {
      "title": "Student Success Officer (Identified)",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-28\nFull time\n\nFull time, continuing (contingent on funding) Provide learning and case management support for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander students completing study with the University Base Salary $107,312 + 17% superannuation   About the opportunity   This position has been designated for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander people only under the provisions of section 126 of the Anti-Discrimination Act 1977 (NSW).     If you identify as an Aboriginal and/or Torres Strait Islander person, please submit a copy of your Confirmation of Aboriginality with your application.   The Gadigal Centre is the University of Sydney's dedicated student centre for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander students, providing academic, pastoral and cultural support services to Indigenous students to help them achieve their full potential.   As a Student Success Officer at the Gadigal Centre, you will liaise with Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander students enrolled in study at the University, supporting them in all aspects of university life. You will work under the direction of the Student Success Manager and within a team of Student Success Officers to serve as the first contact point for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander students.   Your key responsibilities will be to:   provide learning guidance, information, advice, and case-management support to Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander students facilitate student interaction with peers, mentors and academics support student engagement  events and opportunities for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander students liaise with others (Accommodation, Financial Assistance, Disability Services etc.) to ensure services are utilised by Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander students.   About you   knowledge and understanding of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander societies and cultures demonstrated first-hand lived experience and engagement in Aboriginal or Torres Strait Islander communities, and/or working with students computer skills, including MS Office and CRM systems experience working in the tertiary education preferred, but not essential.   The University of Sydney YURA Network, formally the Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Staff Network promotes sharing of experiences, networking and professional support for career development and mentoring. It is supported by the  Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Employment Plan 2022-2024  that aims to increase employment and development opportunities for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander staff.   The University recognises that the cultural load undertaken by Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander staff on behalf of the University culturally enriches the institution. It also advances the University’s commitment to empowering Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander staff and communities. Engagement with cultural duties, engagement with Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander traditional knowledge holders and navigating cross-cultural interactions are recognised as crucial elements of this role and will be allocated appropriate time in workloads, including work plans, and will be considered in criteria for promotion.      Sponsorship / work rights for Australia   You must have unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this employment to be eligible to apply. Visa sponsorship is not available for this appointment.     Pre-employment checks and declarations       Your employment is conditional upon the successful completion of all pre-employment or background checks required for the role in terms satisfactory to the University.  Also, to meet the University’s obligations under the National Higher Education Code to Prevent and Eliminate Gender-Based Violence you will be asked to declare if you have been investigated for, or found to engaged in, sexual harm or gender-based violence in the course of previous employment or in a legal process. Similarly, your ongoing employment is conditional upon the satisfactory maintenance of all relevant clearances and background check requirements. If you do not meet these conditions, the University may take any necessary step, including the termination of your employment.   This position is designated as involving child-related work. To undertake or remain in this position, you are required to apply for and obtain a Working with Children Check clearance in accordance with the Child Protection (Working with Children) Act 2012.   EEO statement     At the University of Sydney, our shared values are trust, accountability and excellence and we strive to be a place where everyone can thrive. We are committed to creating a University community that thrives through diversity and reflects the wider community that we serve. We deliver on this through our commitment to diversity and inclusion , evidenced by our people and culture programs, as well as key strategies to increase participation and support the careers of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander People , women , people living with a disability , people from culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds , and those who identify as LGBTQIA+ . We welcome applications from candidates from all backgrounds.    We are proud to be recognised as an Australian Workplace Equality Index (AWEI) Platinum Employer. Find out more about our work on diversity and inclusion .    How to apply   Applications (including a cover letter, CV, and any additional supporting documentation) can be submitted via the Apply button at the top of the page.     For employees of the University or contingent workers, please login into your Workday account and navigate to the Career icon on your Dashboard.  Click on USYD Find Jobs and apply.   For a confidential discussion about the role, or if you require reasonable adjustment or any documents in alternate formats, please contact Tooba Fida, Recruitment Operations by email to tooba.fida@sydney.edu.au   © The University of Sydney   The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Click to view the Position Description for this role. Applications Close Wednesday 11 November 2026 11:59 PM"
    },
    "https://usyd.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/USYD_EXTERNAL_CAREER_SITE/job/Darlington-Campus/Research-Assistant_0155143-1": {
      "title": "Research Assistant",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-28\nPart time\n\nCasual Research assistant to support the Business School Support research projects involving the impact of AI HEO 6 Step 1 $73.45 per hour plus Superannuation    About the opportunity  PRIMARY FUNCTION The Research Officer leads research projects and optimises grant management processes. They drive the adoption of innovative research methodologies, and oversee the comprehensive management of data. They also nurture partnerships, manage research documentation, and ensure impactful dissemination of research outcomes. DECISION MAKING AND DELEGATIONS OF AUTHORITY The Research Officer works under broad supervision, performing tasks as directed and in accordance with defined responsibilities. They choose appropriate methods of work and plan and prioritise tasks. They also resolve routine matters related to responsibilities and collaborate with others to resolve complex matters. Your key responsibilities will be to: lead and optimise research projects, ensuring methodologies are refined and research goals are met. oversee the lifecycle of grant applications, from conceptualisation to final reporting, ensuring optimal funding acquisition. provide advice on research direction, updating the team on the latest research methodologies and compliance requirements. coordinate data management processes, ensuring thorough analysis and reporting that drives research insights. strengthen partnerships with key stakeholders, enhancing research collaborations and joint initiatives. streamline administrative processes, ensuring efficient management of research documentation, publications, and compliance paperwork. take the lead in handling stakeholder communications, ensuring that concerns are addressed effectively and timely while fostering a culture of trust and collaboration within the work area and beyond. facilitate timely report submission, uphold data integrity according to university systems, and partner with managers to advance compliance initiatives. coordinate purchasing processes and resolve any discrepancies in payment schedules. champion a proactive approach to health and safety, review and provide feedback on work area protocols, and collaborate cross-functionally to ensure consistent safety standards across tasks and projects. undertake other duties appropriate within the classification level as required. About you experience in people management, including managing staff performance and development coordinating and/or managing grant application process coordinating and/or managing grant application process managing, reporting, and disseminating research data   We will be reviewing applications and contacting candidates upon submission, so please kindly apply at your earliest convenience.   Sponsorship / work rights for Australia Visa sponsorship is not available for this position.  This role requires valid work rights for Australia.   Pre-employment checks and declarations   Your employment is conditional upon the successful completion of all pre-employment or background checks required for the role in terms satisfactory to the University.  Also, to meet the University’s obligations under the National Higher Education Code to Prevent and Eliminate Gender-Based Violence you will be asked to declare if you have been investigated for, or found to engaged in, sexual harm or gender-based violence in the course of previous employment or in a legal process. Similarly, your ongoing employment is conditional upon the satisfactory maintenance of all relevant clearances and background check requirements. If you do not meet these conditions, the University may take any necessary step, including the termination of your employment.   EEO statement   At the University of Sydney, our shared values are trust, accountability and excellence and we strive to be a place where everyone can thrive. We are committed to creating a University community that thrives through diversity and reflects the wider community that we serve. We deliver on this through our commitment to diversity and inclusion , evidenced by our people and culture programs, as well as key strategies to increase participation and support the careers of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander People , women , people living with a disability , people from culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds , and those who identify as LGBTQIA+ . We welcome applications from candidates from all backgrounds.    We are proud to be recognised as an Australian Workplace Equality Index (AWEI) Platinum Employer. Find out more about our work on diversity and inclusion .    How to apply Applications (including a cover letter, CV, and any additional supporting documentation) can be submitted via the Apply button at the top of the page.    For employees of the University or contingent workers, please login into your Workday account and navigate to the Career icon on your Dashboard.  Click on USYD Find Jobs and apply.   For a confidential discussion about the role, or if you require reasonable adjustment or any documents in alternate formats, please contact Michael Abelardo , Recruitment Consultant Recruitment Operations, by email to casual-recruitment.admin@sydney.edu.au    © The University of Sydney   The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Click to view the Position Description for this role. Applications Close Tuesday 03 November 2026 11:59 PM"
    },
    "https://usyd.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/USYD_EXTERNAL_CAREER_SITE/job/Camperdown-Campus/Executive-Director--Research-Services_0158799-1": {
      "title": "Executive Director, Research Services",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-28\nFull time\n\nFull time, 5 year fixed term position Contribute to the University of Sydney’s research ambitions by delivering innovative, scalable and researcher-centered services that enable world-class research outcomes. Offering a competitive remuneration package including 17% superannuation   About the opportunity   The University of Sydney is seeking an accomplished and strategic senior leader to join the Research Portfolio as Executive Director, Research Services, with responsibility for leading and transforming the services that enable research across the University.   Reporting to the Deputy Vice-Chancellor (Research), the Executive Director provides senior executive leadership for the University’s researcher-facing services. The role leads five key areas: Research Pipeline and Pre-Award; Post-Award Research Services; Clinical Trials; Research Ethics, Governance and Assurance; and Research Services and Improvement, bringing these functions together through an integrated, end-to-end service model.   The Executive Director will lead the continued transformation of research services, creating a more integrated, consistent and researcher-centred approach that reduces administrative burden, simplifies processes, strengthens accountability and compliance, and makes it easier for researchers to navigate the University’s research environment. The portfolio encompasses research funding and development, pre- and post-award administration, research contracts, clinical trials, ethics and governance, research integrity administration, research data governance, research security, research systems and service improvement.   Working in close partnership with DVCR Executive team faculty and academic leaders, multidisciplinary initiatives, and professional service units, the Executive Director will ensure research services effectively support the University’s strategic ambitions and the delivery of world-class research. The role will provide trusted advice to the Deputy Vice-Chancellor (Research), drive service and operational excellence across the Research Portfolio, and champion continuous improvement and innovation in how researchers are supported.   The role will engage with and maintain exceptional relationships with internal stakeholders including academic leaders and university central portfolios to enable the development and implementation of seamless 'end-to-end' processes and systems that support service delivery, as well as with key external stakeholders to influence policies, processes and systems that impact on the research activities of the portfolio and university.   About you (skills, capabilities and experience)   Significant senior executive leadership experience, with a demonstrated ability to lead large, multidisciplinary professional teams and develop high-performing senior leaders. Deep knowledge and understanding of the research lifecycle and research services environment. A strong track record of leading complex service transformation and organisational change, including redesigning operating models, simplifying processes, improving systems and reducing administrative burden for researchers. Demonstrated capacity to translate strategy into effective operational delivery, establishing clear accountabilities, service standards and performance measures and delivering measurable improvements in service quality and researcher experience. Excellent stakeholder leadership and influencing skills, with the credibility to build trusted relationships with senior academic and professional leaders, researchers, government and funding bodies, regulators, affiliated institutions and external partners. Highly developed judgement and risk management capability, with experience navigating complex regulatory, governance, compliance, reputational and operational issues. Strong financial and organisational acumen, including experience managing significant budgets and resources, workforce planning and making strategic investment and prioritisation decision. Excellent communication, negotiation and executive advisory capability, with the ability to navigate complexity and ambiguity, resolve competing priorities and provide clear, trusted advice on sensitive and high-impact matters. Demonstrated resilience, composure and sound judgement under pressure, with the ability to remain calm and solutions-focused when managing competing priorities, sensitive issues, tight deadlines and complex or high-risk situations.   Key benefits    a University contribution of 17% of your base salary to your superannuation fund  tax-efficient salary packaging options for cars, laptops, and additional contributions to your superannuation fund  generous relocation allowance if moving from interstate or overseas  access to fitness passport, as well as discounted rates on university sports and fitness centers  gain the recognition of working in a leading University.  Sponsorship / work rights for Australia   You must have unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this employment to be eligible to apply. Visa sponsorship is not available for this appointment.   Term and remuneration   The successful candidate will be offered a five-year fixed term position on a full-time basis, with the possibility for renewal subject to performance and funding agreement. The appointment is subject to completion of a satisfactory probation period for new appointees.   Pre-employment checks   Your employment is conditional upon the successful completion of all pre-employment or background checks required for the role in terms satisfactory to the University.  Also, to meet the University’s obligations under the National Higher Education Code to Prevent and Eliminate Gender-Based Violence you will be asked to declare if you have been investigated for, or found to engaged in, sexual harm or gender-based violence in the course of previous employment or in a legal process. Similarly, your ongoing employment is conditional upon the satisfactory maintenance of all relevant clearances and background check requirements. If you do not meet these conditions, the University may take any necessary step, including the termination of your employment.   EEO statement     At the University of Sydney, our shared values are trust, accountability and excellence and we strive to be a place where everyone can thrive. We are committed to creating a University community that thrives through diversity and reflects the wider community that we serve. We deliver on this through our commitment to diversity and inclusion , evidenced by our people and culture programs, as well as key strategies to increase participation and support the careers of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander People , women , people living with a disability , people from culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds , and those who identify as LGBTQIA+ . We welcome applications from candidates from all backgrounds.    We are proud to be recognised as an Australian Workplace Equality Index (AWEI) Platinum Employer. Find out more about our work on diversity and inclusion .    How to apply   Applications ( including a CV and cover letter outlining how your skills, capabilities and experience meet the requirements for the role ) can be submitted via the Apply button at the top of the page. Please refer to the candidate information brochure and position description for further details.   For employees of the University or contingent workers, please login into your Workday account and navigate to the Career icon on your Dashboard.  Click on USYD Find Jobs and apply.   For a confidential discussion about the role, or if you require reasonable adjustment or any documents in alternate formats, please contact John Flynn (Senior Talent Partner, Senior Talent Acquisition, Recruitment Operations) on +61 481 564 562 or by email john.flynn2@sydney.edu.au   © The University of Sydney   The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Click to view the Position Description for this role. Applications Close Monday 16 November 2026 11:59 PM"
    },
    "https://usyd.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/USYD_EXTERNAL_CAREER_SITE/job/Darlington-Campus/Senior-Student-Marketing-Officer--International-_0157679-3": {
      "title": "Senior Student Marketing Officer (International)",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-29\nFull time\n\n• Full time, fixed term until 22 September 2027 • Opportunity to work on University wide Marketing Communications campaigns across international markets • Base Salary $117K + 17% superannuation About the opportunity The Senior Student Marketing Officer (International) plays a pivotal role in driving international student recruitment through the delivery of innovative marketing campaigns, compelling content, and targeted communications. Working collaboratively with Marketing and Communications colleagues and key University stakeholders, the role develops integrated, data-informed initiatives that enhance brand reputation, engage prospective students, and deliver measurable results. About you • strong understanding of international student recruitment trends, with the ability to translate market insights into effective marketing strategies • demonstrated success developing and delivering integrated brand and product marketing campaigns across digital, social, email, web, content, and traditional media channels • experience analysing campaign performance, preparing insightful reports, and using data-driven recommendations to continuously optimise marketing outcomes • exceptional written, verbal, and interpersonal communication skills, with the ability to craft compelling messages for diverse audiences • highly developed project management capabilities, with a proven ability to manage multiple priorities, deadlines, and stakeholders in a fast-paced environment • significant experience building and maintaining strong stakeholder relationships, influencing outcomes, and collaborating effectively within large and complex organisations. Sponsorship / work rights for Australia You must have unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this employment to be eligible to apply. Visa sponsorship is not available for this appointment. Pre-employment checks and declarations Your employment is conditional upon the successful completion of all pre-employment or background checks required for the role in terms satisfactory to the University. Also, to meet the University’s obligations under the National Higher Education Code to Prevent and Eliminate Gender-Based Violence you will be asked to declare if you have been investigated for, or found to engaged in, sexual harm or gender-based violence in the course of previous employment or in a legal process. Similarly, your ongoing employment is conditional upon the satisfactory maintenance of all relevant clearances and background check requirements. If you do not meet these conditions, the University may take any necessary step, including the termination of your employment. EEO statement At the University of Sydney, our shared values are trust, accountability and excellence and we strive to be a place where everyone can thrive. We are committed to creating a University community that thrives through diversity and reflects the wider community that we serve. We deliver on this through our commitment to diversity and inclusion, evidenced by our people and culture programs, as well as key strategies to increase participation and support the careers of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander People, women, people living with a disability, people from culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds, and those who identify as LGBTQIA+. We welcome applications from candidates from all backgrounds. We are proud to be recognised as an Australian Workplace Equality Index (AWEI) Platinum Employer. Find out more about our work on diversity and inclusion. How to apply Applications (including a cover letter, CV, and any additional supporting documentation) can be submitted via the Apply button at the top of the page. For employees of the University or contingent workers, please login into your Workday account and navigate to the Career icon on your Dashboard. Click on USYD Find Jobs and apply. For a confidential discussion about the role, or if you require reasonable adjustment or any documents in alternate formats, please contact Riviera Son, Recruitment Operations by email to riviera.son@sydney.edu.au. © The University of Sydney The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Click to view the Position Description for this role. Applications Close Wednesday 04 November 2026 11:59 PM"
    },
    "https://usyd.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/USYD_EXTERNAL_CAREER_SITE/job/Camperdown-Campus/Lead--Library-Aboriginal-and-Torres-Strait-Islander-Engagement--Identified-_0158167-1": {
      "title": "Lead, Library Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Engagement (Identified)",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-29\nFull time\n\nFull-time, Continuing position Location: Fisher Library, Camperdown on Gadigal Country $152,045 base + 17% Superannuation   Indigenous Identified Role This position has been designated for Aboriginal and/or Torres Strait Islander people only under the provisions of section 126 of the Anti-Discrimination Act 1977 (NSW). Should your application be progressed, applicants will be required to provide confirmation of Aboriginal and/or Torres Strait Islander identity in line with University requirements.   About the opportunity This is a unique opportunity to take a lead role in shaping the future direction of the University Library's Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander initiatives. Working across all Library divisions, you will provide strategic leadership and coordination to deliver the Library's Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Strategic Roadmap and Workforce Plan, partnering closely with senior leaders and key stakeholders across the University.   You'll champion culturally safe practices, strengthen cultural capability and help ensure Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander voices and perspectives are reflected in decision-making and strategic priorities. A key focus of the role is supporting and growing a strong Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander staff community across the Library.   If you're passionate about driving meaningful change and creating lasting impact, this role offers the opportunity to influence strategy while supporting outcomes for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander staff, students and communities.   What success looks like: In this role, you'll work collaboratively across the Library and University to drive strategic initiatives, strengthen relationships, and create meaningful outcomes for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander staff, students and communities. Partner with Library leaders, the Gadigal Centre, DVC Indigenous Strategy and Services and other key stakeholders. Coordinate the implementation of the Library's Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander strategies, plans and priorities. Provide expert advice to strengthen cultural capability and culturally safe workplaces across the Library. Build and maintain strong relationships with Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander staff, students and communities. Foster an engaged and connected Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander staff community across the Library. Lead reporting, evaluation and continuous improvement activities to support strategic outcomes.   About you Identify as an Aboriginal and/or Torres Strait Islander person. Experience leading initiatives that support culturally safe practices and positive outcomes for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples and communities. Experience providing specialist advice and influencing strategic priorities across complex stakeholder environments. Experience leading, developing and supporting teams, including coaching and performance development. Demonstrated knowledge of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander cultures, communities and protocols, and the ability to apply this knowledge in a professional environment. Ability to engage respectfully and effectively with Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander staff, students and communities, applying appropriate cultural protocols. Relevant qualifications and/or equivalent lived, community and professional experience in Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander engagement, library services, archives, museums, education, community development, social sciences, public policy or a related field.   We recognise that candidates may bring different combinations of skills, experience and knowledge. If you're passionate about creating positive outcomes for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples and communities, and believe you have the capability to succeed in this role, we encourage you to apply.   Why work with us We know our people are what make the University's mission possible. Alongside meaningful work, we offer a range of benefits designed to support you financially, personally and professionally.   Key benefits include: Paid Cultural and Ceremonial Leave: Indigenous employees are entitled to 5 days of paid leave and 10 days of unpaid leave each year to participate in cultural and ceremonial activities and fulfil community obligations. Career Development : Access to Indigenous professional and career development programs, including mentoring, leadership programs, and development funding Community and Connection: Be part of a vibrant community with opportunities to connect through staff networks (our YURA Network), mentoring, cultural events and professional Development opportunities Flexible Work and Exceptional Benefits: Enjoy flexible work arrangements, 17% employer superannuation, Fitness Passport, generous leave provisions, staff discounts and access to a wide range of University facilities and services.     Sponsorship / work rights for Australia Please note: You must have unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this employment to apply. Visa sponsorship is not available for this appointment.   Pre-employment checks and declarations   Your employment is conditional upon the successful completion of all pre-employment or background checks required for the role in terms satisfactory to the University.  Also, to meet the University’s obligations under the National Higher Education Code to Prevent and Eliminate Gender-Based Violence you will be asked to declare if you have been investigated for, or found to engaged in, sexual harm or gender-based violence in the course of previous employment or in a legal process. Similarly, your ongoing employment is conditional upon the satisfactory maintenance of all relevant clearances and background check requirements. If you do not meet these conditions, the University may take any necessary step, including the termination of your employment.   EEO statement   At the University of Sydney, our shared values are trust, accountability and excellence and we strive to be a place where everyone can thrive. We are committed to creating a University community that thrives through diversity and reflects the wider community that we serve. We deliver on this through our commitment to diversity and inclusion , evidenced by our people and culture programs, as well as key strategies to increase participation and support the careers of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander People , women , people living with a disability , people from culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds , and those who identify as LGBTIQ+ .   We are proud to be recognised as an Australian Workplace Equality Index (AWEI) Gold employer. Find out more about our work on diversity and inclusion .  How to apply Applications (including a cover letter, CV, and any additional supporting documentation) can be submitted via the Apply button at the top of the page.  This is an identified opportunity for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander people. Applicants will be asked to provide confirmation of Aboriginal and/or Torres Strait Islander identity as part of the application process. We understand that everyone applies differently. If you’d prefer to have an informal conversation before applying, we welcome you to get in touch so we can talk through the role and process. In addition, if you require reasonable adjustment or any documents in alternate formats, please contact Broni Crear, Indigenous Recruitment Consultant by email to Bronwyn.crear@sydney.edu.au © The University of Sydney Click to view the Position Description for this role. Applications Close Wednesday 11 November 2026 11:59 PM"
    },
    "https://usyd.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/USYD_EXTERNAL_CAREER_SITE/job/Camperdown-Campus/Senior-Museum-Registration-Officer---Acquisitions-and-Loans_0157678-2": {
      "title": "Senior Museum Registration Officer - Acquisitions and Loans",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-29\nPart time\n\n3 days part-time, continuing opportunity Unique opportunity for an experienced Senior Museum Registration Officer – Acquisitions and Loans, to work in the University’s Chau Chak Wing Museum Base Salary $107K + 17% superannuation   About the opportunity   The role plays a pivotal role in the stewardship of the Chau Chak Wing Museum's collections, leading the documentation and managing of complex inward and outward loans nationally and internationally, and the administration of acquisitions, including Cultural Gifts. It ensures collection records are accurate, compliant, and accessible, while supporting the safe movement and effective management of collection objects throughout their lifecycle.   As part of the Collections team, the position contributes to collection care, exhibitions, storage, packing, transport, freight and location control, and provides specialist input into policies, procedures, and documentation practices that support best-practice collections management and governance.   About you   extensive experience managing museum or gallery collections databases, including advanced proficiency in EMu, data management, reporting, data migration, and quality assurance processes well-developed analytical and problem-solving skills, with the ability to improve systems, workflows, and documentation practices to enhance collection integrity and accessibility proven ability to build effective relationships and collaborate with internal and external stakeholders experience overseeing the registration, movement, packing, storage, and location control of museum objects, ensuring adherence to professional and industry standards sound knowledge of collection care principles, including preventive conservation practices, quarantine procedures, and collection handling requirements demonstrated ability to manage competing priorities, coordinate complex projects, and deliver outcomes within deadlines in a dynamic environment strong written and verbal communication skills, including the ability to prepare reports, procedures, guidelines, and collection management documentation a commitment to workplace health and safety practices, including the safe handling and movement of collection objects.     Sponsorship / work rights for Australia   You must have unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this employment to be eligible to apply. Visa sponsorship is not available for this appointment.     Pre-employment checks and declarations       Your employment is conditional upon the successful completion of all pre-employment or background checks required for the role in terms satisfactory to the University.  Also, to meet the University’s obligations under the National Higher Education Code to Prevent and Eliminate Gender-Based Violence you will be asked to declare if you have been investigated for, or found to engaged in, sexual harm or gender-based violence in the course of previous employment or in a legal process. Similarly, your ongoing employment is conditional upon the satisfactory maintenance of all relevant clearances and background check requirements. If you do not meet these conditions, the University may take any necessary step, including the termination of your employment.     EEO statement     At the University of Sydney, our shared values are trust, accountability and excellence and we strive to be a place where everyone can thrive. We are committed to creating a University community that thrives through diversity and reflects the wider community that we serve. We deliver on this through our commitment to diversity and inclusion , evidenced by our people and culture programs, as well as key strategies to increase participation and support the careers of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander People , women , people living with a disability , people from culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds , and those who identify as LGBTQIA+ . We welcome applications from candidates from all backgrounds.    We are proud to be recognised as an Australian Workplace Equality Index (AWEI) Platinum Employer. Find out more about our work on diversity and inclusion .    How to apply   Applications (including a cover letter, CV, and any additional supporting documentation) can be submitted via the Apply button at the top of the page.  Applications without a cover letter will not be considered.   For employees of the University or contingent workers, please login into your Workday account and navigate to the Career icon on your Dashboard. Click on USYD Find Jobs and apply.   For a confidential discussion about the role, or if you require reasonable adjustment or any documents in alternate formats, please contact Riviera Son, Recruitment Operations by email to riviera.son@sydney.edu.au.   © The University of Sydney   The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment.   Click to view the Position Description for this role. Applications Close Sunday 15 November 2026 11:59 PM"
    },
    "https://usyd.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/USYD_EXTERNAL_CAREER_SITE/job/Newtown/Privacy-Advisor_0158604-2": {
      "title": "Privacy Advisor",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-29\nFull time\n\nFull-time, fixed-term opportunity until December 2027 Located on Camperdown Campus - Hybrid working arrangements available Base salary from $131,108 plus 17% superannuation   About the opportunity Archives and Records Management Services (ARMS) is seeking a Privacy Advisor to join our small, collaborative privacy team.   The Privacy Advisor will support the University’s privacy compliance function by assessing and triaging requests from internal and external stakeholders, providing timely privacy advice, and managing internal review and information access requests under relevant privacy legislation. The role will also develop practical privacy resources for staff and assist Senior Privacy Specialists with privacy impact assessments where required. This role requires strong knowledge of relevant legislation and high-level communication, administrative and organisational skills to support privacy compliance and risk mitigation.     Your key responsibilities will be to: assess and triage incoming privacy enquiries and requests from internal and external stakeholders provide timely, practical advice on privacy legislation and compliance with the University’s privacy governance framework manage internal review and information access requests under the Privacy and Personal Information Protection Act 1998 (PPIP Act) and the Health Records and Information Privacy Act 2002 (HRIP Act) assist Senior Privacy Specialists with privacy impact assessments where required, including liaising with relevant stakeholders develop practical privacy resources and guidance for staff and contribute to privacy training and awareness activities support the implementation of Privacy by Design and develop tools and documentation to help staff identify and mitigate privacy risks in projects and business-as-usual processes   About you professional experience within the tertiary education or government sector is desirable proven experience working as a Privacy Advisor, or similar, working with privacy legislation within a large and multi-faceted organisation strong communication and stakeholder engagement skills, with experience drafting high-level documents, providing excellent customer service and delivering presentations high-level understanding of Privacy by Design and knowledge information access and recordkeeping legislation comfortable assessing and understanding privacy implications of new and emerging technologies knowledge of the Government Information (Public Access) Act 2009, Privacy and Personal Information Protection Act 1998 and the Health Records and Personal Information Privacy Act 2002     Sponsorship / work rights for Australia You must have unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this employment to be eligible to apply. Visa sponsorship is not available for this appointment.   Pre-employment checks and declarations   Your employment is conditional upon the successful completion of all pre-employment or background checks required for the role in terms satisfactory to the University.  Also, to meet the University’s obligations under the National Higher Education Code to Prevent and Eliminate Gender-Based Violence you will be asked to declare if you have been investigated for, or found to engaged in, sexual harm or gender-based violence in the course of previous employment or in a legal process. Similarly, your ongoing employment is conditional upon the satisfactory maintenance of all relevant clearances and background check requirements. If you do not meet these conditions, the University may take any necessary step, including the termination of your employment.   EEO statement   At the University of Sydney, our shared values are trust, accountability and excellence and we strive to be a place where everyone can thrive. We are committed to creating a University community that thrives through diversity and reflects the wider community that we serve. We deliver on this through our commitment to diversity and inclusion , evidenced by our people and culture programs, as well as key strategies to increase participation and support the careers of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander People , women , people living with a disability , people from culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds , and those who identify as LGBTQIA+ . We welcome applications from candidates from all backgrounds.    We are proud to be recognised as an Australian Workplace Equality Index (AWEI) Platinum Employer. Find out more about our work on diversity and inclusion .    How to apply Applications (including a cover letter, CV, and any additional supporting documentation) can be submitted via the Apply button at the top of the page.     For employees of the University or contingent workers, please log in into your Workday account and navigate to the Career icon on your Dashboard.  Click on USYD Find Jobs and apply.   For a confidential discussion about the role, or if you require a reasonable adjustment or any documents in alternate formats, please contact Broni Crear, Recruitment Operations by email to bronwyn.crear@sydney.edu.au   Click to view the Position Description for this role.   © The University of Sydney   The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Applications Close Sunday 08 November 2026 11:59 PM"
    },
    "https://usyd.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/USYD_EXTERNAL_CAREER_SITE/job/Camperdown-Campus/Postdoctoral-Research-Associate-in-Immunotherapy_0158812-1": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Research Associate in Immunotherapy",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-29\nFull time\n\nFull time, fixed term until December 2029     Exciting opportunity to be a Postdoctoral Research Associate with Professor Cameron Turtle   Base Salary starting from $117,936 + 17% superannuation     About the opportunity   We are seeking a Postdoctoral Research Associate in Immunotherapy to contribute to the School’s research agenda through independent and collaborative research. You will apply and develop your research expertise to advance knowledge within your discipline, conduct original research and scholarly activities, and further develop your research profile while working under broad supervision.   This role offers the opportunity to collaborate with academic colleagues and research partners, provide informal mentoring and advice to students, and contribute to the broader academic activities and strategic priorities of the School and Faculty.     Your key responsibilities will be to:   conduct research and scholarly or professional activities independently or as part of a team, contributing to knowledge and advancement within your discipline develop and refine independent research skills under the guidance of more senior academic staff provide informal mentoring and advice to students within your discipline undertake administrative tasks and contribute to Faculty and School meetings contribute to a positive workplace culture that values diversity of thought, collaboration, excellence and outcomes support and contribute to the University’s and Faculty’s strategic priorities undertake other duties and projects appropriate to the classification of the role.   About you   doctorate or PhD in a relevant field expertise in T cell immunology and analyses of transcription factors in CAR T cells would be an advantage demonstrated understanding of the relevant subject matter and experience conducting original research and/or scholarly activities a developing research profile within your discipline or area of expertise experience developing networks with academic, industry and professional partners or stakeholders strong research, planning and organisational skills effective written and verbal communication skills, including the ability to deliver presentations computer literacy and the ability to work collaboratively with colleagues and stakeholders.   Sponsorship / work rights for Australia   Work Rights: you must have unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this employment to apply. Visa sponsorship is not available for this appointment.     Pre-employment checks   Your employment is conditional upon the successful completion of all pre-employment or background checks required for the role in terms satisfactory to the University. Also, to meet the University’s obligations under the National Higher Education Code to Prevent and Eliminate Gender-Based Violence you will be asked to declare if you have been investigated for, or found to engaged in, sexual harm or gender-based violence in the course of previous employment or in a legal process. Similarly, your ongoing employment is conditional upon the satisfactory maintenance of all relevant clearances and background check requirements. If you do not meet these conditions, the University may take any necessary step, including the termination of your employment.     EEO statement     At the University of Sydney, our shared values are trust, accountability and excellence and we strive to be a place where everyone can thrive. We are committed to creating a University community that thrives through diversity and reflects the wider community that we serve. We deliver on this through our commitment to diversity and inclusion , evidenced by our people and culture programs, as well as key strategies to increase participation and support the careers of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander People , women , people living with a disability , people from culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds , and those who identify as LGBTIQ+ . We welcome applications from candidates from all backgrounds.    We are proud to be recognised as an Australian Workplace Equality Index (AWEI) Gold employer. Find out more about our work on diversity and inclusion .    How to apply   Applications (including a cover letter, CV, and any additional supporting documentation) can be submitted via the Apply button at the top of the page.     For employees of the University or contingent workers, please login into your Workday account and navigate to the Career icon on your Dashboard.  Click on USYD Find Jobs and apply.   For a confidential discussion about the role, or if you require reasonable adjustment or any documents in alternate formats, please contact, Ajaypal Singh, Recruitment Operations by email to ajaypal.singh@sydney.edu.au.   © The University of Sydney   The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment.   Click to view the Position Description for this role. Applications Close Wednesday 11 November 2026 11:59 PM"
