    "https://usyd.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/USYD_EXTERNAL_CAREER_SITE/job/Darlington-Campus/Postdoctoral-Research-Associate---School-of-Medical-Sciences_0158832-1": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Research Associate - School of Medical Sciences",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-29\nFull time\n\nFull time, fixed term until March 2029 Exciting opportunity to be a Postdoctoral Research Associate with Professor Margaret Sunde   Base Salary starting from $117,936 + 17% superannuation     About the opportunity   The School of Medical Sciences is recognised as one of the leading centres for medical science education in the world. With over 100 years of excellence in education, we are proud to be training the next generation of medical scientists who will be leading members of the medical research community with an innovative integrated curriculum. Our team of researchers lead key internationally competitive research from molecules to medicines, from patient to policy across a range of therapeutic areas.   We are seeking a Postdoctoral Research Associate to contribute to the research agenda of the School through independent and collaborative research. You will apply and develop your expertise to advance knowledge within your discipline, conduct original research and scholarly activities, and further develop your research profile while working under broad supervision.   This role offers the opportunity to collaborate with academic colleagues and research partners, provide informal mentoring and advice to students, and contribute to the broader academic activities and strategic priorities of the School and Faculty.     Your key responsibilities will be to:   conduct research and scholarly or professional activities independently or as part of a team, contributing to knowledge within your discipline develop and refine independent research skills under the supervision of more senior academic staff provide informal mentoring and advice to students within your discipline carry out administrative tasks and contribute to Faculty and School meetings contribute to a positive workplace culture that values diversity of thought, collaboration, excellence and the achievement of outcomes support and contribute to the University’s and Faculty’s strategic priorities undertake other duties and contribute to projects appropriate to the classification level.   About you   doctorate or PhD in a relevant field demonstrated understanding of the relevant subject matter and experience conducting original research and/or scholarly activities a developing research profile within your discipline or area of expertise experience developing networks with academic, industry and professional partners or stakeholders strong research, planning and organisational skills effective written and verbal communication skills, including the ability to deliver presentations computer literacy and the ability to work collaboratively with colleagues and stakeholders.   Sponsorship / work rights for Australia   Work Rights: you must have unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this employment to apply. Visa sponsorship is not available for this appointment.     Pre-employment checks   Your employment is conditional upon the successful completion of all pre-employment or background checks required for the role in terms satisfactory to the University. Also, to meet the University’s obligations under the National Higher Education Code to Prevent and Eliminate Gender-Based Violence you will be asked to declare if you have been investigated for, or found to engaged in, sexual harm or gender-based violence in the course of previous employment or in a legal process. Similarly, your ongoing employment is conditional upon the satisfactory maintenance of all relevant clearances and background check requirements. If you do not meet these conditions, the University may take any necessary step, including the termination of your employment.     EEO statement     At the University of Sydney, our shared values are trust, accountability and excellence and we strive to be a place where everyone can thrive. We are committed to creating a University community that thrives through diversity and reflects the wider community that we serve. We deliver on this through our commitment to diversity and inclusion , evidenced by our people and culture programs, as well as key strategies to increase participation and support the careers of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander People , women , people living with a disability , people from culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds , and those who identify as LGBTIQ+ . We welcome applications from candidates from all backgrounds.    We are proud to be recognised as an Australian Workplace Equality Index (AWEI) Gold employer. Find out more about our work on diversity and inclusion .    How to apply   Applications (including a cover letter, CV, and any additional supporting documentation) can be submitted via the Apply button at the top of the page.     For employees of the University or contingent workers, please login into your Workday account and navigate to the Career icon on your Dashboard.  Click on USYD Find Jobs and apply.   For a confidential discussion about the role, or if you require reasonable adjustment or any documents in alternate formats, please contact, Ajaypal Singh, Recruitment Operations by email to ajaypal.singh@sydney.edu.au.   © The University of Sydney   The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Click to view the Position Description for this role. Applications Close Wednesday 11 November 2026 11:59 PM"
    },
    "https://usyd.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/USYD_EXTERNAL_CAREER_SITE/job/Darlington-Campus/Design-Assurance-Architect_0158935": {
      "title": "Design Assurance Architect",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-29\nPart time\n\nCasual Design Assurance Architect opportunity with University Infrastructure Strong experience in architectural design, design management, technical design review, and/or design assurance required HEO 10 Step 1 $111.24 per hour plus superannuation   About the opportunity   The Design Assurance Architect will be responsible for undertaking detailed design compliance and coordination reviews to support the successful delivery of the SBA project. The role focuses on assessing project documentation against the contractual requirements, endorsed design outcomes, and stakeholder-approved room requirements   The successful candidate will possess strong architectural and technical review capabilities, exceptional attention to detail, and a thorough understanding of design documentation coordination, compliance assessment, and stakeholder management. The role requires the ability to critically analyse complex project information, identify gaps and inconsistencies, and ensure all contract requirements and endorsed stakeholder outcomes are effectively translated into the final project design.   Your key responsibilities will be to:   reviewing contract drawings, specifications, schedules, and other design documentation for compliance with project requirements and approved design intent undertaking detailed assessments of Room Layout Sheets (RLS) and Room Data Sheets (RDS) issued by the Project Architect, ensuring alignment with the contract documents and project requirements reviewing Project User Group (PUG) and Working Group (WG) endorsement packages to verify that endorsed stakeholder requirements, comments, and decisions have been appropriately incorporated into the design documentation identifying omissions, inconsistencies, non-conformances, and potential design risks across contract documents, RLSs, RDSs, and endorsed stakeholder requirements verifying that contractual obligations, functional requirements, operational requirements, and endorsed stakeholder outcomes are fully addressed within the contractor's design development submissions preparing clear and comprehensive review comments, design compliance reports, and issue registers for issuance to the Contractor for action and resolution coordinating and consolidating feedback from multiple stakeholders to provide a single, structured review response to the Contractor maintaining an issues and actions register, tracking the resolution of review comments and ensuring matters are addressed in a timely and satisfactory manner monitoring the incorporation of endorsed comments and agreed design changes throughout the design development and design finalisation process working closely with project stakeholders, consultants, and the Contractor to resolve design issues and achieve compliance with project requirements providing regular updates to the Senior Design Manager on design review outcomes, key risks, unresolved issues, and emerging compliance concerns supporting project governance processes by maintaining accurate audit trails, records of reviews, endorsements, decisions, and issue close-out documentation   About you   degree in Architecture or equivalent architectural qualification registered Architect (preferred) minimum 10 years' experience in architectural design, design management, technical design review, or design assurance roles     Sponsorship / work rights for Australia Visa sponsorship is not available for this position.  This role requires valid work rights for Australia.   Pre-employment checks and declarations   Your employment is conditional upon the successful completion of all pre-employment or background checks required for the role in terms satisfactory to the University.  Also, to meet the University’s obligations under the National Higher Education Code to Prevent and Eliminate Gender-Based Violence you will be asked to declare if you have been investigated for, or found to engaged in, sexual harm or gender-based violence in the course of previous employment or in a legal process. Similarly, your ongoing employment is conditional upon the satisfactory maintenance of all relevant clearances and background check requirements. If you do not meet these conditions, the University may take any necessary step, including the termination of your employment.   EEO statement   At the University of Sydney, our shared values are trust, accountability and excellence and we strive to be a place where everyone can thrive. We are committed to creating a University community that thrives through diversity and reflects the wider community that we serve. We deliver on this through our commitment to diversity and inclusion , evidenced by our people and culture programs, as well as key strategies to increase participation and support the careers of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander People , women , people living with a disability , people from culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds , and those who identify as LGBTQIA+ . We welcome applications from candidates from all backgrounds.    We are proud to be recognised as an Australian Workplace Equality Index (AWEI) Platinum Employer. Find out more about our work on diversity and inclusion .    How to apply Applications (including a cover letter, CV, and any additional supporting documentation) can be submitted via the Apply button at the top of the page.    For employees of the University or contingent workers, please login into your Workday account and navigate to the Career icon on your Dashboard.  Click on USYD Find Jobs and apply.   For a confidential discussion about the role, or if you require reasonable adjustment or any documents in alternate formats, please contact Michael Abelardo , Recruitment Consultant Recruitment Operations, by email to casual-recruitment.admin@sydney.edu.au    © The University of Sydney   The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment Click to view the Position Description for this role. Applications Close Wednesday 04 November 2026 11:59 PM"
    },
    "https://usyd.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/USYD_EXTERNAL_CAREER_SITE/job/Camperdown-Campus/Faculty-Research-Infrastructure-Officer---FMH_0158286-2": {
      "title": "Faculty Research Infrastructure Officer - FMH",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-29\nFull time\n\nFull time, fixed term until March 2029 opportunity for a Faculty Research Infrastructure Officer providing administrative and technical support to researchers within FMH Assist in managing projects for implementation of FMH strategic initiatives for developing, maintaining and utilising faculty’s research infrastructure Support the facilitation of the planning, transition, and completion processes for FMH researchers participating in the SBA program Base Salary $107,312 + 17% superannuation  About the opportunity The Faculty of Medicine and Health is dedicated to driving innovation, excellence, and strategic impact across teaching, research, and clinical practice. Our work extends beyond traditional medicine and health to embrace all areas where we can make a meaningful difference. We challenge conventional thinking and actively seek new approaches to improve health and wellbeing. Our mission is to redefine how wellness and healthcare are delivered, creating a lasting impact locally and globally. Guided by curiosity, collaboration, and excellence, we are shaping the future of healthcare.   The Research Infrastructure Team within the Faculty of Medicine and Health is seeking a Faculty Research Infrastructure Officer to support the Faculty’s strategic initiatives in developing, maintaining, and optimising research infrastructure. The role involves providing both administrative and technical support to ensure the effective operation and utilisation of research facilities and resources. The successful candidate will assist researchers in preparing competitive equipment grant applications, and provide guidance on pre- and post-award grant management. The position also offers operational and strategic advice on research infrastructure and related matters to researchers and other key stakeholders within the Faculty.   This is a dynamic and collaborative role requiring strong technical understanding, organisational skills, and experience in research administration. It should be noted that the position does not involve conducting laboratory experiments or direct research activities.   Your key responsibilities will be to: help identify core research equipment infrastructure for the Faculty of Medicine and Health, including identifying and establishing technological priorities for shared use collate information to establish an inventory of FMH level equipment with purchase, commissioning, operational management, repair, and decommissioning details provide support related to the implementation of change programs and strategic reviews for improving the utilisation of Faculty’s research equipment and space coordinates and supports the FMH researchers participating in the SBA program, including planning, resource allocation and implementation complete data collection for auditing / cataloguing assets / research equipment and hazardous materials/chemicals for FMH research space if required apply sound technical knowledge and awareness for equipment operations and maintenance work with the research support teams to prepare submissions and applications to secure funding for research infrastructure. About you To be successful in this role, you have: experience with working in research-intensive environments or research facilities demonstrated administrative and communications skills the ability to travel within multiple sites within precincts numeracy skills together with expenditure reconciliation demonstrated project management skills experience with the development of protocols and manual in accordance with regulatory standards demonstrated ability to interpret and implement policy and procedures and in establishing and reviewing administrative processes excellent time and workload management skills with demonstrated ability to prioritise effectively, manage multiple tasks, meet deadlines, and achieve required outcomes. Sponsorship / work rights for Australia You must have unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this employment to be eligible to apply. Visa sponsorship is not available for this appointment. Pre-employment checks Your employment is conditional upon the completion of all role required pre-employment or background checks in terms satisfactory to the University. Similarly, your ongoing employment is conditional upon the satisfactory maintenance of all relevant clearances and background check requirements. If you do not meet these conditions, the University may take any necessary step, including the termination of your employment. EEO statement   At the University of Sydney, our shared values are trust, accountability and excellence and we strive to be a place where everyone can thrive. We are committed to creating a University community that thrives through diversity and reflects the wider community that we serve. We deliver on this through our commitment to diversity and inclusion , evidenced by our people and culture programs, as well as key strategies to increase participation and support the careers of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander People , women , people living with a disability , people from culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds , and those who identify as LGBTQIA+ . We welcome applications from candidates from all backgrounds.  We are proud to be recognised as an Australian Workplace Equality Index (AWEI) Platinum Employer. Find out more about our work on diversity and inclusion .  How to apply Applications (including a cover letter, CV, and any additional supporting documentation) can be submitted via the Apply button at the top of the page.   For employees of the University or contingent workers, please login into your Workday account and navigate to the Career icon on your Dashboard.  Click on USYD Find Jobs and apply. For a confidential discussion about the role, or if you require reasonable adjustment or any documents in alternate formats, please contact Lena Germinarios, Recruitment Operations by email to recruitmentmedandhealth@sydney.edu.au © The University of Sydney The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment.   Click to view the Position Description for this role. Applications Close Wednesday 04 November 2026 11:59 PM"
    },
    "https://usyd.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/USYD_EXTERNAL_CAREER_SITE/job/Darlington-Campus/Human-Resources-Partner_0158557-3": {
      "title": "Human Resources Partner",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-29\nFull time\n\nFull time continuing opportunity for a HR Partner to provide strategic HR expertise and advice in support of your client’s business objectives and the University’s strategy Collaborate with the other HR Centres of Expertise to deliver an integrated service offering to The University Base Salary $152,045 + 17% superannuation    About the opportunity   The HR Partnering function works alongside leaders to translate the University’s workforce strategy into practical people outcomes. We provide contemporary, evidence-based and pragmatic HR advice that helps leaders navigate complex workforce matters, make informed decisions and deliver on their strategic priorities.   We are seeking an experienced HR Partner to join our team who can build trusted relationships with leaders while remaining comfortable working hands-on across a diverse and fast-paced portfolio. You will bring sound judgement, curiosity and a solutions-focused mindset, balancing organisational objectives, employee experience, risk and compliance when providing advice.   A key part of the role is turning complex HR, industrial relations and policy requirements into clear, practical and actionable advice. You will work with stakeholders to understand the issue behind the issue, assess available evidence and options, and provide advice that is grounded in the Enterprise Agreement, University policy, relevant employment frameworks and contemporary HR practice.   The role operates in an environment where priorities can shift quickly, and case volumes can be high. You will therefore need to be comfortable managing multiple matters concurrently, prioritising effectively, maintaining accurate records and ensuring stakeholders receive timely, considered advice.   Your key responsibilities will include:   partner with leaders to understand their workforce priorities and provide pragmatic, evidence-based HR advice that can be applied in real-world situations provide clear advice on complex people matters, translating policy, industrial instruments and HR frameworks into practical options and recommendations for stakeholders manage a high-volume and varied portfolio of HR matters, applying sound judgement to assess urgency, risk and competing priorities maintain high-quality case records and documentation, ensuring advice, decisions, actions and key stakeholder interactions are appropriately captured work collaboratively with HR Centres of Expertise and other HR colleagues to develop integrated, practical and client-focused solutions provide strategic and operational advice across workforce planning, organisational design, talent and succession, performance, workplace culture, reward and recognition and organisational change support leaders through workforce and organisational change, helping them navigate both the people and operational implications of change use workforce insights, evidence and contemporary HR practice to identify emerging issues, challenge assumptions and support informed decision-making build the capability and confidence of leaders to manage people matters effectively, rather than simply providing transactional HR solutions contribute to continuous improvement across HR Partnering by sharing knowledge, identifying opportunities to simplify ways of working and supporting consistent HR practice support the Senior Manager and Associate Director with broader portfolio and HR initiatives, including capability development, coaching and implementation of improved frameworks, technology and processes.   About you   You are an experienced HR professional who combines strong technical HR capability with commercially minded, pragmatic judgement. You are comfortable moving between strategic workforce conversations and complex day-to-day people matters, and you know when an issue requires deeper analysis, escalation or specialist input.   You will bring:   substantial HR partnering experience within a large, complex and multifaceted organisation, ideally within a tertiary education or Centre of Expertise operating model demonstrated ability to provide clear, pragmatic and solutions-focused advice, grounded in evidence, policy, industrial instruments and relevant employment frameworks strong experience interpreting and applying enterprise agreements, awards, employment legislation and organisational policies to complex workplace situations the ability to work effectively in a high-case-load environment, balancing competing priorities and maintaining a responsive, high-quality service to stakeholders excellent time management, organisation and prioritisation skills, including the ability to exercise judgement about urgency, risk and appropriate escalation strong case management and record-keeping discipline, with the ability to maintain clear, accurate and contemporaneous documentation demonstrated experience supporting organisational change, workforce planning and the delivery of complex people initiatives the confidence to respectfully challenge thinking, test assumptions and provide leaders with balanced options and recommendations highly developed stakeholder management and influencing skills, with the ability to establish credibility and trusted relationships with senior leaders a collaborative approach to working across HR Partnering, specialist HR teams and other professional functions to provide consistent and joined-up advice sound judgement and discretion when managing sensitive, confidential and complex people matters experience engaging with unions, employee representatives, government bodies or other external stakeholders where required experience supporting medical, health or allied health environments would be highly regarded.   Sponsorship / work rights for Australia   You must have unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this employment to be eligible to apply. Visa sponsorship is not available for this appointment.     Pre-employment checks and declarations     Your employment is conditional upon the successful completion of all pre-employment or background checks required for the role in terms satisfactory to the University.  Also, to meet the University’s obligations under the National Higher Education Code to Prevent and Eliminate Gender-Based Violence you will be asked to declare if you have been investigated for, or found to engaged in, sexual harm or gender-based violence in the course of previous employment or in a legal process. Similarly, your ongoing employment is conditional upon the satisfactory maintenance of all relevant clearances and background check requirements. If you do not meet these conditions, the University may take any necessary step, including the termination of your employment.   EEO statement     At the University of Sydney, our shared values are trust, accountability and excellence and we strive to be a place where everyone can thrive. We are committed to creating a University community that thrives through diversity and reflects the wider community that we serve. We deliver on this through our commitment to diversity and inclusion , evidenced by our people and culture programs, as well as key strategies to increase participation and support the careers of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander People , women , people living with a disability , people from culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds , and those who identify as LGBTQIA+ . We welcome applications from candidates from all backgrounds.    We are proud to be recognised as an Australian Workplace Equality Index (AWEI) Platinum Employer. Find out more about our work on diversity and inclusion .    How to apply   Applications (including a cover letter, CV, and any additional supporting documentation) can be submitted via the Apply button at the top of the page.     For employees of the University or contingent workers, please login into your Workday account and navigate to the Career icon on your Dashboard.  Click on USYD Find Jobs and apply.   For a confidential discussion about the role, or if you require reasonable adjustment or any documents in alternate formats, please contact Mouhamed El Chami, Recruitment Operations by email to Mouhamed.elchami@sydney.edu.au   © The University of Sydney   The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment Click to view the Position Description for this role. Applications Close Wednesday 11 November 2026 11:59 PM"
    },
    "https://usyd.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/USYD_EXTERNAL_CAREER_SITE/job/Darlington-Campus/HR-Partner_0156673-2": {
      "title": "HR Partner",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-26\nFull time\n\nFull time, 12months fixed-term opportunity for a HR Partnering professional to provide strategic HR expertise and advice in support of your client’s business objectives and the University’s strategy Collaborate with the other HR Centres of Expertise to deliver an integrated service offering to The University Base Salary $152,045 + 17% superannuation    About the opportunity The HR Partnering function works alongside leaders to translate the University’s workforce strategy into practical people outcomes. We provide contemporary, evidence-based and pragmatic HR advice that helps leaders navigate complex workforce matters, make informed decisions and deliver on their strategic priorities. We are seeking experienced HR Partners who can build trusted relationships with leaders while remaining comfortable working hands-on across a diverse and fast-paced portfolio. You will bring sound judgement, curiosity and a solutions-focused mindset, balancing organisational objectives, employee experience, risk and compliance when providing advice. A key part of the role is turning complex HR, industrial relations and policy requirements into clear, practical and actionable advice. You will work with stakeholders to understand the issue behind the issue, assess available evidence and options, and provide advice that is grounded in the Enterprise Agreement, University policy, relevant employment frameworks and contemporary HR practice. The role operates in an environment where priorities can shift quickly, and case volumes can be high. You will therefore need to be comfortable managing multiple matters concurrently, prioritising effectively, maintaining accurate records and ensuring stakeholders receive timely, considered advice. Your key responsibilities will include: partner with leaders to understand their workforce priorities and provide pragmatic, evidence-based HR advice that can be applied in real-world situations provide clear advice on complex people matters, translating policy, industrial instruments and HR frameworks into practical options and recommendations for stakeholders manage a high-volume and varied portfolio of HR matters, applying sound judgement to assess urgency, risk and competing priorities maintain high-quality case records and documentation, ensuring advice, decisions, actions and key stakeholder interactions are appropriately captured work collaboratively with HR Centres of Expertise and other HR colleagues to develop integrated, practical and client-focused solutions provide strategic and operational advice across workforce planning, organisational design, talent and succession, performance, workplace culture, reward and recognition and organisational change support leaders through workforce and organisational change, helping them navigate both the people and operational implications of change use workforce insights, evidence and contemporary HR practice to identify emerging issues, challenge assumptions and support informed decision-making build the capability and confidence of leaders to manage people matters effectively, rather than simply providing transactional HR solutions contribute to continuous improvement across HR Partnering by sharing knowledge, identifying opportunities to simplify ways of working and supporting consistent HR practice support the Senior Manager and Associate Director with broader portfolio and HR initiatives, including capability development, coaching and implementation of improved frameworks, technology and processes. About you You are an experienced HR professional who combines strong technical HR capability with commercially minded, pragmatic judgement. You are comfortable moving between strategic workforce conversations and complex day-to-day people matters, and you know when an issue requires deeper analysis, escalation or specialist input. You will bring: substantial HR partnering experience within a large, complex and multifaceted organisation, ideally within a tertiary education or Centre of Expertise operating model demonstrated ability to provide clear, pragmatic and solutions-focused advice, grounded in evidence, policy, industrial instruments and relevant employment frameworks strong experience interpreting and applying enterprise agreements, awards, employment legislation and organisational policies to complex workplace situations the ability to work effectively in a high-case-load environment, balancing competing priorities and maintaining a responsive, high-quality service to stakeholders excellent time management, organisation and prioritisation skills, including the ability to exercise judgement about urgency, risk and appropriate escalation strong case management and record-keeping discipline, with the ability to maintain clear, accurate and contemporaneous documentation demonstrated experience supporting organisational change, workforce planning and the delivery of complex people initiatives the confidence to respectfully challenge thinking, test assumptions and provide leaders with balanced options and recommendations highly developed stakeholder management and influencing skills, with the ability to establish credibility and trusted relationships with senior leaders a collaborative approach to working across HR Partnering, specialist HR teams and other professional functions to provide consistent and joined-up advice sound judgement and discretion when managing sensitive, confidential and complex people matters experience engaging with unions, employee representatives, government bodies or other external stakeholders where required experience supporting medical, health or allied health environments would be highly regarded.   Sponsorship / work rights for Australia   You must have unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this employment to be eligible to apply. Visa sponsorship is not available for this appointment.     Pre-employment checks and declarations       Your employment is conditional upon the successful completion of all pre-employment or background checks required for the role in terms satisfactory to the University.  Also, to meet the University’s obligations under the National Higher Education Code to Prevent and Eliminate Gender-Based Violence you will be asked to declare if you have been investigated for, or found to engaged in, sexual harm or gender-based violence in the course of previous employment or in a legal process. Similarly, your ongoing employment is conditional upon the satisfactory maintenance of all relevant clearances and background check requirements. If you do not meet these conditions, the University may take any necessary step, including the termination of your employment.   EEO statement     At the University of Sydney, our shared values are trust, accountability and excellence and we strive to be a place where everyone can thrive. We are committed to creating a University community that thrives through diversity and reflects the wider community that we serve. We deliver on this through our commitment to diversity and inclusion , evidenced by our people and culture programs, as well as key strategies to increase participation and support the careers of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander People , women , people living with a disability , people from culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds , and those who identify as LGBTQIA+ . We welcome applications from candidates from all backgrounds.    We are proud to be recognised as an Australian Workplace Equality Index (AWEI) Platinum Employer. Find out more about our work on diversity and inclusion .    How to apply   Applications (including a cover letter, CV, and any additional supporting documentation) can be submitted via the Apply button at the top of the page.     For employees of the University or contingent workers, please login into your Workday account and navigate to the Career icon on your Dashboard.  Click on USYD Find Jobs and apply.   For a confidential discussion about the role, or if you require reasonable adjustment or any documents in alternate formats, please contact Victoria Ford-Rogers, Recruitment Operations by email to victoria.ford-rogers@sydney.edu.au   © The University of Sydney   The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment Click to view the Position Description for this role. Applications Close Friday 06 November 2026 11:59 PM"
