    "https://usyd.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/USYD_EXTERNAL_CAREER_SITE/job/Camperdown-Campus/Investment-Manager_0157339": {
      "title": "Investment Manager",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-30\nFull time\n\nFull time, 3-year fixed-term opportunity Opportunity to play a central role in shaping and scaling the University’s venture investment ecosystem Base Salary $162,528 + 17% superannuation    About the opportunity   The University of Sydney operates a pre-seed fund and follow-on fund that supports the commercialisation of research and innovation through investment in early-stage ventures. As the Investment Manager , you will lead the operational management of the investment pipeline, ensuring opportunities are efficiently assessed, progressed and prepared for investment committee consideration.   Working across founders, researchers, Commercialisation Managers, Venture Advisers, external investors and senior university investment leaders, you will bring structure, rigour and scalability to the University's investment activities. You will oversee the systems, processes and coordination required to identify high-potential opportunities, manage due diligence and support informed investment decisions, helping transform innovative research into investable and impactful ventures.     Your key responsibilities will be to:   own and manage the end-to-end investment pipeline, progressing opportunities from intake through to committee readiness and investment completion efficiently and quickly assess and prioritise investment opportunities, applying structured evaluation frameworks to determine strategic fit and commercial potential lead and coordinate due diligence activities, synthesising technical, commercial and financial insights into clear investment recommendations oversee investment management systems and reporting, ensuring the quality, integrity and accessibility of investment and portfolio data prepare high-quality investment papers and presentations for committee consideration and decision-making drive process improvement initiatives that enhance investment efficiency, scalability and stakeholder experience   About you   As the Investment Manager, you will bring a combination of venture investment expertise, commercial acumen and stakeholder management capability, enabling you to confidently manage a complex pipeline of early-stage, mostly research-based investment opportunities.   demonstrated experience in venture capital, investment management, commercialisation, start-up or an early-stage innovation environment strong capability in investment evaluation, financial analysis, due diligence and business modelling experience preparing investment recommendations, reports or business cases for senior stakeholders and investment committees proven ability to manage multiple stakeholders and coordinate contributors across complex projects and workstreams empathy with the needs of researcher inventors and founders, both academics and students. experience using CRM, investment management and portfolio management systems, with a focus on data quality and reporting postgraduate qualifications in business, finance, science or engineering, or an equivalent combination of education, training and professional experience; candidates who combine technical domain knowledge with financial or commercial expertise are particularly encouraged to apply   We encourage applications from candidates who bring relevant experiences from adjacent fields including corporate venture, technology transfer, investment banking or venture studio. If you have a genuine interest in early-stage investment, venture building and commercialisation we would love to hear from you. We're keen to attract a diverse candidate pool and value the different perspectives and capabilities that individuals from varied backgrounds can bring to the team.     Sponsorship / work rights for Australia   You must have unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this employment to be eligible to apply. Visa sponsorship is not available for this appointment.     Pre-employment checks and declarations       Your employment is conditional upon the successful completion of all pre-employment or background checks required for the role in terms satisfactory to the University.  Also, to meet the University’s obligations under the National Higher Education Code to Prevent and Eliminate Gender-Based Violence you will be asked to declare if you have been investigated for, or found to engaged in, sexual harm or gender-based violence in the course of previous employment or in a legal process. Similarly, your ongoing employment is conditional upon the satisfactory maintenance of all relevant clearances and background check requirements. If you do not meet these conditions, the University may take any necessary step, including the termination of your employment.   EEO statement     At the University of Sydney, our shared values are trust, accountability and excellence and we strive to be a place where everyone can thrive. We are committed to creating a university community that thrives through diversity and reflects the wider community that we serve. We deliver on this through our commitment to diversity and inclusion , evidenced by our people and culture programs, as well as key strategies to increase participation and support the careers of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander People , women , people living with a disability , people from culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds , and those who identify as LGBTQIA+ . We welcome applications from candidates from all backgrounds.    We are proud to be recognised as an Australian Workplace Equality Index (AWEI) Platinum Employer. Find out more about our work on diversity and inclusion .    How to apply   Applications (including a cover letter, CV, and any additional supporting documentation) can be submitted via the Apply button at the top of the page.     For employees of the University or contingent workers, please login into your Workday account and navigate to the Career icon on your Dashboard.  Click on USYD Find Jobs and apply.   For a confidential discussion about the role, or if you require reasonable adjustment or any documents in alternate formats, please contact Kale Claffey, Recruitment Operations by email to kale.claffey@sydney.edu.au   © The University of Sydney   The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Click to view the Position Description for this role. Applications Close Sunday 22 November 2026 11:59 PM"
    },
    "https://usyd.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/USYD_EXTERNAL_CAREER_SITE/job/Darlington-Campus/Campus-Connect-Consultant_0156433-2": {
      "title": "Campus Connect Consultant",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-30\nFull time\n\nFull time, continuing opportunity. Located at the University Garage (H07) on Gadigal Country Exciting opportunity to utilise your excellent customer service skills to the Shared Service Centre team Hybrid working arrangements Base Salary HEO5, $93,345 - $104,515 p.a. + 17% superannuation      Indigenous Targeted Role    This is a Targeted Aboriginal and/or Torres Strait Islander Position. While we welcome applications from all candidates, this is a targeted role for Aboriginal and/or Torres Strait Islander people. As part of our commitment to diversity and inclusion, Aboriginal and/or Torres Strait Islander candidates will be assessed as a priority. This Exemption is claimed under Section 21 of the Anti-Discrimination Act 1977.      About the opportunity   The University of Sydney is welcoming applications for a Campus Connect Consultant to join the Travel and Expense (T&E) team to play a key role in delivering high-quality customer service, providing proactive problem-solving and -informed guidance in response to Travel and Expense-related enquiries from University of Sydney staff. Enquiries are received through multiple channels, including telephone, email, the service portal, and face-to-face interactions.   As part of the Travel and Expense team, the Campus Connect Consultant will apply a thorough understanding of University policies and procedures to provide informed and helpful responses across a wide range of services. Responsibilities include investigating, analysing, and resolving queries or processing tasks, as well as triaging and escalating cases that require further expert review. The role also involves contributing to the continuous improvement of service delivery by identifying opportunities to refine processes and enhance the customer experience in line with university objectives.   The position is part of a supportive and collaborative team of experienced professionals who are committed to delivering consistent and compliant Travel and Expense support across the University. With a shared dedication to providing reliable and effective assistance, the team takes pride in fostering a positive service culture—ensuring that staff are supported with care, professionalism, and a genuine commitment to excellence.   Additional responsibilities include the investigating, analysing, and resolving more complex enqueries and processing tasks. Where needed, the Campus Connect Consultant will triage and escalate  matters that require further expert analysis from the appropriate specialist for further review or resolution.       What success looks like:    respond promptly to customer enquiries, requests, and processing tasks received through various channels, ensuring a professional and customer-focused approach accurately assess and resolve  Travel and Expense enquiries by applying Knowledge Base articles and other relevant reference materials where appropriate use ServiceNow to create, manageand track tickets, including lodging customer enquiries or requests received via telephone or email maintain up-to-date and detailed ticket records, including enquiry type or category, resolution methods and outcomes, reasons for escalation to higher-tier support, and any requirements for further investigation provide clear and accurate advice on staff Travel and Expense-related enquiries, ensuring compliance with relevant policies and procedures administer all aspects of corporate cardmanagement, including application processing, issuing cards, security controls, compliance monitoring, cancellations, and dispute resolution as required.       About you:   tertiary qualifications in a relevant field or an equivalent combination of training and experience in Travel or Finance (highly desirable) an understanding of, and respect for, the diverse cultures, histories and perspectives of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples relevant experience in a service desk/contact centre or customer service environment, ideally within a tertiary education setting and in domains such as IT, HR, Finance, or Campus Infrastructure highly developed interpersonal and communication skills (both verbal and written), with in the ability to conveying information empathetically to both students and staff across all levels of the organisation proven experience handling client enquiries and delivering effective resolutions or appropriate escalations strong problem-solving, analytical and troubleshooting capabilities demonstrated initiative, with the capacity to work both collaboratively and independently the ability to  interpreting Finance policies and guidelines to deliver accurate and informed advice strong attention to detail and the ability to review information and providing clear, concise guidance on Travel and Expense matters confident navigating online systems, including Concur and other Finance-related platforms ability to work collaboratively with Travel and Expense staff members and management, as well as other groups, cohorts and stakeholders across the University.   Why work with us     We know our people are what make the University's mission possible. Alongside meaningful work, we offer a range of benefits designed to support you financially, personally and professionally.    Key benefits include:     Paid Cultural and Ceremonial Leave: Indigenous employees are entitled to 5 days of paid leave and 10 days of unpaid leave each year to participate in cultural and ceremonial activities and fulfil community obligations.  Career Development : Access to Indigenous professional and career development programs, including mentoring, leadership programs, and development funding  Community and Connection: Be part of a vibrant community with opportunities to connect through staff networks (our YURA Network), mentoring, cultural events and professional Development opportunities  Flexible Work and Exceptional Benefits: Enjoy flexible work arrangements, 17% employer superannuation, Fitness Passport, generous leave provisions, staff discounts and access to a wide range of University facilities and services.      Work Rights:   Please note: You must have unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this employment to apply. Visa sponsorship is not available for this appointment     Pre-employment checks   Your employment is conditional upon the successful completion of all pre-employment or background checks required for the role in terms satisfactory to the University.  Also, to meet the University’s obligations under the National Higher Education Code to Prevent and Eliminate Gender-Based Violence you will be asked to declare if you have been investigated for, or found to engaged in, sexual harm or gender-based violence in the course of previous employment or in a legal process. Similarly, your ongoing employment is conditional upon the satisfactory maintenance of all relevant clearances and background check requirements. If you do not meet these conditions, the University may take any necessary step, including the termination of your employment.      EEO statement     At the University of Sydney, our shared values are trust, accountability and excellence and we strive to be a place where everyone can thrive. We are committed to creating a University community that thrives through diversity and reflects the wider community that we serve. We deliver on this through our commitment to diversity and inclusion , evidenced by our people and culture programs, as well as key strategies to increase participation and support the careers of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander People , women , people living with a disability , people from culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds , and those who identify as LGBTIQ+ . We welcome applications from candidates from all backgrounds.    We are proud to be recognised as an Australian Workplace Equality Index (AWEI) Gold employer. Find out more about our work on diversity and inclusion .      How to apply   Applications (including a cover letter, CV, and any additional supporting documentation) can be submitted via the Apply button at the top of the page.  This is a targeted opportunity for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander people.  For employees of the University or contingent workers, please login into your Workday account and navigate to the Career icon on your Dashboard.  Click on USYD Find Jobs and apply.   For a confidential discussion about the role, or if you require reasonable adjustment or any documents in alternate formats, please contact Kevin Lim, Recruitment Operations by email to kevin.lim@sydney.edu.au.   © The University of Sydney   The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Click to view the Position Description for this role. Applications Close Sunday 15 November 2026 11:59 PM"
    },
    "https://usyd.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/USYD_EXTERNAL_CAREER_SITE/job/Sydney/Manager--Conservatorium-Open-Academy_0158251-2": {
      "title": "Manager, Conservatorium Open Academy",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-30\nFull time\n\nFull time continuing Opportunity to provide leadership in the strategic development and advancement of the Open Academy at the Sydney Conservatorium of Music HEO 9 Base Salary $152,045- $160,430 p.a. + 17% superannuation    About the opportunity   The Manager, Conservatorium Open Academy is responsible for overseeing all operations of the Open Academy unit. The position provides leadership in the strategic development and long-term advancement of the Open Academy, while also offering operational support to identify the most cost-effective and artistically appropriate ways to deliver outstanding continuing education and community engagement services.   Your key responsibilities will be to: lead the strategic direction and long-term growth of the Conservatorium Open Academy, driving innovative community engagement and lifelong learning initiatives across multiple locations oversee the day-to-day operations of the Open Academy, ensuring the successful delivery of programs, services, workshops, courses and events develop and implement business, operational and growth plans, working closely with Faculty leadership to achieve strategic and financial objectives manage budgets, financial planning, forecasting and reporting, ensuring effective stewardship of resources and compliance with funding and grant requirements lead, mentor and develop a multidisciplinary team, including a large cohort of casual teaching staff, fostering a high-performing and collaborative culture build and maintain strong relationships with internal stakeholders, industry partners, sponsors, artists and community organisations to enhance program impact and sustainability drive student recruitment, marketing and outreach initiatives to increase participation, raise public awareness and support revenue growth establish and strengthen partnerships with schools, regional conservatoriums and external organisations to expand engagement opportunities and support educational pathways oversee the planning and delivery of festivals, conferences, workshops and special projects, ensuring high-quality experiences and enhancing the Academy’s profile and reputation.   About you   demonstrated leadership experience with the ability to lead, mentor and develop high-performing teams, including staff supervision, coaching and performance management exceptional stakeholder engagement and relationship management skills, with a proven ability to influence, negotiate and collaborate with diverse internal and external partners strong project management capabilities, with the ability to manage multiple priorities, deliver outcomes to deadlines and drive continuous improvement initiatives proven experience in operational management, including establishing, implementing and reviewing administrative processes and procedures sound financial management skills, including budget development, monitoring, forecasting and resource allocation highly developed communication and interpersonal skills, with the ability to build effective partnerships and represent the organisation professionally strong organisational, critical thinking and decision-making skills, with the ability to adapt to changing priorities and navigate complex environments knowledge of the music education sector and arts industry, including familiarity with secondary and tertiary education environments and key stakeholder networks.   Sponsorship / work rights for Australia   Visa sponsorship is not available for this appointment. You must be an Australian citizen or a permanent resident to apply.   Pre-employment checks and declarations     Your employment is conditional upon the successful completion of all pre-employment or background checks required for the role in terms satisfactory to the University.  Also, to meet the University’s obligations under the National Higher Education Code to Prevent and Eliminate Gender-Based Violence you will be asked to declare if you have been investigated for, or found to engaged in, sexual harm or gender-based violence in the course of previous employment or in a legal process. Similarly, your ongoing employment is conditional upon the satisfactory maintenance of all relevant clearances and background check requirements. If you do not meet these conditions, the University may take any necessary step, including the termination of your employment.     This position is designated as involving child-related work. To undertake or remain in this position, you are required to apply for and obtain a Working with Children Check clearance in accordance with the Child Protection (Working with Children) Act 2012.   EEO statement     At the University of Sydney, our shared values are trust, accountability and excellence and we strive to be a place where everyone can thrive. We are committed to creating a University community that thrives through diversity and reflects the wider community that we serve. We deliver on this through our commitment to diversity and inclusion , evidenced by our people and culture programs, as well as key strategies to increase participation and support the careers of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander People , women , people living with a disability , people from culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds , and those who identify as LGBTQIA+ . We welcome applications from candidates from all backgrounds.    We are proud to be recognised as an Australian Workplace Equality Index (AWEI) Platinum Employer. Find out more about our work on diversity and inclusion .    How to apply   Applications (including a cover letter, CV, and any additional supporting documentation) can be submitted via the Apply button at the top of the page.     For employees of the University or contingent workers, please login into your Workday account and navigate to the Career icon on your Dashboard.  Click on USYD Find Jobs and apply.   For a confidential discussion about the role, or if you require reasonable adjustment or any documents in alternate formats, please contact Rae Hao, Recruitment Operations by email to rae.hao@sydney.edu.au.   © The University of Sydney   The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Click to view the Position Description for this role. Applications Close Sunday 15 November 2026 11:59 PM"
    },
    "https://usyd.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/USYD_EXTERNAL_CAREER_SITE/job/Camperdown-Campus/Proposal-Assurance-and-Submissions-Officer_0158121-2": {
      "title": "Proposal Assurance and Submissions Officer",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-30\nFull time\n\nWork in a fast-paced and dynamic environment within the Research Pre-Award team Full time, fixed term contract until December 2027 Base salary $117,133 - $127,610 (HEO7) + 17% superannuation Hybrid working arrangement available   About the opportunity   The Pipeline and Pre Award team in DVC Research are on the hunt for another Proposal Assurance & Submission Officer to join their squad!   In this role, you will manage the proposal submission administrative services and offer operational support for the Pre-Award team. You will be leading support for funding proposals and submissions for allocated schemes and providing guidance and escalation support to Proposal Assurance & Submission Administrators.   If you enjoy working in a fast-paced environment and want to be surrounded by a high performing team, then we encourage you to read on and then apply if this sounds like you!   Your key responsibilities will be to:   work in the Pipeline and Pre-Award team, reporting to the to Associate Director, Proposal Assurance & Submissions lead the administration submission requirements for allocated schemes by ensuring funding proposal and submission are compliant and meet quality assurance expectations maintain an accurate and systematic database of funding proposals and submissions understand the complexities of the submissions environment and provide insights, guidance, and regular reporting to the Pipeline and Pre-Awards Teams comply with funding rules and regulatory requirements contribute to the Research Support Community of Practice through trusted stakeholder management.   About you   administrative experience in a complex and fast-paced environment excellent negotiating, relationship building, and stakeholder management skills sound judgement, initiative, and discretion with the ability to manage multiple priorities concurrently high-level attention to detail and problem-solving skills excellent computer skills including Microsoft office, databases, excel programs and web-based software interest in, or experience working in, a research-intensive higher education environment.     Sponsorship / work rights for Australia   You must have unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this employment to be eligible to apply. Visa sponsorship is not available for this appointment.   Pre-employment checks   Your employment is conditional upon the completion of all role required pre-employment or background checks in terms satisfactory to the University. Similarly, your ongoing employment is conditional upon the satisfactory maintenance of all relevant clearances and background check requirements. If you do not meet these conditions, the University may take any necessary step, including the termination of your employment.   EEO statement     At the University of Sydney, our shared values are trust, accountability and excellence and we strive to be a place where everyone can thrive. We are committed to creating a University community that thrives through diversity and reflects the wider community that we serve. We deliver on this through our commitment to diversity and inclusion , evidenced by our people and culture programs, as well as key strategies to increase participation and support the careers of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander People , women , people living with a disability , people from culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds , and those who identify as LGBTIQ+ . We welcome applications from candidates from all backgrounds.    We are proud to be recognised as an Australian Workplace Equality Index (AWEI) Gold employer. Find out more about our work on diversity and inclusion .    How to apply   Applications (including a cover letter, CV, and any additional supporting documentation) can be submitted via the Apply button at the top of the page.     For employees of the University or contingent workers, please login into your Workday account and navigate to the Career icon on your Dashboard.  Click on USYD Find Jobs and apply.   For a confidential discussion about the role, or if you require reasonable adjustment or any documents in alternate formats, please contact Nicole Turle, Recruitment Operations by email at nicole.turle@sydney.edu.au   © The University of Sydney   The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment.   Click to view the Position Description for this role. Applications Close Sunday 08 November 2026 11:59 PM"
    }
